export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"takeoffcraft","name":"TakeoffCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Novidades da 0.7","summary":"Desenhos DWG e DXF abrem como folhas com a própria escala, contagens e comprimentos exatos tirados do próprio desenho, a proposta escolhida do subempreiteiro como preço de um pacote, convites de proposta com preços lacrados, ferramentas de assistente para desenhos e USD a partir da vista de conferência em 3D.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["novidades","notas da versão","histórico de alterações","mudanças","0.7","versão","novos recursos","dwg","dxf","desenho","layouts","espaço modelo","escala do desenho","contagem a partir do desenho","contagem cad","blocos","blocos dinâmicos","atributos","camadas","xref","desenho vinculado","medir desenho","polilinha","hachura","pdf marcado","proposta escolhida","usar uma proposta","enviar para propostas","convite de proposta","propostas lacradas","abertura das propostas","proposta atrasada","cadastro de subempreiteiros","csv","assistente","mcp","usd","usdz","unidades","listas de unidades","compacto","painéis","caixas de diálogo","português"],"headings":[{"id":"novidades","text":"Novidades","level":2},{"id":"desenhos-dwg-e-dxf","text":"Desenhos DWG e DXF","level":3},{"id":"contagens-e-comprimentos-exatos-a-partir-de-um-desenho","text":"Contagens e comprimentos exatos a partir de um desenho","level":3},{"id":"propostas","text":"Propostas","level":3},{"id":"assistentes-de-ia","text":"Assistentes de IA","level":3},{"id":"usd","text":"USD","level":3},{"id":"em-todos-os-aplicativos","text":"Em todos os aplicativos","level":2},{"id":"ainda-n-o-nesta-vers-o","text":"Ainda não nesta versão","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIsto é o que mudou no TakeoffCraft 0.7, em comparação com a 0.6. Os desenhos agora abrem como são, sem plotar para PDF antes, e um pacote de proposta pode ir para os subempreiteiros e voltar precificado.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"novidades\"\u003ENovidades\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"desenhos-dwg-e-dxf\"\u003EDesenhos DWG e DXF\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAbra um desenho como folhas.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir plantas\u003C/span\u003E no grupo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArquivo\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, ou solte os arquivos) aceita desenhos DWG e DXF ao lado de PDFs. O espaço modelo e cada layout viram folhas na lista \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFolhas\u003C/span\u003E, e um levantamento pode cobrir desenhos e PDFs juntos. Nada é enviado. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\"\u003EAbrir um desenho DWG ou DXF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA escala vem do desenho.\u003C/strong\u003E Um layout começa na escala de seu maior viewport, nomeada como predefinida quando for uma, como 1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;. O Modelo começa nas unidades do desenho. Um desenho sem unidades mostra &quot;Sem escala&quot; até você calibrar. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrar\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConferir\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPredefinida\u003C/span\u003E continuam prevalecendo. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing#the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-hash=\"the-scale\"\u003EA escala\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECamadas e desenhos vinculados.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECamadas\u003C/span\u003E na barra da folha mostra ou oculta as camadas do desenho. As camadas que o arquivo desligou ou congelou começam ocultas e não são contadas, a menos que você escolha contá-las. Um desenho vinculado (xref) aberto ao lado do desenho principal é desenhado e contado onde foi inserido; enquanto não estiver aberto, a barra de status diz o que falta. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing#layers\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-hash=\"layers\"\u003ECamadas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EO PDF marcado funciona a partir de um desenho.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExportar\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF marcado\u003C/span\u003E desenha primeiro as folhas marcadas de um desenho como PDF, então a cópia mostra suas marcações e a legenda sem etapa de plotagem. É desenhado em preto ou nas cores do desenho, sem tabelas de estilo de plotagem. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-estimate#a-marked-pdf\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-estimate\" data-hash=\"a-marked-pdf\"\u003EUm PDF marcado\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"contagens-e-comprimentos-exatos-a-partir-de-um-desenho\"\u003EContagens e comprimentos exatos a partir de um desenho\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConte o que o desenho diz que existe.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContagem CAD\u003C/span\u003E na barra da folha conta um bloco pelo nome (inclusive blocos aninhados, estados de blocos dinâmicos e matrizes de blocos), tudo de um tipo em uma camada, ou um valor de texto ou de atributo. Conta o que a folha mostra ou o desenho inteiro de uma vez. Adicionar as marcações é um passo de desfazer, e contar de novo adiciona só o que é novo. A \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContagem automática\u003C/span\u003E continua para PDFs e para símbolos que não são blocos. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\"\u003EContar a partir de um desenho\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeça a partir das linhas do próprio desenho.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMedir desenho\u003C/span\u003E transforma em marcações de um item linear ou de área as linhas, arcos, polilinhas, círculos, hachuras e sólidos em que você clica ou que seleciona por janela. Arcos, bulges e os furos de uma hachura saem exatos; elipses e splines são medidas em cordas finas, e a barra avisa. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-from-a-drawing\"\u003EMedir a partir de um desenho\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"propostas\"\u003EPropostas\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUse a proposta de um subempreiteiro como preço do pacote.\u003C/strong\u003E Em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExportar\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPacotes de propostas e equalização…\u003C/span\u003E, escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUsar\u003C/span\u003E em uma proposta. Os itens do pacote continuam como quantitativos, uma linha \u003Cstrong\u003EProposta escolhida\u003C/strong\u003E traz o preço, os itens que a proposta deixa de fora continuam no seu próprio custo, e despesas gerais, lucro e fiança se aplicam a ela. Trocar de proposta é um passo de desfazer; a pasta de trabalho e o resumo das propostas a incluem. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-leveling#use-a-bid-as-the-packages-price\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-leveling\" data-hash=\"use-a-bid-as-the-packages-price\"\u003EUsar uma proposta como preço do pacote\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnvie um pacote para propostas.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnviar para propostas…\u003C/span\u003E sob um pacote envia por e-mail a cada subempreiteiro um link para uma página que não exige conta: ele vê o escopo e as folhas como PDF, digita um preço em cada linha ou a marca como não incluída, adiciona observações e até três arquivos, ou recusa. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EObter preços\u003C/span\u003E traz o que voltou para a planilha de equalização, sinalizado como \u003Cstrong\u003Eweb\u003C/strong\u003E. O envio está aberto apenas às contas que a equipe do CadCraft habilitou enquanto é testado (veja abaixo). Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\"\u003EEnviar um pacote para propostas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELacradas até a abertura.\u003C/strong\u003E Os preços podem ficar lacrados até um horário de abertura que você define: até lá ninguém vê um preço, nem você. Na abertura todas as propostas aparecem de uma vez, e uma proposta que chegou depois do prazo é marcada como atrasada. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-invitations#sealed-bids-and-the-opening\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\" data-hash=\"sealed-bids-and-the-opening\"\u003EPropostas lacradas e a abertura\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECadastro de subempreiteiros.\u003C/strong\u003E Sua conta em getcadcraft.com guarda os contatos, serviços, respostas de qualificação e datas de seguro e licença de cada empresa (sinalizadas quando vencidas ou a menos de 30 dias do vencimento), com as propostas anteriores. Importe e exporte em CSV, e escolha-os ao enviar um pacote. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-invitations#on-the-website\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\" data-hash=\"on-the-website\"\u003ENo site\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"assistentes-de-ia\"\u003EAssistentes de IA\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAssistentes podem acrescentar ao levantamento.\u003C/strong\u003E Com \u003Cstrong\u003EEditar\u003C/strong\u003E marcado, o seu próprio assistente pode adicionar itens (\u003Ccode\u003Eitems_add\u003C/code\u003E) e medir trechos, áreas e contagens em uma folha (\u003Ccode\u003Emarks_add\u003C/code\u003E; comprimentos e áreas só em folha com escala), cada chamada sendo um passo de desfazer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFerramentas de assistente para desenhos.\u003C/strong\u003E Em um DWG ou DXF aberto, um assistente pode listar suas folhas e o que pode ser contado, contar com exatidão, adicionar as marcações da contagem e medir a partir da geometria do desenho. Sem o TakeoffCraft aberto, o servidor MCP do CadCraft abre um desenho como um novo levantamento e o salva, e \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft count\u003C/code\u003E conta pela linha de comando. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/use-with-ai-assistants#what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\" data-hash=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003EO que um assistente pode pedir\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"usd\"\u003EUSD\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA vista de conferência em 3D como USD.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPacote USD (.usdz)\u003C/span\u003E ao lado de \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGLB\u003C/span\u003E na vista de conferência em 3D grava cada parede, laje, chapa, poste de contagem e trecho como um objeto próprio, com o nome do seu item e carregando seu quantitativo, em metros. Um assistente também pode exportá-lo (\u003Ccode\u003Eusd_export\u003C/code\u003E). Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/usd\"\u003EExportar a vista de conferência em 3D como USD\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"em-todos-os-aplicativos\"\u003EEm todos os aplicativos\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnidades.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnidades\u003C/span\u003E tem uma lista para comprimento, área, volume, ângulo e declividade, com os tamanhos exatos que as definições das unidades dão (o pé de levantamento dos EUA é uma unidade própria). Desligue as unidades que você nunca usa e elas saem das listas de unidades; marque uma com estrela e o trabalho novo começa nela. Um arquivo sempre mantém a sua própria unidade. No TakeoffCraft, as listas de \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnidade\u003C/span\u003E de um item seguem as chaves, um item novo começa na unidade de comprimento ou de área marcada com estrela, os squares de cobertura (SQ), acres e hectares são novas unidades de área, e um campo de comprimento aceita a marca de qualquer unidade, como \u003Ccode\u003E3 yd\u003C/code\u003E. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/unit-lists\" data-topic=\"basics/unit-lists\"\u003EUnidades, valores digitados e as listas de unidades\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUm layout mais denso e painéis que se guardam.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDensidade do layout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompacta\u003C/span\u003E coloca mais coisas na tela (telas de toque e celulares mantêm seus botões grandes). Cada painel lateral tem uma barra de título fina: arraste-a para acoplar ou flutuar o painel, solte-a na barra de outro painel para dividir um quadro como guias, fixe-a para que deslize para a borda, ou oculte-a e traga-a de volta pela borda. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#make-the-layout-denser\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"make-the-layout-denser\"\u003EOrganizar os painéis\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAs caixas de diálogo se movem.\u003C/strong\u003E Arraste uma caixa de diálogo pela barra de título para tirá-la da frente do desenho; ela volta onde você a deixou. Dê um clique duplo na barra de título para centralizá-la de novo. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#move-a-dialog\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"move-a-dialog\"\u003EMover uma caixa de diálogo\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReceitas para assistentes de IA.\u003C/strong\u003E Dez pedidos completos para o seu próprio assistente (arrumar um desenho, plotar um conjunto, conferir desenhos, fazer perguntas a um modelo, totalizar um levantamento, experimentar uma variante de peça…), cada um com as ferramentas que chama e as permissões de que precisa, e cada um reexecutado pelos nossos testes. Os aplicativos não contêm IA. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003EReceitas para assistentes de IA\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIdiomas.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Portugal)\u003C/strong\u003E é novo em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIdioma\u003C/span\u003E. Ele, \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Brasil)\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEspañol (México)\u003C/strong\u003E e \u003Cstrong\u003EBahasa Indonesia\u003C/strong\u003E agora têm cada palavra dos aplicativos, como rascunhos ainda não revisados por um falante nativo. Os tópicos de ajuda estão em inglês.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EO CadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E diz por que uma verificação de atualização falhou (ainda não há arquivo para o seu sistema, sem rede, assinatura inválida) em vez de apenas &quot;Concluído&quot;, e deixa de trazer de volta um cartão de Novidades que você fechou até haver uma versão mais nova.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ainda-n-o-nesta-vers-o\"\u003EAinda não nesta versão\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA 0.7 não tem assinatura de código.\u003C/strong\u003E A assinatura, e o pacote da Microsoft Store, passaram para a 0.8 porque a validação do certificado ainda não saiu. Confira o SHA-256 ao lado de cada arquivo em getcadcraft.com/download. No Windows, se o SmartScreen barrar o instalador, escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMais informações\u003C/span\u003E e depois \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExecutar assim mesmo\u003C/span\u003E. No Mac, da primeira vez, clique com o botão direito em CadCraft Studio e escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir\u003C/span\u003E. O CadCraft Studio ainda não está em uma loja de aplicativos, e ainda não há versão para iPad ou iPhone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAs listas de ângulo e declividade em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnidades\u003C/span\u003E são salvas, mas nenhum campo as lê ainda.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOs convites de proposta estão desligados para a maioria das contas.\u003C/strong\u003E Enquanto o recurso é testado, os e-mails para proponentes externos saem apenas de contas que a equipe do CadCraft habilitou. As páginas dos proponentes funcionam para todos os convites já enviados. Todos os demais: baixem o \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV do escopo\u003C/span\u003E, enviem por e-mail e digitem as propostas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUma janela do CadCraft Studio ainda não pode enviar convites de proposta: ela diz para usar o TakeoffCraft no navegador. Abrir um levantamento com solicitações enviadas, a equalização e a escolha de uma proposta funcionam no Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdendos, cartas de adjudicação, uma planilha de escopo que cada proponente assina e lembretes automáticos antes do vencimento de um seguro não estão implementados. Uma proposta escolhida não reprecifica a área da proposta de uma alternativa.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDesenhos: tabelas de estilo de plotagem e arquivos CTB não são aplicados, um DXF binário é recusado (salve-o como DXF ASCII ou DWG), os desenhos vinculados são encontrados apenas entre os arquivos abertos, pelo nome do arquivo, e subposições de PDF, imagens e objetos OLE em um desenho não são desenhados nem contados. As contagens e escalas foram conferidas em desenhos de exemplo, ainda não em desenhos de uma obra real abertos no programa que os criou.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUm assistente pode adicionar itens e marcações, mas não pode mover, alterar ou excluir marcações que já existem.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETodo o resto está em \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EAinda não implementado\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Isto é o que mudou no TakeoffCraft 0.7, em comparação com a 0.6. Os desenhos agora abrem como são, sem plotar para PDF antes, e um pacote de proposta pode ir para os subempreiteiros e voltar precificado. Novidades Desenhos DWG e DXF Abra um desenho como folhas. Abrir plantas no grupo Arquivo (Ctrl+O, ou solte os arquivos) aceita desenhos DWG e DXF ao lado de PDFs. O espaço modelo e cada layout viram folhas na lista Folhas, e um levantamento pode cobrir desenhos e PDFs juntos. Nada é enviado. Veja Abrir um desenho DWG ou DXF. A escala vem do desenho. Um layout começa na escala de seu maior viewport, nomeada como predefinida quando for uma, como 1/2\" = 1'-0\". O Modelo começa nas unidades do desenho. Um desenho sem unidades mostra \"Sem escala\" até você calibrar. Calibrar, Conferir e Predefinida continuam prevalecendo. Veja A escala. Camadas e desenhos vinculados. Camadas na barra da folha mostra ou oculta as camadas do desenho. As camadas que o arquivo desligou ou congelou começam ocultas e não são contadas, a menos que você escolha contá-las. Um desenho vinculado (xref) aberto ao lado do desenho principal é desenhado e contado onde foi inserido; enquanto não estiver aberto, a barra de status diz o que falta. Veja Camadas. O PDF marcado funciona a partir de um desenho. Exportar › PDF marcado desenha primeiro as folhas marcadas de um desenho como PDF, então a cópia mostra suas marcações e a legenda sem etapa de plotagem. É desenhado em preto ou nas cores do desenho, sem tabelas de estilo de plotagem. Veja Um PDF marcado. Contagens e comprimentos exatos a partir de um desenho Conte o que o desenho diz que existe. Contagem CAD na barra da folha conta um bloco pelo nome (inclusive blocos aninhados, estados de blocos dinâmicos e matrizes de blocos), tudo de um tipo em uma camada, ou um valor de texto ou de atributo. Conta o que a folha mostra ou o desenho inteiro de uma vez. Adicionar as marcações é um passo de desfazer, e contar de novo adiciona só o que é novo. A Contagem automática continua para PDFs e para símbolos que não são blocos. Veja Contar a partir de um desenho. Meça a partir das linhas do próprio desenho. Medir desenho transforma em marcações de um item linear ou de área as linhas, arcos, polilinhas, círculos, hachuras e sólidos em que você clica ou que seleciona por janela. Arcos, bulges e os furos de uma hachura saem exatos; elipses e splines são medidas em cordas finas, e a barra avisa. Veja Medir a partir de um desenho. Propostas Use a proposta de um subempreiteiro como preço do pacote. Em Exportar › Pacotes de propostas e equalização…, escolha Usar em uma proposta. Os itens do pacote continuam como quantitativos, uma linha Proposta escolhida traz o preço, os itens que a proposta deixa de fora continuam no seu próprio custo, e despesas gerais, lucro e fiança se aplicam a ela. Trocar de proposta é um passo de desfazer; a pasta de trabalho e o resumo das propostas a incluem. Veja Usar uma proposta como preço do pacote. Envie um pacote para propostas. Enviar para propostas… sob um pacote envia por e-mail a cada subempreiteiro um link para uma página que não exige conta: ele vê o escopo e as folhas como PDF, digita um preço em cada linha ou a marca como não incluída, adiciona observações e até três arquivos, ou recusa. Obter preços traz o que voltou para a planilha de equalização, sinalizado como web. O envio está aberto apenas às contas que a equipe do CadCraft habilitou enquanto é testado (veja abaixo). Veja Enviar um pacote para propostas. Lacradas até a abertura. Os preços podem ficar lacrados até um horário de abertura que você define: até lá ninguém vê um preço, nem você. Na abertura todas as propostas aparecem de uma vez, e uma proposta que chegou depois do prazo é marcada como atrasada. Veja Propostas lacradas e a abertura. Cadastro de subempreiteiros. Sua conta em getcadcraft.com guarda os contatos, serviços, respostas de qualificação e datas de seguro e licença de cada empresa (sinalizadas quando vencidas ou a menos de 30 dias do vencimento), com as propostas anteriores. Importe e exporte em CSV, e escolha-os ao enviar um pacote. Veja No site. Assistentes de IA Assistentes podem acrescentar ao levantamento. Com Editar marcado, o seu próprio assistente pode adicionar itens (items_add) e medir trechos, áreas e contagens em uma folha (marks_add; comprimentos e áreas só em folha com escala), cada chamada sendo um passo de desfazer. Ferramentas de assistente para desenhos. Em um DWG ou DXF aberto, um assistente pode listar suas folhas e o que pode ser contado, contar com exatidão, adicionar as marcações da contagem e medir a partir da geometria do desenho. Sem o TakeoffCraft aberto, o servidor MCP do CadCraft abre um desenho como um novo levantamento e o salva, e cadcraft count conta pela linha de comando. Veja O que um assistente pode pedir. USD A vista de conferência em 3D como USD. Pacote USD (.usdz) ao lado de GLB na vista de conferência em 3D grava cada parede, laje, chapa, poste de contagem e trecho como um objeto próprio, com o nome do seu item e carregando seu quantitativo, em metros. Um assistente também pode exportá-lo (usd_export). Veja Exportar a vista de conferência em 3D como USD. Em todos os aplicativos Unidades. Configurações › Geral › Unidades tem uma lista para comprimento, área, volume, ângulo e declividade, com os tamanhos exatos que as definições das unidades dão (o pé de levantamento dos EUA é uma unidade própria). Desligue as unidades que você nunca usa e elas saem das listas de unidades; marque uma com estrela e o trabalho novo começa nela. Um arquivo sempre mantém a sua própria unidade. No TakeoffCraft, as listas de Unidade de um item seguem as chaves, um item novo começa na unidade de comprimento ou de área marcada com estrela, os squares de cobertura (SQ), acres e hectares são novas unidades de área, e um campo de comprimento aceita a marca de qualquer unidade, como 3 yd. Veja Unidades, valores digitados e as listas de unidades. Um layout mais denso e painéis que se guardam. Configurações › Geral › Densidade do layout › Compacta coloca mais coisas na tela (telas de toque e celulares mantêm seus botões grandes). Cada painel lateral tem uma barra de título fina: arraste-a para acoplar ou flutuar o painel, solte-a na barra de outro painel para dividir um quadro como guias, fixe-a para que deslize para a borda, ou oculte-a e traga-a de volta pela borda. Veja Organizar os painéis. As caixas de diálogo se movem. Arraste uma caixa de diálogo pela barra de título para tirá-la da frente do desenho; ela volta onde você a deixou. Dê um clique duplo na barra de título para centralizá-la de novo. Veja Mover uma caixa de diálogo. Receitas para assistentes de IA. Dez pedidos completos para o seu próprio assistente (arrumar um desenho, plotar um conjunto, conferir desenhos, fazer perguntas a um modelo, totalizar um levantamento, experimentar uma variante de peça…), cada um com as ferramentas que chama e as permissões de que precisa, e cada um reexecutado pelos nossos testes. Os aplicativos não contêm IA. Veja Receitas para assistentes de IA. Idiomas. Português (Portugal) é novo em Configurações › Geral › Idioma. Ele, Português (Brasil), Español (México) e Bahasa Indonesia agora têm cada palavra dos aplicativos, como rascunhos ainda não revisados por um falante nativo. Os tópicos de ajuda estão em inglês. O CadCraft Studio diz por que uma verificação de atualização falhou (ainda não há arquivo para o seu sistema, sem rede, assinatura inválida) em vez de apenas \"Concluído\", e deixa de trazer de volta um cartão de Novidades que você fechou até haver uma versão mais nova. Ainda não nesta versão A 0.7 não tem assinatura de código. A assinatura, e o pacote da Microsoft Store, passaram para a 0.8 porque a validação do certificado ainda não saiu. Confira o SHA-256 ao lado de cada arquivo em getcadcraft.com/download. No Windows, se o SmartScreen barrar o instalador, escolha Mais informações e depois Executar assim mesmo. No Mac, da primeira vez, clique com o botão direito em CadCraft Studio e escolha Abrir. O CadCraft Studio ainda não está em uma loja de aplicativos, e ainda não há versão para iPad ou iPhone. As listas de ângulo e declividade em Unidades são salvas, mas nenhum campo as lê ainda. Os convites de proposta estão desligados para a maioria das contas. Enquanto o recurso é testado, os e-mails para proponentes externos saem apenas de contas que a equipe do CadCraft habilitou. As páginas dos proponentes funcionam para todos os convites já enviados. Todos os demais: baixem o CSV do escopo, enviem por e-mail e digitem as propostas. Uma janela do CadCraft Studio ainda não pode enviar convites de proposta: ela diz para usar o TakeoffCraft no navegador. Abrir um levantamento com solicitações enviadas, a equalização e a escolha de uma proposta funcionam no Studio. Adendos, cartas de adjudicação, uma planilha de escopo que cada proponente assina e lembretes automáticos antes do vencimento de um seguro não estão implementados. Uma proposta escolhida não reprecifica a área da proposta de uma alternativa. Desenhos: tabelas de estilo de plotagem e arquivos CTB não são aplicados, um DXF binário é recusado (salve-o como DXF ASCII ou DWG), os desenhos vinculados são encontrados apenas entre os arquivos abertos, pelo nome do arquivo, e subposições de PDF, imagens e objetos OLE em um desenho não são desenhados nem contados. As contagens e escalas foram conferidas em desenhos de exemplo, ainda não em desenhos de uma obra real abertos no programa que os criou. Um assistente pode adicionar itens e marcações, mas não pode mover, alterar ou excluir marcações que já existem. Todo o resto está em Ainda não implementado.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Primeiros passos com o TakeoffCraft","summary":"O que o TakeoffCraft faz, onde ele roda e seu primeiro item medido e precificado a partir de um conjunto de plantas em PDF.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["início","primeiro levantamento","início rápido","introdução","orçamento","levantamento de quantitativos","conjunto de plantas","pdf"],"headings":[{"id":"onde-ele-roda","text":"Onde ele roda","level":2},{"id":"seu-primeiro-levantamento-em-dez-passos","text":"Seu primeiro levantamento em dez passos","level":2},{"id":"o-que-ler-a-seguir","text":"O que ler a seguir","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EO TakeoffCraft mede conjuntos de plantas, PDFs e desenhos DWG ou DXF, e transforma as medições em um orçamento precificado. Você abre os arquivos (um desenho é contado direto a partir de seus blocos: veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\"\u003EAbrir um desenho DWG ou DXF\u003C/a\u003E), dá uma escala a cada folha (um desenho traz a sua), cria \u003Cstrong\u003Eitens\u003C/strong\u003E (um tipo de parede, um acabamento de piso, uma peça) e clica nas folhas para levantá-los. Quantitativos, custos e o total da obra são atualizados conforme você trabalha. Você pode exportar o orçamento como planilha e uma cópia dos PDFs com o seu levantamento desenhado.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESe você já fez levantamento de quantitativos em outros programas, leia a seguir \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/coming-from\"\u003ESe você vem de outro software de levantamento\u003C/a\u003E. Algumas coisas funcionam de outro jeito aqui.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"onde-ele-roda\"\u003EOnde ele roda\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO TakeoffCraft roda no seu navegador. Abra o TakeoffCraft e a tela fica pronta na hora; não há nada para instalar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESeus PDFs ficam no seu computador.\u003C/strong\u003E O navegador os abre e eles nunca são enviados. Salvar é um download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFunciona nas versões atuais do Chrome, Edge, Firefox e Safari. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003ENavegadores e computadores\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFunciona sem conexão com a internet depois que você escolhe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E na barra do aplicativo e ela mostra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPronto para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003ETrabalhar offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EO salvamento na nuvem ainda não está disponível. Como aplicativo de desktop, o TakeoffCraft vem no CadCraft Studio, com os outros sete aplicativos.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"seu-primeiro-levantamento-em-dez-passos\"\u003ESeu primeiro levantamento em dez passos\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEste roteiro mede um tipo de parede e o precifica. Leva alguns minutos com qualquer planta que você tenha.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir plantas\u003C/span\u003E no grupo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArquivo\u003C/span\u003E da barra de ferramentas (ou pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), selecione um ou mais PDFs e escolha Abrir. Você também pode soltar os PDFs em qualquer lugar da página. As folhas aparecem à esquerda e a primeira folha abre no meio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProcure a escala impressa da folha na legenda ou sob o título de um desenho.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAbra a lista \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPredefinida\u003C/span\u003E no grupo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEscala\u003C/span\u003E e escolha essa escala, por exemplo \u003Ccode\u003E1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. A barra na parte de baixo da folha agora diz &quot;Escala 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConferir\u003C/span\u003E (ou pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) e clique nas duas pontas de uma cota impressa no desenho. A leitura na barra de status, embaixo à esquerda, deve coincidir com a cota impressa. Se não coincidir, veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003EDefinir a escala\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo painel \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItens\u003C/span\u003E, à direita, escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENovo item\u003C/span\u003E, digite um nome como \u003Ccode\u003EParede interna tipo A\u003C/code\u003E, deixe o tipo em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear (comprimento)\u003C/span\u003E e escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdicionar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E na barra de ferramentas (ou pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClique no início de uma parede e depois em cada canto ao longo dela. Dê um clique duplo, pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ou clique com o botão direito para terminar o trecho. O trecho é desenhado na cor do item e seu comprimento aparece ao lado do item.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo editor do item, em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItens\u003C/span\u003E, digite \u003Ccode\u003E9&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAltura\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0.85\u003C/code\u003E em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial $/LF\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Ccode\u003E1.20\u003C/code\u003E em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMão de obra $/LF\u003C/span\u003E. A tabela \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantitativos\u003C/span\u003E, embaixo, mostra o comprimento, o material e a mão de obra estendidos e o \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETotal da obra\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar levantamento\u003C/span\u003E (ou pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Seu navegador baixa um arquivo \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E. Ele guarda seus itens, escalas e marcações, e reabre sobre os mesmos PDFs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAbra o menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExportar\u003C/span\u003E na barra de ferramentas e escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E para baixar o orçamento como planilha.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E para interromper o que estiver fazendo e voltar à ferramenta \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelecionar\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E desfaz qualquer alteração.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-ler-a-seguir\"\u003EO que ler a seguir\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/workspace\"\u003EA tela do TakeoffCraft\u003C/a\u003E dá nome a cada parte da janela.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-plan-set\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-plan-set\"\u003EIniciar um levantamento e abrir um conjunto de plantas\u003C/a\u003E trata de vários PDFs e da lista de folhas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECriar itens de levantamento\u003C/a\u003E explica tipos, unidades, perdas e preços.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EComposições e fórmulas\u003C/a\u003E transformam uma medição em montantes, chapas e mais.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/keyboard\"\u003ETeclado e mouse\u003C/a\u003E lista todas as teclas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"O TakeoffCraft mede conjuntos de plantas, PDFs e desenhos DWG ou DXF, e transforma as medições em um orçamento precificado. Você abre os arquivos (um desenho é contado direto a partir de seus blocos: veja Abrir um desenho DWG ou DXF), dá uma escala a cada folha (um desenho traz a sua), cria itens (um tipo de parede, um acabamento de piso, uma peça) e clica nas folhas para levantá-los. Quantitativos, custos e o total da obra são atualizados conforme você trabalha. Você pode exportar o orçamento como planilha e uma cópia dos PDFs com o seu levantamento desenhado. Se você já fez levantamento de quantitativos em outros programas, leia a seguir Se você vem de outro software de levantamento. Algumas coisas funcionam de outro jeito aqui. Onde ele roda O TakeoffCraft roda no seu navegador. Abra o TakeoffCraft e a tela fica pronta na hora; não há nada para instalar. Seus PDFs ficam no seu computador. O navegador os abre e eles nunca são enviados. Salvar é um download. Funciona nas versões atuais do Chrome, Edge, Firefox e Safari. Veja Navegadores e computadores. Funciona sem conexão com a internet depois que você escolhe Salvar para uso offline na barra do aplicativo e ela mostra Pronto para uso offline. Veja Trabalhar offline. O salvamento na nuvem ainda não está disponível. Como aplicativo de desktop, o TakeoffCraft vem no CadCraft Studio, com os outros sete aplicativos. Seu primeiro levantamento em dez passos Este roteiro mede um tipo de parede e o precifica. Leva alguns minutos com qualquer planta que você tenha. Escolha Abrir plantas no grupo Arquivo da barra de ferramentas (ou pressione Ctrl+O), selecione um ou mais PDFs e escolha Abrir. Você também pode soltar os PDFs em qualquer lugar da página. As folhas aparecem à esquerda e a primeira folha abre no meio. Procure a escala impressa da folha na legenda ou sob o título de um desenho. Abra a lista Predefinida no grupo Escala e escolha essa escala, por exemplo 1/4\" = 1'-0\". A barra na parte de baixo da folha agora diz \"Escala 1/4\" = 1'-0\"\". Escolha Conferir (ou pressione D) e clique nas duas pontas de uma cota impressa no desenho. A leitura na barra de status, embaixo à esquerda, deve coincidir com a cota impressa. Se não coincidir, veja Definir a escala. No painel Itens, à direita, escolha Novo item, digite um nome como Parede interna tipo A, deixe o tipo em Linear (comprimento) e escolha Adicionar. Escolha Linear na barra de ferramentas (ou pressione L). Clique no início de uma parede e depois em cada canto ao longo dela. Dê um clique duplo, pressione Enter ou clique com o botão direito para terminar o trecho. O trecho é desenhado na cor do item e seu comprimento aparece ao lado do item. No editor do item, em Itens, digite 9'-0\" em Altura, 0.85 em Material $/LF e 1.20 em Mão de obra $/LF. A tabela Quantitativos, embaixo, mostra o comprimento, o material e a mão de obra estendidos e o Total da obra. Escolha Salvar levantamento (ou pressione Ctrl+S). Seu navegador baixa um arquivo .takeoff.json. Ele guarda seus itens, escalas e marcações, e reabre sobre os mesmos PDFs. Abra o menu Exportar na barra de ferramentas e escolha XLSX para baixar o orçamento como planilha. Pressione Esc para interromper o que estiver fazendo e voltar à ferramenta Selecionar. Ctrl+Z desfaz qualquer alteração. O que ler a seguir A tela do TakeoffCraft dá nome a cada parte da janela. Iniciar um levantamento e abrir um conjunto de plantas trata de vários PDFs e da lista de folhas. Criar itens de levantamento explica tipos, unidades, perdas e preços. Composições e fórmulas transformam uma medição em montantes, chapas e mais. Teclado e mouse lista todas as teclas.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"A tela do TakeoffCraft","summary":"Um passeio pela janela pelos nomes na tela - a barra de ferramentas, a lista de folhas, a folha, o painel Itens, a tabela Quantitativos e a barra de status.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["tela","layout","layouts","faixa de opções","navegador do projeto","caixa de ferramentas","caixa de informações","barra de ferramentas","painéis","lista de folhas","painel de itens","quantitativos","barra de status","passeio","interface"],"headings":[{"id":"a-barra-de-ferramentas","text":"A barra de ferramentas","level":2},{"id":"folhas-esquerda","text":"Folhas (esquerda)","level":2},{"id":"a-folha-meio","text":"A folha (meio)","level":2},{"id":"itens-direita","text":"Itens (direita)","level":2},{"id":"quantitativos-embaixo","text":"Quantitativos (embaixo)","level":2},{"id":"barra-de-status-embaixo","text":"Barra de status (embaixo)","level":2},{"id":"outros-layouts","text":"Outros layouts","level":2},{"id":"tela-cheia-e-temas","text":"Tela cheia e temas","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EO TakeoffCraft ocupa a janela do navegador sob a barra do aplicativo CadCraft. De cima para baixo e da esquerda para a direita:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EParte\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOnde\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPara que serve\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBarra do aplicativo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo alto\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EO nome do aplicativo, a versão, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESobre o TakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAjuda\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), o botão \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETela cheia\u003C/span\u003E e a chave de tema \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPapel\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBarra de ferramentas\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESob a barra do aplicativo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETodos os comandos, em grupos: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArquivo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExportar\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEscala\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevantamento\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEditar\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExibir\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFolhas\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEsquerda\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETodas as páginas de todos os PDFs abertos, com miniatura, escala e número de marcações.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA folha\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeio\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA página aberta. É aqui que você mede.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItens\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDireita\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeus itens de levantamento, as configurações do item selecionado e a marcação selecionada.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantitativos\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEmbaixo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantitativos e custos por item ou por folha, e o total da obra.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBarra de status\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBem embaixo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA medição ao vivo, mensagens, a dica dos 45° e o zoom.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-barra-de-ferramentas\"\u003EA barra de ferramentas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPasse o mouse sobre qualquer botão para ver o que ele faz e sua tecla.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGrupo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBotões\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArquivo\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENovo\u003C/span\u003E (levantamento vazio), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir plantas\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir levantamento\u003C/span\u003E (um projeto \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar levantamento\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExportar\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUm menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF marcado\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEscala\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrar\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConferir\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) e a lista \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPredefinida\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevantamento\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelecionar\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÁrea\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECírculo\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArco\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, uma chave liga/desliga), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduzir\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, uma chave liga/desliga), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecorte\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContagem\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContagem automática\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEditar\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDesfazer\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERefazer\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopiar\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColar\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMais\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecortar\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopiar\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColar\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColar no mesmo local\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicar\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExcluir\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelecionar tudo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELimpar seleção\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExibir\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAjustar página\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAjustar largura\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA ferramenta que você está usando aparece pressionada. Os botões \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArco\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduzir\u003C/span\u003E ficam pressionados enquanto estão ligados.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"folhas-esquerda\"\u003EFolhas (esquerda)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBuscar folhas\u003C/strong\u003E filtra a lista pelo nome da folha, pelo nome do arquivo ou pelo rótulo da página.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECada folha mostra uma miniatura (desenhada conforme você rola), seu nome e sua escala ou \u003Cstrong\u003ESem escala\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EO lado direito de cada linha mostra quantas marcações a folha tem. Enquanto um item está destacado, mostra quantas marcações desse item estão na folha.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClique em uma folha para abri-la. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E vão para a folha seguinte e a anterior da lista.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EQuando um levantamento cobre PDFs que não estão abertos, uma nota sob a lista os nomeia. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\"\u003ESalvar e reabrir um levantamento\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOs nomes das folhas vêm do PDF: um rótulo de página como \u003Ccode\u003EA-101\u003C/code\u003E vira o nome da folha. Uma página sem rótulo recebe o nome do arquivo e o número da página, como &quot;Plantas, página 3&quot;. Com vários PDFs abertos, as folhas com rótulo mostram também o nome do arquivo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-folha-meio\"\u003EA folha (meio)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA barra sob a folha mostra o nome da folha e sua escala, ou &quot;Ainda sem escala: escolha uma predefinida ou calibre&quot;. Seus botões: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E (quando esse aplicativo consegue receber o PDF; veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-plan-set\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-plan-set\"\u003EIniciar um levantamento e abrir um conjunto de plantas\u003C/a\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComparar\u003C/span\u003E (uma versão mais antiga da folha sobre esta: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/compare-revisions\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/compare-revisions\"\u003EComparar uma folha revisada\u003C/a\u003E), &quot;(n) a conferir&quot; (marcações sinalizadas, quando há) e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E (a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\"\u003Evista de conferência em 3D\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAntes de abrir qualquer PDF, o meio mostra um guia curto de cinco passos.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA roda do mouse dá zoom no ponteiro. Arraste para mover. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/keyboard\"\u003ETeclado e mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEnquanto você usa a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContagem automática\u003C/span\u003E, uma barra com seus controles aparece no alto da folha.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"itens-direita\"\u003EItens (direita)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo alto, a lista \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÁrea da proposta\u003C/span\u003E: o edifício, pavimento ou etapa em que as novas marcações entram (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-areas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-areas\"\u003EÁreas da proposta\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENovo item\u003C/span\u003E abre um pequeno formulário: um nome, um tipo (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear (comprimento)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÁrea\u003C/span\u003E ou \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContagem\u003C/span\u003E) e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdicionar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA lista mostra a cor, o ícone do tipo, o nome e o quantitativo de cada item. Clique em um item para desenhar com ele e destacar suas marcações em todas as folhas. Clique de novo para parar de destacar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESob a lista, \u003Cstrong\u003EItem: (nome)\u003C/strong\u003E é o editor do item: nome, tipo, cor, unidade, altura ou profundidade, perda, preços de material e mão de obra, os valores medidos e a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComposição\u003C/span\u003E. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECriar itens de levantamento\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMarcação selecionada\u003C/strong\u003E aparece quando você seleciona uma marcação: a que item ela pertence, sua folha, suas medidas e botões como \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdicionar um recorte\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExcluir marcação\u003C/span\u003E. Com várias marcações selecionadas, vira \u003Cstrong\u003EMarcações selecionadas\u003C/strong\u003E: quantas de cada item, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopiar\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicar\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecortar\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExcluir marcações\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EO editor do item termina com \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopiar item\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicar item\u003C/span\u003E: um item com sua composição e seus custos, sem marcações. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopiar, colar e duplicar marcações\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"quantitativos-embaixo\"\u003EQuantitativos (embaixo)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPor item\u003C/span\u003E lista cada item e os componentes de sua composição com quantitativo, perda, preços e custos estendidos. Você pode digitar perdas e preços direto na tabela.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPor folha\u003C/span\u003E lista o quantitativo de cada item em cada folha.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUm aviso começando com \u003Cstrong\u003EIncompleto:\u003C/strong\u003E aparece quando algo ficou fora dos totais.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EO \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETotal da obra\u003C/span\u003E fica à direita do cabeçalho.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EO botão de seta no extremo direito oculta ou mostra a tabela para dar mais espaço à folha.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVeja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/read-quantities\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/read-quantities\"\u003ELer os quantitativos e o orçamento\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"barra-de-status-embaixo\"\u003EBarra de status (embaixo)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDa esquerda para a direita: a medição ao vivo enquanto você desenha (comprimento, área, raio ou o resultado de uma conferência), a caixa \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDigite\u003C/span\u003E enquanto você digita um comprimento, as mensagens do TakeoffCraft, o lembrete &quot;Shift prende em 45°&quot; (ou &quot;Prende em 45°; Shift libera o ângulo&quot; quando isso está ligado em \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/settings\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/a\u003E), o botão \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E (prender às linhas do desenho, ligado quando destacado; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) e a porcentagem de zoom.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELeia a barra de status quando algo não acontecer. O TakeoffCraft explica ali por que recusou um clique, por exemplo &quot;Esta folha ainda não tem escala&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"outros-layouts\"\u003EOutros layouts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETudo acima é o layout \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft clássico\u003C/span\u003E, o inicial. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E oferece mais dois; as mesmas ferramentas e teclas funcionam nos três, e escolher \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft clássico\u003C/span\u003E de novo coloca cada controle de volta onde estava.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELayout\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENo TakeoffCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFaixa de opções e navegador do projeto\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFaixa de opções do levantamento\u003C/span\u003E no alto (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, teclas e mouse\u003C/a\u003E), uma barra para a ferramenta em uso, uma guia para cada folha que você abre (o Modelo e os layouts de um desenho também são folhas), com as escalas predefinidas e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAjustar página\u003C/span\u003E sob a folha. O painel da esquerda traz \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPropriedades\u003C/span\u003E (o item, a nota ou a marcação selecionada) sobre o \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENavegador do levantamento\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFolhas\u003C/span\u003E (clique em uma para abri-la em uma guia), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECondições\u003C/span\u003E (clique em uma para desenhar com ela) e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrçamento\u003C/span\u003E (o total de cada item e o total da obra); as miniaturas ficam na segunda guia.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECaixa de ferramentas e caixa de informações\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA barra de ferramentas sobre a folha, uma caixa de informações com a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÁrea da proposta\u003C/span\u003E, a ferramenta em uso ou a seleção e a barra da folha, uma coluna com as ferramentas de levantamento à esquerda, uma guia para cada folha, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E e o zoom sob a folha.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEnquanto a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\"\u003Evista de conferência em 3D\u003C/a\u003E está aberta, as guias das folhas e a barra sob a folha saem do caminho; feche a vista 3D para medir de novo. No celular, o layout para celular é sempre o que roda.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tela-cheia-e-temas\"\u003ETela cheia e temas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO botão \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETela cheia\u003C/span\u003E na barra do aplicativo, ou \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (Control em todas as plataformas, também no Mac), alterna entre normal, tela cheia e tela cheia com as barras ocultas. Com as barras ocultas, leve o ponteiro à borda superior para mostrá-las de novo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPapel\u003C/span\u003E é o tema claro e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelo\u003C/span\u003E o escuro. Em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelo\u003C/span\u003E o desenho aparece invertido (linhas claras sobre fundo escuro) apenas na tela; as exportações nunca são alteradas.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"O TakeoffCraft ocupa a janela do navegador sob a barra do aplicativo CadCraft. De cima para baixo e da esquerda para a direita: Parte Onde Para que serve Barra do aplicativo No alto O nome do aplicativo, a versão, Sobre o TakeoffCraft, Ajuda (F1), Salvar para uso offline, Configurações (Ctrl+,), o botão Tela cheia e a chave de tema Papel / Modelo. Barra de ferramentas Sob a barra do aplicativo Todos os comandos, em grupos: Arquivo, Exportar, Escala, Levantamento, Editar, Exibir. Folhas Esquerda Todas as páginas de todos os PDFs abertos, com miniatura, escala e número de marcações. A folha Meio A página aberta. É aqui que você mede. Itens Direita Seus itens de levantamento, as configurações do item selecionado e a marcação selecionada. Quantitativos Embaixo Quantitativos e custos por item ou por folha, e o total da obra. Barra de status Bem embaixo A medição ao vivo, mensagens, a dica dos 45° e o zoom. A barra de ferramentas Passe o mouse sobre qualquer botão para ver o que ele faz e sua tecla. Grupo Botões Arquivo Novo (levantamento vazio), Abrir plantas (Ctrl+O), Abrir levantamento (um projeto .takeoff.json), Salvar levantamento (Ctrl+S) Exportar Um menu: XLSX, CSV, PDF marcado Escala Calibrar (K), Conferir (D) e a lista Predefinida Levantamento Selecionar (V), Linear (L), Área (A), Círculo (O), Arco (R, uma chave liga/desliga), Deduzir (E, uma chave liga/desliga), Recorte (X), Contagem (C), Contagem automática (S) Editar Desfazer (Ctrl+Z), Refazer (Ctrl+Y), Copiar (Ctrl+C), Colar (Ctrl+V) e Mais: Recortar, Copiar, Colar, Colar no mesmo local, Duplicar, Excluir, Selecionar tudo, Limpar seleção Exibir Ajustar página (F), Ajustar largura (W) A ferramenta que você está usando aparece pressionada. Os botões Arco e Deduzir ficam pressionados enquanto estão ligados. Folhas (esquerda) Buscar folhas filtra a lista pelo nome da folha, pelo nome do arquivo ou pelo rótulo da página. Cada folha mostra uma miniatura (desenhada conforme você rola), seu nome e sua escala ou Sem escala. O lado direito de cada linha mostra quantas marcações a folha tem. Enquanto um item está destacado, mostra quantas marcações desse item estão na folha. Clique em uma folha para abri-la. Page Down e Page Up vão para a folha seguinte e a anterior da lista. Quando um levantamento cobre PDFs que não estão abertos, uma nota sob a lista os nomeia. Veja Salvar e reabrir um levantamento. Os nomes das folhas vêm do PDF: um rótulo de página como A-101 vira o nome da folha. Uma página sem rótulo recebe o nome do arquivo e o número da página, como \"Plantas, página 3\". Com vários PDFs abertos, as folhas com rótulo mostram também o nome do arquivo. A folha (meio) A barra sob a folha mostra o nome da folha e sua escala, ou \"Ainda sem escala: escolha uma predefinida ou calibre\". Seus botões: MarkupCraft (quando esse aplicativo consegue receber o PDF; veja Iniciar um levantamento e abrir um conjunto de plantas), Comparar (uma versão mais antiga da folha sobre esta: Comparar uma folha revisada), \"(n) a conferir\" (marcações sinalizadas, quando há) e 3D (a vista de conferência em 3D). Antes de abrir qualquer PDF, o meio mostra um guia curto de cinco passos. A roda do mouse dá zoom no ponteiro. Arraste para mover. Veja Teclado e mouse. Enquanto você usa a Contagem automática, uma barra com seus controles aparece no alto da folha. Itens (direita) No alto, a lista Área da proposta: o edifício, pavimento ou etapa em que as novas marcações entram (Áreas da proposta). Novo item abre um pequeno formulário: um nome, um tipo (Linear (comprimento), Área ou Contagem) e Adicionar. A lista mostra a cor, o ícone do tipo, o nome e o quantitativo de cada item. Clique em um item para desenhar com ele e destacar suas marcações em todas as folhas. Clique de novo para parar de destacar. Sob a lista, Item: (nome) é o editor do item: nome, tipo, cor, unidade, altura ou profundidade, perda, preços de material e mão de obra, os valores medidos e a Composição. Veja Criar itens de levantamento. Marcação selecionada aparece quando você seleciona uma marcação: a que item ela pertence, sua folha, suas medidas e botões como Adicionar um recorte e Excluir marcação. Com várias marcações selecionadas, vira Marcações selecionadas: quantas de cada item, e Copiar, Duplicar, Recortar e Excluir marcações. O editor do item termina com Copiar item e Duplicar item: um item com sua composição e seus custos, sem marcações. Veja Copiar, colar e duplicar marcações. Quantitativos (embaixo) Por item lista cada item e os componentes de sua composição com quantitativo, perda, preços e custos estendidos. Você pode digitar perdas e preços direto na tabela. Por folha lista o quantitativo de cada item em cada folha. Um aviso começando com Incompleto: aparece quando algo ficou fora dos totais. O Total da obra fica à direita do cabeçalho. O botão de seta no extremo direito oculta ou mostra a tabela para dar mais espaço à folha. Veja Ler os quantitativos e o orçamento. Barra de status (embaixo) Da esquerda para a direita: a medição ao vivo enquanto você desenha (comprimento, área, raio ou o resultado de uma conferência), a caixa Digite enquanto você digita um comprimento, as mensagens do TakeoffCraft, o lembrete \"Shift prende em 45°\" (ou \"Prende em 45°; Shift libera o ângulo\" quando isso está ligado em Configurações), o botão Snap (prender às linhas do desenho, ligado quando destacado; F3) e a porcentagem de zoom. Leia a barra de status quando algo não acontecer. O TakeoffCraft explica ali por que recusou um clique, por exemplo \"Esta folha ainda não tem escala\". Outros layouts Tudo acima é o layout CadCraft clássico, o inicial. Configurações (Ctrl+,) › Global › Layout oferece mais dois; as mesmas ferramentas e teclas funcionam nos três, e escolher CadCraft clássico de novo coloca cada controle de volta onde estava. Layout No TakeoffCraft Faixa de opções e navegador do projeto A Faixa de opções do levantamento no alto (Menus, teclas e mouse), uma barra para a ferramenta em uso, uma guia para cada folha que você abre (o Modelo e os layouts de um desenho também são folhas), com as escalas predefinidas e Ajustar página sob a folha. O painel da esquerda traz Propriedades (o item, a nota ou a marcação selecionada) sobre o Navegador do levantamento: Folhas (clique em uma para abri-la em uma guia), Condições (clique em uma para desenhar com ela) e Orçamento (o total de cada item e o total da obra); as miniaturas ficam na segunda guia. Caixa de ferramentas e caixa de informações A barra de ferramentas sobre a folha, uma caixa de informações com a Área da proposta, a ferramenta em uso ou a seleção e a barra da folha, uma coluna com as ferramentas de levantamento à esquerda, uma guia para cada folha, e Snap e o zoom sob a folha. Enquanto a vista de conferência em 3D está aberta, as guias das folhas e a barra sob a folha saem do caminho; feche a vista 3D para medir de novo. No celular, o layout para celular é sempre o que roda. Tela cheia e temas O botão Tela cheia na barra do aplicativo, ou Ctrl+Shift+F (Control em todas as plataformas, também no Mac), alterna entre normal, tela cheia e tela cheia com as barras ocultas. Com as barras ocultas, leve o ponteiro à borda superior para mostrá-las de novo. Papel é o tema claro e Modelo o escuro. Em Modelo o desenho aparece invertido (linhas claras sobre fundo escuro) apenas na tela; as exportações nunca são alteradas.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"files","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Arquivos que o TakeoffCraft abre e salva","summary":"PDFs na entrada; arquivo de projeto de levantamento, planilhas e PDFs marcados na saída - o que cada um guarda, para onde vai e o que muda online, offline e entre navegadores.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["arquivos","formatos","pdf","takeoff.json","arquivo de projeto","xlsx","csv","pdf marcado","baixar","salvar","abrir","offline"],"headings":[{"id":"o-que-ele-abre","text":"O que ele abre","level":2},{"id":"o-que-ele-salva","text":"O que ele salva","level":2},{"id":"para-onde-v-o-os-downloads","text":"Para onde vão os downloads","level":2},{"id":"por-que-o-arquivo-de-projeto-e-os-pdfs-s-o-separados","text":"Por que o arquivo de projeto e os PDFs são separados","level":2},{"id":"online-e-offline","text":"Online e offline","level":2},{"id":"planilhas","text":"Planilhas","level":2},{"id":"desktop-e-nuvem","text":"Desktop e nuvem","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-ele-abre\"\u003EO que ele abre\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EArquivo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EComo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EObservações\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPDF (\u003Ccode\u003E.pdf\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir plantas\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, ou soltar na página\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVários de uma vez. Cada página vira uma folha.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProjeto de levantamento (\u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir levantamento\u003C/span\u003E, ou soltar na página\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeus itens, escalas e marcações. Você pode soltá-lo junto com seus PDFs.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOutros arquivos soltos na página são ignorados, e a barra de status os nomeia.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPDFs protegidos por senha\u003C/strong\u003E não abrem: &quot;(nome) está protegido por senha. Abra uma cópia sem proteção.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EO mesmo PDF duas vezes\u003C/strong\u003E é aberto uma só vez: &quot;(nome) já está aberto.&quot; O TakeoffCraft reconhece os arquivos pelo conteúdo, não pelo nome.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPDFs digitalizados\u003C/strong\u003E abrem e são medidos como qualquer outro; o TakeoffCraft não precisa do texto do PDF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConjuntos de plantas grandes\u003C/strong\u003E: cada PDF é lido por inteiro ao abrir (para calcular sua impressão digital), então um arquivo muito grande leva um momento. As miniaturas são desenhadas uma de cada vez conforme você rola.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-ele-salva\"\u003EO que ele salva\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETodo salvamento é um download. Nada é gravado de volta nos seus PDFs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EVocê escolhe\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EVocê recebe\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EO que há nele\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar levantamento\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(nome).takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItens, composições, preços, escalas das folhas e todas as marcações, ligados a cada PDF pelo conteúdo. Sem páginas de PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(nome).xlsx\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuatro planilhas: Orçamento (com fórmulas vivas), Por folha, Marcações, Escalas.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(nome).csv\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EO orçamento em valores simples, uma linha por item e componente.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF marcado\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(nome do PDF) (levantamento).pdf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUma cópia de cada PDF aberto que tem marcações, com o levantamento desenhado nas páginas e uma legenda. Um arquivo por PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO nome vem do primeiro PDF que você abriu neste levantamento. Ainda não há campo para renomear: renomeie o arquivo baixado se quiser outro nome.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVeja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-estimate\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-estimate\"\u003EExportar o orçamento e um PDF marcado\u003C/a\u003E e \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-columns\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-columns\"\u003EColunas da exportação\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"para-onde-v-o-os-downloads\"\u003EPara onde vão os downloads\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESeu navegador os salva onde salva todo download, normalmente a pasta Downloads. Encontre-a no \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EExplorador de Arquivos\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eseu gerenciador de arquivos\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E. Se o navegador estiver configurado para perguntar onde salvar cada arquivo, ele pergunta.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF marcado\u003C/span\u003E com vários PDFs marcados baixa vários arquivos de uma vez. O Chrome e o Edge podem perguntar uma vez se este site pode baixar vários arquivos. Escolha Permitir.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO Safari salva em Downloads, a menos que você tenha mudado o local de download nos Ajustes do Safari, em Geral.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"por-que-o-arquivo-de-projeto-e-os-pdfs-s-o-separados\"\u003EPor que o arquivo de projeto e os PDFs são separados\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO arquivo \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E guarda só o seu levantamento. Ele reencontra seus PDFs pelo conteúdo (uma impressão digital de cada arquivo), então:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERenomear ou mover um PDF não importa.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUm PDF alterado ou salvo de novo por outro programa conta como um arquivo diferente, mesmo com o mesmo nome.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMarcações em PDFs que não estão abertos continuam contando nos totais.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVeja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\"\u003ESalvar e reabrir um levantamento\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-e-offline\"\u003EOnline e offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESeus PDFs e levantamentos nunca saem do seu computador, online ou offline. A conexão só importa para carregar o próprio TakeoffCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPara trabalhar sem conexão, salve o aplicativo antes, enquanto estiver online:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E na barra do aplicativo, no alto. O botão faz a contagem, &quot;Salvando 12 de 40&quot;, enquanto salva.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEspere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPronto para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESalvar o TakeoffCraft guarda no navegador o aplicativo, esta ajuda, o leitor de PDF, o gravador de PDF usado por \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF marcado\u003C/span\u003E e o processo da contagem automática, cerca de 6 MB. Quando aparecer \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPronto para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E, tudo funciona offline: abrir PDFs, medir, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContagem automática\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar levantamento\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF marcado\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EO botão diz\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESignificado\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAinda não salvo neste navegador. Escolha-o para salvar.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESalvando (n) de (m)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESalvando agora.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPronto para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESalvo. Escolha-o para \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar de novo (obter a versão mais recente)\u003C/span\u003E ou \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemover a cópia offline\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENão foi possível salvar\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlguns arquivos não foram baixados. Verifique sua conexão e escolha o botão de novo.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline indisponível\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEsta janela do navegador não consegue guardar uma cópia offline (janelas privadas e alguns computadores gerenciados bloqueiam isso).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMais, inclusive quanto tempo dura uma cópia salva em cada navegador: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003ETrabalhar offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVocê está offline. Se a barra do aplicativo mostra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPronto para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E, tudo funciona. Se não mostra, as partes que o TakeoffCraft ainda não carregou nesta guia (o gravador de PDF para \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF marcado\u003C/span\u003E, o processo da contagem automática) não podem carregar agora, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF marcado\u003C/span\u003E para com &quot;Não foi possível gravar o PDF marcado&quot;. Seu levantamento está seguro de qualquer forma: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar levantamento\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E não precisam de conexão. Não recarregue a guia até voltar a ficar online.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:online\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOnline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVocê está online. Antes de uma visita à obra ou de um voo, escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E e espere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPronto para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"planilhas\"\u003EPlanilhas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO XLSX mantém fórmulas vivas, então um programa de planilhas recalcula o orçamento ao abrir o arquivo e o mantém correto quando você muda um preço ou uma perda lá. A pasta de trabalho foi verificada no LibreOffice Calc, que recalculou todos os totais até o mesmo centavo. Ela ainda não foi aberta no Excel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO CSV traz valores simples com uma marca de ordem de bytes, para que os programas de planilhas leiam corretamente caracteres como m².\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"desktop-e-nuvem\"\u003EDesktop e nuvem\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"shell:desktop\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EDesktop app\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA partir da 0.5, o aplicativo de desktop é o CadCraft Studio: o TakeoffCraft e os outros sete aplicativos em uma só instalação. Não há instalador separado do TakeoffCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO salvamento na nuvem ainda não está disponível. Guarde seus arquivos \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E com os PDFs da obra e faça cópia de segurança deles como de qualquer outro arquivo. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EPara onde vão seus arquivos\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"O que ele abre Arquivo Como Observações PDF (.pdf) Abrir plantas, Ctrl+O, ou soltar na página Vários de uma vez. Cada página vira uma folha. Projeto de levantamento (.takeoff.json) Abrir levantamento, ou soltar na página Seus itens, escalas e marcações. Você pode soltá-lo junto com seus PDFs. Outros arquivos soltos na página são ignorados, e a barra de status os nomeia. PDFs protegidos por senha não abrem: \"(nome) está protegido por senha. Abra uma cópia sem proteção.\" O mesmo PDF duas vezes é aberto uma só vez: \"(nome) já está aberto.\" O TakeoffCraft reconhece os arquivos pelo conteúdo, não pelo nome. PDFs digitalizados abrem e são medidos como qualquer outro; o TakeoffCraft não precisa do texto do PDF. Conjuntos de plantas grandes: cada PDF é lido por inteiro ao abrir (para calcular sua impressão digital), então um arquivo muito grande leva um momento. As miniaturas são desenhadas uma de cada vez conforme você rola. O que ele salva Todo salvamento é um download. Nada é gravado de volta nos seus PDFs. Você escolhe Você recebe O que há nele Salvar levantamento (nome).takeoff.json Itens, composições, preços, escalas das folhas e todas as marcações, ligados a cada PDF pelo conteúdo. Sem páginas de PDF. XLSX (nome).xlsx Quatro planilhas: Orçamento (com fórmulas vivas), Por folha, Marcações, Escalas. CSV (nome).csv O orçamento em valores simples, uma linha por item e componente. PDF marcado (nome do PDF) (levantamento).pdf Uma cópia de cada PDF aberto que tem marcações, com o levantamento desenhado nas páginas e uma legenda. Um arquivo por PDF. O nome vem do primeiro PDF que você abriu neste levantamento. Ainda não há campo para renomear: renomeie o arquivo baixado se quiser outro nome. Veja Exportar o orçamento e um PDF marcado e Colunas da exportação. Para onde vão os downloads Seu navegador os salva onde salva todo download, normalmente a pasta Downloads. Encontre-a no Finder / Explorador de Arquivos / seu gerenciador de arquivos. Se o navegador estiver configurado para perguntar onde salvar cada arquivo, ele pergunta. PDF marcado com vários PDFs marcados baixa vários arquivos de uma vez. O Chrome e o Edge podem perguntar uma vez se este site pode baixar vários arquivos. Escolha Permitir. O Safari salva em Downloads, a menos que você tenha mudado o local de download nos Ajustes do Safari, em Geral. Por que o arquivo de projeto e os PDFs são separados O arquivo .takeoff.json guarda só o seu levantamento. Ele reencontra seus PDFs pelo conteúdo (uma impressão digital de cada arquivo), então: Renomear ou mover um PDF não importa. Um PDF alterado ou salvo de novo por outro programa conta como um arquivo diferente, mesmo com o mesmo nome. Marcações em PDFs que não estão abertos continuam contando nos totais. Veja Salvar e reabrir um levantamento. Online e offline Seus PDFs e levantamentos nunca saem do seu computador, online ou offline. A conexão só importa para carregar o próprio TakeoffCraft. Para trabalhar sem conexão, salve o aplicativo antes, enquanto estiver online: Escolha Salvar para uso offline na barra do aplicativo, no alto. O botão faz a contagem, \"Salvando 12 de 40\", enquanto salva. Espere Pronto para uso offline. Salvar o TakeoffCraft guarda no navegador o aplicativo, esta ajuda, o leitor de PDF, o gravador de PDF usado por PDF marcado e o processo da contagem automática, cerca de 6 MB. Quando aparecer Pronto para uso offline, tudo funciona offline: abrir PDFs, medir, Contagem automática, Salvar levantamento, XLSX, CSV e PDF marcado. O botão diz Significado Salvar para uso offline Ainda não salvo neste navegador. Escolha-o para salvar. Salvando (n) de (m) Salvando agora. Pronto para uso offline Salvo. Escolha-o para Salvar de novo (obter a versão mais recente) ou Remover a cópia offline. Não foi possível salvar Alguns arquivos não foram baixados. Verifique sua conexão e escolha o botão de novo. Offline indisponível Esta janela do navegador não consegue guardar uma cópia offline (janelas privadas e alguns computadores gerenciados bloqueiam isso). Mais, inclusive quanto tempo dura uma cópia salva em cada navegador: Trabalhar offline. Você está offline. Se a barra do aplicativo mostra Pronto para uso offline, tudo funciona. Se não mostra, as partes que o TakeoffCraft ainda não carregou nesta guia (o gravador de PDF para PDF marcado, o processo da contagem automática) não podem carregar agora, e PDF marcado para com \"Não foi possível gravar o PDF marcado\". Seu levantamento está seguro de qualquer forma: Salvar levantamento, XLSX e CSV não precisam de conexão. Não recarregue a guia até voltar a ficar online. Você está online. Antes de uma visita à obra ou de um voo, escolha Salvar para uso offline e espere Pronto para uso offline. Planilhas O XLSX mantém fórmulas vivas, então um programa de planilhas recalcula o orçamento ao abrir o arquivo e o mantém correto quando você muda um preço ou uma perda lá. A pasta de trabalho foi verificada no LibreOffice Calc, que recalculou todos os totais até o mesmo centavo. Ela ainda não foi aberta no Excel. O CSV traz valores simples com uma marca de ordem de bytes, para que os programas de planilhas leiam corretamente caracteres como m². Desktop e nuvem A partir da 0.5, o aplicativo de desktop é o CadCraft Studio: o TakeoffCraft e os outros sete aplicativos em uma só instalação. Não há instalador separado do TakeoffCraft. O salvamento na nuvem ainda não está disponível. Guarde seus arquivos .takeoff.json com os PDFs da obra e faça cópia de segurança deles como de qualquer outro arquivo. Veja Para onde vão seus arquivos.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"open-plan-set","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Start a takeoff and open a plan set","summary":"Open one or several PDFs as a plan set, find your way round the sheets, and move between them.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["open","plan set","several pdfs","multiple files","sheets","drawings","page labels","sheet list","navigate","drop","handoff","open in another app","markup"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-pdfs","text":"Open the PDFs","level":2},{"id":"drawings-beside-pdfs","text":"Drawings beside PDFs","level":2},{"id":"a-pdf-handed-over-from-another-app","text":"A PDF handed over from another app","level":2},{"id":"find-a-sheet","text":"Find a sheet","level":2},{"id":"how-sheets-are-named","text":"How sheets are named","level":2},{"id":"look-around-a-sheet","text":"Look around a sheet","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA plan set is often several PDFs: architectural, structural, mechanical. TakeoffCraft opens them side by side as one list of sheets, and one takeoff can cover all of them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-pdfs\"\u003EOpen the PDFs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. (The other menu styles call it \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen PDFs…\u003C/span\u003E.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the file dialog, select every PDF of the set. Hold \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003ECommand\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E to pick several, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to pick a range.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose Open. A &quot;Opening (name)…&quot; message shows while each file is read. Then every page appears under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E and the first page of the first new PDF opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also drag the PDFs from \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E and drop them anywhere on TakeoffCraft. A &quot;Drop PDFs, DWG or DXF drawings, or a .takeoff.json&quot; frame appears while you drag.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo add more PDFs later, do the same again. PDFs that are already open are skipped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe start screen also lists the PDFs and takeoffs you opened recently, in browsers that let a page open a file again (Chrome and Edge). Click one to open it; the browser may ask for permission first. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EYour files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing is uploaded. The browser reads the PDFs on your computer, and they are gone from TakeoffCraft when you close the tab or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings-beside-pdfs\"\u003EDrawings beside PDFs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E also takes DWG and DXF drawings, alone or together with PDFs: each drawing&#039;s model space and layouts become sheets in the same list. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\"\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E, then \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\"\u003ECount from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pdf-handed-over-from-another-app\"\u003EA PDF handed over from another app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnother app of the lineup can hand you the PDF it has open: it opens TakeoffCraft in a new tab and the PDF opens there as if you had dropped it. The status bar says &quot;Opened (name), handed over from (app). The original file is unchanged.&quot; Nothing is uploaded; the file passes through this browser&#039;s own storage and is gone from it once it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the status bar says the PDF &quot;did not arrive&quot;, the tab waited too long or the PDF was already opened in another tab: open it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe other way round, when MarkupCraft runs where you are and can take a file, the sheet bar under the drawing has a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E button: it opens the PDF of the sheet on screen in MarkupCraft, in a new tab, to review and mark it up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-a-sheet\"\u003EFind a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E list on the left and click a sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in \u003Cstrong\u003ESearch sheets\u003C/strong\u003E at the top of the list to filter by sheet number, file name or page label. The count beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E shows &quot;3 of 40&quot; while a filter is on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the next sheet and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the previous one. They follow the list as filtered.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac keyboard without Page Up and Page Down keys, press fn with the up or down arrow.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-sheets-are-named\"\u003EHow sheets are named\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA page whose PDF gives it a label, such as \u003Ccode\u003EA-101\u003C/code\u003E, is named by that label.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA page with no label (or a label that is just its page number) is named &quot;(file name), page (n)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith several PDFs open, labeled sheets also carry the file name: &quot;A-101 — Architectural&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheet names cannot be typed in by hand yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-around-a-sheet\"\u003ELook around a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn the mouse wheel to zoom at the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag with the left or middle button to pan. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E, or when you start on a mark&#039;s corner, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag, or drag with the middle button.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit page\u003C/span\u003E to see the whole sheet; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit width\u003C/span\u003E fits its width.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"=\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to zoom in and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to zoom out, around the middle of the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach sheet remembers its own zoom and position while it stays open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore you can measure a sheet, it needs a scale: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A plan set is often several PDFs: architectural, structural, mechanical. TakeoffCraft opens them side by side as one list of sheets, and one takeoff can cover all of them. Open the PDFs Choose Open plans in the File group, or press Ctrl+O. (The other menu styles call it Open PDFs….) In the file dialog, select every PDF of the set. Hold Command / Ctrl / Ctrl to pick several, or Shift to pick a range. Choose Open. A \"Opening (name)…\" message shows while each file is read. Then every page appears under Sheets and the first page of the first new PDF opens. You can also drag the PDFs from Finder / File Explorer / your file manager and drop them anywhere on TakeoffCraft. A \"Drop PDFs, DWG or DXF drawings, or a .takeoff.json\" frame appears while you drag. To add more PDFs later, do the same again. PDFs that are already open are skipped. The start screen also lists the PDFs and takeoffs you opened recently, in browsers that let a page open a file again (Chrome and Edge). Click one to open it; the browser may ask for permission first. See Your files. Nothing is uploaded. The browser reads the PDFs on your computer, and they are gone from TakeoffCraft when you close the tab or choose New. Drawings beside PDFs Open plans also takes DWG and DXF drawings, alone or together with PDFs: each drawing's model space and layouts become sheets in the same list. See Open a DWG or DXF drawing, then Count from a drawing. A PDF handed over from another app Another app of the lineup can hand you the PDF it has open: it opens TakeoffCraft in a new tab and the PDF opens there as if you had dropped it. The status bar says \"Opened (name), handed over from (app). The original file is unchanged.\" Nothing is uploaded; the file passes through this browser's own storage and is gone from it once it opens. If the status bar says the PDF \"did not arrive\", the tab waited too long or the PDF was already opened in another tab: open it with Open plans. The other way round, when MarkupCraft runs where you are and can take a file, the sheet bar under the drawing has a MarkupCraft button: it opens the PDF of the sheet on screen in MarkupCraft, in a new tab, to review and mark it up. Find a sheet Scroll the Sheets list on the left and click a sheet. Type in Search sheets at the top of the list to filter by sheet number, file name or page label. The count beside Sheets shows \"3 of 40\" while a filter is on. Press Page Down for the next sheet and Page Up for the previous one. They follow the list as filtered. On a Mac keyboard without Page Up and Page Down keys, press fn with the up or down arrow. How sheets are named A page whose PDF gives it a label, such as A-101, is named by that label. A page with no label (or a label that is just its page number) is named \"(file name), page (n)\". With several PDFs open, labeled sheets also carry the file name: \"A-101 — Architectural\". Sheet names cannot be typed in by hand yet. Look around a sheet Turn the mouse wheel to zoom at the pointer. Drag with the left or middle button to pan. With Auto count, or when you start on a mark's corner, hold Space while you drag, or drag with the middle button. Press F or choose Fit page to see the whole sheet; W or Fit width fits its width. Press + or = to zoom in and − to zoom out, around the middle of the view. Each sheet remembers its own zoom and position while it stays open. Next Before you can measure a sheet, it needs a scale: Set the scale.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"open-a-drawing","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Open a DWG or DXF drawing","summary":"Open a drawing file as sheets (model space and every layout), start with the scale the drawing gives, show or hide its layers, and bring in linked drawings.","section":"tasks","order":12,"keywords":["dwg","dxf","cad","drawing","open a drawing","layouts","model space","paper space","sheets","units","insunits","viewport scale","xref","linked drawing","layers","frozen","no pdf","plot","without plotting"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-drawing","text":"Open the drawing","level":2},{"id":"sheets","text":"Sheets","level":2},{"id":"the-scale","text":"The scale","level":2},{"id":"layers","text":"Layers","level":2},{"id":"linked-drawings-xrefs","text":"Linked drawings (xrefs)","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft opens a DWG or DXF drawing as it opens a PDF: you do not have to plot it to PDF first. Counting from the drawing itself is exact, because the drawing knows what each object is. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\"\u003ECount from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-drawing\"\u003EOpen the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the DWG or DXF file. You can select several, and PDFs with them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose Open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also drop drawing files on TakeoffCraft, like PDFs. Nothing is uploaded: the browser reads the file on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft reads DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 and ASCII DXF from R12 to 2018. It refuses a binary DXF (save it as ASCII, or as a DWG) and a file that is neither, and says why. A damaged file opens with what could be read, and a message says so; its counts may be incomplete.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets\"\u003ESheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModel\u003C/strong\u003E is the drawing&#039;s model space.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEach layout\u003C/strong\u003E (paper space tab) is a sheet, named by its tab, in tab order. A layout shows the model through its viewports, as it plots.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EZoom, pan, snapping to the drawing&#039;s own lines, marks, notes, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E and the rest work as on a PDF. A drawing&#039;s sheets are saved in the takeoff by the file&#039;s name and a fingerprint of its contents, like a PDF: open the same file again and the marks are on its sheets.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-scale\"\u003EThe scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sheet starts with the scale the drawing gives:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA layout\u003C/strong\u003E takes the scale of its biggest viewport. A scale such as 1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; is named as the preset it is; any other is shown as &quot;From the drawing&#039;s viewport: 1&quot; = 2.5&#039;&quot;. If a layout has viewports at other scales, the biggest sets the sheet&#039;s scale: check the others with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModel\u003C/strong\u003E takes the drawing&#039;s units (inches, millimeters, feet…) as its scale, &quot;Drawing units (inches)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA drawing with no units\u003C/strong\u003E (the drawing never said whether a unit is an inch or a millimeter) has no scale: the sheet says &quot;No scale&quot;, and measuring on it is refused, as on a PDF with no scale. Counting needs no scale. Give it one with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list change a scale at any time and win over the drawing&#039;s. The preset list is off on Model, which has no paper size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"layers\"\u003ELayers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar lists the drawing&#039;s layers with how many objects each has on this sheet. Layers the file turned off or froze start hidden. Untick a layer to hide it, or tick a hidden one to show it: the sheet draws again, and what is hidden is left out of counts and of the objects offered to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure drawing\u003C/span\u003E, unless you ask to count hidden layers too. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E shows every layer; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the file&#039;s layers\u003C/span\u003E undoes your changes. Layer choices belong to this session, not to the takeoff file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"linked-drawings-xrefs\"\u003ELinked drawings (xrefs)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing that links other drawings shows them where they are inserted. TakeoffCraft finds a linked drawing among the \u003Cstrong\u003Eopen files\u003C/strong\u003E, by its file name: open the host drawing and its linked drawing together (or one after the other). Until then the status bar says how many linked drawings were not found, what they draw is missing, and counts say so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EObjects TakeoffCraft cannot read or place (proxy objects, unknown classes) are listed in the count window by type; they are never dropped without a word.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVery large drawings are drawn in part, and counts stop at a cap, saying so. Open a PDF plot of such a drawing instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA drawing is drawn in black and in its own colors, darkened for white paper. Plot style tables and pen assignments are not applied.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft opens a DWG or DXF drawing as it opens a PDF: you do not have to plot it to PDF first. Counting from the drawing itself is exact, because the drawing knows what each object is. See Count from a drawing. Open the drawing Choose Open plans in the File group, or press Ctrl+O. Select the DWG or DXF file. You can select several, and PDFs with them. Choose Open. You can also drop drawing files on TakeoffCraft, like PDFs. Nothing is uploaded: the browser reads the file on your computer. TakeoffCraft reads DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 and ASCII DXF from R12 to 2018. It refuses a binary DXF (save it as ASCII, or as a DWG) and a file that is neither, and says why. A damaged file opens with what could be read, and a message says so; its counts may be incomplete. Sheets Model is the drawing's model space. Each layout (paper space tab) is a sheet, named by its tab, in tab order. A layout shows the model through its viewports, as it plots. Zoom, pan, snapping to the drawing's own lines, marks, notes, Calibrate and the rest work as on a PDF. A drawing's sheets are saved in the takeoff by the file's name and a fingerprint of its contents, like a PDF: open the same file again and the marks are on its sheets. The scale A sheet starts with the scale the drawing gives: A layout takes the scale of its biggest viewport. A scale such as 1/2\" = 1'-0\" is named as the preset it is; any other is shown as \"From the drawing's viewport: 1\" = 2.5'\". If a layout has viewports at other scales, the biggest sets the sheet's scale: check the others with Check. Model takes the drawing's units (inches, millimeters, feet…) as its scale, \"Drawing units (inches)\". A drawing with no units (the drawing never said whether a unit is an inch or a millimeter) has no scale: the sheet says \"No scale\", and measuring on it is refused, as on a PDF with no scale. Counting needs no scale. Give it one with Calibrate. Calibrate, Check and the Preset list change a scale at any time and win over the drawing's. The preset list is off on Model, which has no paper size. Layers Layers on the sheet bar lists the drawing's layers with how many objects each has on this sheet. Layers the file turned off or froze start hidden. Untick a layer to hide it, or tick a hidden one to show it: the sheet draws again, and what is hidden is left out of counts and of the objects offered to Measure drawing, unless you ask to count hidden layers too. Show all shows every layer; Back to the file's layers undoes your changes. Layer choices belong to this session, not to the takeoff file. Linked drawings (xrefs) A drawing that links other drawings shows them where they are inserted. TakeoffCraft finds a linked drawing among the open files, by its file name: open the host drawing and its linked drawing together (or one after the other). Until then the status bar says how many linked drawings were not found, what they draw is missing, and counts say so. Limits Objects TakeoffCraft cannot read or place (proxy objects, unknown classes) are listed in the count window by type; they are never dropped without a word. Very large drawings are drawn in part, and counts stop at a cap, saying so. Open a PDF plot of such a drawing instead. A drawing is drawn in black and in its own colors, darkened for white paper. Plot style tables and pen assignments are not applied.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"open-an-ifc-file","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Bring in quantities from an IFC 4.3 file","summary":"Open an IFC 4.3 file of a road, railway, bridge or earthworks and get a count per class, with the lengths, areas and volumes the file states, as typed quantities.","section":"tasks","order":13,"keywords":["ifc","ifc4x3","ifc 4.3","road","railway","bridge","pavement","kerb","course","earthworks","bearing","infrastructure","quantities","bim","civil","typed quantities"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-file","text":"Open the file","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-do","text":"What it does not do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn IFC 4.3 file can hold roads, railways, bridges, marine works and the parts they are made of: pavements, courses, kerbs, rails, earthworks, bearings, piles and more. TakeoffCraft reads those and turns them into typed quantities you can price.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-file\"\u003EOpen the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the IFC file (you can select it with PDFs), or drop it on TakeoffCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne typed quantity is made for each class and predefined type, for example &quot;Pavement (flexible): count&quot;, and, when the file states them, &quot;length&quot; in m, &quot;area&quot; in m² and &quot;volume&quot; in m³. The note on each item says how many elements were counted and how many stated no quantity. Opening a newer file of the same name updates those quantities instead of adding more.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-do\"\u003EWhat it does not do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt reads the quantity sets (IfcElementQuantity) the file carries. It does not measure the 3D shapes, so an element with no quantity set is counted but adds no length, area or volume.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly the infrastructure classes of IFC 4.3 are read. A building file (walls, doors, slabs) is not turned into quantities here; a file that is not IFC 4.3 says so and brings nothing in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe units are the file&#039;s own, converted to metres.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"An IFC 4.3 file can hold roads, railways, bridges, marine works and the parts they are made of: pavements, courses, kerbs, rails, earthworks, bearings, piles and more. TakeoffCraft reads those and turns them into typed quantities you can price. Open the file Choose Open plans in the File group, or press Ctrl+O. Select the IFC file (you can select it with PDFs), or drop it on TakeoffCraft. One typed quantity is made for each class and predefined type, for example \"Pavement (flexible): count\", and, when the file states them, \"length\" in m, \"area\" in m² and \"volume\" in m³. The note on each item says how many elements were counted and how many stated no quantity. Opening a newer file of the same name updates those quantities instead of adding more. What it does not do It reads the quantity sets (IfcElementQuantity) the file carries. It does not measure the 3D shapes, so an element with no quantity set is counted but adds no length, area or volume. Only the infrastructure classes of IFC 4.3 are read. A building file (walls, doors, slabs) is not turned into quantities here; a file that is not IFC 4.3 says so and brings nothing in. The units are the file's own, converted to metres.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"set-the-scale","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Set the scale","summary":"Give each sheet a scale with a preset or by calibrating on a known dimension, check it, and understand why unscaled sheets are left out of the totals.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["detect scale","read scale","scale from text","all sheets","scale","calibrate","preset","check scale","verify","known dimension","unscaled","no scale","ratio","set scale"],"headings":[{"id":"use-a-preset-when-the-pdf-is-printed-at-full-size","text":"Use a preset (when the PDF is printed at full size)","level":2},{"id":"read-the-scales-from-the-sheets-text","text":"Read the scales from the sheets' text","level":2},{"id":"calibrate-on-a-known-dimension","text":"Calibrate on a known dimension","level":2},{"id":"check-the-scale","text":"Check the scale","level":2},{"id":"change-a-scale","text":"Change a scale","level":2},{"id":"why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero","text":"Why unscaled sheets are not counted as zero","level":2},{"id":"tips","text":"Tips","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery sheet has its own scale. You set it, or let TakeoffCraft read it from the sheets&#039; text (below), and check it. Until a sheet has one, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E refuse to measure on it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E work without a scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sheets list shows each sheet&#039;s scale, or \u003Cstrong\u003ENo scale\u003C/strong\u003E. The bar under the open sheet says the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-a-preset-when-the-pdf-is-printed-at-full-size\"\u003EUse a preset (when the PDF is printed at full size)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the scale printed on the drawing, for example \u003Ccode\u003E1/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E under Architectural. The status bar says &quot;Scale for this sheet: 1/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck it (below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA preset assumes the PDF is the real paper size the drawing was made for. If someone made the PDF with &quot;fit to page&quot;, or it is a half-size set, the preset is wrong. Calibrate instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe full list is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/scale-presets\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/scale-presets\"\u003EScale presets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-scales-from-the-sheets-text\"\u003ERead the scales from the sheets&#039; text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost drawings say their scale: \u003Ccode\u003ESCALE: 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E in the title block, \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E under a view. TakeoffCraft can read it and set every sheet at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom the sheets&#039; text…\u003C/span\u003E (at the top).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe list shows every sheet: its scale now, and what its text says — \u003Cstrong\u003ESays 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ENo scale in its text\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003ESays not to scale\u003C/strong\u003E (NTS).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESheets without a scale that say one are ticked. Tick or untick any, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet the ticked scales\u003C/span\u003E. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes them all back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck one dimension on each sheet with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E (below): a preset is only right when the PDF is printed at full size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt reads architectural (\u003Ccode\u003E3/16&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E), engineering (\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 20&#039;\u003C/code\u003E) and metric (\u003Ccode\u003E1:50\u003C/code\u003E) notations that match a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/scale-presets\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/scale-presets\"\u003Epreset\u003C/a\u003E. When a sheet says more than one scale (a plan with enlarged details), it takes the one it says most often, and one written after the word SCALE counts double; the others are shown in red so you can check. A scanned sheet has no text: set its scale by hand.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"calibrate-on-a-known-dimension\"\u003ECalibrate on a known dimension\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse this when there is no printed scale you trust, the set was resized, or the sheet says &quot;not to scale&quot; but has dimensions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the sheet and zoom in on a long dimension you know, such as a grid line spacing or an overall building dimension. The longer, the more accurate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one end of the dimension. Zoom in on each end for precision; you can zoom and pan between the clicks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the other end. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the line at 0°, 45° or 90°. The \u003Cstrong\u003ECalibrate this sheet\u003C/strong\u003E dialog opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the real distance in \u003Cstrong\u003EReal distance\u003C/strong\u003E, for example \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E24.5\u003C/code\u003E (decimal feet), \u003Ccode\u003E30&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E7.5 m\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/entry-formats\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/entry-formats\"\u003ELength, rate and percent entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/span\u003E. The status bar shows the scale as &quot;1&quot; = (n)&#039;&quot;, and when it is within 0.5% of a preset it says which one, for example &quot;which matches 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; (pick that preset for an exact scale)&quot;. TakeoffCraft goes back to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E tool.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the calibration matches a preset, picking that preset gives an exact scale instead of one that depends on where you clicked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-the-scale\"\u003ECheck the scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlways check a new scale on a second dimension.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick both ends of a dimension printed on the drawing. The status bar shows &quot;Check: 12&#039;-6&quot;&quot; with the same length in decimal feet and meters, and the check line stays on the sheet with its length.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECompare it with the printed dimension. If it is off, calibrate on a longer dimension.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to clear the check line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-scale\"\u003EChange a scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick another preset or calibrate again. If the sheet already has measurements, TakeoffCraft asks \u003Cstrong\u003EChange this sheet&#039;s scale?\u003C/strong\u003E and says how many measurements will be measured again. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChange the scale\u003C/span\u003E. Every run and area on the sheet is re-measured at the new scale. You can undo it with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero\"\u003EWhy unscaled sheets are not counted as zero\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA mark on a sheet with no scale has no real length or area. TakeoffCraft never counts it as zero, which would quietly make your estimate too low. Instead it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eleaves those marks out of the totals,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eshows &quot;+(n) unscaled&quot; next to the item in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eshows &quot;Incomplete: (n) marks on sheets with no scale (not in the totals)&quot; in the table header,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ewrites the count into the notes column of the spreadsheet export.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis can happen when you open a project whose scales were never set. Give the sheet a scale and the marks are counted at once. Count marks are always counted; they need no scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tips\"\u003ETips\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the scale on every sheet you will measure before you start. Scan the sheets list for \u003Cstrong\u003ENo scale\u003C/strong\u003E to find the ones left (the search box looks at sheet names, not scales).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDetails and enlarged plans on the same sheet often use another scale. TakeoffCraft has one scale per sheet; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMetric drawings: pick a metric preset such as \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E, or calibrate with a metric distance like \u003Ccode\u003E6000 mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Every sheet has its own scale. You set it, or let TakeoffCraft read it from the sheets' text (below), and check it. Until a sheet has one, Linear, Area, Circle and Cut-out refuse to measure on it. Count and Auto count work without a scale. The sheets list shows each sheet's scale, or No scale. The bar under the open sheet says the same. Use a preset (when the PDF is printed at full size) Open the sheet. Open the Preset list in the Scale group. Pick the scale printed on the drawing, for example 1/8\" = 1'-0\" under Architectural. The status bar says \"Scale for this sheet: 1/8\" = 1'-0\".\" Check it (below). A preset assumes the PDF is the real paper size the drawing was made for. If someone made the PDF with \"fit to page\", or it is a half-size set, the preset is wrong. Calibrate instead. The full list is in Scale presets. Read the scales from the sheets' text Most drawings say their scale: SCALE: 1/4\" = 1'-0\" in the title block, 1:100 under a view. TakeoffCraft can read it and set every sheet at once. Open the Preset list and choose From the sheets' text… (at the top). The list shows every sheet: its scale now, and what its text says — Says 1/4\" = 1'-0\", No scale in its text, or Says not to scale (NTS). Sheets without a scale that say one are ticked. Tick or untick any, then choose Set the ticked scales. One Ctrl+Z takes them all back. Check one dimension on each sheet with Check (below): a preset is only right when the PDF is printed at full size. It reads architectural (3/16\" = 1'-0\", 1 1/2\" = 1'-0\"), engineering (1\" = 20') and metric (1:50) notations that match a preset. When a sheet says more than one scale (a plan with enlarged details), it takes the one it says most often, and one written after the word SCALE counts double; the others are shown in red so you can check. A scanned sheet has no text: set its scale by hand. Calibrate on a known dimension Use this when there is no printed scale you trust, the set was resized, or the sheet says \"not to scale\" but has dimensions. Open the sheet and zoom in on a long dimension you know, such as a grid line spacing or an overall building dimension. The longer, the more accurate. Choose Calibrate in the Scale group, or press K. Click one end of the dimension. Zoom in on each end for precision; you can zoom and pan between the clicks. Click the other end. Hold Shift to keep the line at 0°, 45° or 90°. The Calibrate this sheet dialog opens. Type the real distance in Real distance, for example 24'-6 1/2\", 24.5 (decimal feet), 30\" or 7.5 m. See Length, rate and percent entry. Choose Set the scale. The status bar shows the scale as \"1\" = (n)'\", and when it is within 0.5% of a preset it says which one, for example \"which matches 1/4\" = 1'-0\" (pick that preset for an exact scale)\". TakeoffCraft goes back to the Select tool. When the calibration matches a preset, picking that preset gives an exact scale instead of one that depends on where you clicked. Check the scale Always check a new scale on a second dimension. Choose Check in the Scale group, or press D. Click both ends of a dimension printed on the drawing. The status bar shows \"Check: 12'-6\"\" with the same length in decimal feet and meters, and the check line stays on the sheet with its length. Compare it with the printed dimension. If it is off, calibrate on a longer dimension. Press Esc to clear the check line. Change a scale Pick another preset or calibrate again. If the sheet already has measurements, TakeoffCraft asks Change this sheet's scale? and says how many measurements will be measured again. Choose Change the scale. Every run and area on the sheet is re-measured at the new scale. You can undo it with Ctrl+Z. Why unscaled sheets are not counted as zero A mark on a sheet with no scale has no real length or area. TakeoffCraft never counts it as zero, which would quietly make your estimate too low. Instead it: leaves those marks out of the totals, shows \"+(n) unscaled\" next to the item in Quantities, shows \"Incomplete: (n) marks on sheets with no scale (not in the totals)\" in the table header, writes the count into the notes column of the spreadsheet export. This can happen when you open a project whose scales were never set. Give the sheet a scale and the marks are counted at once. Count marks are always counted; they need no scale. Tips Set the scale on every sheet you will measure before you start. Scan the sheets list for No scale to find the ones left (the search box looks at sheet names, not scales). Details and enlarged plans on the same sheet often use another scale. TakeoffCraft has one scale per sheet; see What is not built yet. Metric drawings: pick a metric preset such as 1:100, or calibrate with a metric distance like 6000 mm.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"create-items","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Create takeoff items","summary":"Make linear, area and count items and set their unit, height or depth, waste and material and labor rates.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["crew","production rate","crew size","wage","hours","man-hours","worker-hours","crew-days","labor hours","item","new item","condition","multi-condition","also measure","several types","room","linear","area","count","unit","height","depth","waste","material","labor","rate","color","cost"],"headings":[{"id":"typed-quantities-no-drawing","text":"Typed quantities (no drawing)","level":2},{"id":"make-an-item","text":"Make an item","level":2},{"id":"pick-the-item-you-draw-with","text":"Pick the item you draw with","level":2},{"id":"set-up-the-item","text":"Set up the item","level":2},{"id":"one-item-several-kinds-of-marks","text":"One item, several kinds of marks","level":2},{"id":"rates-in-the-table","text":"Rates in the table","level":2},{"id":"how-pricing-works","text":"How pricing works","level":2},{"id":"labor-by-crew","text":"Labor by crew","level":2},{"id":"metric-items","text":"Metric items","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003Eitem\u003C/strong\u003E is one thing you measure and price: a wall type, a floor finish, a ceiling, a door, a light fixture. Every mark you draw belongs to an item. Other programs call these conditions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach item has a type (and can take other kinds of marks too, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items#one-item-several-kinds-of-marks\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-hash=\"one-item-several-kinds-of-marks\"\u003EOne item, several kinds of marks\u003C/a\u003E):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou draw\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt reports\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUnits\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERuns of connected points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength, number of runs, and wall area when it has a height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF or m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClosed outlines, with cut-outs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea, perimeter, number of areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESF, SY or m²\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne click per thing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEA\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyped quantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing: you type the quantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe number you typed (or one brought in from CivilCraft)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELS, EA, CY, m³, LF, m, SF, SY or m²\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typed-quantities-no-drawing\"\u003ETyped quantities (no drawing)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome things in an estimate are never drawn: mobilization, permits, a contingency, a lump-sum subcontract, the cut and fill CivilCraft worked out. Make them a \u003Cstrong\u003ETyped quantity\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E, type a name, pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETyped quantity (no drawing)\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the item editor pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E (LS for a lump sum) and type the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantity\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2.5\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1,250\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet waste and rates as for any other item. An assembly&#039;s formulas use \u003Ccode\u003EQ\u003C/code\u003E, the quantity, for example \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(Q / 12)\u003C/code\u003E truck loads.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA typed quantity takes no marks and never makes the estimate incomplete. When the takeoff has bid areas, the editor also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid area\u003C/span\u003E: the quantity then counts in that area, its alternates and its totals, just as a mark there would. Quantities brought in from another app say where they came from, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/earthwork#cut-and-fill-from-civilcraft\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/earthwork\" data-hash=\"cut-and-fill-from-civilcraft\"\u003EEarthwork and spot elevations\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-an-item\"\u003EMake an item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a name, like \u003Ccode\u003EInterior wall type A\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ECarpet tile\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the type: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear (length)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). The item appears in the list with its own color, becomes the item you draw with, and its editor opens below the list. The status bar tells you which tool to pick next.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in the form to close it without adding an item.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you pick a measuring tool first, TakeoffCraft uses the last item of the right type, or opens the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E form with that type already chosen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-the-item-you-draw-with\"\u003EPick the item you draw with\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick an item in the list. It becomes the item new marks go to, and its marks are highlighted on every sheet while the others fade. If the open sheet has none of its marks, TakeoffCraft jumps to a sheet that has. Click the item again to stop highlighting; it stays the item you draw with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicking a mark with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E tool also makes its item the current one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-up-the-item\"\u003ESet up the item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe item editor (\u003Cstrong\u003EItem: (name)\u003C/strong\u003E) has these fields. Each one is saved when you leave the field or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and each change can be undone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShown in the list, the tables and the exports.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear, Area or Count. It is locked once the item has marks (&quot;An item with marks keeps its type&quot;).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe color of its marks on screen and in the marked PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat the quantity is reported in: LF or m for linear; SF, SY or m² for area; EA for count.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlso measure\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe other kinds of marks the item takes, each with its own unit. See below.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E (linear)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA wall height. The item then also reports wall area (length × height).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E (area)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA depth or thickness, used in assembly formulas (for example concrete volume).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWaste %\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdded to the quantity before pricing: 10 means quantity × 1.10.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial $/LF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial cost per unit. The label follows the unit: $/SF, $/EA and so on.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELabor $/LF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabor cost per unit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSI number\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts CSI MasterFormat number, like \u003Ccode\u003E09 21 16\u003C/code\u003E (or just the division, \u003Ccode\u003E09\u003C/code\u003E). The workbook&#039;s By division worksheet groups costs by it. Items from \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/trade-packs\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/trade-packs\"\u003Etrade packs\u003C/a\u003E have one already.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHeight and depth take feet and inches (\u003Ccode\u003E9&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E8&#039; 6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E) or decimal feet (\u003Ccode\u003E9\u003C/code\u003E) for imperial units, and meters (\u003Ccode\u003E2.7\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2700 mm\u003C/code\u003E) for metric units. Rates take amounts like \u003Ccode\u003E12.50\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E$1,250\u003C/code\u003E, up to four decimals. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/entry-formats\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/entry-formats\"\u003ELength, rate and percent entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelow the fields, \u003Cstrong\u003EMeasured\u003C/strong\u003E shows the totals the item&#039;s formulas can use, with their letters: Length (L), Runs (N), Wall area (A) for linear; Area (A), Perimeter (P), Areas (N) for area; Count (N) for count.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"one-item-several-kinds-of-marks\"\u003EOne item, several kinds of marks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome things are measured more than one way: a room has a floor (an area), a base along its walls (a length) and doors (a count). Instead of three items, make one:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake the item with its main type, such as an area item \u003Ccode\u003ERoom 101\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlso measure\u003C/span\u003E in its editor, tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E or both. Each one gets a unit next to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw with any tool whose kind the item takes: with \u003Ccode\u003ERoom 101\u003C/code\u003E current, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E draws its floor, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E its base and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E its doors. TakeoffCraft does not ask for another item.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe items list shows every quantity (\u003Ccode\u003E50.00 SF + 2 EA\u003C/code\u003E). In the quantities table the item&#039;s row has its main quantity, and each other kind gets its own row below it, such as \u003Cstrong\u003ERoom 101 (count)\u003C/strong\u003E, with its own waste and material and labor rates. The exports, the per-sheet table and the marked PDF&#039;s legend list them the same way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe main type&#039;s fields stay the main type&#039;s own: the height or depth, the pitch, back-outs and the 3D shape apply to its marks only. A base run drawn for an area item is measured plainly along the plan, and shows flat in 3D. Assembly formulas use the main type&#039;s totals.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA kind can be unticked only while the item has no marks of it; delete them, or move them to another item under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E, first. Marks can move to any item that takes their kind.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFurther down is the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E: components such as studs or board, each with its own formula and rates. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the bottom: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove up\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove down\u003C/span\u003E change the item&#039;s place in the list and in the exports, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete item\u003C/span\u003E removes it with all its marks (after asking; you can undo it).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EArea items also have \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E (roof planes measured on the slope: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/roofs-and-slopes\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/roofs-and-slopes\"\u003ERoofs and slopes\u003C/a\u003E) and \u003Cstrong\u003EBack-out\u003C/strong\u003E (their marks come off other items&#039; areas: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-areas#back-outs-columns-shafts-and-openings-taken-out-of-several-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-areas\" data-hash=\"back-outs-columns-shafts-and-openings-taken-out-of-several-items\"\u003EMeasure areas\u003C/a\u003E). Linear items have \u003Cstrong\u003ESlope\u003C/strong\u003E (Level, Rake, Hip or valley) and, for rakes and hips, \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rates-in-the-table\"\u003ERates in the table\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also type \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWaste %\u003C/span\u003E, material and labor rates straight into the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table at the bottom (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy item\u003C/span\u003E). It is the same setting as in the editor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-pricing-works\"\u003EHow pricing works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor each item and each assembly component:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWith waste\u003C/strong\u003E = quantity × (1 + waste %).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial\u003C/strong\u003E = with waste × material per unit, rounded to the cent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELabor\u003C/strong\u003E = with waste × labor per unit, rounded to the cent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETotal\u003C/strong\u003E = material + labor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJob total\u003C/span\u003E adds every item and every component. An item can carry its own rates and an assembly at the same time; both are counted, so leave the item&#039;s rates at 0 when its components carry the cost.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"labor-by-crew\"\u003ELabor by crew\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInstead of a labor rate per unit, an item&#039;s labor can come from a crew: how much it puts in per hour, how many people, and what each earns. Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELabor by crew\u003C/span\u003E in the item editor, tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrice labor from a crew\u003C/span\u003E and fill in:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExample\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProduction\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E20\u003C/code\u003E LF an hour (in the item&#039;s unit) for the whole crew\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECrew size\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E people\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWage $/hour each\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E45\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft works out, from the quantity with waste:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Ecrew-hours\u003C/strong\u003E = quantity ÷ production (110 LF ÷ 20 = 5.5),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eworker-hours\u003C/strong\u003E = crew-hours × crew size (11),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Ecrew-days\u003C/strong\u003E = crew-hours ÷ 8 (0.69),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Elabor\u003C/strong\u003E = worker-hours × wage ($495.00).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe editor shows the result and the rate it comes to ($4.50/LF). The item&#039;s own labor rate is then read-only, in the editor and in the table. The header of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table adds the job&#039;s worker-hours and crew-days. The exports note each crew&#039;s hours, and in the workbook the labor cell is a formula on the crew (\u003Ccode\u003EROUND(F2/20*2*45,2)\u003C/code\u003E). Untick the box to go back to a rate per unit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"metric-items\"\u003EMetric items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick m (linear) or m² (area) as the unit. Quantities, heights and formula values are then in meters. The sheet scale can be any preset or calibration; units and scales are independent.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-linear\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-linear\"\u003EMeasure linear runs\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-areas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-areas\"\u003EMeasure areas with cut-outs\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-by-hand\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-by-hand\"\u003ECount by hand\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"An item is one thing you measure and price: a wall type, a floor finish, a ceiling, a door, a light fixture. Every mark you draw belongs to an item. Other programs call these conditions. Each item has a type (and can take other kinds of marks too, see One item, several kinds of marks): Type You draw It reports Units Linear Runs of connected points Length, number of runs, and wall area when it has a height LF or m Area Closed outlines, with cut-outs Area, perimeter, number of areas SF, SY or m² Count One click per thing How many EA Typed quantity Nothing: you type the quantity The number you typed (or one brought in from CivilCraft) LS, EA, CY, m³, LF, m, SF, SY or m² Typed quantities (no drawing) Some things in an estimate are never drawn: mobilization, permits, a contingency, a lump-sum subcontract, the cut and fill CivilCraft worked out. Make them a Typed quantity: Choose New item, type a name, pick Typed quantity (no drawing) and choose Add. In the item editor pick the Unit (LS for a lump sum) and type the Quantity: 1, 2.5 or 1,250. Set waste and rates as for any other item. An assembly's formulas use Q, the quantity, for example ceil(Q / 12) truck loads. A typed quantity takes no marks and never makes the estimate incomplete. When the takeoff has bid areas, the editor also has Bid area: the quantity then counts in that area, its alternates and its totals, just as a mark there would. Quantities brought in from another app say where they came from, see Earthwork and spot elevations. Make an item In the Items panel on the right, choose New item. Type a name, like Interior wall type A or Carpet tile. Pick the type: Linear (length), Area or Count. Choose Add (or press Enter). The item appears in the list with its own color, becomes the item you draw with, and its editor opens below the list. The status bar tells you which tool to pick next. Press Esc in the form to close it without adding an item. If you pick a measuring tool first, TakeoffCraft uses the last item of the right type, or opens the New item form with that type already chosen. Pick the item you draw with Click an item in the list. It becomes the item new marks go to, and its marks are highlighted on every sheet while the others fade. If the open sheet has none of its marks, TakeoffCraft jumps to a sheet that has. Click the item again to stop highlighting; it stays the item you draw with. Clicking a mark with the Select tool also makes its item the current one. Set up the item The item editor (Item: (name)) has these fields. Each one is saved when you leave the field or press Enter, and each change can be undone. Field What it does Name Shown in the list, the tables and the exports. Type Linear, Area or Count. It is locked once the item has marks (\"An item with marks keeps its type\"). Color The color of its marks on screen and in the marked PDF. Unit What the quantity is reported in: LF or m for linear; SF, SY or m² for area; EA for count. Also measure The other kinds of marks the item takes, each with its own unit. See below. Height (linear) A wall height. The item then also reports wall area (length × height). Depth (area) A depth or thickness, used in assembly formulas (for example concrete volume). Waste % Added to the quantity before pricing: 10 means quantity × 1.10. Material $/LF Material cost per unit. The label follows the unit: $/SF, $/EA and so on. Labor $/LF Labor cost per unit. CSI number Its CSI MasterFormat number, like 09 21 16 (or just the division, 09). The workbook's By division worksheet groups costs by it. Items from trade packs have one already. Height and depth take feet and inches (9'-0\", 8' 6\") or decimal feet (9) for imperial units, and meters (2.7, 2700 mm) for metric units. Rates take amounts like 12.50 or $1,250, up to four decimals. See Length, rate and percent entry. Below the fields, Measured shows the totals the item's formulas can use, with their letters: Length (L), Runs (N), Wall area (A) for linear; Area (A), Perimeter (P), Areas (N) for area; Count (N) for count. One item, several kinds of marks Some things are measured more than one way: a room has a floor (an area), a base along its walls (a length) and doors (a count). Instead of three items, make one: Make the item with its main type, such as an area item Room 101. Under Also measure in its editor, tick Linear, Count or both. Each one gets a unit next to it. Draw with any tool whose kind the item takes: with Room 101 current, Area draws its floor, Linear its base and Count its doors. TakeoffCraft does not ask for another item. The items list shows every quantity (50.00 SF + 2 EA). In the quantities table the item's row has its main quantity, and each other kind gets its own row below it, such as Room 101 (count), with its own waste and material and labor rates. The exports, the per-sheet table and the marked PDF's legend list them the same way. The main type's fields stay the main type's own: the height or depth, the pitch, back-outs and the 3D shape apply to its marks only. A base run drawn for an area item is measured plainly along the plan, and shows flat in 3D. Assembly formulas use the main type's totals. A kind can be unticked only while the item has no marks of it; delete them, or move them to another item under Selected mark, first. Marks can move to any item that takes their kind. Further down is the Assembly: components such as studs or board, each with its own formula and rates. See Assemblies and formulas. At the bottom: Move up and Move down change the item's place in the list and in the exports, and Delete item removes it with all its marks (after asking; you can undo it). Area items also have Pitch (roof planes measured on the slope: Roofs and slopes) and Back-out (their marks come off other items' areas: Measure areas). Linear items have Slope (Level, Rake, Hip or valley) and, for rakes and hips, Pitch. Rates in the table You can also type Waste %, material and labor rates straight into the Quantities table at the bottom (By item). It is the same setting as in the editor. How pricing works For each item and each assembly component: With waste = quantity × (1 + waste %). Material = with waste × material per unit, rounded to the cent. Labor = with waste × labor per unit, rounded to the cent. Total = material + labor. The Job total adds every item and every component. An item can carry its own rates and an assembly at the same time; both are counted, so leave the item's rates at 0 when its components carry the cost. Labor by crew Instead of a labor rate per unit, an item's labor can come from a crew: how much it puts in per hour, how many people, and what each earns. Under Labor by crew in the item editor, tick Price labor from a crew and fill in: Field Example Production 20 LF an hour (in the item's unit) for the whole crew Crew size 2 people Wage $/hour each 45 TakeoffCraft works out, from the quantity with waste: crew-hours = quantity ÷ production (110 LF ÷ 20 = 5.5), worker-hours = crew-hours × crew size (11), crew-days = crew-hours ÷ 8 (0.69), labor = worker-hours × wage ($495.00). The editor shows the result and the rate it comes to ($4.50/LF). The item's own labor rate is then read-only, in the editor and in the table. The header of the Quantities table adds the job's worker-hours and crew-days. The exports note each crew's hours, and in the workbook the labor cell is a formula on the crew (ROUND(F2/20*2*45,2)). Untick the box to go back to a rate per unit. Metric items Pick m (linear) or m² (area) as the unit. Quantities, heights and formula values are then in meters. The sheet scale can be any preset or calibration; units and scales are independent. Next Measure linear runs Measure areas with cut-outs Count by hand","lang":"en"},{"slug":"measure-from-a-drawing","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Measure from a drawing","summary":"Take lengths and areas from the drawing's own lines, arcs, polylines and hatches by picking them, with exact arcs and holes, instead of tracing by clicks.","section":"tasks","order":33,"keywords":["measure drawing","geometry","polyline","arc","circle","hatch","bulge","exact length","exact area","pick","window","trace","from cad","linear","area"],"headings":[{"id":"take-objects-as-a-linear-or-area-item","text":"Take objects as a linear or area item","level":2},{"id":"what-each-kind-of-item-takes","text":"What each kind of item takes","level":2},{"id":"exact-and-nearly-exact","text":"Exact, and nearly exact","level":2},{"id":"what-it-takes-from-where","text":"What it takes from where","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure drawing\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar of a DWG or DXF sheet takes the drawing&#039;s own objects as marks, so you do not trace them by clicks. A polyline&#039;s length, an arc&#039;s length, a circle&#039;s area and a hatch&#039;s area with its holes come out exact, from the arithmetic the takeoff uses for every mark.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-objects-as-a-linear-or-area-item\"\u003ETake objects as a linear or area item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake or click a \u003Cstrong\u003Elinear\u003C/strong\u003E or an \u003Cstrong\u003Earea\u003C/strong\u003E item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E. The sheet needs a scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure drawing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the objects on the sheet to pick them, or drag a window round them: everything entirely inside it is picked. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E adds a window to what is picked; click a picked object again to drop it. The bar shows the total: &quot;6 runs: 108&#039;-9 1/8&quot; in all.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the add button, which names the number and the item, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd 6 to Edge\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Each object becomes one mark of the item, in one undo step. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E clears what you picked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-each-kind-of-item-takes\"\u003EWhat each kind of item takes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Elinear\u003C/strong\u003E item takes lines, arcs, polylines, circles and splines by length. A closed shape counts by its perimeter. A hatch is left out: it has an area, not a length, and the bar says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003Earea\u003C/strong\u003E item takes closed shapes: closed polylines, circles, 2D solids, and hatches with their holes. Open runs are left out, and the bar says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"exact-and-nearly-exact\"\u003EExact, and nearly exact\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines, arcs (any bulge), polylines with bulges and circles are exact: lengths and areas use the arc&#039;s radius and sweep, not a picture of it. Ellipses, splines and hatches whose boundary is made of edges are measured in chords of one degree or finer, which is within a hundredth of a percent for ellipses and a tenth for the rest; the bar says how many of what you picked are curves of this kind.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-takes-from-where\"\u003EWhat it takes from where\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModel\u003C/strong\u003E: objects in model space. \u003Cstrong\u003EA layout\u003C/strong\u003E: its own lines, and the model objects inside a viewport, scaled through it. An object that crosses a viewport&#039;s edge is not offered (it plots clipped); an object two viewports both show is taken once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly layers the sheet shows. Blocks are counted, not measured: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\"\u003ECount from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E. 3D polylines, meshes, solids and regions are not offered.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Measure drawing on the sheet bar of a DWG or DXF sheet takes the drawing's own objects as marks, so you do not trace them by clicks. A polyline's length, an arc's length, a circle's area and a hatch's area with its holes come out exact, from the arithmetic the takeoff uses for every mark. Take objects as a linear or area item Make or click a linear or an area item under Items. The sheet needs a scale. Choose Measure drawing. Click the objects on the sheet to pick them, or drag a window round them: everything entirely inside it is picked. Shift adds a window to what is picked; click a picked object again to drop it. The bar shows the total: \"6 runs: 108'-9 1/8\" in all.\" Choose the add button, which names the number and the item, such as Add 6 to Edge (or press Enter). Each object becomes one mark of the item, in one undo step. Esc clears what you picked. What each kind of item takes A linear item takes lines, arcs, polylines, circles and splines by length. A closed shape counts by its perimeter. A hatch is left out: it has an area, not a length, and the bar says so. An area item takes closed shapes: closed polylines, circles, 2D solids, and hatches with their holes. Open runs are left out, and the bar says so. Exact, and nearly exact Lines, arcs (any bulge), polylines with bulges and circles are exact: lengths and areas use the arc's radius and sweep, not a picture of it. Ellipses, splines and hatches whose boundary is made of edges are measured in chords of one degree or finer, which is within a hundredth of a percent for ellipses and a tenth for the rest; the bar says how many of what you picked are curves of this kind. What it takes from where Model: objects in model space. A layout: its own lines, and the model objects inside a viewport, scaled through it. An object that crosses a viewport's edge is not offered (it plots clipped); an object two viewports both show is taken once. Only layers the sheet shows. Blocks are counted, not measured: see Count from a drawing. 3D polylines, meshes, solids and regions are not offered.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"snap-to-the-drawing","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Snap to the drawing's lines","summary":"Points jump onto the PDF's own linework while you measure or calibrate: ends, midpoints, crossings or anywhere on a line, with a marker that says which.","section":"tasks","order":35,"keywords":["snap","osnap","object snap","endpoint","midpoint","intersection","nearest","vector","linework","lines","precise","exact","f3","magnet","corner"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-snaps-to","text":"What it snaps to","level":2},{"id":"where-it-works","text":"Where it works","level":2},{"id":"sheets-it-cannot-snap-on","text":"Sheets it cannot snap on","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMost plan sets are vector PDFs: their walls and edges are real lines, not pixels. While you measure, TakeoffCraft snaps your clicks onto those lines, so a run starts exactly at a wall&#039;s corner and a calibration reads the dimension the drafter drew, not the pixel you hit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESnap is on when you start. Turn it off and on with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; the setting is kept in this browser (it is also under \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-snaps-to\"\u003EWhat it snaps to\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the pointer comes within about 10 screen pixels of a line, a marker shows where the click will land, with its name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMarker\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EName\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhere\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESquare\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnd\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe end of a line, or a corner where lines meet. A curve&#039;s two ends count; the points along it do not.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriangle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMidpoint\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of a straight line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECross\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIntersection\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere two lines cross, or where one ends on another.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHourglass\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn the line\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe nearest point on a line, when no end, midpoint or crossing is close.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe nearest end, midpoint or crossing wins. Only when none is close does the point go onto the line itself. Zoom in to choose between points that sit close together: the 10 pixels are screen pixels, so at a higher zoom they cover less of the sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-it-works\"\u003EWhere it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESnap works in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E, and when you drag a selected mark&#039;s corner. It also places arc points. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E and pasting are not snapped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA snapped point wins over the 45° snap of \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: to draw at 45° away from the lines, turn Snap off with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECalibrate with Snap on. Click near the two ends of a dimension line or a wall you know: both points land exactly on the drawing, and the scale comes out exact (the status bar then names the preset it matches).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets-it-cannot-snap-on\"\u003ESheets it cannot snap on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScanned sheets\u003C/strong\u003E are pictures with no lines to snap to. The status bar says &quot;This sheet has no lines to snap to (a scanned page is a picture).&quot; and points go where you click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVery large drawings\u003C/strong\u003E: snapping reads up to 400,000 line segments per sheet. On a sheet with more, it says so and snaps to the first ones only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText\u003C/strong\u003E is not snapped to, unless the PDF draws its letters as lines (some plotters do).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sheet&#039;s lines are read the first time you open it, in the background; a big sheet can take a second before the markers appear.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Most plan sets are vector PDFs: their walls and edges are real lines, not pixels. While you measure, TakeoffCraft snaps your clicks onto those lines, so a run starts exactly at a wall's corner and a calibration reads the dimension the drafter drew, not the pixel you hit. Snap is on when you start. Turn it off and on with Snap in the status bar or F3; the setting is kept in this browser (it is also under Settings). What it snaps to When the pointer comes within about 10 screen pixels of a line, a marker shows where the click will land, with its name: Marker Name Where Square End The end of a line, or a corner where lines meet. A curve's two ends count; the points along it do not. Triangle Midpoint The middle of a straight line. Cross Intersection Where two lines cross, or where one ends on another. Hourglass On the line The nearest point on a line, when no end, midpoint or crossing is close. The nearest end, midpoint or crossing wins. Only when none is close does the point go onto the line itself. Zoom in to choose between points that sit close together: the 10 pixels are screen pixels, so at a higher zoom they cover less of the sheet. Where it works Snap works in Linear, Area, Circle, Cut-out, Calibrate and Check, and when you drag a selected mark's corner. It also places arc points. Count, Auto count and pasting are not snapped. A snapped point wins over the 45° snap of Shift: to draw at 45° away from the lines, turn Snap off with F3 first. Calibrate with Snap on. Click near the two ends of a dimension line or a wall you know: both points land exactly on the drawing, and the scale comes out exact (the status bar then names the preset it matches). Sheets it cannot snap on Scanned sheets are pictures with no lines to snap to. The status bar says \"This sheet has no lines to snap to (a scanned page is a picture).\" and points go where you click. Very large drawings: snapping reads up to 400,000 line segments per sheet. On a sheet with more, it says so and snaps to the first ones only. Text is not snapped to, unless the PDF draws its letters as lines (some plotters do). The sheet's lines are read the first time you open it, in the background; a big sheet can take a second before the markers appear.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"pick-the-lines","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Pick the PDF's lines","summary":"Click a line of a vector PDF to measure its whole connected path, or click inside a closed outline for its area, with the gap tolerance shown and one undo per segment.","section":"tasks","order":36,"keywords":["path","pick","linework","lines","connected","whole run","trace","outline","perimeter","gap","tolerance","bridge","segments","tab","vector","area from lines","p"],"headings":[{"id":"pick-a-path-or-an-outline","text":"Pick a path or an outline","level":2},{"id":"the-gap-tolerance","text":"The gap tolerance","level":2},{"id":"keys","text":"Keys","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOn a vector PDF the walls and edges are real lines. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPath\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) takes them as they are drawn: one click on a line measures everything joined to it, end to end, so you do not trace a wall by clicking its corners. The sheet needs a scale. A scanned page has no lines to pick; use \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-linear\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-linear\"\u003ELinear\u003C/a\u003E or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-areas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-areas\"\u003EArea\u003C/a\u003E there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-a-path-or-an-outline\"\u003EPick a path or an outline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPath\u003C/span\u003E. The line under the pointer is outlined, with the other paths it could be drawn thinly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a line: the whole connected path is picked. The bar under the toolbar gives its segments, length, the gap tolerance and how many gaps were bridged. A path that comes back to its start is closed and also reports its perimeter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr click inside a closed outline, away from any line: the outline around that point is picked as an area. The smallest one comes first; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E tries the next larger one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or right-click, or double-click) to add the marks. A picked path becomes a mark of a \u003Cstrong\u003Elinear\u003C/strong\u003E item, an outline a mark of an \u003Cstrong\u003Earea\u003C/strong\u003E item: the active item if it has that kind, else the newest one of that kind. With none, the new-item form opens and nothing is added until you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. Everything picked is added in one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-gap-tolerance\"\u003EThe gap tolerance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrafters leave small holes where lines should meet. Two lines join when their ends are closer than the \u003Cstrong\u003Egap tolerance\u003C/strong\u003E. It starts at 1.5 points on the sheet (shown in feet and inches at the sheet&#039;s scale), and a dashed ring that wide follows the pointer and sits on each end of the path under it. Each hole that was bridged is ringed in orange on a picked path, and the bar counts them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"]\"\u003E]\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E]\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E makes the tolerance wider, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"[\"\u003E[\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E[\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E smaller. A picked path is joined again at once at the new tolerance. At a junction the path keeps going straight on, the way that bends least, and leaves the branch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys\"\u003EKeys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick the path under the pointer, or the outline around it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd another path or outline to what is picked.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETry the next path under the pointer, or the next larger outline.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETake the last segment off the last picked path, one segment at a time. A path with no segments left goes. With nothing picked, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is the ordinary undo.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"[\"\u003E[\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E[\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"]\"\u003E]\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E]\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENarrower and wider gap tolerance.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd the marks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClear what is picked.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA curve in the drawing is a chain of short straight pieces, so its length is a hair under the true arc. For an exact arc, measure a drawing&#039;s own file (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-from-a-drawing\"\u003EMeasure from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA bridged gap counts as a straight hop across it, so a closed outline&#039;s perimeter includes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly strokes and fills in the page&#039;s own paths are lines. Text drawn as letters, and pictures, are not.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"On a vector PDF the walls and edges are real lines. Path (P) takes them as they are drawn: one click on a line measures everything joined to it, end to end, so you do not trace a wall by clicking its corners. The sheet needs a scale. A scanned page has no lines to pick; use Linear or Area there. Pick a path or an outline Choose Path. The line under the pointer is outlined, with the other paths it could be drawn thinly. Click a line: the whole connected path is picked. The bar under the toolbar gives its segments, length, the gap tolerance and how many gaps were bridged. A path that comes back to its start is closed and also reports its perimeter. Or click inside a closed outline, away from any line: the outline around that point is picked as an area. The smallest one comes first; Tab tries the next larger one. Press Enter (or right-click, or double-click) to add the marks. A picked path becomes a mark of a linear item, an outline a mark of an area item: the active item if it has that kind, else the newest one of that kind. With none, the new-item form opens and nothing is added until you press Enter again. Everything picked is added in one undo step. The gap tolerance Drafters leave small holes where lines should meet. Two lines join when their ends are closer than the gap tolerance. It starts at 1.5 points on the sheet (shown in feet and inches at the sheet's scale), and a dashed ring that wide follows the pointer and sits on each end of the path under it. Each hole that was bridged is ringed in orange on a picked path, and the bar counts them. ] makes the tolerance wider, [ smaller. A picked path is joined again at once at the new tolerance. At a junction the path keeps going straight on, the way that bends least, and leaves the branch. Keys Key or action What it does Click Pick the path under the pointer, or the outline around it. Shift + click Add another path or outline to what is picked. Tab Try the next path under the pointer, or the next larger outline. Backspace or Ctrl+Z Take the last segment off the last picked path, one segment at a time. A path with no segments left goes. With nothing picked, Ctrl+Z is the ordinary undo. [ and ] Narrower and wider gap tolerance. Enter Add the marks. Esc Clear what is picked. Good to know A curve in the drawing is a chain of short straight pieces, so its length is a hair under the true arc. For an exact arc, measure a drawing's own file (Measure from a drawing). A bridged gap counts as a straight hop across it, so a closed outline's perimeter includes it. Only strokes and fills in the page's own paths are lines. Text drawn as letters, and pictures, are not.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"measure-linear","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Measure linear runs","summary":"Take off walls, piping, trim and other lengths as runs, with straight segments, arcs and the Shift 45-degree snap.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["linear","length","run","polyline","walls","lf","linear feet","arc","curve","45 degrees","ortho","snap","segments","polylength","deduct","opening","door","gap","type","typed","coordinates","distance","direct distance","polar"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-a-run","text":"Draw a run","level":2},{"id":"straight-lines-at-45-steps","text":"Straight lines at 45° steps","level":2},{"id":"arcs-and-curved-walls","text":"Arcs and curved walls","level":2},{"id":"type-lengths-and-coordinates","text":"Type lengths and coordinates","level":2},{"id":"deduct-a-part-of-a-run","text":"Deduct a part of a run","level":2},{"id":"look-at-a-run-s-details","text":"Look at a run's details","level":2},{"id":"fix-a-run","text":"Fix a run","level":2},{"id":"wall-area","text":"Wall area","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA linear item measures length. Each run is a chain of points: straight segments, and arcs where you turn arc mode on. An item can have any number of runs on any number of sheets.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore you start: the sheet needs a scale (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E) and you need a linear item (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a vector PDF, your clicks snap onto the drawing&#039;s own lines: ends, midpoints and crossings (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\"\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s lines\u003C/a\u003E; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E turns it off).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-run\"\u003EDraw a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the linear item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the start of the run.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each corner along it. The status bar shows &quot;Length&quot; (the run so far, to the pointer) and &quot;segment&quot; (the segment you are drawing), in feet and inches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFinish the run: double-click the last point, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or right-click. The run is drawn in the item&#039;s color and becomes the selected mark. The item&#039;s length in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E list and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table goes up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tool stays on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, so you can start the next run straight away. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile drawing:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) removes the last point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E throws the unfinished run away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDragging pans the sheet and the wheel zooms, without adding points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac trackpad, right-click is a two-finger click (turn on secondary click in System Settings, under Trackpad). TakeoffCraft reads only the secondary button, so a Control-click may be taken as a plain click and add a point. Double-click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always finish.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen, tap to place points, drag one finger to pan and pinch with two to zoom. Finish with a long press, a double tap, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinish\u003C/span\u003E in the bar under the sheet (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E takes the last point back). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/troubleshooting#touch-screens\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"touch-screens\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"straight-lines-at-45-steps\"\u003EStraight lines at 45° steps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you place a point. The segment from the last point snaps to the nearest 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° direction, keeping the length you dragged along it. The status bar reminds you: &quot;Shift snaps to 45°&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn arc mode it applies to where an arc ends: the arc&#039;s chord goes at 45° steps from where it starts. The point you click on the curve is never snapped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arcs-and-curved-walls\"\u003EArcs and curved walls\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile drawing a run, place the point where the curve starts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on arc mode: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The button stays pressed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick any point on the curve, somewhere along the middle of it. The status bar adds &quot;(arc: click where it ends)&quot; and the preview bends through your point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the curve ends. The arc is added. Its length is the exact arc length, not a string of short chords.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick another point on the next curve and its end to keep drawing arcs, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to go back to straight segments.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first drop the pending point on the curve; press again to remove points as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-lengths-and-coordinates\"\u003EType lengths and coordinates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter the first point, you can type where the next one goes instead of clicking. Type a digit, \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E: a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E box opens in the status bar with what you typed. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to place the point; the box stays open for the next one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThe next point goes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12&#039;-6&quot; from the last point, toward the pointer (point the way, then type the length)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;&lt;45\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12&#039;-6&quot; at 45°, counted counter-clockwise from the screen&#039;s right (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;90\u003C/code\u003E is up, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;-90\u003C/code\u003E down)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@6&#039;,-4&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6&#039; right and 4&#039; down from the last point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6&#039;,4&#039;\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E#6&#039;,4&#039;\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6&#039; right and 4&#039; up from the first point of this run\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELengths are typed as everywhere in TakeoffCraft (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/entry-formats\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/entry-formats\"\u003EEntry formats\u003C/a\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12.5\u003C/code\u003E (feet), \u003Ccode\u003E30&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3.2 m\u003C/code\u003E. &quot;Right&quot; and &quot;up&quot; are as you see the sheet, also when the PDF page is turned.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on an empty box finishes the run, like \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on the sheet; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the box and keeps the run going. Typed points go through the same rules as clicks: deducts, arcs and closing an area all work. The same box works in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E; after a circle&#039;s center it takes the radius.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"deduct-a-part-of-a-run\"\u003EDeduct a part of a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA wall run that passes a door or a storefront can be drawn straight through and still count only the wall: the segments across the opening are \u003Cstrong\u003Ededucts\u003C/strong\u003E. They are drawn dashed and thin, and not counted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile drawing:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlace the point where the opening starts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The button stays pressed and the status bar says the next segments are deducts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the opening ends. The segment is drawn dashed; &quot;Length&quot; in the status bar leaves it out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again and carry on along the wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter drawing: select the run. Under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E, each segment has a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E box: tick it to deduct that segment, untick it to count it again. Each change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA run always keeps at least one segment that counts. The panel then shows \u003Cstrong\u003EDeducts\u003C/strong\u003E (the deducted length) and \u003Cstrong\u003EDrawn\u003C/strong\u003E (the whole run); \u003Cstrong\u003ELength\u003C/strong\u003E, the item&#039;s quantity, the wall area and the costs all use the length without the deducts. The 3D check view leaves a gap in the wall, the marked PDF draws the deducts dashed, and the exported \u003Cstrong\u003EMarks\u003C/strong\u003E sheet lists them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMoving a corner keeps the deducts. Adding or removing points (by drawing the run again) starts without them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-at-a-run-s-details\"\u003ELook at a run&#039;s details\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click the run. \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E on the right shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe item it belongs to (pick another linear item in the list to move the run there),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe sheet,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELength\u003C/strong\u003E in feet and inches and in LF,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe number of \u003Cstrong\u003ESegments\u003C/strong\u003E, and the \u003Cstrong\u003EWall area\u003C/strong\u003E when the item has a height,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eeach segment&#039;s length, with &quot;(arc)&quot; after arcs, and its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the sheet, each segment of the selected run is labeled with its length.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fix-a-run\"\u003EFix a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove a point\u003C/strong\u003E: select the run, then drag one of its square corner handles. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to snap the moved segment to 45° steps. An arc keeps its bulge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBend an arc\u003C/strong\u003E: a selected run shows a round handle in the middle of each arc. Drag it: the arc passes through where you let go, so it bows more, less, or the other way. Dragged onto the straight line between its ends, it becomes a straight segment. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it (&quot;Bend arc&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete a run\u003C/strong\u003E: select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete mark\u003C/span\u003E in the panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; redo with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou cannot insert a point in the middle of a run yet. Delete the run and draw it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"wall-area\"\u003EWall area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGive the linear item a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E (for example \u003Ccode\u003E9&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E). It then reports \u003Cstrong\u003EWall area (A)\u003C/strong\u003E = length × height, in SF (or m² for a metric item), and the wall area goes into the notes column of the spreadsheet export. Use it in assemblies for board, paint or insulation: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A linear item measures length. Each run is a chain of points: straight segments, and arcs where you turn arc mode on. An item can have any number of runs on any number of sheets. Before you start: the sheet needs a scale (Set the scale) and you need a linear item (Create takeoff items). On a vector PDF, your clicks snap onto the drawing's own lines: ends, midpoints and crossings (Snap to the drawing's lines; F3 turns it off). Draw a run Click the linear item under Items. Choose Linear in the toolbar, or press L. Click the start of the run. Click each corner along it. The status bar shows \"Length\" (the run so far, to the pointer) and \"segment\" (the segment you are drawing), in feet and inches. Finish the run: double-click the last point, press Enter, or right-click. The run is drawn in the item's color and becomes the selected mark. The item's length in the Items list and the Quantities table goes up. The tool stays on Linear, so you can start the next run straight away. Press Esc to go back to Select. While drawing: Backspace (or Delete) removes the last point. Esc throws the unfinished run away. Dragging pans the sheet and the wheel zooms, without adding points. On a Mac trackpad, right-click is a two-finger click (turn on secondary click in System Settings, under Trackpad). TakeoffCraft reads only the secondary button, so a Control-click may be taken as a plain click and add a point. Double-click or Enter always finish. On a touch screen, tap to place points, drag one finger to pan and pinch with two to zoom. Finish with a long press, a double tap, or Finish in the bar under the sheet (Back takes the last point back). See Troubleshooting. Straight lines at 45° steps Hold Shift while you place a point. The segment from the last point snaps to the nearest 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° direction, keeping the length you dragged along it. The status bar reminds you: \"Shift snaps to 45°\". In arc mode it applies to where an arc ends: the arc's chord goes at 45° steps from where it starts. The point you click on the curve is never snapped. Arcs and curved walls While drawing a run, place the point where the curve starts. Turn on arc mode: choose Arc in the toolbar or press R. The button stays pressed. Click any point on the curve, somewhere along the middle of it. The status bar adds \"(arc: click where it ends)\" and the preview bends through your point. Click where the curve ends. The arc is added. Its length is the exact arc length, not a string of short chords. Click another point on the next curve and its end to keep drawing arcs, or press R again to go back to straight segments. Backspace or Esc first drop the pending point on the curve; press again to remove points as usual. Type lengths and coordinates After the first point, you can type where the next one goes instead of clicking. Type a digit, @ or #: a Type box opens in the status bar with what you typed. Press Enter to place the point; the box stays open for the next one. Type The next point goes 12'6\" 12'-6\" from the last point, toward the pointer (point the way, then type the length) 12'6\"\u003C45 12'-6\" at 45°, counted counter-clockwise from the screen's right (\u003C90 is up, \u003C-90 down) @6',-4' 6' right and 4' down from the last point 6',4' (or #6',4') 6' right and 4' up from the first point of this run Lengths are typed as everywhere in TakeoffCraft (Entry formats): 12'-6 1/2\", 12.5 (feet), 30\", 3.2 m. \"Right\" and \"up\" are as you see the sheet, also when the PDF page is turned. Enter on an empty box finishes the run, like Enter on the sheet; Esc closes the box and keeps the run going. Typed points go through the same rules as clicks: deducts, arcs and closing an area all work. The same box works in Area and Cut-out; after a circle's center it takes the radius. Deduct a part of a run A wall run that passes a door or a storefront can be drawn straight through and still count only the wall: the segments across the opening are deducts. They are drawn dashed and thin, and not counted. While drawing: Place the point where the opening starts. Choose Deduct in the toolbar or press E. The button stays pressed and the status bar says the next segments are deducts. Click where the opening ends. The segment is drawn dashed; \"Length\" in the status bar leaves it out. Press E again and carry on along the wall. After drawing: select the run. Under Selected mark, each segment has a Deduct box: tick it to deduct that segment, untick it to count it again. Each change is one undo step. A run always keeps at least one segment that counts. The panel then shows Deducts (the deducted length) and Drawn (the whole run); Length, the item's quantity, the wall area and the costs all use the length without the deducts. The 3D check view leaves a gap in the wall, the marked PDF draws the deducts dashed, and the exported Marks sheet lists them. Moving a corner keeps the deducts. Adding or removing points (by drawing the run again) starts without them. Look at a run's details Choose Select (V) and click the run. Selected mark on the right shows: the item it belongs to (pick another linear item in the list to move the run there), the sheet, Length in feet and inches and in LF, the number of Segments, and the Wall area when the item has a height, each segment's length, with \"(arc)\" after arcs, and its Deduct box. On the sheet, each segment of the selected run is labeled with its length. Fix a run Move a point: select the run, then drag one of its square corner handles. Hold Shift to snap the moved segment to 45° steps. An arc keeps its bulge. Bend an arc: a selected run shows a round handle in the middle of each arc. Drag it: the arc passes through where you let go, so it bows more, less, or the other way. Dragged onto the straight line between its ends, it becomes a straight segment. Ctrl+Z undoes it (\"Bend arc\"). Delete a run: select it and press Delete, choose Delete in the toolbar's More menu, or Delete mark in the panel. Undo: Ctrl+Z; redo with Ctrl+Y. You cannot insert a point in the middle of a run yet. Delete the run and draw it again. Wall area Give the linear item a Height (for example 9'-0\"). It then reports Wall area (A) = length × height, in SF (or m² for a metric item), and the wall area goes into the notes column of the spreadsheet export. Use it in assemblies for board, paint or insulation: see Assemblies and formulas.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"measure-areas","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Measure areas with cut-outs","summary":"Outline floors, ceilings and slabs, cut holes out of them, add curved edges, circles and round cut-outs, and read area and perimeter.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["room fill","flood fill","paint bucket","click inside a room","auto area","back-out","backout","column","shaft","deduct from several items","area","polygon","outline","square feet","sf","cut-out","hole","deduct","circle","round","arc","perimeter","floor","ceiling","slab"],"headings":[{"id":"outline-an-area","text":"Outline an area","level":2},{"id":"curved-edges","text":"Curved edges","level":2},{"id":"a-circle","text":"A circle","level":2},{"id":"fill-a-room-with-one-click","text":"Fill a room with one click","level":2},{"id":"cut-a-hole-out-of-an-area","text":"Cut a hole out of an area","level":2},{"id":"back-outs-columns-shafts-and-openings-taken-out-of-several-items","text":"Back-outs: columns, shafts and openings taken out of several items","level":2},{"id":"read-the-area","text":"Read the area","level":2},{"id":"fix-an-area","text":"Fix an area","level":2},{"id":"depth-for-volumes","text":"Depth for volumes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn area item measures enclosed area. Each outline can have holes cut out of it (a shaft, a column, a planter), and its edges can be straight or curved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore you start: the sheet needs a scale (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E) and you need an area item (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"outline-an-area\"\u003EOutline an area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the area item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each corner of the room or slab in turn. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for 0°, 45° and 90° edges. From the third point on, the outline fills in and the status bar shows &quot;Area&quot; and &quot;perimeter&quot;. A small ring appears round the first point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose the outline: click the first point (inside its ring), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, double-click, or right-click. The area is filled lightly in the item&#039;s color, labeled with its square feet, and becomes the selected mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes the last corner; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E throws the outline away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn outline that crosses itself is refused: &quot;The outline crosses itself, so its area would be wrong. Draw it again without crossing.&quot; Draw a figure-eight shape as two areas instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"curved-edges\"\u003ECurved edges\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile outlining, turn on arc mode (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). After a corner, click a point along the curve, then where the curve ends. To close the outline with a curve, make that last click on the first point. The area and perimeter use the exact curve. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again for straight edges. To change a curve afterwards, select the area and drag the round handle in the middle of the arc. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-linear#arcs-and-curved-walls\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-linear\" data-hash=\"arcs-and-curved-walls\"\u003EMeasure linear runs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-circle\"\u003EA circle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith an area item, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the center.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick any point on the edge. While you move, the status bar shows the radius and area. The circle is added as an area mark with an exact area and perimeter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-a-room-with-one-click\"\u003EFill a room with one click\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the rooms are drawn as closed walls, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) takes a floor off in one click:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick an area item (a floor finish, say) and give the sheet its scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick inside a room, in the open floor (not on a line or a label).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft reads the sheet&#039;s lines, finds the space around the click and outlines it along the walls&#039; inner faces, as an area of the current item. Door openings up to \u003Cstrong\u003E4&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E are treated as closed, so the outline runs straight across the doorway; \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings › TakeoffCraft › Room fill closes openings up to\u003C/strong\u003E changes that width (3&#039;-0&quot; to 8&#039;-0&quot;). Room names, furniture and fixtures inside the room do not make holes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt refuses, saying why, when:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe click is on a line (&quot;That is on a line&quot;);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe space is not closed: an opening wider than the setting lets it run out to the sheet&#039;s edge. Raise the setting, or outline that room by hand with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe space is tiny (a symbol, not a room).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a door is wider than the setting, the fill runs into the next room and outlines both together: undo (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), raise the setting, and click again. Check the outline like any area: drag its corners to fix it. A scanned sheet works the same, but its lines must be dark enough; light grey linework may not stop the fill.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-a-hole-out-of-an-area\"\u003ECut a hole out of an area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click the area. It shows square handles at its corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a cut-out\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corners of the hole, inside the area. Arc mode works here too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose it like an area: click its first point, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, double-click or right-click. The hole is left unfilled, and the status bar says &quot;Cut-out added. Draw another, or press Esc.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw more holes in the same area, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a round hole, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a round cut-out\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E, click its center, then a point on its edge.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA cut-out must sit fully inside the area and must not overlap another cut-out. Otherwise TakeoffCraft refuses it: &quot;A cut-out has to sit fully inside the area.&quot; or &quot;Cut-outs cannot overlap each other.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo take the last hole out again, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove last cut-out\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"back-outs-columns-shafts-and-openings-taken-out-of-several-items\"\u003EBack-outs: columns, shafts and openings taken out of several items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA cut-out belongs to one area mark. A \u003Cstrong\u003Eback-out\u003C/strong\u003E is drawn once and comes out of every area it overlaps, for the items you choose: draw the columns once, and they come off the floor finish, the ceiling and the slab together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake an area item for them, such as &quot;Columns&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn its item settings, tick \u003Cstrong\u003EBack-out: its marks take their area out of other items\u003C/strong\u003E. A list of your other area items appears, all ticked: untick the ones the back-outs should not touch (paint on the walls, say).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETake off the columns with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, as usual. Each one is drawn dashed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery area mark of a ticked item on the same sheet loses the part a back-out covers: the whole back-out when it sits inside, only the overlapping part when it crosses the edge, and nothing inside a cut-out. The selected area&#039;s panel shows \u003Cstrong\u003EBack-outs\u003C/strong\u003E and the area left. The item&#039;s quantity, its assembly formulas, the costs, the 3D check view and the export all use the area left; the estimate&#039;s notes say how much back-outs took out. The back-out item itself counts its own area (leave its costs at zero if you do not price it).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA back-out has to be \u003Cstrong\u003Econvex\u003C/strong\u003E: a rectangle, a circle or any outline without an inward corner. TakeoffCraft refuses to draw one that is not; draw an L-shaped shaft as two back-outs. Back-outs cannot have cut-outs, and two back-outs that overlap each other both count.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe perimeter (\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E) does not change: back-outs take out area only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-area\"\u003ERead the area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the area. \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe net area: outline minus cut-outs. This is what the item counts.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOutline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe gross area inside the outline (shown when there are cut-outs).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut-outs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many holes, and their total area.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPerimeter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe outline&#039;s edge length, \u003Cstrong\u003Eplus\u003C/strong\u003E the edges of every cut-out (&quot;with cut-outs&quot;).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe perimeter includes cut-out edges on purpose, for things like edge forms or base that run round a hole as well. If you need only the outer edge, keep holes out of that item or measure the outer edge as a linear run.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe item&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E decides how the total is reported: SF, SY or m². The \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E panel and the status bar always show square feet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fix-an-area\"\u003EFix an area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove a corner\u003C/strong\u003E: select the area and drag a corner handle (holes have handles too). If the new shape would cross itself or push a hole outside, the corner is put back: &quot;The point was put back.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove to another item\u003C/strong\u003E: pick another area item in the \u003Cstrong\u003EItem\u003C/strong\u003E list under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete mark\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"depth-for-volumes\"\u003EDepth for volumes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGive the area item a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E (for example \u003Ccode\u003E0&#039;-4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E for a slab) and use \u003Ccode\u003EA * H / 27\u003C/code\u003E in an assembly component to get cubic yards. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"An area item measures enclosed area. Each outline can have holes cut out of it (a shaft, a column, a planter), and its edges can be straight or curved. Before you start: the sheet needs a scale (Set the scale) and you need an area item (Create takeoff items). Outline an area Click the area item under Items. Choose Area in the toolbar, or press A. Click each corner of the room or slab in turn. Hold Shift for 0°, 45° and 90° edges. From the third point on, the outline fills in and the status bar shows \"Area\" and \"perimeter\". A small ring appears round the first point. Close the outline: click the first point (inside its ring), press Enter, double-click, or right-click. The area is filled lightly in the item's color, labeled with its square feet, and becomes the selected mark. Backspace removes the last corner; Esc throws the outline away. An outline that crosses itself is refused: \"The outline crosses itself, so its area would be wrong. Draw it again without crossing.\" Draw a figure-eight shape as two areas instead. Curved edges While outlining, turn on arc mode (Arc or R). After a corner, click a point along the curve, then where the curve ends. To close the outline with a curve, make that last click on the first point. The area and perimeter use the exact curve. Press R again for straight edges. To change a curve afterwards, select the area and drag the round handle in the middle of the arc. See Measure linear runs. A circle With an area item, choose Circle or press O. Click the center. Click any point on the edge. While you move, the status bar shows the radius and area. The circle is added as an area mark with an exact area and perimeter. Fill a room with one click When the rooms are drawn as closed walls, Room (B) takes a floor off in one click: Pick an area item (a floor finish, say) and give the sheet its scale. Choose Room in the Takeoff group, or press B. Click inside a room, in the open floor (not on a line or a label). TakeoffCraft reads the sheet's lines, finds the space around the click and outlines it along the walls' inner faces, as an area of the current item. Door openings up to 4'-0\" are treated as closed, so the outline runs straight across the doorway; Settings › TakeoffCraft › Room fill closes openings up to changes that width (3'-0\" to 8'-0\"). Room names, furniture and fixtures inside the room do not make holes. It refuses, saying why, when: the click is on a line (\"That is on a line\"); the space is not closed: an opening wider than the setting lets it run out to the sheet's edge. Raise the setting, or outline that room by hand with Area; the space is tiny (a symbol, not a room). When a door is wider than the setting, the fill runs into the next room and outlines both together: undo (Ctrl+Z), raise the setting, and click again. Check the outline like any area: drag its corners to fix it. A scanned sheet works the same, but its lines must be dark enough; light grey linework may not stop the fill. Cut a hole out of an area Choose Select (V) and click the area. It shows square handles at its corners. Choose Cut-out in the toolbar (or press X), or Add a cut-out under Selected mark. Click the corners of the hole, inside the area. Arc mode works here too. Close it like an area: click its first point, press Enter, double-click or right-click. The hole is left unfilled, and the status bar says \"Cut-out added. Draw another, or press Esc.\" Draw more holes in the same area, or press Esc. For a round hole, choose Add a round cut-out under Selected mark, click its center, then a point on its edge. A cut-out must sit fully inside the area and must not overlap another cut-out. Otherwise TakeoffCraft refuses it: \"A cut-out has to sit fully inside the area.\" or \"Cut-outs cannot overlap each other.\" To take the last hole out again, choose Remove last cut-out. Back-outs: columns, shafts and openings taken out of several items A cut-out belongs to one area mark. A back-out is drawn once and comes out of every area it overlaps, for the items you choose: draw the columns once, and they come off the floor finish, the ceiling and the slab together. Make an area item for them, such as \"Columns\". In its item settings, tick Back-out: its marks take their area out of other items. A list of your other area items appears, all ticked: untick the ones the back-outs should not touch (paint on the walls, say). Take off the columns with Area or Circle, as usual. Each one is drawn dashed. Every area mark of a ticked item on the same sheet loses the part a back-out covers: the whole back-out when it sits inside, only the overlapping part when it crosses the edge, and nothing inside a cut-out. The selected area's panel shows Back-outs and the area left. The item's quantity, its assembly formulas, the costs, the 3D check view and the export all use the area left; the estimate's notes say how much back-outs took out. The back-out item itself counts its own area (leave its costs at zero if you do not price it). A back-out has to be convex: a rectangle, a circle or any outline without an inward corner. TakeoffCraft refuses to draw one that is not; draw an L-shaped shaft as two back-outs. Back-outs cannot have cut-outs, and two back-outs that overlap each other both count. The perimeter (P) does not change: back-outs take out area only. Read the area Select the area. Selected mark shows: Line Meaning Area The net area: outline minus cut-outs. This is what the item counts. Outline The gross area inside the outline (shown when there are cut-outs). Cut-outs How many holes, and their total area. Perimeter The outline's edge length, plus the edges of every cut-out (\"with cut-outs\"). The perimeter includes cut-out edges on purpose, for things like edge forms or base that run round a hole as well. If you need only the outer edge, keep holes out of that item or measure the outer edge as a linear run. The item's Unit decides how the total is reported: SF, SY or m². The Selected mark panel and the status bar always show square feet. Fix an area Move a corner: select the area and drag a corner handle (holes have handles too). If the new shape would cross itself or push a hole outside, the corner is put back: \"The point was put back.\" Move to another item: pick another area item in the Item list under Selected mark. Delete: Delete, Delete in the toolbar's More menu, or Delete mark. Depth for volumes Give the area item a Depth (for example 0'-4\" for a slab) and use A * H / 27 in an assembly component to get cubic yards. See Assemblies and formulas.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"roofs-and-slopes","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Roofs and slopes","summary":"Give an area its pitch to measure the roof surface from the plan, and measure rakes, hips and valleys along their slope.","section":"tasks","order":55,"keywords":["roof","pitch","slope","slope factor","rise","run","6:12","rake","hip","valley","gable","roofing","squares","sloped area","true length","plan area"],"headings":[{"id":"typing-a-pitch","text":"Typing a pitch","level":2},{"id":"roof-areas","text":"Roof areas","level":2},{"id":"rakes-hips-and-valleys","text":"Rakes, hips and valleys","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA roof plan shows each roof plane flat, as seen from above. The roof itself is bigger: a plane at 6:12 has about 12% more surface than its plan, and its sloped edges are longer than they look. Give the item its pitch and TakeoffCraft measures along the slope.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-a-pitch\"\u003ETyping a pitch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pitch is the rise per 12 of run, as on the roof plan&#039;s pitch symbols. Type it as \u003Ccode\u003E6:12\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6/12\u003C/code\u003E or just \u003Ccode\u003E6\u003C/code\u003E; another run works too (\u003Ccode\u003E4:10\u003C/code\u003E is 4.8:12). An angle works with a degree sign: \u003Ccode\u003E26.57°\u003C/code\u003E. Blank or \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E is flat.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"roof-areas\"\u003ERoof areas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake an area item for the roofing, or open the one you have.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn its settings, type the \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E, such as \u003Ccode\u003E6:12\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOutline each roof plane on the plan with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach plane counts its plan area times the slope factor: √(1 + (rise ÷ 12)²), 1.118 at 6:12 and 1.414 at 12:12. Select a plane to see its \u003Cstrong\u003EArea\u003C/strong\u003E (on the slope), its \u003Cstrong\u003EPlan area\u003C/strong\u003E and the \u003Cstrong\u003ESlope factor\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlanes with another pitch: select the plane and type its own \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E in its panel. Leave it blank to use the item&#039;s. The 3D check view, the assemblies (\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E is the sloped area), the costs and the export all use the sloped area. Back-outs on a pitched plane, such as skylights, come off at the same slope.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe perimeter (\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E) stays the plan perimeter. Measure eaves, rakes, hips and valleys as runs (below).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rakes-hips-and-valleys\"\u003ERakes, hips and valleys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake a linear item, such as &quot;Rake edge&quot; or &quot;Hips&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet its \u003Cstrong\u003ESlope\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003ERake\u003C/strong\u003E (a sloped edge, like a gable&#039;s) or \u003Cstrong\u003EHip or valley\u003C/strong\u003E (the line where two planes meet), and type the \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E of the roof planes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the runs on the plan with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA rake counts its plan length times the slope factor. A hip or valley runs at 45° across the plan between two planes of the same pitch, so its true length is its plan length times √(1 + (rise ÷ 12)² ÷ 2): 1.061 at 6:12, 1.225 at 12:12.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne run can mix them. Select it: each segment in its panel has a slope list — \u003Cstrong\u003EItem&#039;s\u003C/strong\u003E (the item&#039;s setting), \u003Cstrong\u003ELevel\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ERake\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EHip or valley\u003C/strong\u003E — and its length along the slope. A run&#039;s own \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E in the panel replaces the item&#039;s for all its segments. The panel also shows the \u003Cstrong\u003EPlan length\u003C/strong\u003E, and the labels on the sheet show the lengths along the slope.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDeducts still work: a deducted segment counts nothing, sloped or not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHips and valleys assume both planes have the same pitch. For planes of different pitches, measure the hip by its true length elsewhere, or draw it as a rake with an equivalent pitch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"A roof plan shows each roof plane flat, as seen from above. The roof itself is bigger: a plane at 6:12 has about 12% more surface than its plan, and its sloped edges are longer than they look. Give the item its pitch and TakeoffCraft measures along the slope. Typing a pitch A pitch is the rise per 12 of run, as on the roof plan's pitch symbols. Type it as 6:12, 6/12 or just 6; another run works too (4:10 is 4.8:12). An angle works with a degree sign: 26.57°. Blank or 0 is flat. Roof areas Make an area item for the roofing, or open the one you have. In its settings, type the Pitch, such as 6:12. Outline each roof plane on the plan with Area, as usual. Each plane counts its plan area times the slope factor: √(1 + (rise ÷ 12)²), 1.118 at 6:12 and 1.414 at 12:12. Select a plane to see its Area (on the slope), its Plan area and the Slope factor. Planes with another pitch: select the plane and type its own Pitch in its panel. Leave it blank to use the item's. The 3D check view, the assemblies (A is the sloped area), the costs and the export all use the sloped area. Back-outs on a pitched plane, such as skylights, come off at the same slope. The perimeter (P) stays the plan perimeter. Measure eaves, rakes, hips and valleys as runs (below). Rakes, hips and valleys Make a linear item, such as \"Rake edge\" or \"Hips\". Set its Slope to Rake (a sloped edge, like a gable's) or Hip or valley (the line where two planes meet), and type the Pitch of the roof planes. Draw the runs on the plan with Linear. A rake counts its plan length times the slope factor. A hip or valley runs at 45° across the plan between two planes of the same pitch, so its true length is its plan length times √(1 + (rise ÷ 12)² ÷ 2): 1.061 at 6:12, 1.225 at 12:12. One run can mix them. Select it: each segment in its panel has a slope list — Item's (the item's setting), Level, Rake or Hip or valley — and its length along the slope. A run's own Pitch in the panel replaces the item's for all its segments. The panel also shows the Plan length, and the labels on the sheet show the lengths along the slope. Deducts still work: a deducted segment counts nothing, sloped or not. Hips and valleys assume both planes have the same pitch. For planes of different pitches, measure the hip by its true length elsewhere, or draw it as a rake with an equivalent pitch.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"count-by-hand","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Count by hand","summary":"Click each door, fixture or device to count it, check your counts, and fix mistakes.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["count","each","ea","fixtures","doors","devices","symbols","tally","click count","manual count"],"headings":[{"id":"count","text":"Count","level":2},{"id":"fix-a-count","text":"Fix a count","level":2},{"id":"many-identical-symbols","text":"Many identical symbols","level":2},{"id":"using-counts-in-formulas","text":"Using counts in formulas","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA count item counts things: doors, light fixtures, outlets, plumbing fixtures. Each click on the sheet adds one count mark. Counting does not need a scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"count\"\u003ECount\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake or click a count item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E type).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each one on the sheet. Each click puts a dot in the item&#039;s color, and the item&#039;s count goes up by one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the next sheet (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and keep counting. The tool stays on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when you are done. TakeoffCraft goes back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDragging still pans and the wheel still zooms while you count, so you can move round the sheet without adding dots.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the count item to highlight its dots on every sheet. The sheets list then shows how many are on each sheet, which helps you find the sheets you have not counted yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fix-a-count\"\u003EFix a count\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove a dot\u003C/strong\u003E: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), click the dot, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete mark\u003C/span\u003E. Or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E straight after a wrong click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove a dot\u003C/strong\u003E: select it, then drag it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECounted under the wrong item\u003C/strong\u003E: select the dot and pick the right count item in the \u003Cstrong\u003EItem\u003C/strong\u003E list under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"many-identical-symbols\"\u003EMany identical symbols\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a symbol that repeats many times on a sheet, try \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/auto-count\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/auto-count\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/a\u003E: box one, review what it finds, and add them all in one step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"using-counts-in-formulas\"\u003EUsing counts in formulas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA count item&#039;s quantity is \u003Cstrong\u003EN\u003C/strong\u003E. Assembly components can use it, for example hinges \u003Ccode\u003EN * 3\u003C/code\u003E for doors. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A count item counts things: doors, light fixtures, outlets, plumbing fixtures. Each click on the sheet adds one count mark. Counting does not need a scale. Count Make or click a count item under Items (Count type). Choose Count in the toolbar, or press C. Click each one on the sheet. Each click puts a dot in the item's color, and the item's count goes up by one. Move to the next sheet (Page Down) and keep counting. The tool stays on Count. Press Esc when you are done. TakeoffCraft goes back to Select. Dragging still pans and the wheel still zooms while you count, so you can move round the sheet without adding dots. Click the count item to highlight its dots on every sheet. The sheets list then shows how many are on each sheet, which helps you find the sheets you have not counted yet. Fix a count Remove a dot: choose Select (V), click the dot, and press Delete or choose Delete mark. Or press Ctrl+Z straight after a wrong click. Move a dot: select it, then drag it. Counted under the wrong item: select the dot and pick the right count item in the Item list under Selected mark. Many identical symbols For a symbol that repeats many times on a sheet, try Auto count: box one, review what it finds, and add them all in one step. Using counts in formulas A count item's quantity is N. Assembly components can use it, for example hinges N * 3 for doors. See Assemblies and formulas.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"auto-count","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Auto count a symbol","summary":"Box one symbol, let TakeoffCraft find the others on this sheet or every open sheet, review the matches with the slider, and add them in one step.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["any angle","rotated","turned","other scale","different scale","auto count","automatic count","symbol search","find symbols","pattern","template","match","detect","count tool"],"headings":[{"id":"find-the-symbols","text":"Find the symbols","level":2},{"id":"review-the-matches","text":"Review the matches","level":2},{"id":"add-them","text":"Add them","level":2},{"id":"what-it-finds-and-what-it-misses","text":"What it finds, and what it misses","level":2},{"id":"speed-and-browsers","text":"Speed and browsers","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E finds every copy of a symbol after you box one. You then review the matches on the sheet and add the ones you accept as count marks. It is a helper, not a guarantee: always review before you add.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-the-symbols\"\u003EFind the symbols\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake or click a count item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a sheet where the symbol appears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A bar appears at the top of the sheet: &quot;Drag a box around one symbol on this sheet.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo search every open sheet, not just this one, tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESearch all open sheets\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZoom in so the symbol is easy to see, then drag a box snugly round one copy of it. Include the whole symbol; blank paper round it is trimmed off. The bar shows &quot;Searching…&quot; with a progress bar and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E button. The page stays responsive: you can zoom, pan and move between sheets. Choosing another tool, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, cancels the search.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen it finishes, the matches appear on the sheet as boxes in the item&#039;s color, and the bar shows a summary such as &quot;22 matches, 19 to add (3 already counted). Click a box to reject or restore it.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E, dragging on the sheet draws a box instead of panning. To pan, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag, or drag with the middle button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"review-the-matches\"\u003EReview the matches\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClick a box\u003C/strong\u003E to reject it. A rejected box is dashed and crossed out. Click it again to restore it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch\u003C/span\u003E slider\u003C/strong\u003E (50% to 95%, starting at 75%). Higher shows only close matches; lower shows looser ones. The slider only filters what was found; it does not search again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlready counted\u003C/strong\u003E: matches on a spot that already has a count mark of this item start rejected, so you do not count anything twice.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther sheets\u003C/strong\u003E: when you searched all open sheets, move between sheets with the sheets list or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and review each one. The summary says how many sheets have matches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-them\"\u003EAdd them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E. The button says how many, for example &quot;Add 19 marks&quot;. The count marks are added at the center of each accepted box: &quot;Added 19 count marks to &quot;Duplex receptacle&quot;. Ctrl+Z removes them all.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole batch is one undo step, so one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo drop the review without adding anything, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Picking another tool also discards it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-finds-and-what-it-misses\"\u003EWhat it finds, and what it misses\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft compares the drawing, as pixels, with the symbol you boxed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt finds the symbol turned 0°, 90°, 180° and 270°. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003EAny angle (slower)\u003C/strong\u003E on the auto count bar before you draw the box to find it at any angle as well: it tries every 15°, then fine-tunes each find to the degree and a half. That takes about six times as long. It does not find a mirrored symbol (unless the symbol looks the same mirrored).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cstrong\u003ESearch all open sheets\u003C/strong\u003E, a sheet at another scale is searched at the size the symbol has there: a fixture drawn to scale on a 1/8&quot; plan is half the size it is on a 1/4&quot; plan, and is still found. Such a sheet is also searched at the box&#039;s own size, for tags and symbols printed at one size on every sheet. This needs a scale on both sheets; a sheet with no scale is searched at the box&#039;s size only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVery similar symbols can match each other: a duplex and a quad receptacle, or two fixture tags that differ by one letter. Raise the slider until only the right ones remain, and reject the rest by hand.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA symbol crossed by other lines, hatching or text scores lower and may need a lower slider setting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVery small boxes are refused: &quot;That box is too small. Draw it around the whole symbol.&quot; An empty box is refused too: &quot;That box is almost empty. Draw it around a symbol.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA sheet that would be very large at the size needed for this symbol is skipped, and the summary says &quot;1 sheet was too large to search at this symbol size.&quot; Box a larger symbol, or count that sheet by hand.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf nothing is found, the status bar says &quot;No matches. Lower the match slider, or box the symbol more tightly.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"speed-and-browsers\"\u003ESpeed and browsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe search runs in a background worker, so the page stays responsive and you can cancel at any time. Each sheet is first drawn at the working size on the page itself, which can make a very large sheet pause for a moment. It runs in current Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari. Searching many sheets takes longer; the progress bar moves sheet by sheet. Offline, auto count works when the app bar shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E keeps the worker too. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Auto count finds every copy of a symbol after you box one. You then review the matches on the sheet and add the ones you accept as count marks. It is a helper, not a guarantee: always review before you add. Find the symbols Make or click a count item under Items. Open a sheet where the symbol appears. Choose Auto count in the toolbar, or press S. A bar appears at the top of the sheet: \"Drag a box around one symbol on this sheet.\" To search every open sheet, not just this one, tick Search all open sheets. Zoom in so the symbol is easy to see, then drag a box snugly round one copy of it. Include the whole symbol; blank paper round it is trimmed off. The bar shows \"Searching…\" with a progress bar and a Cancel button. The page stays responsive: you can zoom, pan and move between sheets. Choosing another tool, or Esc, cancels the search. When it finishes, the matches appear on the sheet as boxes in the item's color, and the bar shows a summary such as \"22 matches, 19 to add (3 already counted). Click a box to reject or restore it.\" In Auto count, dragging on the sheet draws a box instead of panning. To pan, hold Space and drag, or drag with the middle button. Review the matches Click a box to reject it. A rejected box is dashed and crossed out. Click it again to restore it. Move the Match slider (50% to 95%, starting at 75%). Higher shows only close matches; lower shows looser ones. The slider only filters what was found; it does not search again. Already counted: matches on a spot that already has a count mark of this item start rejected, so you do not count anything twice. Other sheets: when you searched all open sheets, move between sheets with the sheets list or Page Down and review each one. The summary says how many sheets have matches. Add them Choose Add. The button says how many, for example \"Add 19 marks\". The count marks are added at the center of each accepted box: \"Added 19 count marks to \"Duplex receptacle\". Ctrl+Z removes them all.\" The whole batch is one undo step, so one Ctrl+Z removes it. To drop the review without adding anything, choose Discard or press Esc. Picking another tool also discards it. What it finds, and what it misses TakeoffCraft compares the drawing, as pixels, with the symbol you boxed. It finds the symbol turned 0°, 90°, 180° and 270°. Tick Any angle (slower) on the auto count bar before you draw the box to find it at any angle as well: it tries every 15°, then fine-tunes each find to the degree and a half. That takes about six times as long. It does not find a mirrored symbol (unless the symbol looks the same mirrored). With Search all open sheets, a sheet at another scale is searched at the size the symbol has there: a fixture drawn to scale on a 1/8\" plan is half the size it is on a 1/4\" plan, and is still found. Such a sheet is also searched at the box's own size, for tags and symbols printed at one size on every sheet. This needs a scale on both sheets; a sheet with no scale is searched at the box's size only. Very similar symbols can match each other: a duplex and a quad receptacle, or two fixture tags that differ by one letter. Raise the slider until only the right ones remain, and reject the rest by hand. A symbol crossed by other lines, hatching or text scores lower and may need a lower slider setting. Very small boxes are refused: \"That box is too small. Draw it around the whole symbol.\" An empty box is refused too: \"That box is almost empty. Draw it around a symbol.\" A sheet that would be very large at the size needed for this symbol is skipped, and the summary says \"1 sheet was too large to search at this symbol size.\" Box a larger symbol, or count that sheet by hand. If nothing is found, the status bar says \"No matches. Lower the match slider, or box the symbol more tightly.\" Speed and browsers The search runs in a background worker, so the page stays responsive and you can cancel at any time. Each sheet is first drawn at the working size on the page itself, which can make a very large sheet pause for a moment. It runs in current Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari. Searching many sheets takes longer; the progress bar moves sheet by sheet. Offline, auto count works when the app bar shows Offline ready: Save for offline keeps the worker too. See Working offline.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"count-everything","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Count everything in the set","summary":"Box a symbol legend once and TakeoffCraft counts every symbol in it on every open sheet in one run. Each count is a link to its sheet, and you review before anything is added.","section":"tasks","order":71,"keywords":["count everything","legend","symbol legend","whole set","every sheet","all sheets","every door","every fixture","devices","schedule","symbols","auto count","count all","set of plans","200 sheets","match level","look alike"],"headings":[{"id":"count-a-legend","text":"Count a legend","level":2},{"id":"review-the-counts","text":"Review the counts","level":2},{"id":"add-them","text":"Add them","level":2},{"id":"with-your-assistant","text":"With your assistant","level":2},{"id":"symbols-at-any-angle","text":"Symbols at any angle","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount everything\u003C/span\u003E reads a symbol legend and then counts each symbol in it on every open sheet. It is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E for a whole legend at once. It is plain symbol matching on your own sheets, not a trained model, and nothing leaves your computer. Review the counts before you add them: it is a helper, not a guarantee.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"count-a-legend\"\u003ECount a legend\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the plans. A legend on any sheet will do: the symbols with their names beside them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount everything\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a box around the legend, symbols and names. A title with no symbol beside it (SYMBOL LEGEND) is left out by itself. The panel lists each symbol with its picture and the name printed beside it. The name becomes the item&#039;s name, so rename it here if you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick any symbol you do not want counted. A symbol that cannot be searched (too small, or blank) says why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount on N sheets\u003C/span\u003E. A bar shows the sheet being searched; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E stops the run. The page stays usable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe legend&#039;s own symbols are not counted on its sheet. A sheet drawn at another scale is searched at that scale, and the symbols are found turned in any right angle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"review-the-counts\"\u003EReview the counts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach symbol shows its total and, under it, a link per sheet that has any (&quot;E-103: 4&quot;). Click one to go to that sheet. The finds are boxed in the item&#039;s color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClick a box\u003C/strong\u003E to leave that find out. It is dashed and crossed out; click it again to bring it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove the match level\u003C/strong\u003E (50% to 100%, starting at your Settings level). Higher keeps only close matches. The slider only filters what was found; nothing is searched again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo symbols that look alike (a duplex and a quad outlet) can match the same spot. The better match takes it and the panel says how many spots that happened on, so you can check them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA sheet too large to search at the symbol&#039;s size is skipped, and the panel says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-them\"\u003EAdd them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd N marks\u003C/span\u003E. Each symbol becomes a count item with a mark on every find. A name that is already a count item of yours is reused (nothing is doubled where you already marked by hand). The whole run is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Z\"\u003E⌃Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes every item and mark back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat you accept also feeds \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearned\u003C/span\u003E: the symbols are remembered in the takeoff&#039;s file for next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-your-assistant\"\u003EWith your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you allow assistants in Settings, they can read a legend (legend_open), run the count in the background (legend_count_start, then legend_count_status), read the counts (legend_count_result) and add them (legend_count_add, which can first preview what it would add). It is the same run you see in the panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"symbols-at-any-angle\"\u003ESymbols at any angle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESymbols at any angle\u003C/span\u003E before you count to also find symbols drawn at an angle that is not a right angle (a diagonal wall plug, a fixture turned to follow a roof). The search then turns each symbol every 15 degrees; a round symbol still needs one search and a symbol that is not round can take up to six times as long. Leave it off for plans drawn square.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt needs symbols drawn the same way as in the legend. A legend drawn at a different size from the sheets is fine when the sheets&#039; scales are set; without scales, the symbols are searched at the legend&#039;s size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA symbol with a wall or a long line drawn straight through it can be missed, because it no longer looks like the legend&#039;s picture. Check the finds on the first sheet before trusting the rest.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA scan with no text still works: the symbols are found by their rows, and you type the names.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Count everything reads a symbol legend and then counts each symbol in it on every open sheet. It is Auto count for a whole legend at once. It is plain symbol matching on your own sheets, not a trained model, and nothing leaves your computer. Review the counts before you add them: it is a helper, not a guarantee. Count a legend Open the plans. A legend on any sheet will do: the symbols with their names beside them. Choose Count everything in the toolbar. Drag a box around the legend, symbols and names. A title with no symbol beside it (SYMBOL LEGEND) is left out by itself. The panel lists each symbol with its picture and the name printed beside it. The name becomes the item's name, so rename it here if you like. Untick any symbol you do not want counted. A symbol that cannot be searched (too small, or blank) says why. Choose Count on N sheets. A bar shows the sheet being searched; Cancel stops the run. The page stays usable. The legend's own symbols are not counted on its sheet. A sheet drawn at another scale is searched at that scale, and the symbols are found turned in any right angle. Review the counts Each symbol shows its total and, under it, a link per sheet that has any (\"E-103: 4\"). Click one to go to that sheet. The finds are boxed in the item's color. Click a box to leave that find out. It is dashed and crossed out; click it again to bring it back. Move the match level (50% to 100%, starting at your Settings level). Higher keeps only close matches. The slider only filters what was found; nothing is searched again. Two symbols that look alike (a duplex and a quad outlet) can match the same spot. The better match takes it and the panel says how many spots that happened on, so you can check them. A sheet too large to search at the symbol's size is skipped, and the panel says so. Add them Choose Add N marks. Each symbol becomes a count item with a mark on every find. A name that is already a count item of yours is reused (nothing is doubled where you already marked by hand). The whole run is one step: Ctrl+Z takes every item and mark back. What you accept also feeds Learned: the symbols are remembered in the takeoff's file for next time. With your assistant If you allow assistants in Settings, they can read a legend (legend_open), run the count in the background (legend_count_start, then legend_count_status), read the counts (legend_count_result) and add them (legend_count_add, which can first preview what it would add). It is the same run you see in the panel. Symbols at any angle Tick Symbols at any angle before you count to also find symbols drawn at an angle that is not a right angle (a diagonal wall plug, a fixture turned to follow a roof). The search then turns each symbol every 15 degrees; a round symbol still needs one search and a symbol that is not round can take up to six times as long. Leave it off for plans drawn square. Good to know It needs symbols drawn the same way as in the legend. A legend drawn at a different size from the sheets is fine when the sheets' scales are set; without scales, the symbols are searched at the legend's size. A symbol with a wall or a long line drawn straight through it can be missed, because it no longer looks like the legend's picture. Check the finds on the first sheet before trusting the rest. A scan with no text still works: the symbols are found by their rows, and you type the names.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"count-from-a-drawing","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Count from a drawing","summary":"Count blocks, objects on a layer, or text straight from a DWG or DXF: exact, with nested blocks, dynamic block variants and block arrays handled, in one undo step.","section":"tasks","order":71,"keywords":["count from drawing","cad count","block count","count blocks","insert","block name","nested blocks","dynamic block","variant","minsert","array","layer","entity type","circles","text","attribute","tag","hidden layers","exact count","how to","doors","fixtures"],"headings":[{"id":"count-a-block","text":"Count a block","level":2},{"id":"what-counts","text":"What counts","level":3},{"id":"what-does-not","text":"What does not","level":3},{"id":"what-this-sheet-shows-or-the-whole-drawing","text":"What this sheet shows, or the whole drawing","level":2},{"id":"count-counts-again","text":"Count counts again","level":2},{"id":"by-layer-and-type","text":"By layer and type","level":2},{"id":"by-text","text":"By text","level":2},{"id":"when-to-use-auto-count-instead","text":"When to use Auto count instead","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECAD count\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar (the command \u003Cstrong\u003ECount from the drawing\u003C/strong\u003E in the other menu styles) counts what a DWG or DXF says is there. Where \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E looks for a picture of a symbol and can miss one or find a false one, this counts the objects: every insert of a block named \u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-36\u003C/code\u003E is one door. Open the drawing first: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\"\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"count-a-block\"\u003ECount a block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a sheet of the drawing. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECAD count\u003C/span\u003E is on the sheet bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose it. The window lists the blocks with how many of each there are. Search by name, then pick one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the line under the list: &quot;22 found, 22 to add.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd to\u003C/span\u003E, pick a count item, or leave \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA new count item\u003C/span\u003E and give it a name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the add button, which names the number: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd 22 marks\u003C/span\u003E. The marks appear at the block&#039;s insertion points, and the item&#039;s quantity is the count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is one undo step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the marks (and a new item) away together. The estimate, the spreadsheet and the marked PDF treat the item like any other count item.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-counts\"\u003EWhat counts\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery insert of the block name\u003C/strong\u003E, whatever its scale and angle, in model space or paper space. Block names do not care about case.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBlock arrays\u003C/strong\u003E (MINSERT): each copy in the array is one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENested blocks.\u003C/strong\u003E A block placed inside another block counts at every depth. The list says &quot;6 inside other blocks&quot;; untick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount blocks inside other blocks too\u003C/span\u003E to count only inserts placed directly in the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDynamic blocks.\u003C/strong\u003E A dynamic block counts under its own name, not under the anonymous copies (\u003Ccode\u003E*U12\u003C/code\u003E) the file keeps. Its states are listed apart (&quot;Single 12, Double 4&quot;): pick a state to count only those, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery variant\u003C/span\u003E. When a file does not say which state a copy shows, those are listed as &quot;(state not known)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayer filter.\u003C/strong\u003E A block on several layers: pick one under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOn layer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-does-not\"\u003EWhat does not\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHidden layers.\u003C/strong\u003E An object on a layer that is off or frozen in the file (or that you hid in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E) is left out, and the line says how many: &quot;3 on hidden layers left out&quot;. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount them too\u003C/span\u003E to include them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinked drawings that are not open\u003C/strong\u003E are not counted; the window says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EObjects stacked exactly on another of the same kind\u003C/strong\u003E are counted as two (they are two objects) and the window says so: it is usually a drafting slip worth a look.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-this-sheet-shows-or-the-whole-drawing\"\u003EWhat this sheet shows, or the whole drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhat this sheet shows\u003C/strong\u003E counts the objects the sheet shows: on a layout, the model objects inside its viewports (a layer frozen in a viewport is not counted there) plus the layout&#039;s own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe whole drawing, every object once\u003C/strong\u003E counts model space once, with the marks on the Model sheet, and does not count what several layouts show again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECounting what a layout shows and counting what Model shows give the same number for the same objects; do not add both to one item.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"count-counts-again\"\u003ECount counts again\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECounting the same block again with the same item adds only what is new: &quot;22 already counted.&quot; Each mark stands for one object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-layer-and-type\"\u003EBy layer and type\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy layer and type\u003C/span\u003E pick a layer and an object type (circles, polylines, hatches…): &quot;all circles on E-LITE&quot;. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlso count objects inside blocks\u003C/span\u003E to include those inside block definitions, at each place the block is inserted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-text\"\u003EBy text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy text\u003C/span\u003E type the text, such as \u003Ccode\u003EA3\u003C/code\u003E, and say whether it is exactly that, contains it, or has it as a whole word. Text objects, multileader text and block \u003Cstrong\u003Eattribute values\u003C/strong\u003E all count (a tag attribute \u003Ccode\u003EITEM = A3\u003C/code\u003E is found); pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn attribute\u003C/span\u003E to count one attribute only. The most common texts are listed to click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-to-use-auto-count-instead\"\u003EWhen to use Auto count instead\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E for PDFs and for symbols in a drawing that are not blocks (drawn loose, or exploded). Drawn as blocks, count them here.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"CAD count on the sheet bar (the command Count from the drawing in the other menu styles) counts what a DWG or DXF says is there. Where Auto count looks for a picture of a symbol and can miss one or find a false one, this counts the objects: every insert of a block named DOOR-36 is one door. Open the drawing first: Open a DWG or DXF drawing. Count a block Open a sheet of the drawing. CAD count is on the sheet bar. Choose it. The window lists the blocks with how many of each there are. Search by name, then pick one. Check the line under the list: \"22 found, 22 to add.\" Under Add to, pick a count item, or leave A new count item and give it a name. Choose the add button, which names the number: Add 22 marks. The marks appear at the block's insertion points, and the item's quantity is the count. It is one undo step: Ctrl+Z takes the marks (and a new item) away together. The estimate, the spreadsheet and the marked PDF treat the item like any other count item. What counts Every insert of the block name, whatever its scale and angle, in model space or paper space. Block names do not care about case. Block arrays (MINSERT): each copy in the array is one. Nested blocks. A block placed inside another block counts at every depth. The list says \"6 inside other blocks\"; untick Count blocks inside other blocks too to count only inserts placed directly in the drawing. Dynamic blocks. A dynamic block counts under its own name, not under the anonymous copies (*U12) the file keeps. Its states are listed apart (\"Single 12, Double 4\"): pick a state to count only those, or Every variant. When a file does not say which state a copy shows, those are listed as \"(state not known)\". Layer filter. A block on several layers: pick one under On layer. What does not Hidden layers. An object on a layer that is off or frozen in the file (or that you hid in Layers) is left out, and the line says how many: \"3 on hidden layers left out\". Tick Count them too to include them. Linked drawings that are not open are not counted; the window says so. Objects stacked exactly on another of the same kind are counted as two (they are two objects) and the window says so: it is usually a drafting slip worth a look. What this sheet shows, or the whole drawing What this sheet shows counts the objects the sheet shows: on a layout, the model objects inside its viewports (a layer frozen in a viewport is not counted there) plus the layout's own. The whole drawing, every object once counts model space once, with the marks on the Model sheet, and does not count what several layouts show again. Counting what a layout shows and counting what Model shows give the same number for the same objects; do not add both to one item. Count counts again Counting the same block again with the same item adds only what is new: \"22 already counted.\" Each mark stands for one object. By layer and type On By layer and type pick a layer and an object type (circles, polylines, hatches…): \"all circles on E-LITE\". Tick Also count objects inside blocks to include those inside block definitions, at each place the block is inserted. By text On By text type the text, such as A3, and say whether it is exactly that, contains it, or has it as a whole word. Text objects, multileader text and block attribute values all count (a tag attribute ITEM = A3 is found); pick In attribute to count one attribute only. The most common texts are listed to click. When to use Auto count instead Use Auto count for PDFs and for symbols in a drawing that are not blocks (drawn loose, or exploded). Drawn as blocks, count them here.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"what-it-learned","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"What it learned from your takeoffs","summary":"TakeoffCraft keeps the symbols you confirmed with Auto count, your condition sets, the assemblies you use most and patterns you repeat on every page, all inside the takeoff's own file. See it, use it, delete it.","section":"tasks","order":72,"keywords":["learn","learned","learning","assistant","remember","symbols","templates","same as last job","last job","condition set","conditions","reuse","assemblies","most used","suggestions","repeat on every page","every page","title block","pattern","clear all","forget","privacy"],"headings":[{"id":"symbols","text":"Symbols","level":2},{"id":"condition-sets","text":"Condition sets","level":2},{"id":"same-as-last-job","text":"Same as last job","level":2},{"id":"assemblies-you-use-most","text":"Assemblies you use most","level":2},{"id":"repeat-on-every-page","text":"Repeat on every page","level":2},{"id":"see-it-delete-it","text":"See it, delete it","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft gets quicker the more takeoffs you do with it. It learns \u003Cstrong\u003Eonly from what you accept\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe symbols you boxed with \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/auto-count\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/auto-count\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/a\u003E and then added,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe condition sets you save,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe assemblies you use (once per job),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe patterns of marks you repeat on every page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is plain matching on your own sheets, the same search Auto count runs. It is not a trained model. Everything it learns is kept \u003Cstrong\u003Ein this takeoff&#039;s file\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E), and nothing is sent anywhere. To carry it to the next job, use \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/what-it-learned#same-as-last-job\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/what-it-learned\" data-hash=\"same-as-last-job\"\u003ESame as last job\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearned\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar (the light bulb, next to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E) to see all of it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"symbols\"\u003ESymbols\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you box a symbol with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E, the symbol you boxed is kept, with the match level you accepted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearned\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind on this sheet\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind on every sheet\u003C/span\u003E. The usual Auto count review opens: click a box to leave it out, move the slider, then add them. Nothing is added until you do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you open a sheet that has none of a learned symbol yet, a bar at the top offers \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind …\u003C/span\u003E for it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E hides it for that sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the takeoff has no item of that name, adding the marks also adds the item (one undo step).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdding the same item&#039;s symbol again counts toward the one it knows; a different-looking symbol is kept as a second one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELike Auto count, it finds the symbol at right angles and on sheets at another scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"condition-sets\"\u003ECondition sets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA set keeps a takeoff&#039;s conditions (items with their units, rates, waste and assemblies), without marks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearned\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave this takeoff&#039;s conditions as a set…\u003C/span\u003E and give it a name. A set with the same name is replaced.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a takeoff that has the set, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E puts in the conditions it does not have yet. Conditions with the same name and type are left alone. It is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"same-as-last-job\"\u003ESame as last job\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour last job&#039;s file holds what it learned. To start a new job from it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the new plans, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearned\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESame as last job…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the last job&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick what to bring in: its conditions (only the ones this takeoff does not have), and what it learned (symbols, sets, assemblies, patterns). Then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBring them in\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIts conditions are also kept as a set named after that job. Its marks and quantities stay in its own file. The whole thing is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assemblies-you-use-most\"\u003EAssemblies you use most\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn item that has an assembly counts as used once per job when it has a quantity and you save the takeoff. A job is the set of plan files it is taken off, so saving the same takeoff again does not count twice. The list under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssemblies you use most\u003C/span\u003E puts the ones you use most first. It leaves out the ones this takeoff already has. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E puts one in as you last used it, with all its parts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"repeat-on-every-page\"\u003ERepeat on every page\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor marks that are the same on every page of a set, like a title block count or a typical detail:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the marks on one sheet (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/copy-paste#select-the-marks\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/copy-paste\" data-hash=\"select-the-marks\"\u003ESelect the marks\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearned\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepeat the selected marks on every page…\u003C/span\u003E, name the pattern and confirm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey go on every other open page \u003Cstrong\u003Eof the same size\u003C/strong\u003E, at the same place, in one undo step. Lengths and areas only go on pages with the same scale; counts go on any page of that size. A page that already has them is skipped. The pattern is kept, so pages you open later get the offer in the bar, or use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepeat on every page\u003C/span\u003E in the list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-it-delete-it\"\u003ESee it, delete it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything learned is listed in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearned\u003C/span\u003E. The bin deletes one thing. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear all\u003C/span\u003E deletes everything learned and leaves your items and marks alone. Both can be undone until you close the takeoff, and both are saved with the takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo stop learning in one takeoff, clear \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearn from this takeoff\u003C/span\u003E. Nothing new is learned, and what is listed stays until you delete it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOlder versions of TakeoffCraft open a file with learned things normally. They ignore that part, and drop it if they save the file again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA learned symbol is a small picture of what you boxed, kept in the file (a few kilobytes each).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELearned symbols are searched like Auto count searches, so the same limits apply: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/auto-count\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/auto-count\"\u003EAuto count a symbol\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft gets quicker the more takeoffs you do with it. It learns only from what you accept: the symbols you boxed with Auto count and then added, the condition sets you save, the assemblies you use (once per job), the patterns of marks you repeat on every page. It is plain matching on your own sheets, the same search Auto count runs. It is not a trained model. Everything it learns is kept in this takeoff's file (.takeoff.json), and nothing is sent anywhere. To carry it to the next job, use Same as last job. Choose Learned on the toolbar (the light bulb, next to Auto count) to see all of it. Symbols When you box a symbol with Auto count and choose Add, the symbol you boxed is kept, with the match level you accepted. In Learned, choose Find on this sheet or Find on every sheet. The usual Auto count review opens: click a box to leave it out, move the slider, then add them. Nothing is added until you do. When you open a sheet that has none of a learned symbol yet, a bar at the top offers Find … for it. Not now hides it for that sheet. If the takeoff has no item of that name, adding the marks also adds the item (one undo step). Adding the same item's symbol again counts toward the one it knows; a different-looking symbol is kept as a second one. Like Auto count, it finds the symbol at right angles and on sheets at another scale. Condition sets A set keeps a takeoff's conditions (items with their units, rates, waste and assemblies), without marks. In Learned, choose Save this takeoff's conditions as a set… and give it a name. A set with the same name is replaced. In a takeoff that has the set, Add puts in the conditions it does not have yet. Conditions with the same name and type are left alone. It is one undo step. Same as last job Your last job's file holds what it learned. To start a new job from it: Open the new plans, then choose Learned › Same as last job…. Pick the last job's .takeoff.json. Tick what to bring in: its conditions (only the ones this takeoff does not have), and what it learned (symbols, sets, assemblies, patterns). Then Bring them in. Its conditions are also kept as a set named after that job. Its marks and quantities stay in its own file. The whole thing is one undo step. Assemblies you use most An item that has an assembly counts as used once per job when it has a quantity and you save the takeoff. A job is the set of plan files it is taken off, so saving the same takeoff again does not count twice. The list under Assemblies you use most puts the ones you use most first. It leaves out the ones this takeoff already has. Add puts one in as you last used it, with all its parts. Repeat on every page For marks that are the same on every page of a set, like a title block count or a typical detail: Select the marks on one sheet (Select the marks). In Learned, choose Repeat the selected marks on every page…, name the pattern and confirm. They go on every other open page of the same size, at the same place, in one undo step. Lengths and areas only go on pages with the same scale; counts go on any page of that size. A page that already has them is skipped. The pattern is kept, so pages you open later get the offer in the bar, or use Repeat on every page in the list. See it, delete it Everything learned is listed in Learned. The bin deletes one thing. Clear all deletes everything learned and leaves your items and marks alone. Both can be undone until you close the takeoff, and both are saved with the takeoff. To stop learning in one takeoff, clear Learn from this takeoff. Nothing new is learned, and what is listed stays until you delete it. Good to know Older versions of TakeoffCraft open a file with learned things normally. They ignore that part, and drop it if they save the file again. A learned symbol is a small picture of what you boxed, kept in the file (a few kilobytes each). Learned symbols are searched like Auto count searches, so the same limits apply: Auto count a symbol.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"price-memory","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Price memory from your past jobs","summary":"TakeoffCraft remembers the rates you used on past takeoffs, on this computer only, so a new takeoff can start from what you paid last time. See it, use it, forget it.","section":"tasks","order":73,"keywords":["price memory","prices","remember prices","last job","past jobs","typical price","median","rates","unpriced","reprice","learn from a past takeoff","forget","library"],"headings":[{"id":"what-gets-remembered","text":"What gets remembered","level":2},{"id":"use-it-on-a-new-takeoff","text":"Use it on a new takeoff","level":2},{"id":"see-it-change-it-forget-it","text":"See it, change it, forget it","level":2},{"id":"with-your-assistant","text":"With your assistant","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrices\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar (the dollar sign) opens what TakeoffCraft remembers about the prices you used. It is a plain list: for each item name, the rates from your last jobs. It is kept in this browser (in Studio, its own web view) beside the price Library, never inside a takeoff file, and nothing is sent anywhere. It is not a trained model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-gets-remembered\"\u003EWhat gets remembered\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you save a takeoff, each item that has a price and is used (it has a mark, or a typed quantity above zero) is remembered under its name and type: material rate, labor rate, waste and unit. A job is the plan files it is taken off, so saving the same takeoff again replaces its own prices instead of adding another job. The last 12 jobs are kept for each name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo start with your older work, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearn from a past takeoff…\u003C/span\u003E and pick a saved takeoff (.json). Its priced, used items are remembered the same way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENames match when their words match, whatever the case or punctuation (&quot;2x4 Stud, 8 ft&quot; and &quot;2x4 stud 8 FT&quot; are the same). The same words for another kind of item (a linear item and a count item) are not the same price.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-it-on-a-new-takeoff\"\u003EUse it on a new takeoff\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake the items of the new takeoff.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrices\u003C/span\u003E. Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThis takeoff\u003C/span\u003E you see every item that has a remembered price, what you have on it now, the last job&#039;s rates, the typical rates (the middle of the remembered jobs) and the range.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse last\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse typical\u003C/span\u003E on one item, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrice N unpriced items from memory\u003C/span\u003E to fill every item that has no price yet from the last job.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach use is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Z\"\u003E⌃Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back. An item that only has a similar name (one name inside the other) says so, and the buttons use the similar name&#039;s rates. A price remembered per each is never put on an item per foot: the dialog says the units differ and leaves it alone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-it-change-it-forget-it\"\u003ESee it, change it, forget it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEverything remembered\u003C/span\u003E lists every name with its rates and the number of jobs. The trash button forgets one name; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear all prices\u003C/span\u003E forgets them all. Turn off \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemember my prices when I save a takeoff\u003C/span\u003E to stop remembering; what is already there stays until you forget it. Your takeoffs and the price Library are never touched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-your-assistant\"\u003EWith your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you allow assistants in Settings, they can read what is remembered for the takeoff&#039;s items (prices_list) and price items from it (prices_apply, which can preview first). It is the same list as the dialog.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Prices on the toolbar (the dollar sign) opens what TakeoffCraft remembers about the prices you used. It is a plain list: for each item name, the rates from your last jobs. It is kept in this browser (in Studio, its own web view) beside the price Library, never inside a takeoff file, and nothing is sent anywhere. It is not a trained model. What gets remembered When you save a takeoff, each item that has a price and is used (it has a mark, or a typed quantity above zero) is remembered under its name and type: material rate, labor rate, waste and unit. A job is the plan files it is taken off, so saving the same takeoff again replaces its own prices instead of adding another job. The last 12 jobs are kept for each name. To start with your older work, choose Learn from a past takeoff… and pick a saved takeoff (.json). Its priced, used items are remembered the same way. Names match when their words match, whatever the case or punctuation (\"2x4 Stud, 8 ft\" and \"2x4 stud 8 FT\" are the same). The same words for another kind of item (a linear item and a count item) are not the same price. Use it on a new takeoff Make the items of the new takeoff. Choose Prices. Under This takeoff you see every item that has a remembered price, what you have on it now, the last job's rates, the typical rates (the middle of the remembered jobs) and the range. Choose Use last or Use typical on one item, or Price N unpriced items from memory to fill every item that has no price yet from the last job. Each use is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. An item that only has a similar name (one name inside the other) says so, and the buttons use the similar name's rates. A price remembered per each is never put on an item per foot: the dialog says the units differ and leaves it alone. See it, change it, forget it Everything remembered lists every name with its rates and the number of jobs. The trash button forgets one name; Clear all prices forgets them all. Turn off Remember my prices when I save a takeoff to stop remembering; what is already there stays until you forget it. Your takeoffs and the price Library are never touched. With your assistant If you allow assistants in Settings, they can read what is remembered for the takeoff's items (prices_list) and price items from it (prices_apply, which can preview first). It is the same list as the dialog.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"supplier-stock","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Check supplier stock and lead times","summary":"Add a supplier's stock list (CSV or spreadsheet) and TakeoffCraft checks what the takeoff needs, with waste, against it: enough in stock, and how long the wait is. Shortages and long waits show in the Problems panel.","section":"tasks","order":74,"keywords":["supplier","stock","in stock","lead time","availability","shortage","backorder","order list","csv","sku","vendor","materials","supply","problems"],"headings":[{"id":"the-file","text":"The file","level":2},{"id":"check-the-takeoff","text":"Check the takeoff","level":2},{"id":"spreadsheet-files-and-the-price-library","text":"Spreadsheet files and the price Library","level":2},{"id":"in-the-problems-panel","text":"In the Problems panel","level":2},{"id":"with-your-assistant","text":"With your assistant","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESupply\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar (the truck) checks what your takeoff needs against a supplier&#039;s stock list. The supplier&#039;s rows that matter are kept in the takeoff&#039;s own file. Nothing is sent anywhere and nothing asks a supplier: you bring the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-file\"\u003EThe file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA CSV file with a header row. It needs a name or SKU column and a stock or lead-time column. These headers are recognised (any order, any case): Item, Name, Description or Product; SKU, Part or Item #; In stock, On hand, Available or Quantity; Lead time or Delivery days; Unit or UOM; Price or Cost; Supplier or Vendor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStock can be a number (1,200), &quot;In stock&quot; or &quot;Out of stock&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA lead time can be &quot;5 days&quot;, &quot;2 weeks&quot;, &quot;2-3 weeks&quot; (the longer is used), &quot;3 months&quot;, &quot;same day&quot; or &quot;backorder&quot;. A plain number is in the header&#039;s unit (days, unless the header says weeks or months).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExcel files are not read: save the sheet as CSV first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-the-takeoff\"\u003ECheck the takeoff\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESupply\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a supplier file…\u003C/span\u003E and pick the CSV. Add more than one: up to five files, 2,000 rows kept in all, those that match your items first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach item, second condition and assembly part with a quantity is matched to a row by name, or by SKU in its name. A similar name (one inside the other) is used when there is no exact one, and says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the takeoff needs\u003C/span\u003E, short ones come first: &quot;Short by 120 EA&quot;, &quot;Lead time 21 days&quot;, &quot;On backorder&quot;, &quot;Unit differs&quot;, &quot;No stock figure&quot; or &quot;In stock&quot;. The need includes waste. A supplier unit that is not the estimate&#039;s (a ton against a foot) is reported and never compared. Each row has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect the item\u003C/span\u003E, which jumps to the item.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELongest wait wanted (days)\u003C/span\u003E (default 14). An item that is not in stock and waits longer than that is flagged, and the summary names the longest wait that matters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrder list (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E downloads the short items with the amount short, the supplier and the lead time. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave out\u003C/span\u003E takes a need out of the check (it is an undo step).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"spreadsheet-files-and-the-price-library\"\u003ESpreadsheet files and the price Library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA supplier&#039;s list can be an Excel workbook (.xlsx) as well as a CSV: the first sheet is read, with the same column names. Formulas are never run; the value the file holds is what is read.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnce a file with prices has been added, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrices into the Library\u003C/span\u003E in the dialog sets the material price of each \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/price-library\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/price-library\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/a\u003E entry the file prices: same name, and a unit that is the same (or not said). Labour, waste, height and assemblies stay as they are, and the entry&#039;s note says which file the price came from. An entry in metres is not priced from a list in feet, and one with no matching name is left alone; the status line says how many were skipped for the unit. Reprice from the Library to bring the new prices into this takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-problems-panel\"\u003EIn the Problems panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA shortage is a warning and a long wait is a note, in the shared Problems panel (&quot;Supplier stock&quot;), so they sit with every other check. You can accept one with a reason, and the acceptance is saved in the takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-your-assistant\"\u003EWith your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you allow assistants in Settings, they can read the check (supply_check). Adding a supplier file is yours to do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStock figures are only as current as the file you add. Add the file again to refresh it: a file with the same name replaces the earlier one.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Supply on the toolbar (the truck) checks what your takeoff needs against a supplier's stock list. The supplier's rows that matter are kept in the takeoff's own file. Nothing is sent anywhere and nothing asks a supplier: you bring the file. The file A CSV file with a header row. It needs a name or SKU column and a stock or lead-time column. These headers are recognised (any order, any case): Item, Name, Description or Product; SKU, Part or Item #; In stock, On hand, Available or Quantity; Lead time or Delivery days; Unit or UOM; Price or Cost; Supplier or Vendor. Stock can be a number (1,200), \"In stock\" or \"Out of stock\". A lead time can be \"5 days\", \"2 weeks\", \"2-3 weeks\" (the longer is used), \"3 months\", \"same day\" or \"backorder\". A plain number is in the header's unit (days, unless the header says weeks or months). Excel files are not read: save the sheet as CSV first. Check the takeoff Choose Supply, then Add a supplier file… and pick the CSV. Add more than one: up to five files, 2,000 rows kept in all, those that match your items first. Each item, second condition and assembly part with a quantity is matched to a row by name, or by SKU in its name. A similar name (one inside the other) is used when there is no exact one, and says so. Under What the takeoff needs, short ones come first: \"Short by 120 EA\", \"Lead time 21 days\", \"On backorder\", \"Unit differs\", \"No stock figure\" or \"In stock\". The need includes waste. A supplier unit that is not the estimate's (a ton against a foot) is reported and never compared. Each row has Select the item, which jumps to the item. Set Longest wait wanted (days) (default 14). An item that is not in stock and waits longer than that is flagged, and the summary names the longest wait that matters. Order list (CSV) downloads the short items with the amount short, the supplier and the lead time. Leave out takes a need out of the check (it is an undo step). Spreadsheet files and the price Library A supplier's list can be an Excel workbook (.xlsx) as well as a CSV: the first sheet is read, with the same column names. Formulas are never run; the value the file holds is what is read. Once a file with prices has been added, Prices into the Library in the dialog sets the material price of each Library entry the file prices: same name, and a unit that is the same (or not said). Labour, waste, height and assemblies stay as they are, and the entry's note says which file the price came from. An entry in metres is not priced from a list in feet, and one with no matching name is left alone; the status line says how many were skipped for the unit. Reprice from the Library to bring the new prices into this takeoff. In the Problems panel A shortage is a warning and a long wait is a note, in the shared Problems panel (\"Supplier stock\"), so they sit with every other check. You can accept one with a reason, and the acceptance is saved in the takeoff. With your assistant If you allow assistants in Settings, they can read the check (supply_check). Adding a supplier file is yours to do. Good to know Stock figures are only as current as the file you add. Add the file again to refresh it: a file with the same name replaces the earlier one.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"copy-paste","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Copy, paste and duplicate marks","summary":"Copy marks with their items, paste them where you click or in the same place on another sheet, duplicate them, cut, delete and select all, each one step to undo.","section":"tasks","order":75,"keywords":["copy","paste","cut","duplicate","paste in place","clipboard","select all","select none","multiple marks","typical floor","repeat","same plan","another sheet","copy item","duplicate item"],"headings":[{"id":"select-the-marks","text":"Select the marks","level":2},{"id":"copy-and-paste-where-you-click","text":"Copy and paste where you click","level":2},{"id":"paste-in-place","text":"Paste in place","level":2},{"id":"the-scale-must-match","text":"The scale must match","level":2},{"id":"duplicate","text":"Duplicate","level":2},{"id":"cut-and-delete","text":"Cut and delete","level":2},{"id":"copy-an-item","text":"Copy an item","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group of the toolbar holds \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E. Its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu has the rest, each with its key: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E. Every one of them is one step to undo with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-the-marks\"\u003ESelect the marks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click a mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click more marks to add them. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click on a selected mark takes it out again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E) to take every mark on the open sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith several marks selected, the right panel shows \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected marks\u003C/strong\u003E: how many of each item, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete marks\u003C/span\u003E. Corner handles show only when one mark is selected. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E) clears the selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-and-paste-where-you-click\"\u003ECopy and paste where you click\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the marks and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E). The marks are copied with their items, including each item&#039;s assembly, waste and costs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGo to the sheet you want them on (it can be the same sheet).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E). A dashed copy follows the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it goes. The middle of the copied marks lands at the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels and adds nothing. The pasted marks are selected afterwards, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes them away again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the same takeoff, pasted marks go on the \u003Cstrong\u003Esame items\u003C/strong\u003E, so their quantities add to those items. In another takeoff (another tab, or after \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E), an item with the same name and type is used; otherwise the item is added with its assembly. If the name is taken by an item of another type, the added item is called &quot;(name) (copy)&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPasted marks go in the bid area chosen at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panel (or in none). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-areas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-areas\"\u003EBid areas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-in-place\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E) puts the copy at exactly the same spot on the open sheet, by its position on the page. Use it for a typical floor: take off level 2, copy, open level 3, paste in place.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPaste in place needs a sheet the \u003Cstrong\u003Esame size\u003C/strong\u003E as the one you copied from. On a sheet of another size TakeoffCraft says so and pastes nothing; use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E and click instead. On the sheet you copied from, the copy lands on top of the originals.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-scale-must-match\"\u003EThe scale must match\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELengths and areas depend on the sheet&#039;s scale. A run that measures 10&#039; at 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; would measure 20&#039; on a sheet at 1/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;. So TakeoffCraft pastes runs and areas only onto a sheet with the \u003Cstrong\u003Esame scale\u003C/strong\u003E as the sheet they came from, and says plainly when it does not:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe open sheet has no scale yet: give it one first (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E), then paste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe open sheet has another scale: the message names both scales. Paste on a sheet at the same scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECounts need no scale: copied counts paste anywhere.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"duplicate\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E) copies the selected marks and puts the copy on the pointer straight away; click where it goes. Nothing is added until you click, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E leaves nothing. Duplicate does not change what \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E pastes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-and-delete\"\u003ECut and delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E) copies the selected marks and deletes them. Paste them where they belong, or undo the cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E) deletes the selected marks without copying them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-an-item\"\u003ECopy an item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe item editor (under \u003Cstrong\u003EItems\u003C/strong\u003E) ends with:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy item\u003C/span\u003E: copies the item with its assembly, waste and costs, without marks. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E then adds a copy of it to this takeoff, or to another one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate item\u003C/span\u003E: adds a copy at once, named &quot;(name) (copy)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays on this computer: it is kept in this tab and in the browser&#039;s storage, so another TakeoffCraft tab can paste it. Nothing is uploaded, and the system clipboard is not used, so you cannot paste marks into other programs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA copy comes from one sheet at a time. Marks are pasted as they are: there is no rotating or mirroring a copy yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The Edit group of the toolbar holds Undo, Redo, Copy and Paste. Its More menu has the rest, each with its key: Cut, Copy, Paste, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Select all and Select none. Every one of them is one step to undo with Ctrl+Z. Select the marks Pick Select (V) and click a mark. Hold Shift and click more marks to add them. Shift + click on a selected mark takes it out again. Or press Ctrl+A (Select all) to take every mark on the open sheet. With several marks selected, the right panel shows Selected marks: how many of each item, with Copy, Duplicate, Cut and Delete marks. Corner handles show only when one mark is selected. Esc (Select none) clears the selection. Copy and paste where you click Select the marks and press Ctrl+C (Copy). The marks are copied with their items, including each item's assembly, waste and costs. Go to the sheet you want them on (it can be the same sheet). Press Ctrl+V (Paste). A dashed copy follows the pointer. Click where it goes. The middle of the copied marks lands at the pointer. Esc cancels and adds nothing. The pasted marks are selected afterwards, so Delete or Ctrl+Z takes them away again. In the same takeoff, pasted marks go on the same items, so their quantities add to those items. In another takeoff (another tab, or after New), an item with the same name and type is used; otherwise the item is added with its assembly. If the name is taken by an item of another type, the added item is called \"(name) (copy)\". Pasted marks go in the bid area chosen at the top of the Items panel (or in none). See Bid areas. Paste in place Ctrl+Shift+V (Paste in place) puts the copy at exactly the same spot on the open sheet, by its position on the page. Use it for a typical floor: take off level 2, copy, open level 3, paste in place. Paste in place needs a sheet the same size as the one you copied from. On a sheet of another size TakeoffCraft says so and pastes nothing; use Paste and click instead. On the sheet you copied from, the copy lands on top of the originals. The scale must match Lengths and areas depend on the sheet's scale. A run that measures 10' at 1/4\" = 1'-0\" would measure 20' on a sheet at 1/8\" = 1'-0\". So TakeoffCraft pastes runs and areas only onto a sheet with the same scale as the sheet they came from, and says plainly when it does not: The open sheet has no scale yet: give it one first (Set the scale), then paste. The open sheet has another scale: the message names both scales. Paste on a sheet at the same scale. Counts need no scale: copied counts paste anywhere. Duplicate Ctrl+D (Duplicate) copies the selected marks and puts the copy on the pointer straight away; click where it goes. Nothing is added until you click, and Esc leaves nothing. Duplicate does not change what Paste pastes. Cut and delete Ctrl+X (Cut) copies the selected marks and deletes them. Paste them where they belong, or undo the cut. Delete or Backspace (Delete) deletes the selected marks without copying them. Copy an item The item editor (under Items) ends with: Copy item: copies the item with its assembly, waste and costs, without marks. Paste then adds a copy of it to this takeoff, or to another one. Duplicate item: adds a copy at once, named \"(name) (copy)\". The copy stays on this computer: it is kept in this tab and in the browser's storage, so another TakeoffCraft tab can paste it. Nothing is uploaded, and the system clipboard is not used, so you cannot paste marks into other programs. A copy comes from one sheet at a time. Marks are pasted as they are: there is no rotating or mirroring a copy yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"carbon-and-5d-prices","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Embodied carbon and a 5D price table","summary":"Carbon adds up the embodied carbon of the takeoff from each item's quantity and an open factor table, and sends the takeoff's rates to BimCraft as a price table for 5D costs.","section":"tasks","order":76,"keywords":["carbon","embodied carbon","kgco2e","co2","factors","a1-a3","transport","waste","5d","price table","cost","bimcraft","epd"],"headings":[{"id":"embodied-carbon","text":"Embodied carbon","level":2},{"id":"a-price-table-for-5d-costs","text":"A price table for 5D costs","level":2},{"id":"with-your-assistant","text":"With your assistant","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECarbon\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar adds up the embodied carbon of what you have measured, and turns your rates into a price table the building model can use. It is an estimate, not an assessment.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"embodied-carbon\"\u003EEmbodied carbon\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach item&#039;s quantity, with its waste, is multiplied by a row of the factor table. A row is matched by the words of the item&#039;s name (&quot;concrete slab&quot; finds a row with the keyword concrete). The table is the same open table BimCraft uses, so a file you load here works there. \u003Cstrong\u003EThe rows that come with the program are starter values: generic, unchecked and not from any database.\u003C/strong\u003E The dialog says so beside the figures; replace them with your own source before you rely on a number.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECarbon\u003C/span\u003E. The counted items are listed with their kilograms of CO2e; the total is above.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA4 transport\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA5 site waste\u003C/span\u003E to count those stages too, for the rows that give a figure for them. The product stage is always counted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo use another row for an item, or none, use the list on its line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFactors from a file…\u003C/span\u003E loads a factor table (JSON or CSV) made from your own source; its rows replace the starter rows with the same id. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFactors (JSON)\u003C/span\u003E saves yours.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA factor is per metre, square metre, cubic metre, item or kilogram. A line measured in feet or square feet is converted. A kilogram factor needs a volume (cubic yards or metres) and the row&#039;s density. An item whose factor needs a quantity it does not measure is listed under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft out\u003C/span\u003E with the reason, never guessed. The running total in the status bar shows the same figure.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour factor rows, your choices and the stages you count are saved in the takeoff&#039;s file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-price-table-for-5d-costs\"\u003EA price table for 5D costs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E5D prices (JSON)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E5D prices (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E save the takeoff&#039;s rates as a price table: one row per priced item with its material and labour rate per unit and its waste. Load it in BimCraft&#039;s cost pane (Embodied carbon and 5D cost) to price the building model&#039;s tasks with the same rates that made the bid. Items measured in a unit the table does not have, and items with no rate, are left out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-your-assistant\"\u003EWith your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you allow assistants in Settings, they can read the carbon (carbon_check). Loading factors is yours to do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing is sent anywhere. The figures are only as good as the factors you load.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Carbon on the toolbar adds up the embodied carbon of what you have measured, and turns your rates into a price table the building model can use. It is an estimate, not an assessment. Embodied carbon Each item's quantity, with its waste, is multiplied by a row of the factor table. A row is matched by the words of the item's name (\"concrete slab\" finds a row with the keyword concrete). The table is the same open table BimCraft uses, so a file you load here works there. The rows that come with the program are starter values: generic, unchecked and not from any database. The dialog says so beside the figures; replace them with your own source before you rely on a number. Choose Carbon. The counted items are listed with their kilograms of CO2e; the total is above. Tick A4 transport or A5 site waste to count those stages too, for the rows that give a figure for them. The product stage is always counted. To use another row for an item, or none, use the list on its line. Factors from a file… loads a factor table (JSON or CSV) made from your own source; its rows replace the starter rows with the same id. Factors (JSON) saves yours. A factor is per metre, square metre, cubic metre, item or kilogram. A line measured in feet or square feet is converted. A kilogram factor needs a volume (cubic yards or metres) and the row's density. An item whose factor needs a quantity it does not measure is listed under Left out with the reason, never guessed. The running total in the status bar shows the same figure. Your factor rows, your choices and the stages you count are saved in the takeoff's file. A price table for 5D costs 5D prices (JSON) and 5D prices (CSV) save the takeoff's rates as a price table: one row per priced item with its material and labour rate per unit and its waste. Load it in BimCraft's cost pane (Embodied carbon and 5D cost) to price the building model's tasks with the same rates that made the bid. Items measured in a unit the table does not have, and items with no rate, are left out. With your assistant If you allow assistants in Settings, they can read the carbon (carbon_check). Loading factors is yours to do. Good to know Nothing is sent anywhere. The figures are only as good as the factors you load.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"tag-schedule","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Bring a tag schedule in as quantities","summary":"A tag schedule exported from the building model (CSV) becomes typed quantities in the takeoff, one per tag, so the model's counts and areas sit beside what you measured.","section":"tasks","order":77,"keywords":["tags","tag schedule","building model","bimcraft","ifc","quantities","csv","schedule","import","typed quantity"],"headings":[{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETags\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar brings in a tag schedule that BimCraft saved as a CSV file. Each distinct tag becomes a typed quantity, the same kind CivilCraft&#039;s cut and fill become.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn BimCraft, make the tag schedule you want and save it as CSV.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETags\u003C/span\u003E and pick the file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESay which column names the line (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName the line by\u003C/span\u003E, usually the tag) and which holds the number (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantity from\u003C/span\u003E). With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount the rows\u003C/span\u003E each row counts as one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the unit. A length in millimetres becomes metres with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMultiply by\u003C/span\u003E 0.001.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd the quantities\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERows with the same tag are added together. A cell that is not a number is left out and counted in the summary. Bringing the same schedule again updates the quantities you already have instead of adding more; their names, rates and waste stay yours.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA typed quantity is not measured on a sheet, so it has no marks. Nothing is sent anywhere; the file is read here.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Tags on the toolbar brings in a tag schedule that BimCraft saved as a CSV file. Each distinct tag becomes a typed quantity, the same kind CivilCraft's cut and fill become. In BimCraft, make the tag schedule you want and save it as CSV. Choose Tags and pick the file. Say which column names the line (Name the line by, usually the tag) and which holds the number (Quantity from). With Count the rows each row counts as one. Choose the unit. A length in millimetres becomes metres with Multiply by 0.001. Add the quantities. Rows with the same tag are added together. A cell that is not a number is left out and counted in the summary. Bringing the same schedule again updates the quantities you already have instead of adding more; their names, rates and waste stay yours. Good to know A typed quantity is not measured on a sheet, so it has no marks. Nothing is sent anywhere; the file is read here.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"assemblies","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Assemblies and formulas","summary":"Add components to an item and let formulas turn its length, area or count into studs, plates, sheets of board, concrete and hardware, with a worked wall example.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["assembly","components","formula","studs","drywall","sheets","plates","concrete","cubic yards","hardware","recipe","typical","item parts"],"headings":[{"id":"variables-you-can-use","text":"Variables you can use","level":2},{"id":"worked-example-an-interior-wall","text":"Worked example: an interior wall","level":2},{"id":"more-formulas","text":"More formulas","level":2},{"id":"when-a-formula-is-wrong","text":"When a formula is wrong","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2},{"id":"remove-a-component","text":"Remove a component","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn item&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Eassembly\u003C/strong\u003E is a list of components, each with a quantity formula and its own waste and rates. The formula uses the item&#039;s measured totals, so one takeoff of a wall gives you studs, plates and board at once, and they update as you measure.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"variables-you-can-use\"\u003EVariables you can use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EVariables\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E length, \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E height, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E wall area (L × H), \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E number of runs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E area, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E perimeter (outline + cut-outs), \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E depth or thickness, \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E number of areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E count\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EValues are in the item&#039;s own units: feet and square feet for LF and SF items, meters and square meters for m and m² items. For an SY item, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E is in square yards while \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E stay in feet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFormulas use \u003Ccode\u003E+ - * /\u003C/code\u003E (or × ÷), parentheses, numbers, and the functions \u003Ccode\u003Eceil\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Efloor\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eround\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emin\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emax\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eabs\u003C/code\u003E. The full rules are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/formulas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/formulas\"\u003EFormula language\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"worked-example-an-interior-wall\"\u003EWorked example: an interior wall\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESay you have taken off \u003Cstrong\u003EInterior wall type A\u003C/strong\u003E as a linear item: 4 runs totaling 120 LF, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E set to \u003Ccode\u003E9&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. Its \u003Cstrong\u003EMeasured\u003C/strong\u003E values read L = 120, N = 4, A = 1,080 (SF of wall, one face).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd component\u003C/span\u003E. A component called &quot;Component&quot; appears, with the formula \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and its result below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EStuds 16 in o.c.\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EEA\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantity formula\u003C/span\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(L * 12 / 16) + N\u003C/code\u003E. The result updates as you type: &quot;= 94 EA&quot;. That is one stud every 16 inches along 120 feet (90), plus one extra to close each of the 4 runs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd component\u003C/span\u003E again for the plates: name \u003Ccode\u003EPlates, top and bottom\u003C/code\u003E, unit \u003Ccode\u003ELF\u003C/code\u003E, formula \u003Ccode\u003EL * 2\u003C/code\u003E. Result: 240 LF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the board: name \u003Ccode\u003EGypsum board 4x8, both sides\u003C/code\u003E, unit \u003Ccode\u003Esheets\u003C/code\u003E, formula \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(A * 2 / 32)\u003C/code\u003E. Result: 68 sheets (2,160 SF of board ÷ 32 SF per sheet = 67.5, rounded up).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in each component&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWaste %\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial $\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELabor $\u003C/span\u003E. Each component gets its own row under the item in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E, priced like an item, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJob total\u003C/span\u003E includes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the wall cost is all in the components, leave the item&#039;s own material and labor rates at 0, or it is counted twice.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWaste is applied after the formula and is not rounded: 68 sheets with 10% waste is 74.8 sheets. To order whole sheets, put the waste inside the formula, like \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(A * 2 / 32 * 1.1)\u003C/code\u003E, and leave the component&#039;s waste at 0.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-formulas\"\u003EMore formulas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormula\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStuds at 24 in on center\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(L / 2) + N\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInsulation batts, SF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear with height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaint, both sides, gallons at 350 SF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear with height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(A * 2 / 350)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETile cartons at 15 SF, 10% extra\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(A * 1.1 / 15)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBase or edge trim, LF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConcrete, cubic yards\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea with depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA * H / 27\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConcrete rounded up to a quarter yard\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea with depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(A * H / 27 * 4) / 4\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHinges, three per door\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN * 3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAt least one box, one per 12\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emax(1, ceil(N / 12))\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-formula-is-wrong\"\u003EWhen a formula is wrong\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line under the formula turns into an error message, for example \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;X&quot; is not a measured value here. Use L, H, A, N.\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EA &quot;)&quot; is missing.\u003C/code\u003E The component then has no amounts, its row in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E shows &quot;Formula error: …&quot;, and the header says &quot;Incomplete: 1 formula error (not in the totals)&quot;. Fix the formula and the totals update.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA formula that divides by zero is an error too (&quot;The formula divides by zero.&quot;), for example \u003Ccode\u003EA / H\u003C/code\u003E on an item with no depth.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFormulas run on the item&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Etotals\u003C/strong\u003E, not run by run. \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(L * 12 / 16) + N\u003C/code\u003E adds one stud per run; there is no per-run rounding yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA formula can only use its own item&#039;s variables, not another item&#039;s.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUp to 50 components per item, 500 characters per formula.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"remove-a-component\"\u003ERemove a component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove component\u003C/span\u003E under it. You can undo it with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"An item's assembly is a list of components, each with a quantity formula and its own waste and rates. The formula uses the item's measured totals, so one takeoff of a wall gives you studs, plates and board at once, and they update as you measure. Variables you can use Item type Variables Linear L length, H height, A wall area (L × H), N number of runs Area A area, P perimeter (outline + cut-outs), H depth or thickness, N number of areas Count N count Values are in the item's own units: feet and square feet for LF and SF items, meters and square meters for m and m² items. For an SY item, A is in square yards while P and H stay in feet. Formulas use + - * / (or × ÷), parentheses, numbers, and the functions ceil, floor, round, min, max and abs. The full rules are in Formula language. Worked example: an interior wall Say you have taken off Interior wall type A as a linear item: 4 runs totaling 120 LF, with Height set to 9'-0\". Its Measured values read L = 120, N = 4, A = 1,080 (SF of wall, one face). Select the item under Items. In Assembly, choose Add component. A component called \"Component\" appears, with the formula L and its result below. Type Studs 16 in o.c. in Name and EA in Unit. In Quantity formula, type ceil(L * 12 / 16) + N. The result updates as you type: \"= 94 EA\". That is one stud every 16 inches along 120 feet (90), plus one extra to close each of the 4 runs. Choose Add component again for the plates: name Plates, top and bottom, unit LF, formula L * 2. Result: 240 LF. Add the board: name Gypsum board 4x8, both sides, unit sheets, formula ceil(A * 2 / 32). Result: 68 sheets (2,160 SF of board ÷ 32 SF per sheet = 67.5, rounded up). Fill in each component's Waste %, Material $ and Labor $. Each component gets its own row under the item in Quantities, priced like an item, and the Job total includes it. If the wall cost is all in the components, leave the item's own material and labor rates at 0, or it is counted twice. Waste is applied after the formula and is not rounded: 68 sheets with 10% waste is 74.8 sheets. To order whole sheets, put the waste inside the formula, like ceil(A * 2 / 32 * 1.1), and leave the component's waste at 0. More formulas What Item Formula Studs at 24 in on center Linear ceil(L / 2) + N Insulation batts, SF Linear with height A Paint, both sides, gallons at 350 SF Linear with height ceil(A * 2 / 350) Tile cartons at 15 SF, 10% extra Area ceil(A * 1.1 / 15) Base or edge trim, LF Area P Concrete, cubic yards Area with depth A * H / 27 Concrete rounded up to a quarter yard Area with depth ceil(A * H / 27 * 4) / 4 Hinges, three per door Count N * 3 At least one box, one per 12 Count max(1, ceil(N / 12)) When a formula is wrong The line under the formula turns into an error message, for example \"X\" is not a measured value here. Use L, H, A, N. or A \")\" is missing. The component then has no amounts, its row in Quantities shows \"Formula error: …\", and the header says \"Incomplete: 1 formula error (not in the totals)\". Fix the formula and the totals update. A formula that divides by zero is an error too (\"The formula divides by zero.\"), for example A / H on an item with no depth. Limits Formulas run on the item's totals, not run by run. ceil(L * 12 / 16) + N adds one stud per run; there is no per-run rounding yet. A formula can only use its own item's variables, not another item's. Up to 50 components per item, 500 characters per formula. Remove a component Choose Remove component under it. You can undo it with Ctrl+Z.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"price-library","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Price library","summary":"Keep your items, rates and assemblies in a library, import it from a CSV or a past estimate, add items from it to any takeoff, and update a takeoff's prices from it.","section":"tasks","order":82,"keywords":["library","trade packs","price list","price book","cost database","import csv","rates","assemblies","reuse","template items","update prices","reprice"],"headings":[{"id":"fill-the-library","text":"Fill the library","level":2},{"id":"use-it-in-a-takeoff","text":"Use it in a takeoff","level":2},{"id":"the-csv-columns","text":"The CSV columns","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003Eprice library\u003C/strong\u003E holds items you use on job after job: their type, unit, height, waste, material and labor rates, and their assembly. It is kept in this browser, apart from any takeoff, so every takeoff you open here can use it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-the-library\"\u003EFill the library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport CSV…\u003C/span\u003E reads a spreadsheet saved as CSV (see the columns below). Items whose name and type are already in the library are replaced; the rest are added. Rows it cannot read are skipped, and it says which and why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd this takeoff&#039;s items\u003C/span\u003E copies every item of the open takeoff into the library, with its rates and assembly (not its marks).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe estimate CSV TakeoffCraft exports (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-estimate\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-estimate\"\u003EExport the estimate\u003C/a\u003E) imports as it is: last job&#039;s prices seed the next one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrade packs…\u003C/span\u003E installs free assemblies for a trade, such as drywall, electrical or concrete, without prices. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/trade-packs\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/trade-packs\"\u003ETrade packs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport CSV\u003C/span\u003E saves the library as a CSV, to keep a copy, to edit it in a spreadsheet or to give it to a colleague. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E empties it (click twice).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe library lives in this browser&#039;s storage. Another browser, another computer or a private window has its own (or none). Export it to move it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-it-in-a-takeoff\"\u003EUse it in a takeoff\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick items (type in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind in the library\u003C/span\u003E to narrow the list) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd to takeoff\u003C/span\u003E. They are added with their assemblies in one step that \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back. A name already in the takeoff gets a number, like \u003Ccode\u003ECarpet (2)\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate prices\u003C/span\u003E gives the takeoff&#039;s items the library&#039;s waste and rates, for every item whose name and type match a library item, and the same for assembly components of the same name. Heights, units and formulas stay as they are. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts all the old prices back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-csv-columns\"\u003EThe CSV columns\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first row names the columns, in any order; other columns are ignored.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat goes in it\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem (or Name)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s name. Required.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear, Area or Count (LF, SF and EA work too). Left empty, the unit decides.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF or m; SF, SY or m²; EA. Empty: the type&#039;s usual unit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWaste %\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E10%\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial per unit, Labor per unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERates like \u003Ccode\u003E2.10\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E$1,250\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHeight (or Depth)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E9&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E9\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003E2.7\u003C/code\u003E for metric units.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EComponent, Formula\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA row with a Component name (or the Type \u003Ccode\u003EComponent\u003C/code\u003E) is part of the assembly of the item above it. Its Unit, Waste and rates are its own; its Formula uses the item&#039;s letters, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EItem,Type,Unit,Waste %,Material per unit,Labor per unit,Height,Component,Formula\nPartition type A,Linear,LF,10,2.10,3.33,9&#039;-0&quot;,,\n,Component,EA,,4.25,,,Studs 16 in. o.c.,ceil(L / 1.333) + N\nVCT flooring,Area,SF,5,1.85,1.10,,,\nDuplex outlets,Count,EA,,3.50,45,,,\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECommas, semicolons or tabs between columns all work.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The price library holds items you use on job after job: their type, unit, height, waste, material and labor rates, and their assembly. It is kept in this browser, apart from any takeoff, so every takeoff you open here can use it. Open it with Library at the top of the Items panel. Fill the library Import CSV… reads a spreadsheet saved as CSV (see the columns below). Items whose name and type are already in the library are replaced; the rest are added. Rows it cannot read are skipped, and it says which and why. Add this takeoff's items copies every item of the open takeoff into the library, with its rates and assembly (not its marks). The estimate CSV TakeoffCraft exports (Export the estimate) imports as it is: last job's prices seed the next one. Trade packs… installs free assemblies for a trade, such as drywall, electrical or concrete, without prices. See Trade packs. Export CSV saves the library as a CSV, to keep a copy, to edit it in a spreadsheet or to give it to a colleague. Clear empties it (click twice). The library lives in this browser's storage. Another browser, another computer or a private window has its own (or none). Export it to move it. Use it in a takeoff Tick items (type in Find in the library to narrow the list) and choose Add to takeoff. They are added with their assemblies in one step that Ctrl+Z takes back. A name already in the takeoff gets a number, like Carpet (2). Update prices gives the takeoff's items the library's waste and rates, for every item whose name and type match a library item, and the same for assembly components of the same name. Heights, units and formulas stay as they are. One Ctrl+Z puts all the old prices back. The CSV columns The first row names the columns, in any order; other columns are ignored. Column What goes in it Item (or Name) The item's name. Required. Type Linear, Area or Count (LF, SF and EA work too). Left empty, the unit decides. Unit LF or m; SF, SY or m²; EA. Empty: the type's usual unit. Waste % 10 or 10%. Material per unit, Labor per unit Rates like 2.10 or $1,250. Height (or Depth) 9'-0\", 9, or 2.7 for metric units. Component, Formula A row with a Component name (or the Type Component) is part of the assembly of the item above it. Its Unit, Waste and rates are its own; its Formula uses the item's letters, see Assemblies and formulas. Item,Type,Unit,Waste %,Material per unit,Labor per unit,Height,Component,Formula Partition type A,Linear,LF,10,2.10,3.33,9'-0\",, ,Component,EA,,4.25,,,Studs 16 in. o.c.,ceil(L / 1.333) + N VCT flooring,Area,SF,5,1.85,1.10,,, Duplex outlets,Count,EA,,3.50,45,,, Commas, semicolons or tabs between columns all work.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"trade-packs","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Trade packs","summary":"Install free assemblies for a trade into your price library, remove them in one step, and share your own library as a pack.","section":"tasks","order":83,"keywords":["trade pack","pack","assemblies","templates","starter items","electrical","drywall","framing","painting","concrete","flooring","masonry","roofing","siding","carpentry","plumbing","hvac","insulation","landscape","decking","general contractor","csi","masterformat","cc0","install","remove","share"],"headings":[{"id":"install-a-pack","text":"Install a pack","level":2},{"id":"remove-a-pack","text":"Remove a pack","level":2},{"id":"what-is-in-a-pack","text":"What is in a pack","level":2},{"id":"share-your-library-as-a-pack","text":"Share your library as a pack","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Etrade pack\u003C/strong\u003E is a set of ready-made items for one trade: each with its unit, its height or depth, its waste and its \u003Cstrong\u003Eassembly\u003C/strong\u003E, the parts and formulas that turn a measured run, area or count into materials. A drywall partition, for example, brings its track, studs, sheets, screws, tape and compound.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPacks install into your \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/price-library\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/price-library\"\u003Eprice library\u003C/a\u003E, and from there you add their items to any takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"install-a-pack\"\u003EInstall a pack\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panel, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrade packs…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E: imperial (LF, SF), metric (m, m²) or both. Every assembly comes in both systems.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInstall\u003C/span\u003E beside a pack.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pack&#039;s items appear in the library, tagged with the pack&#039;s name. To use them, tick them in the library and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd to takeoff\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA pack brings no prices.\u003C/strong\u003E Its items show &quot;set your rate&quot; in the library. Rates are yours: type them in the library or the item editor, or import your price list as a CSV and use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate prices\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you already have an item of the same name and type in your library, the pack leaves yours as it is. Installing a pack again replaces its own items with the new version.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"remove-a-pack\"\u003ERemove a pack\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrade packs…\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E beside an installed pack, then once more to confirm. Every item that came from it leaves the library in one step. Items you already added to a takeoff stay in that takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-a-pack\"\u003EWhat is in a pack\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESources and details\u003C/span\u003E under a pack to see what each assembly was written from: building codes, standards and trade associations&#039; installation guides. In the library, hold the pointer over an item to read its sources and its assumptions, such as stud spacing or a paint&#039;s spread rate. Check them against your job and your products&#039; data sheets, and change the formulas where they differ (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery pack is marked \u003Cstrong\u003EStarter: not reviewed by an estimator yet\u003C/strong\u003E until working estimators have reviewed it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach item also carries its CSI MasterFormat number, such as \u003Ccode\u003E09 21 16\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-your-library-as-a-pack\"\u003EShare your library as a pack\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport as pack…\u003C/span\u003E in the library saves it as a pack file (\u003Ccode\u003E.ccpack.json\u003C/code\u003E) that anyone can install with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a pack file…\u003C/span\u003E. By default it leaves out the items that came from installed packs, and it carries no rates. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInclude my rates as sample rates\u003C/span\u003E to send your rates too: they go out labelled with the region and the date you give, and whoever installs the pack chooses whether to use them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPack files are plain data, never programs. TakeoffCraft checks every part of one before it uses it and leaves out anything it cannot read.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A trade pack is a set of ready-made items for one trade: each with its unit, its height or depth, its waste and its assembly, the parts and formulas that turn a measured run, area or count into materials. A drywall partition, for example, brings its track, studs, sheets, screws, tape and compound. Packs install into your price library, and from there you add their items to any takeoff. Install a pack Open Library at the top of the Items panel, then Trade packs…. Choose the Units: imperial (LF, SF), metric (m, m²) or both. Every assembly comes in both systems. Choose Install beside a pack. The pack's items appear in the library, tagged with the pack's name. To use them, tick them in the library and choose Add to takeoff. A pack brings no prices. Its items show \"set your rate\" in the library. Rates are yours: type them in the library or the item editor, or import your price list as a CSV and use Update prices. If you already have an item of the same name and type in your library, the pack leaves yours as it is. Installing a pack again replaces its own items with the new version. Remove a pack In Trade packs…, choose Remove beside an installed pack, then once more to confirm. Every item that came from it leaves the library in one step. Items you already added to a takeoff stay in that takeoff. What is in a pack Choose Sources and details under a pack to see what each assembly was written from: building codes, standards and trade associations' installation guides. In the library, hold the pointer over an item to read its sources and its assumptions, such as stud spacing or a paint's spread rate. Check them against your job and your products' data sheets, and change the formulas where they differ (see Assemblies and formulas). Every pack is marked Starter: not reviewed by an estimator yet until working estimators have reviewed it. Each item also carries its CSI MasterFormat number, such as 09 21 16. Share your library as a pack Export as pack… in the library saves it as a pack file (.ccpack.json) that anyone can install with Open a pack file…. By default it leaves out the items that came from installed packs, and it carries no rates. Tick Include my rates as sample rates to send your rates too: they go out labelled with the region and the date you give, and whoever installs the pack chooses whether to use them. Pack files are plain data, never programs. TakeoffCraft checks every part of one before it uses it and leaves out anything it cannot read.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"earthwork","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Earthwork and spot elevations","summary":"Mark spot elevations on a site plan, hand them to CivilCraft for the surface and cut and fill, and take simple excavation quantities here.","section":"tasks","order":84,"keywords":["earthwork","excavation","cut","fill","cut and fill","grading","spot elevation","elevations","topo","surface","triangulation","civilcraft","points","pnezd","trench"],"headings":[{"id":"simple-excavation-here","text":"Simple excavation here","level":2},{"id":"spot-elevations-for-civilcraft","text":"Spot elevations for CivilCraft","level":2},{"id":"cut-and-fill-from-civilcraft","text":"Cut and fill from CivilCraft","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft measures what is drawn on a sheet. Earthwork needs a ground surface, so it is shared with CivilCraft, which builds surfaces from points, grades them and works out cut and fill and haul.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"simple-excavation-here\"\u003ESimple excavation here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a footprint dug to one depth, an area item with a \u003Cstrong\u003Edepth\u003C/strong\u003E is enough: the volume is area × depth. The general contractor \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/trade-packs\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/trade-packs\"\u003Etrade pack\u003C/a\u003E has a bulk excavation item (bank volume, loose volume and truck loads) and a utility trench item (excavation, bedding and backfill by length and depth).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"spot-elevations-for-civilcraft\"\u003ESpot elevations for CivilCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the site sheet&#039;s scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake a \u003Cstrong\u003ECount\u003C/strong\u003E item, for example &quot;Existing grade&quot;, and click each spot elevation on the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a mark and type its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E in the Selected mark panel: \u003Ccode\u003E102.5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E102&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E31.24 m\u003C/code\u003E. Negative and zero elevations are fine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E menu and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpot elevations\u003C/span\u003E. You get a points file (\u003Ccode\u003E.csv\u003C/code\u003E) with Point, Northing, Easting, Elevation and Description, one row per marked point of the open sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen that file in CivilCraft (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/import-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/import-points\"\u003EImport points\u003C/a\u003E). It builds the surface from the points (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/build-surface\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/build-surface\"\u003EBuild a surface\u003C/a\u003E); from there it compares surfaces and gives cut and fill (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/volumes\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/volumes\"\u003EVolumes\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENorthing and easting are the sheet&#039;s own coordinates in feet, measured from the page&#039;s corner at its scale: distances and volumes between the points are true, but they are not your site&#039;s survey coordinates. Move them onto the site&#039;s coordinates in CivilCraft when you need to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoints from different sheets do not share coordinates, so the export takes the open sheet only. Elevations are saved with the takeoff and carried by copy and paste.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-and-fill-from-civilcraft\"\u003ECut and fill from CivilCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft&#039;s earthwork comes back as two items you can price:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CivilCraft, grade your pads and corridors, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend to TakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/volumes\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/volumes\"\u003EEarthwork volumes\u003C/a\u003E). It saves a quantities file (\u003Ccode\u003E.quantities.json\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn TakeoffCraft, open it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen takeoff\u003C/span\u003E, or drop it on the app. Your takeoff stays open: the file adds \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork: cut\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork: fill\u003C/strong\u003E as \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items#typed-quantities-no-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-hash=\"typed-quantities-no-drawing\"\u003Etyped quantities\u003C/a\u003E in cubic yards (CY) for a feet project, or cubic metres (m³) for a metre project. It is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERename them, set waste, rates and assemblies as you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the design changes, send again from CivilCraft and open the new file: the same two items get the new quantities. Their names, rates, waste and assemblies stay as you set them. If the project&#039;s unit changed, the status bar says so: check the rates. A file from another CivilCraft project adds its own two items.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe quantities are bank volumes, as CivilCraft measured them (grid method): no shrink or swell. Add those as waste, or with a formula such as \u003Ccode\u003EQ * 1.25\u003C/code\u003E for loose volume. The item editor shows where each one came from and when; the estimate&#039;s notes column says it too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe quantities file is plain JSON (\u003Ccode\u003E&quot;format&quot;: &quot;cadcraft-quantities&quot;\u003C/code\u003E), so a script or another program can write one too: an app name, a project, and items with a key, a name, a unit and a quantity.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft measures what is drawn on a sheet. Earthwork needs a ground surface, so it is shared with CivilCraft, which builds surfaces from points, grades them and works out cut and fill and haul. Simple excavation here For a footprint dug to one depth, an area item with a depth is enough: the volume is area × depth. The general contractor trade pack has a bulk excavation item (bank volume, loose volume and truck loads) and a utility trench item (excavation, bedding and backfill by length and depth). Spot elevations for CivilCraft Set the site sheet's scale. Make a Count item, for example \"Existing grade\", and click each spot elevation on the sheet. Select a mark and type its Elevation in the Selected mark panel: 102.5, 102'-6\" or 31.24 m. Negative and zero elevations are fine. Open the Export menu and choose Spot elevations. You get a points file (.csv) with Point, Northing, Easting, Elevation and Description, one row per marked point of the open sheet. Open that file in CivilCraft (see Import points). It builds the surface from the points (Build a surface); from there it compares surfaces and gives cut and fill (Volumes). Northing and easting are the sheet's own coordinates in feet, measured from the page's corner at its scale: distances and volumes between the points are true, but they are not your site's survey coordinates. Move them onto the site's coordinates in CivilCraft when you need to. Points from different sheets do not share coordinates, so the export takes the open sheet only. Elevations are saved with the takeoff and carried by copy and paste. Cut and fill from CivilCraft CivilCraft's earthwork comes back as two items you can price: In CivilCraft, grade your pads and corridors, then choose Send to TakeoffCraft under Earthwork (Earthwork volumes). It saves a quantities file (.quantities.json). In TakeoffCraft, open it with Open takeoff, or drop it on the app. Your takeoff stays open: the file adds Earthwork: cut and Earthwork: fill as typed quantities in cubic yards (CY) for a feet project, or cubic metres (m³) for a metre project. It is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. Rename them, set waste, rates and assemblies as you like. When the design changes, send again from CivilCraft and open the new file: the same two items get the new quantities. Their names, rates, waste and assemblies stay as you set them. If the project's unit changed, the status bar says so: check the rates. A file from another CivilCraft project adds its own two items. The quantities are bank volumes, as CivilCraft measured them (grid method): no shrink or swell. Add those as waste, or with a formula such as Q * 1.25 for loose volume. The item editor shows where each one came from and when; the estimate's notes column says it too. The quantities file is plain JSON (\"format\": \"cadcraft-quantities\"), so a script or another program can write one too: an app name, a project, and items with a key, a name, a unit and a quantity.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"bid-areas","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Bid areas","summary":"Tag marks by building, floor or phase, take off into one area at a time, and read quantities and costs for each area.","section":"tasks","order":85,"keywords":["add alternate","deduct alternate","base bid","alternates","bid area","area","building","floor","level","phase","zone","alternate","breakdown","split","typical floor","subtotal","by area"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-bid-area","text":"Make a bid area","level":2},{"id":"take-off-into-an-area","text":"Take off into an area","level":2},{"id":"move-marks-to-another-area","text":"Move marks to another area","level":2},{"id":"rename-reorder-or-delete","text":"Rename, reorder or delete","level":2},{"id":"read-the-quantities-by-area","text":"Read the quantities by area","level":2},{"id":"alternates-base-bid-plus-adds-and-deducts","text":"Alternates: base bid plus adds and deducts","level":2},{"id":"in-the-export","text":"In the export","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA bid area is a part of the job you price on its own: a building, a floor, a phase, a wing. Each mark can be in one bid area. The quantities and the estimate can then be read for each area, with a subtotal for each, while the job total stays the sum of everything.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-bid-area\"\u003EMake a bid area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panel, open the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid area\u003C/span\u003E list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003ENew bid area…\u003C/strong\u003E, type a name such as &quot;Building A&quot; or &quot;Level 2&quot;, and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EMake it\u003C/strong\u003E. The new area becomes the current one. The status bar says &quot;New marks go in (name). Marks in other bid areas are dimmed.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-off-into-an-area\"\u003ETake off into an area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a bid area is chosen in the list, every new mark goes in it: runs, areas, circles, counts, the marks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E adds, and what you paste. Marks in other bid areas, and marks in none, are dimmed on the sheet so you can see what belongs where.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003ENo bid area\u003C/strong\u003E in the list to take off without one; nothing is dimmed then.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETypical floors: take off Level 2, select its marks (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on the sheet), copy them, choose Level 3 in the list and paste. The copies go in Level 3. When the floors are the same and you do not need them apart, a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/typical-groups\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/typical-groups\"\u003Etypical group\u003C/a\u003E counts one floor several times instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-marks-to-another-area\"\u003EMove marks to another area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect one mark or several (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected marks\u003C/strong\u003E), pick the area in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid area\u003C/span\u003E field. The status bar says &quot;3 marks moved to Level 2.&quot; It is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rename-reorder-or-delete\"\u003ERename, reorder or delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EManage bid areas…\u003C/strong\u003E in the list. Type over a name to rename it, use the arrows to change the order (the tables and the workbook follow it), or delete one with the bin. Deleting a bid area keeps its marks: they are then in no bid area. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes each change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-quantities-by-area\"\u003ERead the quantities by area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy area\u003C/span\u003E (it shows once the takeoff has a bid area). Each area lists the items it holds, with their quantity, unit and total, then a subtotal row. Marks in no bid area come last, as \u003Cstrong\u003ENo bid area\u003C/strong\u003E. Click an item row to highlight the item and make that area the current one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssemblies are worked out for each area from that area&#039;s own totals. A formula that rounds up, such as \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(L / 4)\u003C/code\u003E, can then give a little more in the areas together than for the whole job, as it would on a bid priced area by area.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"alternates-base-bid-plus-adds-and-deducts\"\u003EAlternates: base bid plus adds and deducts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA bid area can be an \u003Cstrong\u003Ealternate\u003C/strong\u003E: work the owner may add to the bid, or take out of it. In \u003Cstrong\u003EManage bid areas…\u003C/strong\u003E, each area has a list next to its name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoice\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBase bid\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn the bid, as usual.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd alternate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPriced on its own and \u003Cstrong\u003Eleft out of the base bid\u003C/strong\u003E: a carpet upgrade, an extra canopy.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct alternate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn the base bid, and offered as a deduction: &quot;delete the canopy&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETake off an alternate&#039;s work into its bid area like any other area. Once the takeoff has an alternate:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe header of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table says \u003Cstrong\u003EBase bid\u003C/strong\u003E instead of Job total: the whole takeoff without the add alternates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy area\u003C/span\u003E ends with the base bid and one row per alternate: \u003Cstrong\u003E+$300.00\u003C/strong\u003E for an add, \u003Cstrong\u003E−$1,050.00\u003C/strong\u003E for a deduct.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn alternate prices only the items that have marks in it, with their assemblies, so an assembly part of another item does not creep in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the exports, the CSV ends with the base bid and the alternates, and the workbook gets an \u003Cstrong\u003EAlternates\u003C/strong\u003E worksheet: the base bid, each alternate&#039;s items and its change, and the base bid with every add alternate taken.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-export\"\u003EIn the export\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E adds a \u003Cstrong\u003EBy bid area\u003C/strong\u003E worksheet: each area&#039;s items and components with quantity, quantity with waste, material, labor and total, a subtotal per area (a \u003Ccode\u003ESUM\u003C/code\u003E formula) and \u003Cstrong\u003EAll bid areas\u003C/strong\u003E at the end. The \u003Cstrong\u003EMarks\u003C/strong\u003E worksheet gets a \u003Cstrong\u003EBid area\u003C/strong\u003E column. A takeoff without bid areas exports as before. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-columns\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-columns\"\u003EExport columns\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe bid areas are saved in the project file with the marks.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A bid area is a part of the job you price on its own: a building, a floor, a phase, a wing. Each mark can be in one bid area. The quantities and the estimate can then be read for each area, with a subtotal for each, while the job total stays the sum of everything. Make a bid area At the top of the Items panel, open the Bid area list. Choose New bid area…, type a name such as \"Building A\" or \"Level 2\", and choose Make it. The new area becomes the current one. The status bar says \"New marks go in (name). Marks in other bid areas are dimmed.\" Take off into an area While a bid area is chosen in the list, every new mark goes in it: runs, areas, circles, counts, the marks Auto count adds, and what you paste. Marks in other bid areas, and marks in none, are dimmed on the sheet so you can see what belongs where. Choose No bid area in the list to take off without one; nothing is dimmed then. Typical floors: take off Level 2, select its marks (Ctrl+A on the sheet), copy them, choose Level 3 in the list and paste. The copies go in Level 3. When the floors are the same and you do not need them apart, a typical group counts one floor several times instead. Move marks to another area Select one mark or several (Shift + click, or Ctrl+A). Under Selected mark (or Selected marks), pick the area in the Bid area field. The status bar says \"3 marks moved to Level 2.\" It is one undo step. Rename, reorder or delete Choose Manage bid areas… in the list. Type over a name to rename it, use the arrows to change the order (the tables and the workbook follow it), or delete one with the bin. Deleting a bid area keeps its marks: they are then in no bid area. Ctrl+Z undoes each change. Read the quantities by area In the Quantities table, choose By area (it shows once the takeoff has a bid area). Each area lists the items it holds, with their quantity, unit and total, then a subtotal row. Marks in no bid area come last, as No bid area. Click an item row to highlight the item and make that area the current one. Assemblies are worked out for each area from that area's own totals. A formula that rounds up, such as ceil(L / 4), can then give a little more in the areas together than for the whole job, as it would on a bid priced area by area. Alternates: base bid plus adds and deducts A bid area can be an alternate: work the owner may add to the bid, or take out of it. In Manage bid areas…, each area has a list next to its name: Choice What it does Base bid In the bid, as usual. Add alternate Priced on its own and left out of the base bid: a carpet upgrade, an extra canopy. Deduct alternate In the base bid, and offered as a deduction: \"delete the canopy\". Take off an alternate's work into its bid area like any other area. Once the takeoff has an alternate: The header of the Quantities table says Base bid instead of Job total: the whole takeoff without the add alternates. By area ends with the base bid and one row per alternate: +$300.00 for an add, −$1,050.00 for a deduct. An alternate prices only the items that have marks in it, with their assemblies, so an assembly part of another item does not creep in. In the exports, the CSV ends with the base bid and the alternates, and the workbook gets an Alternates worksheet: the base bid, each alternate's items and its change, and the base bid with every add alternate taken. In the export XLSX adds a By bid area worksheet: each area's items and components with quantity, quantity with waste, material, labor and total, a subtotal per area (a SUM formula) and All bid areas at the end. The Marks worksheet gets a Bid area column. A takeoff without bid areas exports as before. See Export columns. The bid areas are saved in the project file with the marks.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"typical-groups","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Typical groups","summary":"Take off a typical unit, room or floor once and count it as many times as it repeats, change the count later, and see which marks are typical.","section":"tasks","order":86,"keywords":["typical","typical group","repeat","repeat count","multiply","times","unit","hotel room","apartment","typical floor","x24","copies"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-typical-group","text":"Make a typical group","level":2},{"id":"change-it-later","text":"Change it later","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA building often repeats: 24 identical hotel rooms, 6 typical floors, 40 parking bays with the same striping. A \u003Cstrong\u003Etypical group\u003C/strong\u003E lets you take off one of them and count it as many times as it repeats, without drawing the copies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-typical-group\"\u003EMake a typical group\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETake off one unit: its walls, its floor, its outlets — any items.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect its marks with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E tool: click one, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click the others (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/copy-paste#select-the-marks\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/copy-paste\" data-hash=\"select-the-marks\"\u003ESelect the marks\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETypical\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew typical group…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name (\u003Ccode\u003EUnit A\u003C/code\u003E) and how many times it repeats (\u003Ccode\u003E24\u003C/code\u003E), then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake group\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery quantity of those marks now counts 24 times: a 10 ft wall run gives 240 ft, an 80 SF floor 1,920 SF, two outlets 48. Costs follow, and assembly formulas see the repeated totals (\u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E counts each run 24 times too).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the sheet, a dashed box goes round the group&#039;s marks with its name and count, like \u003Cstrong\u003EUnit A ×24\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-it-later\"\u003EChange it later\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe groups are listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ETypical groups\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange the number after \u003Cstrong\u003E×\u003C/strong\u003E to change how many times it counts (1 to 10,000). Every quantity updates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERename it in its name box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe box icon selects its marks (and shows their sheet).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe bin deletes the group. Its marks stay, counted once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo add marks to a group or take them out, select them and pick the group (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot typical (×1)\u003C/span\u003E) under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETypical\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA group&#039;s marks can be on several sheets, and of any items. Each mark is in one group at most.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopies you paste are not in the group: a pasted copy of a typical unit would otherwise count 24 times again. Use a group for &quot;the same thing N times&quot;, and copy and paste for the copies you draw.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe marks are drawn once, so the 3D view and the marked PDF show one unit; the quantities, the exports and the per-sheet table count it ×N. The \u003Cstrong\u003EMarks\u003C/strong\u003E sheet of the Excel export says which marks are typical and how many times they count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith bid areas, a group&#039;s marks count ×N in their own bid area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A building often repeats: 24 identical hotel rooms, 6 typical floors, 40 parking bays with the same striping. A typical group lets you take off one of them and count it as many times as it repeats, without drawing the copies. Make a typical group Take off one unit: its walls, its floor, its outlets — any items. Select its marks with the Select tool: click one, then Shift + click the others (Select the marks). Under Selected mark, open Typical and choose New typical group…. Give it a name (Unit A) and how many times it repeats (24), then choose Make group. Every quantity of those marks now counts 24 times: a 10 ft wall run gives 240 ft, an 80 SF floor 1,920 SF, two outlets 48. Costs follow, and assembly formulas see the repeated totals (N counts each run 24 times too). On the sheet, a dashed box goes round the group's marks with its name and count, like Unit A ×24. Change it later The groups are listed under Typical groups in the right panel: Change the number after × to change how many times it counts (1 to 10,000). Every quantity updates. Rename it in its name box. The box icon selects its marks (and shows their sheet). The bin deletes the group. Its marks stay, counted once. To add marks to a group or take them out, select them and pick the group (or Not typical (×1)) under Typical. Good to know A group's marks can be on several sheets, and of any items. Each mark is in one group at most. Copies you paste are not in the group: a pasted copy of a typical unit would otherwise count 24 times again. Use a group for \"the same thing N times\", and copy and paste for the copies you draw. The marks are drawn once, so the 3D view and the marked PDF show one unit; the quantities, the exports and the per-sheet table count it ×N. The Marks sheet of the Excel export says which marks are typical and how many times they count. With bid areas, a group's marks count ×N in their own bid area.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"read-quantities","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Read quantities and the estimate","summary":"Use the Quantities table by item and by sheet, the job total, highlighting, and the Incomplete warning.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["quantities","totals","by item","by sheet","job total","estimate","summary","report","highlight","incomplete","costs"],"headings":[{"id":"by-item","text":"By item","level":2},{"id":"by-sheet","text":"By sheet","level":2},{"id":"by-area","text":"By area","level":2},{"id":"the-incomplete-warning","text":"The Incomplete warning","level":2},{"id":"highlight-an-item","text":"Highlight an item","level":2},{"id":"item-totals-in-the-side-panel","text":"Item totals in the side panel","level":2},{"id":"marks-on-pdfs-that-are-not-open","text":"Marks on PDFs that are not open","level":2},{"id":"more-room","text":"More room","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table at the bottom of the window is your live estimate. It changes the moment you draw, delete, rescale or edit a rate. Quantities are never stored; they are measured again from the marks every time, so they cannot drift out of date.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-item\"\u003EBy item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy item\u003C/span\u003E (the default). Each item has a row, and each of its assembly components a row under it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName, with the item&#039;s color. &quot;+(n) unscaled&quot; means some marks are on sheets with no scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measured total in the item&#039;s unit (components: the formula result).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF, m, SF, SY, m², EA, or the component&#039;s unit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWaste %\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyped here or in the item editor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith waste\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity × (1 + waste).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial/unit, Labor/unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERates; you can type them here.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial, Labor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith waste × rate, rounded to the cent.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial + labor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe last row, \u003Cstrong\u003EJob total\u003C/strong\u003E, adds the material, labor and total columns. The same total shows at the right of the table&#039;s header.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType into a waste or rate cell and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click away to apply it. A value TakeoffCraft cannot read is outlined in red and the status bar says why, for example &quot;Type an amount like 12.50.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick an item&#039;s row to highlight its marks on every sheet and jump to a sheet that has them (the same as clicking it under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-sheet\"\u003EBy sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy sheet\u003C/span\u003E to see each item&#039;s quantity on each sheet, with the number of marks. Click a row to open that sheet with the item highlighted. Use it to check that every sheet was taken off, or to split quantities by floor or area of work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-area\"\u003EBy area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the takeoff has bid areas, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy area\u003C/span\u003E lists each area&#039;s items and a subtotal. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-areas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-areas\"\u003EBid areas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-incomplete-warning\"\u003EThe Incomplete warning\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen something is left out of the totals, the header shows a warning, for example:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;Incomplete: 1 formula error, 6 marks on sheets with no scale (not in the totals)&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFormula errors\u003C/strong\u003E: an assembly component&#039;s formula cannot be worked out. Its row says &quot;Formula error: …&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies#when-a-formula-is-wrong\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-hash=\"when-a-formula-is-wrong\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMarks on sheets with no scale\u003C/strong\u003E: give those sheets a scale. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale#why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-hash=\"why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same notes go into the exported spreadsheet, so an incomplete estimate never looks complete.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"highlight-an-item\"\u003EHighlight an item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick an item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E or in the table. Its marks stay at full strength on every sheet and the rest fade. The sheets list shows &quot;(n) highlighted&quot; on each sheet with its marks. Click the item again to stop highlighting.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"item-totals-in-the-side-panel\"\u003EItem totals in the side panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach item in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E list shows its quantity too. The item editor&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EMeasured\u003C/strong\u003E block shows the values its formulas use: length, runs and wall area; area, perimeter and number of areas; or count.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"marks-on-pdfs-that-are-not-open\"\u003EMarks on PDFs that are not open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA takeoff can include marks on PDFs you have not opened this time. They still count in every total; the sheets list names those PDFs. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-room\"\u003EMore room\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe arrow button at the right of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E header hides the table so the sheet gets more room. Choose it again to bring the table back.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Quantities table at the bottom of the window is your live estimate. It changes the moment you draw, delete, rescale or edit a rate. Quantities are never stored; they are measured again from the marks every time, so they cannot drift out of date. By item Choose By item (the default). Each item has a row, and each of its assembly components a row under it. Column Meaning Item Name, with the item's color. \"+(n) unscaled\" means some marks are on sheets with no scale. Quantity The measured total in the item's unit (components: the formula result). Unit LF, m, SF, SY, m², EA, or the component's unit. Waste % Typed here or in the item editor. With waste Quantity × (1 + waste). Material/unit, Labor/unit Rates; you can type them here. Material, Labor With waste × rate, rounded to the cent. Total Material + labor. The last row, Job total, adds the material, labor and total columns. The same total shows at the right of the table's header. Type into a waste or rate cell and press Enter or click away to apply it. A value TakeoffCraft cannot read is outlined in red and the status bar says why, for example \"Type an amount like 12.50.\" Click an item's row to highlight its marks on every sheet and jump to a sheet that has them (the same as clicking it under Items). By sheet Choose By sheet to see each item's quantity on each sheet, with the number of marks. Click a row to open that sheet with the item highlighted. Use it to check that every sheet was taken off, or to split quantities by floor or area of work. By area When the takeoff has bid areas, By area lists each area's items and a subtotal. See Bid areas. The Incomplete warning When something is left out of the totals, the header shows a warning, for example: \"Incomplete: 1 formula error, 6 marks on sheets with no scale (not in the totals)\" Formula errors: an assembly component's formula cannot be worked out. Its row says \"Formula error: …\". See Assemblies and formulas. Marks on sheets with no scale: give those sheets a scale. See Set the scale. The same notes go into the exported spreadsheet, so an incomplete estimate never looks complete. Highlight an item Click an item under Items or in the table. Its marks stay at full strength on every sheet and the rest fade. The sheets list shows \"(n) highlighted\" on each sheet with its marks. Click the item again to stop highlighting. Item totals in the side panel Each item in the Items list shows its quantity too. The item editor's Measured block shows the values its formulas use: length, runs and wall area; area, perimeter and number of areas; or count. Marks on PDFs that are not open A takeoff can include marks on PDFs you have not opened this time. They still count in every total; the sheets list names those PDFs. See Save and reopen a takeoff. More room The arrow button at the right of the Quantities header hides the table so the sheet gets more room. Choose it again to bring the table back.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"check-in-3d","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Check the takeoff in 3D","summary":"See the open sheet's walls, slabs and counts standing on the drawing, orbit round them, and click one to select its mark.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["png","image","picture","screenshot","stack","floors","levels","elevation","glb","gltf","export 3d","wall thickness","base height","opening","door head","3d","three d","check","view","walls","wall height","slab","depth","volume","orbit","model","picking","isometric","perspective"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-3d-view","text":"Open the 3D view","level":2},{"id":"what-you-see","text":"What you see","level":2},{"id":"look-around","text":"Look around","level":2},{"id":"walls-with-a-thickness-a-base-and-openings","text":"Walls with a thickness, a base and openings","level":2},{"id":"stack-the-floors","text":"Stack the floors","level":2},{"id":"save-the-3d-view-as-an-image","text":"Save the 3D view as an image","level":2},{"id":"save-the-3d-view-as-a-model","text":"Save the 3D view as a model","level":2},{"id":"select-a-mark-from-3d","text":"Select a mark from 3D","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe 3D view is a check, not a second drawing: it stands the takeoff of the sheet on screen up on the sheet itself, so a missing wall, a doubled slab or a stray count shows at a glance.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore you start: the sheet needs a scale (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E). Heights and depths need real feet, so on a sheet with no scale \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E says so and stays on the flat sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-3d-view\"\u003EOpen the 3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShow the sheet you want to check.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E on the bar that shows the sheet&#039;s name and scale, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first time, the 3D view takes a moment to load. The sheet is drawn on the ground, and the takeoff stands on it in each item&#039;s color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo go back, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E again, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the sheet\u003C/span\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Choosing a drawing tool (Linear, Area, Count and so on) also goes back, since marks are drawn on the flat sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt shows the sheet on screen; change sheets in the sheets list, or with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and the 3D view follows. To see several floors at once, stack them (below).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-see\"\u003EWhat you see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMark\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn 3D\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear run, item with a height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA wall of that height along the run. Curved runs follow the curve.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear run, item with no height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line on the sheet. Give the item a height under \u003Cstrong\u003EItem\u003C/strong\u003E to stand it up.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea, item with a depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA slab of that depth, with its cut-outs open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea, item with no depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA thin 2&quot; plate, so it still shows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA small post on each counted point.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe height is a linear item&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E field and the depth an area item&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E field, under \u003Cstrong\u003EItem\u003C/strong\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E). Change it, and the 3D view updates at once. The note under the sheet name says what the sheet holds, for example &quot;This sheet in 3D: 2 walls, 1 slab, 3 counts&quot;, and how many runs or areas have no height or depth. Clicks pass through it to the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe numbers do not change in 3D: a wall&#039;s area is its exact length times its height, and a slab&#039;s volume is its net area (outline minus cut-outs, curves exact) times its depth. Curves are drawn as short straight pieces only on screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-around\"\u003ELook around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag, or drag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit round the takeoff.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + drag, or drag with the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in and out at the pointer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger swipe on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pans). A pinch zooms.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStandard views, framed on the takeoff. The 3D view opens in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerspective\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPerspective on or off (parallel when off).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom extents\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe whole sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow or hide the drawing on the ground.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe view cube, top right\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a face, edge or corner to look from there; drag it to orbit; drag its compass ring to turn; the house button goes back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe corner shows a view cube, a small X, Y, Z axis icon, or nothing: choose in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, on the TakeoffCraft tab, under \u003Cstrong\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/settings\"\u003ETakeoffCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E). The flat sheet has no corner indicator.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne finger orbits; two fingers pinch to zoom and move to pan. A tap selects.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"walls-with-a-thickness-a-base-and-openings\"\u003EWalls with a thickness, a base and openings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA linear item with a height stands as a thin strip. To see it as a real wall, give the item its shape under \u003Cstrong\u003EIn 3D\u003C/strong\u003E in its settings:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWall thickness\u003C/strong\u003E (8&quot;, 0.2 m): the wall becomes a solid, centred on the line you drew, with mitred corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBase height\u003C/strong\u003E: the wall starts that far above the floor (a soffit, an upper cabinet run). Area items have it too: a ceiling at 9&#039;-0&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening head\u003C/strong\u003E: a deducted segment (a door, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-linear#deduct-a-part-of-a-run\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-linear\" data-hash=\"deduct-a-part-of-a-run\"\u003EDeduct a part of a run\u003C/a\u003E) keeps the wall above that height, so a door shows as a door. Without it, a deducted segment leaves a gap to the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese only shape the 3D view: the quantities (length, wall area, costs) stay as they were.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stack-the-floors\"\u003EStack the floors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the PDFs of every floor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the 3D view, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevels…\u003C/span\u003E and type each floor&#039;s elevation, such as \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E for the ground floor and \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;\u003C/code\u003E for the next. Blank leaves a sheet out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStack\u003C/span\u003E. Every sheet with a level shows its takeoff at its height; the sheet on screen lies under its own floor, the others are outlined. The note says how many floors are stacked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheets line up by their place on the page, so this works for floor plans plotted alike (the same sheet size and position). A sheet with a level but no scale is left out, and the note says so. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStack\u003C/span\u003E again for one sheet at a time. The levels are saved with the takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-3d-view-as-an-image\"\u003ESave the 3D view as an image\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPNG\u003C/span\u003E on the 3D bar for a picture of the view as it is on screen (\u003Ccode\u003E… 3D.png\u003C/code\u003E); \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPNG\u003C/span\u003E makes it twice the size (\u003Ccode\u003E… 3D@2x.png\u003C/code\u003E), for print.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-3d-view-as-a-model\"\u003ESave the 3D view as a model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGLB\u003C/span\u003E on the 3D bar. You get a \u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E file (glTF, binary) of what the view shows — the floor on screen, or every stacked floor — that other 3D programs open: one object per mark, named after its item, in the item&#039;s colour, in metres with Y up. The sheet itself and the outlines are not in it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-a-mark-from-3d\"\u003ESelect a mark from 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a wall, slab, plate or post. Its mark becomes the selected mark, exactly as if you had clicked it on the sheet: it shows under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E, its item becomes current, and the status bar gives its length and height, or its area, depth and cubic yards. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click adds marks to the selection or takes them out. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deletes the selected marks and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings them back; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E selects none. Back on the sheet, the same marks stay selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing follows the theme: in Model, the sheet on the ground goes dark like the flat view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The 3D view is a check, not a second drawing: it stands the takeoff of the sheet on screen up on the sheet itself, so a missing wall, a doubled slab or a stray count shows at a glance. Before you start: the sheet needs a scale (Set the scale). Heights and depths need real feet, so on a sheet with no scale 3D says so and stays on the flat sheet. Open the 3D view Show the sheet you want to check. Choose 3D on the bar that shows the sheet's name and scale, or press 3. The first time, the 3D view takes a moment to load. The sheet is drawn on the ground, and the takeoff stands on it in each item's color. To go back, choose 3D again, Back to the sheet, or press 3. Choosing a drawing tool (Linear, Area, Count and so on) also goes back, since marks are drawn on the flat sheet. It shows the sheet on screen; change sheets in the sheets list, or with Page Down and Page Up, and the 3D view follows. To see several floors at once, stack them (below). What you see Mark In 3D Linear run, item with a height A wall of that height along the run. Curved runs follow the curve. Linear run, item with no height A line on the sheet. Give the item a height under Item to stand it up. Area, item with a depth A slab of that depth, with its cut-outs open. Area, item with no depth A thin 2\" plate, so it still shows. Count A small post on each counted point. The height is a linear item's Height field and the depth an area item's Depth field, under Item (see Create takeoff items). Change it, and the 3D view updates at once. The note under the sheet name says what the sheet holds, for example \"This sheet in 3D: 2 walls, 1 slab, 3 counts\", and how many runs or areas have no height or depth. Clicks pass through it to the view. The numbers do not change in 3D: a wall's area is its exact length times its height, and a slab's volume is its net area (outline minus cut-outs, curves exact) times its depth. Curves are drawn as short straight pieces only on screen. Look around Action What it does Drag, or drag with the middle button Orbit round the takeoff. Shift + drag, or drag with the right button Pan. Mouse wheel Zoom in and out at the pointer. Two-finger swipe on a trackpad Orbit (Shift pans). A pinch zooms. Iso, Top, Front, Back, Left, Right, Bottom Standard views, framed on the takeoff. The 3D view opens in Iso. Perspective Perspective on or off (parallel when off). Zoom extents or F The whole sheet. Sheet Show or hide the drawing on the ground. The view cube, top right Click a face, edge or corner to look from there; drag it to orbit; drag its compass ring to turn; the house button goes back to Iso. The corner shows a view cube, a small X, Y, Z axis icon, or nothing: choose in Settings, on the TakeoffCraft tab, under Corner indicator (TakeoffCraft settings). The flat sheet has no corner indicator. One finger orbits; two fingers pinch to zoom and move to pan. A tap selects. Walls with a thickness, a base and openings A linear item with a height stands as a thin strip. To see it as a real wall, give the item its shape under In 3D in its settings: Wall thickness (8\", 0.2 m): the wall becomes a solid, centred on the line you drew, with mitred corners. Base height: the wall starts that far above the floor (a soffit, an upper cabinet run). Area items have it too: a ceiling at 9'-0\". Opening head: a deducted segment (a door, Deduct a part of a run) keeps the wall above that height, so a door shows as a door. Without it, a deducted segment leaves a gap to the top. These only shape the 3D view: the quantities (length, wall area, costs) stay as they were. Stack the floors Open the PDFs of every floor. In the 3D view, choose Levels… and type each floor's elevation, such as 0 for the ground floor and 12' for the next. Blank leaves a sheet out. Choose Stack. Every sheet with a level shows its takeoff at its height; the sheet on screen lies under its own floor, the others are outlined. The note says how many floors are stacked. Sheets line up by their place on the page, so this works for floor plans plotted alike (the same sheet size and position). A sheet with a level but no scale is left out, and the note says so. Choose Stack again for one sheet at a time. The levels are saved with the takeoff. Save the 3D view as an image Choose PNG on the 3D bar for a picture of the view as it is on screen (… 3D.png); Shift + click PNG makes it twice the size (… 3D@2x.png), for print. Save the 3D view as a model Choose GLB on the 3D bar. You get a .glb file (glTF, binary) of what the view shows — the floor on screen, or every stacked floor — that other 3D programs open: one object per mark, named after its item, in the item's colour, in metres with Y up. The sheet itself and the outlines are not in it. Select a mark from 3D Click a wall, slab, plate or post. Its mark becomes the selected mark, exactly as if you had clicked it on the sheet: it shows under Selected mark, its item becomes current, and the status bar gives its length and height, or its area, depth and cubic yards. Shift + click adds marks to the selection or takes them out. Delete deletes the selected marks and Ctrl+Z brings them back; Esc selects none. Back on the sheet, the same marks stay selected. The drawing follows the theme: in Model, the sheet on the ground goes dark like the flat view.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"markups","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Notes, clouds and highlights","summary":"Mark questions and changes on the sheets with notes, revision clouds and highlights, track each as open, answered or closed, and send them in the marked PDF.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["markup","markups","note","comment","question","rfi","cloud","revision cloud","highlight","status","open","answered","closed","review","markups list"],"headings":[{"id":"add-one","text":"Add one","level":2},{"id":"status-and-editing","text":"Status and editing","level":2},{"id":"the-list","text":"The list","level":2},{"id":"share-them","text":"Share them","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMarkups are for what you need to remember or ask while taking off: &quot;confirm the base type&quot;, a revision cloud round a changed wall, a highlighted note on the plan. They sit on the sheet with a status, are listed in one place, and print in the marked PDF. They are \u003Cstrong\u003Enever counted\u003C/strong\u003E: no quantity, no cost.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-one\"\u003EAdd one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkup\u003C/span\u003E on the bar above the sheet and pick:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMarkup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENote\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick where it goes, type the note, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E. It shows as a round pin with its first line beside it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick one corner, then the opposite one, and type a note (or leave it empty). A scalloped revision cloud goes round the box.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHighlight\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick two corners: a see-through yellow box, with a note if you like.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after the first corner cancels. The tool stays on for the next one; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E goes back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"status-and-editing\"\u003EStatus and editing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a markup with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E to select it. The right panel shows \u003Cstrong\u003EMarkup\u003C/strong\u003E: its kind and sheet, its \u003Cstrong\u003EStatus\u003C/strong\u003E — Open, Answered or Closed — and its text, which you can change. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E) removes it; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA note&#039;s pin is orange while open, blue once answered and grey when closed; a closed cloud turns grey.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-list\"\u003EThe list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMarkups\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel lists every markup of the takeoff with its status, and says how many are still open. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen only\u003C/strong\u003E to see what is left to settle. Click a row to go to its sheet and select it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-them\"\u003EShare them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E draws them on the pages: highlights, clouds and pins, each with a box giving its status in capitals (OPEN, ANSWERED, CLOSED) and its text. A page with markups but no takeoff marks is included too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E adds a \u003Cstrong\u003EMarkups\u003C/strong\u003E worksheet: sheet, kind, status and text, one row each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThey are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E and in a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen#keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-hash=\"keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Markups are for what you need to remember or ask while taking off: \"confirm the base type\", a revision cloud round a changed wall, a highlighted note on the plan. They sit on the sheet with a status, are listed in one place, and print in the marked PDF. They are never counted: no quantity, no cost. Add one Open Markup on the bar above the sheet and pick: Markup How Note Click where it goes, type the note, choose Add. It shows as a round pin with its first line beside it. Cloud Click one corner, then the opposite one, and type a note (or leave it empty). A scalloped revision cloud goes round the box. Highlight Click two corners: a see-through yellow box, with a note if you like. Esc after the first corner cancels. The tool stays on for the next one; V goes back to Select. Status and editing Click a markup with Select to select it. The right panel shows Markup: its kind and sheet, its Status — Open, Answered or Closed — and its text, which you can change. Delete (or Delete) removes it; Ctrl+Z brings it back. A note's pin is orange while open, blue once answered and grey when closed; a closed cloud turns grey. The list Markups in the right panel lists every markup of the takeoff with its status, and says how many are still open. Tick Open only to see what is left to settle. Click a row to go to its sheet and select it. Share them Marked PDF draws them on the pages: highlights, clouds and pins, each with a box giving its status in capitals (OPEN, ANSWERED, CLOSED) and its text. A page with markups but no takeoff marks is included too. XLSX adds a Markups worksheet: sheet, kind, status and text, one row each. They are saved in the .takeoff.json and in a PDF with the takeoff inside.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"export-estimate","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Export the estimate and a marked PDF","summary":"Download the estimate as an XLSX workbook with live formulas or as CSV, and copies of your PDFs with the takeoff drawn in.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["proposal","bid summary","export","xlsx","spreadsheet","workbook","csv","marked pdf","print takeoff","report","share","send","legend"],"headings":[{"id":"the-estimate-as-a-spreadsheet-xlsx","text":"The estimate as a spreadsheet (XLSX)","level":2},{"id":"the-estimate-as-csv","text":"The estimate as CSV","level":2},{"id":"a-marked-pdf","text":"A marked PDF","level":2},{"id":"nothing-to-export","text":"Nothing to export?","level":2},{"id":"save-the-takeoff-too","text":"Save the takeoff too","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EExports are downloads. They are made on your computer from the takeoff and the open PDFs; nothing is sent anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-estimate-as-a-spreadsheet-xlsx\"\u003EThe estimate as a spreadsheet (XLSX)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E menu in the toolbar and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E. Your browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E(name).xlsx\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says &quot;Exported (name).xlsx (estimate with live formulas, by sheet, marks, scales).&quot; and adds a warning when the estimate is incomplete.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen it in your spreadsheet program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe workbook has four worksheets, and the reports below when they apply:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWorksheet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it holds\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEstimate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA row per item and per component: quantity, waste, quantity with waste, rates, material, labor, total, notes. The job total at the bottom.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBy sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach item&#039;s quantity on each sheet, with the number of marks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery mark: sheet, item, type, quantity, and detail (segment lengths, gross and cut-out areas, perimeter).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScales\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach sheet that has a scale or marks: its scale, how it was set (preset, or calibrated on which distance), and feet per PDF unit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoff summary\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach item&#039;s quantity, CSI number, marks and sheets, without prices.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery assembly part added up across the takeoff (the same part used by several items is one line), with waste and material cost, and the items that use it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuote request\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe material list to send a supplier: quantities with waste, an empty unit-price column, and extensions and a total that fill in as prices are typed.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBy division\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial, labor and total by CSI MasterFormat division, when items have a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSI number\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-summary\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-summary\"\u003Ebid summary\u003C/a\u003E, the Estimate worksheet also ends with its markups (labor burden, sales tax, overhead, profit, bond and the bid), as live formulas.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Estimate worksheet keeps \u003Cstrong\u003Elive formulas\u003C/strong\u003E: quantity with waste, the rounded material and labor amounts, each row&#039;s total and the job total. Spreadsheet programs recalculate them, so you can change a rate or waste in the spreadsheet and the totals follow. The numbers TakeoffCraft computed are stored in the cells as well, and match to the cent.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe notes column lists each component&#039;s formula, formula errors, marks on sheets with no scale, wall area and perimeter. When anything is left out, the job total row says &quot;Incomplete: see the notes above&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe workbook has been checked in LibreOffice Calc, which recalculated it to the same totals. It has not been opened in Excel yet. Column by column details are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-columns\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-columns\"\u003EExport columns\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-estimate-as-csv\"\u003EThe estimate as CSV\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E menu and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E. You get \u003Ccode\u003E(name).csv\u003C/code\u003E: the same rows and columns as the Estimate worksheet, as plain values (no formulas), and the job total. Use it to paste into another estimating or accounting system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe CSV has only the estimate; for quantities by sheet and the mark list, use XLSX. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial list\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu saves the material list alone as CSV, for ordering.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-marked-pdf\"\u003EA marked PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the PDFs whose marks you want drawn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E menu and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E. &quot;Writing the marked PDF…&quot; shows, then your browser downloads one file per marked PDF, named \u003Ccode\u003E(PDF name) (takeoff).pdf\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the copy:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eruns are drawn as lines in their item&#039;s color,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eareas are filled lightly with a solid outline, and cut-outs left open,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ecounts are dots,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eeach page with marks gets a small legend in its bottom-left corner: each item&#039;s color, name and quantity on that page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour original PDFs are not changed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENotes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly PDFs that are open are written. Marks on PDFs that are not open stay in the totals but are not drawn; open those PDFs first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENames in the legend use a standard PDF font. Letters it cannot show print as &quot;?&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn encrypted PDF cannot be copied with marks: &quot;This PDF is password-protected or encrypted, so a marked copy cannot be written. The takeoff itself is fine; export the spreadsheet instead.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe first \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E in a tab loads the PDF writer. Offline, it is there only if the app bar shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith several marked PDFs, Chrome and Edge may ask once whether this site may download multiple files. Choose Allow, or you get only the first file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nothing-to-export\"\u003ENothing to export?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E need at least one item: &quot;Nothing to export yet: make an item and take it off.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E needs marks on an open PDF: &quot;No marks on the open PDFs yet.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-takeoff-too\"\u003ESave the takeoff too\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn export is a report. To keep working on the takeoff later, also choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo add overhead, profit, tax and bond and send a proposal, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-summary\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-summary\"\u003EBid summary and proposal\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Exports are downloads. They are made on your computer from the takeoff and the open PDFs; nothing is sent anywhere. The estimate as a spreadsheet (XLSX) Open the Export menu in the toolbar and choose XLSX. Your browser downloads (name).xlsx. The status bar says \"Exported (name).xlsx (estimate with live formulas, by sheet, marks, scales).\" and adds a warning when the estimate is incomplete. Open it in your spreadsheet program. The workbook has four worksheets, and the reports below when they apply: Worksheet What it holds Estimate A row per item and per component: quantity, waste, quantity with waste, rates, material, labor, total, notes. The job total at the bottom. By sheet Each item's quantity on each sheet, with the number of marks. Marks Every mark: sheet, item, type, quantity, and detail (segment lengths, gross and cut-out areas, perimeter). Scales Each sheet that has a scale or marks: its scale, how it was set (preset, or calibrated on which distance), and feet per PDF unit. Takeoff summary Each item's quantity, CSI number, marks and sheets, without prices. Material list Every assembly part added up across the takeoff (the same part used by several items is one line), with waste and material cost, and the items that use it. Quote request The material list to send a supplier: quantities with waste, an empty unit-price column, and extensions and a total that fill in as prices are typed. By division Material, labor and total by CSI MasterFormat division, when items have a CSI number. With a bid summary, the Estimate worksheet also ends with its markups (labor burden, sales tax, overhead, profit, bond and the bid), as live formulas. The Estimate worksheet keeps live formulas: quantity with waste, the rounded material and labor amounts, each row's total and the job total. Spreadsheet programs recalculate them, so you can change a rate or waste in the spreadsheet and the totals follow. The numbers TakeoffCraft computed are stored in the cells as well, and match to the cent. The notes column lists each component's formula, formula errors, marks on sheets with no scale, wall area and perimeter. When anything is left out, the job total row says \"Incomplete: see the notes above\". The workbook has been checked in LibreOffice Calc, which recalculated it to the same totals. It has not been opened in Excel yet. Column by column details are in Export columns. The estimate as CSV Open the Export menu and choose CSV. You get (name).csv: the same rows and columns as the Estimate worksheet, as plain values (no formulas), and the job total. Use it to paste into another estimating or accounting system. The CSV has only the estimate; for quantities by sheet and the mark list, use XLSX. Material list in the same menu saves the material list alone as CSV, for ordering. A marked PDF Open the PDFs whose marks you want drawn. Open the Export menu and choose Marked PDF. \"Writing the marked PDF…\" shows, then your browser downloads one file per marked PDF, named (PDF name) (takeoff).pdf. In the copy: runs are drawn as lines in their item's color, areas are filled lightly with a solid outline, and cut-outs left open, counts are dots, each page with marks gets a small legend in its bottom-left corner: each item's color, name and quantity on that page. Your original PDFs are not changed. Notes: Only PDFs that are open are written. Marks on PDFs that are not open stay in the totals but are not drawn; open those PDFs first. Names in the legend use a standard PDF font. Letters it cannot show print as \"?\". An encrypted PDF cannot be copied with marks: \"This PDF is password-protected or encrypted, so a marked copy cannot be written. The takeoff itself is fine; export the spreadsheet instead.\" The first Marked PDF in a tab loads the PDF writer. Offline, it is there only if the app bar shows Offline ready; see Working offline. With several marked PDFs, Chrome and Edge may ask once whether this site may download multiple files. Choose Allow, or you get only the first file. Nothing to export? XLSX and CSV need at least one item: \"Nothing to export yet: make an item and take it off.\" Marked PDF needs marks on an open PDF: \"No marks on the open PDFs yet.\" Save the takeoff too An export is a report. To keep working on the takeoff later, also choose Save takeoff: see Save and reopen a takeoff. To add overhead, profit, tax and bond and send a proposal, see Bid summary and proposal.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"bid-summary","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Bid summary and proposal","summary":"Add labor burden, sales tax, overhead, profit and bond to the estimate, write inclusions and exclusions, and download a proposal PDF to send.","section":"tasks","order":101,"keywords":["bid","labor burden","burden","payroll","proposal","quote","overhead","profit","markup","margin","sales tax","bond","inclusions","exclusions","terms","signature","client","price"],"headings":[{"id":"the-markups","text":"The markups","level":2},{"id":"the-proposal","text":"The proposal","level":2},{"id":"in-the-exports","text":"In the exports","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe estimate prices what you took off. The \u003Cstrong\u003Ebid summary\u003C/strong\u003E turns it into a price: it adds sales tax, overhead, profit and bond, and makes a \u003Cstrong\u003Eproposal PDF\u003C/strong\u003E with your inclusions and exclusions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen it from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid summary &amp; proposal…\u003C/span\u003E. Everything in it is saved with the takeoff, and each change can be undone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-markups\"\u003EThe markups\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType a percent in any of the five (leave the others at 0). They are worked out in this order, each on what is above it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWorked out as\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial, Labor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe estimate&#039;s totals (the base bid when the takeoff has \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-areas#alternates-base-bid-plus-adds-and-deducts\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-areas\" data-hash=\"alternates-base-bid-plus-adds-and-deducts\"\u003Ealternates\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabor burden\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBurden % × labor: payroll taxes, insurance and benefits on your crews&#039; wages.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECost\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial + labor + labor burden.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESales tax on material\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETax % × material.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOverhead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOverhead % × (cost + tax).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfit % × (cost + tax + overhead).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBond\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBond % × everything above.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBid\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sum of the lines.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach line is rounded to the cent. The workbook shows the same lines under the Estimate worksheet&#039;s total, with live formulas. The summary updates as you type: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes a percent. Each alternate is shown with the same markups on its own cost, so the owner sees what taking it really changes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-proposal\"\u003EThe proposal\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFill in what the proposal should say:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject\u003C/strong\u003E (the takeoff&#039;s name when empty), \u003Cstrong\u003ETo\u003C/strong\u003E (the client) and \u003Cstrong\u003EFrom\u003C/strong\u003E (your company).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInclusions\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EExclusions\u003C/strong\u003E: one per line; a leading dash or bullet is fine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETerms\u003C/strong\u003E: free text, such as &quot;Valid for 30 days.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EList the items and quantities\u003C/strong\u003E: the scope, item by item, with quantities and no prices. Untick it for a lump-sum proposal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProposal PDF\u003C/span\u003E. The PDF (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;takeoff&gt; - proposal.pdf\u003C/code\u003E) has the date, the price block with each markup and the bid, the alternates, the scope, the inclusions and exclusions, the terms, and lines to sign and date. If some quantities could not be measured (a sheet without a scale), it says so under the price.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-exports\"\u003EIn the exports\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnce the takeoff has a bid summary, the CSV ends with it and the workbook gets a \u003Cstrong\u003EBid summary\u003C/strong\u003E sheet, with each line, its percent and its amount. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-estimate\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-estimate\"\u003EExport the estimate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The estimate prices what you took off. The bid summary turns it into a price: it adds sales tax, overhead, profit and bond, and makes a proposal PDF with your inclusions and exclusions. Open it from Export › Bid summary & proposal…. Everything in it is saved with the takeoff, and each change can be undone. The markups Type a percent in any of the five (leave the others at 0). They are worked out in this order, each on what is above it: Line Worked out as Material, Labor The estimate's totals (the base bid when the takeoff has alternates). Labor burden Burden % × labor: payroll taxes, insurance and benefits on your crews' wages. Cost Material + labor + labor burden. Sales tax on material Tax % × material. Overhead Overhead % × (cost + tax). Profit Profit % × (cost + tax + overhead). Bond Bond % × everything above. Bid The sum of the lines. Each line is rounded to the cent. The workbook shows the same lines under the Estimate worksheet's total, with live formulas. The summary updates as you type: Enter takes a percent. Each alternate is shown with the same markups on its own cost, so the owner sees what taking it really changes. The proposal Fill in what the proposal should say: Project (the takeoff's name when empty), To (the client) and From (your company). Inclusions and Exclusions: one per line; a leading dash or bullet is fine. Terms: free text, such as \"Valid for 30 days.\" List the items and quantities: the scope, item by item, with quantities and no prices. Untick it for a lump-sum proposal. Choose Proposal PDF. The PDF (\u003Ctakeoff\u003E - proposal.pdf) has the date, the price block with each markup and the bid, the alternates, the scope, the inclusions and exclusions, the terms, and lines to sign and date. If some quantities could not be measured (a sheet without a scale), it says so under the price. In the exports Once the takeoff has a bid summary, the CSV ends with it and the workbook gets a Bid summary sheet, with each line, its percent and its amount. See Export the estimate.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"bid-leveling","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Bid packages and bid leveling","summary":"Split the estimate into packages by trade, send each package's scope to subcontractors, compare the bids that come back on the same scope, and use the chosen bid as the package's price.","section":"tasks","order":102,"keywords":["bid leveling","leveling","bid package","subcontractor","sub bid","scope","plug","trade","csi division","compare bids","low bid","bid tab","bidder","award","chosen bid","use a bid","subcontract price"],"headings":[{"id":"packages","text":"Packages","level":2},{"id":"enter-the-bids","text":"Enter the bids","level":2},{"id":"how-the-bids-are-leveled","text":"How the bids are leveled","level":2},{"id":"use-a-bid-as-the-package-s-price","text":"Use a bid as the package's price","level":2},{"id":"bids-that-come-back-by-email","text":"Bids that come back by email","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA general contractor sends each trade&#039;s scope to subcontractors and compares what comes back. TakeoffCraft does both from the takeoff: open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid packages and leveling…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"packages\"\u003EPackages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is one package for each CSI division your items use (the first two digits of an item&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSI number\u003C/span\u003E, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E). Items without a number make a package of their own. Each package shows its items, how many sheets their marks are on, and our own cost of it (material and labor before markups, from the base bid).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScope CSV\u003C/span\u003E to download what to send a subcontractor: each item with its CSI number, quantity, unit and the sheets it is on. It has no prices. For material suppliers, the workbook&#039;s quote request has the parts list (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-estimate\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-estimate\"\u003EExport the estimate\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"enter-the-bids\"\u003EEnter the bids\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the empty row under the package, type the bidder&#039;s name and the amount of the bid, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd the bid\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf a bid leaves part of the scope out, open its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft out\u003C/span\u003E list and tick those items.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo correct a name or an amount, type over it in the table; the × button removes a bid.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change is one step that \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back, and the bids are saved with the takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-bids-are-leveled\"\u003EHow the bids are leveled\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBids rarely cover the same scope. Leveling fills each left-out item with a \u003Cstrong\u003Eplug\u003C/strong\u003E at our own cost of it, so every bid is compared on the whole package:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeveled = the bid + the plugs\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe lowest leveled bid is marked \u003Cstrong\u003ELow\u003C/strong\u003E; on a tie, the one entered first. \u003Cstrong\u003EAgainst our cost\u003C/strong\u003E is the leveled bid minus our own cost of the package: negative means under it. A bid that looks lowest can lose once its missing scope is counted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe workbook gets a \u003Cstrong\u003EBid leveling\u003C/strong\u003E sheet with every package that has bids: our estimate, each bid, its plugs, the leveled total and the difference, as live formulas, and what each bid left out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-a-bid-as-the-package-s-price\"\u003EUse a bid as the package&#039;s price\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse\u003C/span\u003E on a bid and it becomes the price of the whole package in your estimate: the package&#039;s own pricing (the items, their second conditions and assembly parts) drops out of the job total, the items stay as quantities with your own rates for reference, and one line, \u003Cstrong\u003EChosen bid\u003C/strong\u003E, carries the bid&#039;s amount. Items the bid leaves out stay at your own cost, which is what leveling already counted as the plug. Choose another bid, or choose the same one again to take it back; each is one step that \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the bid summary the sub bids are one line inside the cost: overhead, profit and bond are added on top of them like any other cost; sales tax (on material only) and labor burden (on labor) are not. The workbook&#039;s Estimate sheet carries the chosen bid as its own row and the markups block adds it, with live formulas. The material list leaves out an awarded package&#039;s material, because the subcontractor supplies it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA package inside an alternate&#039;s bid area is not repriced: alternates and bid-area subtotals still use your own prices.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bids-that-come-back-by-email\"\u003EBids that come back by email\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou do not have to type the bids in. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend for bids…\u003C/span\u003E under a package emails each subcontractor a link to a page where they type a price for every line; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGet prices\u003C/span\u003E pulls what they sent into this table. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\"\u003ESend a package for bids\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETyping a bid in by hand still works, and a hand-typed bid and one from the site sit side by side. Bids that came back from the site are flagged \u003Cstrong\u003Eweb\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003Eweb, late\u003C/strong\u003E when they arrived after the due time); their price changes only when the bidder sends a new version, so your own edits to it stay until then.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"A general contractor sends each trade's scope to subcontractors and compares what comes back. TakeoffCraft does both from the takeoff: open Export › Bid packages and leveling…. Packages There is one package for each CSI division your items use (the first two digits of an item's CSI number, see Create takeoff items). Items without a number make a package of their own. Each package shows its items, how many sheets their marks are on, and our own cost of it (material and labor before markups, from the base bid). Choose Scope CSV to download what to send a subcontractor: each item with its CSI number, quantity, unit and the sheets it is on. It has no prices. For material suppliers, the workbook's quote request has the parts list (see Export the estimate). Enter the bids In the empty row under the package, type the bidder's name and the amount of the bid, then choose Add the bid. If a bid leaves part of the scope out, open its Left out list and tick those items. To correct a name or an amount, type over it in the table; the × button removes a bid. Each change is one step that Ctrl+Z takes back, and the bids are saved with the takeoff. How the bids are leveled Bids rarely cover the same scope. Leveling fills each left-out item with a plug at our own cost of it, so every bid is compared on the whole package: Leveled = the bid + the plugs The lowest leveled bid is marked Low; on a tie, the one entered first. Against our cost is the leveled bid minus our own cost of the package: negative means under it. A bid that looks lowest can lose once its missing scope is counted. The workbook gets a Bid leveling sheet with every package that has bids: our estimate, each bid, its plugs, the leveled total and the difference, as live formulas, and what each bid left out. Use a bid as the package's price Choose Use on a bid and it becomes the price of the whole package in your estimate: the package's own pricing (the items, their second conditions and assembly parts) drops out of the job total, the items stay as quantities with your own rates for reference, and one line, Chosen bid, carries the bid's amount. Items the bid leaves out stay at your own cost, which is what leveling already counted as the plug. Choose another bid, or choose the same one again to take it back; each is one step that Ctrl+Z undoes. In the bid summary the sub bids are one line inside the cost: overhead, profit and bond are added on top of them like any other cost; sales tax (on material only) and labor burden (on labor) are not. The workbook's Estimate sheet carries the chosen bid as its own row and the markups block adds it, with live formulas. The material list leaves out an awarded package's material, because the subcontractor supplies it. A package inside an alternate's bid area is not repriced: alternates and bid-area subtotals still use your own prices. Bids that come back by email You do not have to type the bids in. Send for bids… under a package emails each subcontractor a link to a page where they type a price for every line; Get prices pulls what they sent into this table. See Send a package for bids. Typing a bid in by hand still works, and a hand-typed bid and one from the site sit side by side. Bids that came back from the site are flagged web (or web, late when they arrived after the due time); their price changes only when the bidder sends a new version, so your own edits to it stay until then.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"bid-invitations","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Send a package for bids","summary":"Email a bid package to subcontractors, let them type prices on a page that needs no account, keep the prices sealed until the opening time, and pull them into the leveling sheet.","section":"tasks","order":103,"keywords":["send for bids","invitation","bid invitation","bidder","subcontractor","invite","email","price request","sealed bids","bid opening","bid day","late bid","due date","reminder","subcontractor records","insurance","qualification","csv","connect","sheets pdf"],"headings":[{"id":"before-you-send","text":"Before you send","level":2},{"id":"send-a-package","text":"Send a package","level":2},{"id":"what-the-bidder-sees","text":"What the bidder sees","level":2},{"id":"sealed-bids-and-the-opening","text":"Sealed bids and the opening","level":2},{"id":"get-the-prices","text":"Get the prices","level":2},{"id":"on-the-website","text":"On the website","level":2},{"id":"who-sees-what-and-how-long","text":"Who sees what, and how long","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid packages and leveling…\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend for bids…\u003C/span\u003E sends one package to the people you choose. The website emails each of them a link; they open a page, see the scope and type a price for every line. You see the prices in the leveling sheet, ready to compare (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-leveling\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-leveling\"\u003EBid packages and bid leveling\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESending is \u003Cstrong\u003Enot open to everyone yet.\u003C/strong\u003E While the feature is being tested it works for accounts the CadCraft team has enabled; if yours is not, the dialog says so. The bidder&#039;s page works for every invitation that was sent.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"before-you-send\"\u003EBefore you send\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYou need a CadCraft account with a verified email address. TakeoffCraft in the browser asks you to \u003Cstrong\u003EConnect\u003C/strong\u003E: a tab opens on getcadcraft.com, you press Connect, and that tab gets a sign-in that works for 30 days, for bid packages only, until you close the TakeoffCraft tab. You can remove it any time on your security page. A Studio window cannot send yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive each item a CSI number: a package is the items of one CSI division (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScale the sheets the package&#039;s marks are on, so the quantities the bidders see are measured.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-package\"\u003ESend a package\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend for bids…\u003C/span\u003E under the package.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in the title, project and an optional message, and the \u003Cstrong\u003Edue time\u003C/strong\u003E in your own time zone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave \u003Cstrong\u003EKeep prices sealed\u003C/strong\u003E ticked to hide every price until the \u003Cstrong\u003Eopening time\u003C/strong\u003E (not before the due time), or untick it to see prices as they arrive.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave the sheets box ticked to send a PDF of the sheets that hold the package&#039;s marks, with the marks drawn in. Prices of yours are never in what is sent: only item names, CSI numbers, quantities, units and sheet names.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the people to invite, one per line, as \u003Ccode\u003EName &lt;name@company.com&gt;\u003C/code\u003E or just the address, or tick people from your subcontractor records. Up to 60 at a time, 100 per package.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend invitations\u003C/span\u003E. Each person gets one plain email with your name and email address, the due time and the link; replies go to you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog tells you how many were sent and names anyone skipped: already invited to this package, asked not to get bid invitations, not a valid address, or over a sending limit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-bidder-sees\"\u003EWhat the bidder sees\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA page at a long private link, with no sign-in. It shows who invited them, the due time, whether prices are sealed, your message and the sheets PDF, then every line with its quantity. For each line they type a price or tick \u003Cstrong\u003ENot included\u003C/strong\u003E, and may add a note; they add notes for the whole package and up to 3 files (a quote, a scope letter, a certificate). They can save again and change their prices until the opening, or \u003Cstrong\u003Edecline\u003C/strong\u003E, with a box to ask that CadCraft not send them bid invitations again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sealed-bids-and-the-opening\"\u003ESealed bids and the opening\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore the opening time \u003Cstrong\u003Enobody\u003C/strong\u003E sees a price, a note or a file: not you, not in TakeoffCraft, not on your Bid packages page. You see who was invited and whether they answered. At the opening time every bid shows at once. A bid saved after the due time is accepted and marked \u003Cstrong\u003Elate\u003C/strong\u003E; the leveling still lists it, and lateness is a mark, not a ruling. After the opening a bidder cannot change a price. With no sealing, a bid is final once it is due.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-the-prices\"\u003EGet the prices\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGet prices\u003C/span\u003E asks the website and adds each answer to the package as a bid, flagged \u003Cstrong\u003Eweb\u003C/strong\u003E. TakeoffCraft also asks quietly each time you open the dialog while it is connected. A bid whose bidder saved a new version is updated in place; a bid you edited stays as you left it until they send a new version. Each pull is one step that \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes. Left-out lines come across as the scope the bid leaves out, so leveling fills them at your own cost.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-the-website\"\u003EOn the website\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour account has two more pages once you can send: \u003Cstrong\u003EBid packages\u003C/strong\u003E lists what you sent, who answered, the late bids and the files, lets you send a reminder (a fresh link; the old one stops working), close, reopen or cancel a package; \u003Cstrong\u003ESubcontractors\u003C/strong\u003E keeps a record for each company: contacts, trades, qualification answers (years in business, bonding capacity, EMR, safety record, union or open shop, references), license and insurance expiry dates, flagged once past or within 30 days, and their past bids. Import and export the records as CSV: one row per contact, with the columns the export shows; a company you already have is updated, never repeated.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"who-sees-what-and-how-long\"\u003EWho sees what, and how long\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA bidder&#039;s prices, notes and files go to you and to the CadCraft team, who run the page. They are kept for 24 months after the due date, the files for 180 days; then they are deleted. The bidder&#039;s email address is the one you entered; it is used only to send the invitation and any reminder for it. One address gets at most 3 invitations a day from everyone together, and you can send up to 200 a day.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAddenda, award letters, a signed-off scope sheet per bidder and automatic reminders before an insurance date runs out are not built. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Export › Bid packages and leveling… › Send for bids… sends one package to the people you choose. The website emails each of them a link; they open a page, see the scope and type a price for every line. You see the prices in the leveling sheet, ready to compare (Bid packages and bid leveling). Sending is not open to everyone yet. While the feature is being tested it works for accounts the CadCraft team has enabled; if yours is not, the dialog says so. The bidder's page works for every invitation that was sent. Before you send You need a CadCraft account with a verified email address. TakeoffCraft in the browser asks you to Connect: a tab opens on getcadcraft.com, you press Connect, and that tab gets a sign-in that works for 30 days, for bid packages only, until you close the TakeoffCraft tab. You can remove it any time on your security page. A Studio window cannot send yet. Give each item a CSI number: a package is the items of one CSI division (Create takeoff items). Scale the sheets the package's marks are on, so the quantities the bidders see are measured. Send a package Choose Send for bids… under the package. Fill in the title, project and an optional message, and the due time in your own time zone. Leave Keep prices sealed ticked to hide every price until the opening time (not before the due time), or untick it to see prices as they arrive. Leave the sheets box ticked to send a PDF of the sheets that hold the package's marks, with the marks drawn in. Prices of yours are never in what is sent: only item names, CSI numbers, quantities, units and sheet names. Type the people to invite, one per line, as Name \u003Cname@company.com\u003E or just the address, or tick people from your subcontractor records. Up to 60 at a time, 100 per package. Choose Send invitations. Each person gets one plain email with your name and email address, the due time and the link; replies go to you. The dialog tells you how many were sent and names anyone skipped: already invited to this package, asked not to get bid invitations, not a valid address, or over a sending limit. What the bidder sees A page at a long private link, with no sign-in. It shows who invited them, the due time, whether prices are sealed, your message and the sheets PDF, then every line with its quantity. For each line they type a price or tick Not included, and may add a note; they add notes for the whole package and up to 3 files (a quote, a scope letter, a certificate). They can save again and change their prices until the opening, or decline, with a box to ask that CadCraft not send them bid invitations again. Sealed bids and the opening Before the opening time nobody sees a price, a note or a file: not you, not in TakeoffCraft, not on your Bid packages page. You see who was invited and whether they answered. At the opening time every bid shows at once. A bid saved after the due time is accepted and marked late; the leveling still lists it, and lateness is a mark, not a ruling. After the opening a bidder cannot change a price. With no sealing, a bid is final once it is due. Get the prices Get prices asks the website and adds each answer to the package as a bid, flagged web. TakeoffCraft also asks quietly each time you open the dialog while it is connected. A bid whose bidder saved a new version is updated in place; a bid you edited stays as you left it until they send a new version. Each pull is one step that Ctrl+Z undoes. Left-out lines come across as the scope the bid leaves out, so leveling fills them at your own cost. On the website Your account has two more pages once you can send: Bid packages lists what you sent, who answered, the late bids and the files, lets you send a reminder (a fresh link; the old one stops working), close, reopen or cancel a package; Subcontractors keeps a record for each company: contacts, trades, qualification answers (years in business, bonding capacity, EMR, safety record, union or open shop, references), license and insurance expiry dates, flagged once past or within 30 days, and their past bids. Import and export the records as CSV: one row per contact, with the columns the export shows; a company you already have is updated, never repeated. Who sees what, and how long A bidder's prices, notes and files go to you and to the CadCraft team, who run the page. They are kept for 24 months after the due date, the files for 180 days; then they are deleted. The bidder's email address is the one you entered; it is used only to send the invitation and any reminder for it. One address gets at most 3 invitations a day from everyone together, and you can send up to 200 a day. Not built yet Addenda, award letters, a signed-off scope sheet per bidder and automatic reminders before an insurance date runs out are not built. See Not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"save-and-reopen","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Save and reopen a takeoff","summary":"Save the takeoff as a .takeoff.json project, reopen it with the same PDFs, and what happens when a PDF is missing or has changed.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["takeoff inside pdf","embed","attachment","save","open","reopen","project","takeoff.json","resume","continue","revised pdf","changed pdf","missing pdf","backup","autosave","recover","crash"],"headings":[{"id":"save","text":"Save","level":2},{"id":"keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf","text":"Keep the takeoff inside the PDF","level":2},{"id":"if-the-tab-or-the-browser-stops","text":"If the tab or the browser stops","level":2},{"id":"reopen","text":"Reopen","level":2},{"id":"when-a-pdf-is-missing","text":"When a PDF is missing","level":2},{"id":"when-a-pdf-has-changed","text":"When a PDF has changed","level":2},{"id":"a-damaged-or-foreign-file","text":"A damaged or foreign file","level":2},{"id":"undo-history","text":"Undo history","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA takeoff is saved as a small project file, \u003Ccode\u003E(name).takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E. It holds your items, assemblies, rates, every sheet&#039;s scale and every mark. It does \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E hold the PDFs; it finds them again by their contents.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save\"\u003ESave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Your browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E(name).takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says &quot;Saved (name).takeoff.json. It reopens on top of the same PDFs (they are matched by their contents).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the file next to the job&#039;s PDFs, so the two stay together.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach save downloads a new file. Your browser may name the second one \u003Ccode\u003E(name) (1).takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E; keep the newest.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe name comes from the first PDF you opened in this takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESave takeoff is still the only way to keep a takeoff as a file. While you work, TakeoffCraft also keeps a recovery copy in this browser (see below), and cloud saving is not available yet. If you try to close the tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks whether to leave.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf\"\u003EKeep the takeoff inside the PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/span\u003E downloads a copy of each open PDF, named like \u003Ccode\u003EPlans (with takeoff).pdf\u003C/code\u003E, that carries the whole takeoff as an attachment: items, marks, scales, bid areas, everything the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E holds. Its pages are untouched, so it looks and prints like the original, and other PDF readers list the takeoff under their attachments.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen that PDF in TakeoffCraft (any way you open PDFs) and it asks \u003Cstrong\u003EThis PDF has a takeoff inside\u003C/strong\u003E: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the takeoff\u003C/span\u003E to get it back, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJust the PDF\u003C/span\u003E to only open the drawing. The copy stands for the PDF it was made from, so a \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E saved from either opens on both.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith several PDFs, each copy carries the whole takeoff; open them together. Saving again from a copy replaces the takeoff inside it, it does not add a second one. The \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E is still the file to keep: it is small, and it is what autosave and the desktop app write.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-the-tab-or-the-browser-stops\"\u003EIf the tab or the browser stops\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA couple of seconds after each change, TakeoffCraft keeps a copy of the takeoff in this browser&#039;s own storage, together with its PDFs when they add up to 64 MB or less. Nothing is uploaded. When you save the takeoff, start a new one or open another project, the copy goes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the tab crashes, the browser or computer stops, or you leave with unsaved changes, the next time you open TakeoffCraft it asks \u003Cstrong\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/strong\u003E and lists the takeoff by name. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003ERecover\u003C/strong\u003E to get it back: the items, marks and scales return, and its PDFs open again if they were kept. The status bar says &quot;Recovered (name). It is not saved to a file yet: Save takeoff keeps it.&quot; When the PDFs were too large to keep, open them again; the marks appear on their sheets. More: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover your work\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reopen\"\u003EReopen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen TakeoffCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file and its PDFs on the page together. Or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E for the PDFs and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen takeoff\u003C/span\u003E for the project, in either order. The status bar says &quot;Opened (name): 12 items, 340 marks.&quot; Every sheet gets its scale back and the marks appear on their sheets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe order of the PDFs does not matter, and neither do their file names or folders. TakeoffCraft recognizes each PDF by a fingerprint of its contents.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you open a project while the current takeoff has unsaved changes, TakeoffCraft asks first: \u003Cstrong\u003ECancel\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003ESave takeoff first\u003C/strong\u003E. Opening a project replaces the current items and marks; PDFs that are already open stay open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-pdf-is-missing\"\u003EWhen a PDF is missing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you open the project without all its PDFs, the status bar adds &quot;Open its PDFs to see the marks (listed under Sheets).&quot; Under the sheets list, a note says:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;This takeoff also covers PDFs that are not open. Their quantities are counted; open them to see and edit their marks:&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Efollowed by the file names and sizes. Their marks still count in every total and export, with the scales they were measured at. Open those PDFs to see and edit the marks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-pdf-has-changed\"\u003EWhen a PDF has changed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA PDF that was revised, or just saved again by another program, has different contents, so it is a \u003Cstrong\u003Edifferent file\u003C/strong\u003E to TakeoffCraft, even with the same name. In the note under the sheets list, such a file says &quot;a file with this name is open, but its contents differ&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe marks stay attached to the old version. They keep counting, at their old positions and scales.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe revised PDF opens as new sheets with no scale and no marks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo move the takeoff onto the revision, open both PDFs and use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E on the new sheet: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/compare-revisions\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/compare-revisions\"\u003ECompare a revised sheet and move the takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-damaged-or-foreign-file\"\u003EA damaged or foreign file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft checks every project file before using it. Messages you may see:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;(file) is not a TakeoffCraft project (it is not valid JSON).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This is not a TakeoffCraft project file.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This project was saved by a newer TakeoffCraft. Open it there.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;3 marks were damaged and left out.&quot; (the rest opens)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-history\"\u003EUndo history\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUndo history starts fresh when you open a project. Saving does not clear it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A takeoff is saved as a small project file, (name).takeoff.json. It holds your items, assemblies, rates, every sheet's scale and every mark. It does not hold the PDFs; it finds them again by their contents. Save Choose Save takeoff in the File group, or press Ctrl+S. Your browser downloads (name).takeoff.json. The status bar says \"Saved (name).takeoff.json. It reopens on top of the same PDFs (they are matched by their contents).\" Move the file next to the job's PDFs, so the two stay together. Each save downloads a new file. Your browser may name the second one (name) (1).takeoff.json; keep the newest. The name comes from the first PDF you opened in this takeoff. Save takeoff is still the only way to keep a takeoff as a file. While you work, TakeoffCraft also keeps a recovery copy in this browser (see below), and cloud saving is not available yet. If you try to close the tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks whether to leave. Keep the takeoff inside the PDF Export › PDF with the takeoff inside downloads a copy of each open PDF, named like Plans (with takeoff).pdf, that carries the whole takeoff as an attachment: items, marks, scales, bid areas, everything the .takeoff.json holds. Its pages are untouched, so it looks and prints like the original, and other PDF readers list the takeoff under their attachments. Open that PDF in TakeoffCraft (any way you open PDFs) and it asks This PDF has a takeoff inside: choose Open the takeoff to get it back, or Just the PDF to only open the drawing. The copy stands for the PDF it was made from, so a .takeoff.json saved from either opens on both. With several PDFs, each copy carries the whole takeoff; open them together. Saving again from a copy replaces the takeoff inside it, it does not add a second one. The .takeoff.json is still the file to keep: it is small, and it is what autosave and the desktop app write. If the tab or the browser stops A couple of seconds after each change, TakeoffCraft keeps a copy of the takeoff in this browser's own storage, together with its PDFs when they add up to 64 MB or less. Nothing is uploaded. When you save the takeoff, start a new one or open another project, the copy goes. If the tab crashes, the browser or computer stops, or you leave with unsaved changes, the next time you open TakeoffCraft it asks Recover your work? and lists the takeoff by name. Choose Recover to get it back: the items, marks and scales return, and its PDFs open again if they were kept. The status bar says \"Recovered (name). It is not saved to a file yet: Save takeoff keeps it.\" When the PDFs were too large to keep, open them again; the marks appear on their sheets. More: Recover your work. Reopen Open TakeoffCraft. Drop the .takeoff.json file and its PDFs on the page together. Or choose Open plans for the PDFs and Open takeoff for the project, in either order. The status bar says \"Opened (name): 12 items, 340 marks.\" Every sheet gets its scale back and the marks appear on their sheets. The order of the PDFs does not matter, and neither do their file names or folders. TakeoffCraft recognizes each PDF by a fingerprint of its contents. If you open a project while the current takeoff has unsaved changes, TakeoffCraft asks first: Cancel, Discard changes, or Save takeoff first. Opening a project replaces the current items and marks; PDFs that are already open stay open. When a PDF is missing If you open the project without all its PDFs, the status bar adds \"Open its PDFs to see the marks (listed under Sheets).\" Under the sheets list, a note says: \"This takeoff also covers PDFs that are not open. Their quantities are counted; open them to see and edit their marks:\" followed by the file names and sizes. Their marks still count in every total and export, with the scales they were measured at. Open those PDFs to see and edit the marks. When a PDF has changed A PDF that was revised, or just saved again by another program, has different contents, so it is a different file to TakeoffCraft, even with the same name. In the note under the sheets list, such a file says \"a file with this name is open, but its contents differ\". The marks stay attached to the old version. They keep counting, at their old positions and scales. The revised PDF opens as new sheets with no scale and no marks. To move the takeoff onto the revision, open both PDFs and use Compare on the new sheet: Compare a revised sheet and move the takeoff. A damaged or foreign file TakeoffCraft checks every project file before using it. Messages you may see: \"(file) is not a TakeoffCraft project (it is not valid JSON).\" \"This is not a TakeoffCraft project file.\" \"This project was saved by a newer TakeoffCraft. Open it there.\" \"3 marks were damaged and left out.\" (the rest opens) Undo history Undo history starts fresh when you open a project. Saving does not clear it.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"compare-revisions","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Compare a revised sheet and move the takeoff","summary":"Lay the old sheet over its revision, line them up, see what changed, and move the takeoff onto the new sheet with the marks to check flagged.","section":"tasks","order":115,"keywords":["revision","revised","compare","overlay","old","new","changes","addendum","bulletin","reissue","move takeoff","transfer","carry over","align","flagged","check"],"headings":[{"id":"compare","text":"Compare","level":2},{"id":"line-them-up","text":"Line them up","level":2},{"id":"find-what-changed","text":"Find what changed","level":2},{"id":"move-the-takeoff","text":"Move the takeoff","level":2},{"id":"check-the-flagged-marks","text":"Check the flagged marks","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhen a sheet is reissued, you do not have to take it off again. Open the old PDF and the new one, compare the two sheets, and move the takeoff across. TakeoffCraft flags every mark where the drawing changed, so you check those and keep the rest.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"compare\"\u003ECompare\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen both PDFs: the old set and the revised one (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E; they can be in one file or several).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShow the \u003Cstrong\u003Enew\u003C/strong\u003E sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar under the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the old sheet in the list. TakeoffCraft suggests the sheet with the same number in the other PDF, or the same page. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003ECompare\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe old drawing now lies over the new one in red. Where the two agree, the red lines sit on the black ones; where they differ, you see red lines alone (removed) and black lines alone (added). A bar at the bottom of the sheet holds the steps below; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E ends comparing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"line-them-up\"\u003ELine them up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReissued sheets are often plotted a little off: moved on the page, or at a slightly different size. If the red drawing does not sit on the black one, choose \u003Cstrong\u003EAlign by two points\u003C/strong\u003E and click four times:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea point on the old (red) drawing, such as a column center or a building corner,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe same point on the new (black) drawing,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea second point on the red drawing, far from the first,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe same point on the black drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe red drawing moves, turns and scales onto the black one. The status bar says how much bigger the new drawing is (&quot;size × 1.000&quot; when it is the same). Zoom in for the picks: the closer you click, the better the fit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-what-changed\"\u003EFind what changed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EFind changes\u003C/strong\u003E. TakeoffCraft compares the two drawings&#039; ink: ink only on the old sheet turns red, ink only on the new sheet turns blue, and the status bar counts them. Lines that moved by a hair (a re-plot) are not counted as changes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt also looks at every mark on the old sheet and counts the ones that sit where the drawing changed:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea run or an area when the drawing changed at one of its ends or corners, or along at least about a sixth of its length;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea count when anything changed right next to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-the-takeoff\"\u003EMove the takeoff\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EMove takeoff here\u003C/strong\u003E. Every mark on the old sheet moves to the new one, through the alignment, in one undo step. The ones next to a change are flagged: they are drawn with an orange halo, and the sheet bar shows &quot;(n) to check&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the new sheet had no scale, it takes the old sheet&#039;s scale (adjusted when the drawings had to be scaled to line up). If it already had a different scale, TakeoffCraft asks first: moved marks are measured at the new sheet&#039;s scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-the-flagged-marks\"\u003ECheck the flagged marks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose &quot;(n) to check&quot; on the sheet bar. It zooms to the next flagged mark and selects it; its panel says why it is flagged.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFix the mark if the drawing changed under it (drag its corners, add or delete marks, or take it off again).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EChecked\u003C/strong\u003E in its panel. The flag goes, and the count on the sheet bar drops.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe flags are saved with the takeoff, so you can check them later.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EComparing works on vector PDFs and on scans alike, because it compares what is drawn. A scan that is skewed or stretched may line up only roughly with two points; check more marks by eye then.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"When a sheet is reissued, you do not have to take it off again. Open the old PDF and the new one, compare the two sheets, and move the takeoff across. TakeoffCraft flags every mark where the drawing changed, so you check those and keep the rest. Compare Open both PDFs: the old set and the revised one (Open plans; they can be in one file or several). Show the new sheet. Choose Compare on the sheet bar under the drawing. Pick the old sheet in the list. TakeoffCraft suggests the sheet with the same number in the other PDF, or the same page. Choose Compare. The old drawing now lies over the new one in red. Where the two agree, the red lines sit on the black ones; where they differ, you see red lines alone (removed) and black lines alone (added). A bar at the bottom of the sheet holds the steps below; Esc or Close ends comparing. Line them up Reissued sheets are often plotted a little off: moved on the page, or at a slightly different size. If the red drawing does not sit on the black one, choose Align by two points and click four times: a point on the old (red) drawing, such as a column center or a building corner, the same point on the new (black) drawing, a second point on the red drawing, far from the first, the same point on the black drawing. The red drawing moves, turns and scales onto the black one. The status bar says how much bigger the new drawing is (\"size × 1.000\" when it is the same). Zoom in for the picks: the closer you click, the better the fit. Find what changed Choose Find changes. TakeoffCraft compares the two drawings' ink: ink only on the old sheet turns red, ink only on the new sheet turns blue, and the status bar counts them. Lines that moved by a hair (a re-plot) are not counted as changes. It also looks at every mark on the old sheet and counts the ones that sit where the drawing changed: a run or an area when the drawing changed at one of its ends or corners, or along at least about a sixth of its length; a count when anything changed right next to it. Move the takeoff Choose Move takeoff here. Every mark on the old sheet moves to the new one, through the alignment, in one undo step. The ones next to a change are flagged: they are drawn with an orange halo, and the sheet bar shows \"(n) to check\". If the new sheet had no scale, it takes the old sheet's scale (adjusted when the drawings had to be scaled to line up). If it already had a different scale, TakeoffCraft asks first: moved marks are measured at the new sheet's scale. Check the flagged marks Choose \"(n) to check\" on the sheet bar. It zooms to the next flagged mark and selects it; its panel says why it is flagged. Fix the mark if the drawing changed under it (drag its corners, add or delete marks, or take it off again). Choose Checked in its panel. The flag goes, and the count on the sheet bar drops. The flags are saved with the takeoff, so you can check them later. Comparing works on vector PDFs and on scans alike, because it compares what is drawn. A scan that is skewed or stretched may line up only roughly with two points; check more marks by eye then.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"use-with-ai-assistants","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Use TakeoffCraft with an AI assistant","summary":"Let your own AI assistant or script read the takeoff's quantities and estimate, add items and marks, count and measure on a DWG or DXF, and export: permissions, connecting through CadCraft Studio, and the activity log.","section":"tasks","order":118,"keywords":["ai","assistant","agent","mcp","model context protocol","script","automation","quantities","estimate","permissions","disconnect","activity"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"connect-your-assistant","text":"Connect your assistant","level":2},{"id":"you-stay-in-charge","text":"You stay in charge","level":2},{"id":"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for","text":"What an assistant can ask for","level":2},{"id":"if-something-doesn-t-work","text":"If something doesn't work","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with TakeoffCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft has \u003Cstrong\u003Eno AI of its own\u003C/strong\u003E and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). TakeoffCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use TakeoffCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. It is off until you do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what it may do: \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked to start with; tick \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESave\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E only when you need them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, TakeoffCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset TakeoffCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E turns it off too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"connect-your-assistant\"\u003EConnect your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssistants connect through \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect an assistant\u003C/span\u003E and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings to paste). Never share the token.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser tab can&#039;t open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"you-stay-in-charge\"\u003EYou stay in charge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery change is one undo step\u003C/strong\u003E, labelled with the assistant&#039;s name for it (for example &quot;Assistant: …&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back the assistant&#039;s last change as a whole.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou know when one is connected.\u003C/strong\u003E A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActivity.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActivity\u003C/span\u003E lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the log\u003C/span\u003E keeps a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003EWhat an assistant can ask for\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPermission\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eapp_info\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esheets_list\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe takeoff open, its PDFs and sheets, each sheet&#039;s scale and marks\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eitems_list\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe items with their units, rates and assembly formulas\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_quantities\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantities per item or per sheet; marks on sheets with no scale are listed, never counted as zero\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_estimate\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe priced estimate as TakeoffCraft computes it, with its totals\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_export\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft&#039;s own CSV or XLSX export\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eitems_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdds items with their units, rates and waste\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emarks_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasures runs, areas and counts on a sheet. Lengths and areas only on a sheet with a scale, never on an unscaled one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ecad_sheets\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecad_catalog\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecad_count\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn a DWG or DXF you opened: its sheets and their scales, what can be counted (blocks, layers and types, texts), and exact counts, as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECAD count\u003C/span\u003E makes them (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\"\u003ECount from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ecad_count_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECounts in the drawing and puts a count mark on each object found, in one step; counting again adds only what is new\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ecad_measure\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasures a linear or an area item from the drawing&#039;s own lines, arcs, polylines and hatches, as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure drawing\u003C/span\u003E does (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-from-a-drawing\"\u003EMeasure from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E); only on a sheet with a scale\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eusd_export\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA scaled sheet&#039;s measured runs, areas and counts as a 3D check model in USD (\u003Ccode\u003E.usdz\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.usda\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.usdc\u003C/code\u003E), as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD package (.usdz)\u003C/span\u003E in the 3D check view writes it (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/usd\"\u003EExport the 3D check view as USD\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E ticked, an assistant adds items and marks (each call one undo step); it cannot move, change or delete marks that are already there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same quantities and estimate are available without opening TakeoffCraft: the CadCraft MCP server opens a \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file in a folder you name (\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_open\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_quantities\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_estimate\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_export\u003C/code\u003E), and \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft info Job.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E prints them on the command line. It also opens a DWG or DXF as a new takeoff on its sheets (scaled from the drawing as TakeoffCraft scales them), counts and measures on it with the same \u003Ccode\u003Ecad_*\u003C/code\u003E tools, adds items and saves the takeoff (\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_save\u003C/code\u003E); \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft count plan.dwg --block SHELF-18X48 --sheet K-101\u003C/code\u003E counts from the command line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-something-doesn-t-work\"\u003EIf something doesn&#039;t work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe assistant sees no TakeoffCraft tools:\u003C/strong\u003E check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;needs the export permission&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E tick \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, then ask again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;401&quot; or &quot;token&quot; errors:\u003C/strong\u003E the token changed (Studio restarted, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew token\u003C/span\u003E). Copy the new one into the assistant&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-also\"\u003ESee also\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003ERecipes for AI assistants\u003C/a\u003E: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with TakeoffCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch. TakeoffCraft has no AI of its own and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). TakeoffCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,) and go to the TakeoffCraft tab. Under Assistants, tick Let AI assistants and scripts use TakeoffCraft. It is off until you do. Choose what it may do: Read is ticked to start with; tick Edit, Save or Export only when you need them. Tools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, TakeoffCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once; Reset TakeoffCraft settings turns it off too. Connect your assistant Assistants connect through CadCraft Studio on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose Connect an assistant and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings to paste). Never share the token. A browser tab can't open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer. You stay in charge Every change is one undo step, labelled with the assistant's name for it (for example \"Assistant: …\"). Ctrl+Z takes back the assistant's last change as a whole. You know when one is connected. A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to Disconnect it. Activity. Settings › TakeoffCraft › Assistants › Activity lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. Save the log keeps a copy. What an assistant can ask for Tool Permission What it does app_info, sheets_list Read The takeoff open, its PDFs and sheets, each sheet's scale and marks items_list Read The items with their units, rates and assembly formulas takeoff_quantities Read Quantities per item or per sheet; marks on sheets with no scale are listed, never counted as zero takeoff_estimate Read The priced estimate as TakeoffCraft computes it, with its totals takeoff_export Export TakeoffCraft's own CSV or XLSX export items_add Edit Adds items with their units, rates and waste marks_add Edit Measures runs, areas and counts on a sheet. Lengths and areas only on a sheet with a scale, never on an unscaled one cad_sheets, cad_catalog, cad_count Read On a DWG or DXF you opened: its sheets and their scales, what can be counted (blocks, layers and types, texts), and exact counts, as CAD count makes them (Count from a drawing) cad_count_add Edit Counts in the drawing and puts a count mark on each object found, in one step; counting again adds only what is new cad_measure Edit Measures a linear or an area item from the drawing's own lines, arcs, polylines and hatches, as Measure drawing does (Measure from a drawing); only on a sheet with a scale usd_export Export A scaled sheet's measured runs, areas and counts as a 3D check model in USD (.usdz, .usda or .usdc), as USD package (.usdz) in the 3D check view writes it (Export the 3D check view as USD) With Edit ticked, an assistant adds items and marks (each call one undo step); it cannot move, change or delete marks that are already there. The same quantities and estimate are available without opening TakeoffCraft: the CadCraft MCP server opens a .takeoff.json file in a folder you name (takeoff_open, takeoff_quantities, takeoff_estimate, takeoff_export), and cadcraft info Job.takeoff.json prints them on the command line. It also opens a DWG or DXF as a new takeoff on its sheets (scaled from the drawing as TakeoffCraft scales them), counts and measures on it with the same cad_* tools, adds items and saves the takeoff (takeoff_save); cadcraft count plan.dwg --block SHELF-18X48 --sheet K-101 counts from the command line. If something doesn't work The assistant sees no TakeoffCraft tools: check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio. \"needs the export permission\": tick Export under Assistants, then ask again. \"401\" or \"token\" errors: the token changed (Studio restarted, or New token). Copy the new one into the assistant's settings. See also Recipes for AI assistants: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-live","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Take off one job together with Live TeamWork","summary":"What changes in TakeoffCraft while you are in a Live TeamWork session from CadCraft Studio: everyone's items and marks in one takeoff, the same totals for all, your own undo, and joining from a browser on the same network.","section":"tasks","order":119,"keywords":["live","live teamwork","studio live","together","collaborate","session","share live","join","items","marks","totals","estimate","undo","host"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"everyone-measures-at-once","text":"Everyone measures at once","level":2},{"id":"undo-undoes-your-own-work","text":"Undo undoes your own work","level":2},{"id":"join-from-a-browser","text":"Join from a browser","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat happens to a takeoff while several people measure the same job at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the Live TeamWork session (Share live, Join); this page is about the takeoff itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everyone-measures-at-once\"\u003EEveryone measures at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host&#039;s PDFs come to everyone who joins. Add items, measure runs, areas and counts, set scales and prices as usual. Your own marks show at once; other people&#039;s appear a moment after they make them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETotals are the same for everyone:\u003C/strong\u003E each window works the quantities and the estimate out from the marks, so nobody&#039;s totals can drift.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo people change one item or mark:\u003C/strong\u003E different things are both kept; the same thing goes to the later change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA mark on an item someone just deleted\u003C/strong\u003E is taken back, and the status bar says why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach person&#039;s new items and marks get numbers of their own, so two people never make the same one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-undoes-your-own-work\"\u003EUndo undoes your own work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes your own last change and leaves everyone else&#039;s alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-from-a-browser\"\u003EJoin from a browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. TakeoffCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host&#039;s takeoff and its PDFs once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the host saves the takeoff. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn What happens to a takeoff while several people measure the same job at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the Live TeamWork session (Share live, Join); this page is about the takeoff itself. Everyone measures at once The host's PDFs come to everyone who joins. Add items, measure runs, areas and counts, set scales and prices as usual. Your own marks show at once; other people's appear a moment after they make them. Totals are the same for everyone: each window works the quantities and the estimate out from the marks, so nobody's totals can drift. Two people change one item or mark: different things are both kept; the same thing goes to the later change. A mark on an item someone just deleted is taken back, and the status bar says why. Each person's new items and marks get numbers of their own, so two people never make the same one. Undo undoes your own work Ctrl+Z undoes your own last change and leaves everyone else's alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who. Join from a browser A teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host's code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. TakeoffCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host's takeoff and its PDFs once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet. When the session ends Only the host saves the takeoff. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"new-takeoff","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Start a new takeoff","summary":"Clear everything and start an empty takeoff without reloading the page, and keep your work safe first.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["new","new takeoff","clear","reset","start over","empty","close pdfs","next job"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E closes every PDF and starts an empty takeoff on the same page.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave the current takeoff if you want to keep it: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf there are unsaved changes, TakeoffCraft asks \u003Cstrong\u003EUnsaved takeoff\u003C/strong\u003E: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff first\u003C/span\u003E (it downloads the project, then clears), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E. The window goes back to its empty state: no PDFs, no items, no marks, no scales. The status bar says &quot;New takeoff. Open PDFs to begin.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat is cleared:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eevery open PDF, its sheets and thumbnails,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eitems, assemblies, marks and scales,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eeach sheet&#039;s zoom and position,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe undo history (you cannot undo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Earc mode, and any auto count search or review.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour files on disk are not touched. To pick up the old takeoff again, open its \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E and PDFs: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach new takeoff starts with no items. To bring items over, copy them first: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy item\u003C/span\u003E under Items (or copy marks, which carry their items), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E in the new takeoff. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate marks\u003C/a\u003E. For items you use on every job, keep them in your \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/price-library\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/price-library\"\u003Eprice library\u003C/a\u003E and add them from there, or install a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/trade-packs\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/trade-packs\"\u003Etrade pack\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"New closes every PDF and starts an empty takeoff on the same page. Save the current takeoff if you want to keep it: Save takeoff or Ctrl+S. Choose New in the File group. If there are unsaved changes, TakeoffCraft asks Unsaved takeoff: choose Save takeoff first (it downloads the project, then clears), Discard changes, or Cancel. The window goes back to its empty state: no PDFs, no items, no marks, no scales. The status bar says \"New takeoff. Open PDFs to begin.\" What is cleared: every open PDF, its sheets and thumbnails, items, assemblies, marks and scales, each sheet's zoom and position, the undo history (you cannot undo New), arc mode, and any auto count search or review. Your files on disk are not touched. To pick up the old takeoff again, open its .takeoff.json and PDFs: see Save and reopen a takeoff. Each new takeoff starts with no items. To bring items over, copy them first: Copy item under Items (or copy marks, which carry their items), then Paste in the new takeoff. See Copy, paste and duplicate marks. For items you use on every job, keep them in your price library and add them from there, or install a trade pack.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"usd","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Export the 3D check view as USD","summary":"Write the walls, slabs, plates and runs of the 3D check view as a USD package, each with its item and quantity.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["usd","usdz","openusd","export","3d","check view","walls","slabs","rendering"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIn the 3D check view, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD package (.usdz)\u003C/span\u003E beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGLB\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach object (a wall, a slab, a plate, a count post or a run) is one object in the file, named after its item, carrying the item&#039;s name and the quantity the view measured. Slabs and plates are see-through as in the view. The file is in metres with Z up.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"In the 3D check view, choose USD package (.usdz) beside GLB. Each object (a wall, a slab, a plate, a count post or a run) is one object in the file, named after its item, carrying the item's name and the quantity the view measured. Slabs and plates are see-through as in the view. The file is in metres with Z up.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Coming from other takeoff software","summary":"How the habits of dedicated takeoff programs and PDF markup tools map to TakeoffCraft, and the differences that trip people up.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switching","migrate","conditions","parts","measurement tools","markups list","quantity link","takeoff habits","differences","compare"],"headings":[{"id":"habits-mapped","text":"Habits mapped","level":2},{"id":"differences-that-trip-people-up","text":"Differences that trip people up","level":2},{"id":"not-here-yet","text":"Not here yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you have done takeoff in PlanSwift or with the measurement tools in Bluebeam Revu, most of TakeoffCraft will feel familiar: a plan set, a scale per sheet, things you measure, and a priced total. The way the pieces fit together is different in places, and those places are below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you know PlanSwift or On-Screen Takeoff, pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff desk\u003C/span\u003E menu style in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E: a menu bar with File, Edit, View, Scale, Takeoff, Items, Markup and Export over a row of the takeoff tools, with the scale presets on the sheet bar. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"habits-mapped\"\u003EHabits mapped\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou are used to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn TakeoffCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening a job or project that stores the plans (PlanSwift)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the PDFs and the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E project side by side. The project stores only the takeoff; the PDFs stay your own files.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA markups list with notes, clouds and highlights (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkup\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar: notes, clouds and highlights with a status (Open, Answered, Closed), listed under \u003Cstrong\u003EMarkups\u003C/strong\u003E, drawn in the marked PDF. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/markups\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/markups\"\u003ENotes, clouds and highlights\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasurements saved as markups inside the PDF (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe takeoff lives in the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E writes a copy with the takeoff drawn in, for sharing; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/span\u003E writes a copy that carries the takeoff, to reopen in TakeoffCraft.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoff items (PlanSwift), custom measurement tools saved in the Tool Chest (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EItems\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E: name, type, color, unit, height or depth, waste, rates.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParts and assemblies with formulas (PlanSwift)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E: components with a formula using \u003Ccode\u003EL H A N P\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESetting the page scale, or calibrating (both)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E group, per sheet. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E measures a known dimension.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApplying one scale to a page range\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot yet: set each sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EViewports with different scales on one sheet (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot yet: one scale per sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylength and area tools (Bluebeam), linear and area takeoff (PlanSwift)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHolding Shift for straight lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E snaps to 45° steps, the same idea.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnapping to the drawing&#039;s lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn by default: ends, midpoints, intersections and the nearest point on a line, with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn it off, as in CAD programs.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount tool (both)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVisual search for symbols (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): box one symbol, review, add.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut-outs or deductions in areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) on a selected area, and round cut-outs.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDeductions on linear runs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the run straight through and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E) for the opening. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-linear\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-linear\"\u003EMeasure linear runs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArc segments in a polyline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): click a point on the curve, then its end.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe Markups list with quantity columns (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy item\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy sheet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEstimating grid with pricing (PlanSwift)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same table: waste, material and labor per unit, extended totals, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJob total\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExporting to Excel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E with live formulas, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEscape to stop a tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels and returns to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click to finish\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click finishes a run or closes an area here too. There is no right-click menu.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-that-trip-people-up\"\u003EDifferences that trip people up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe takeoff is not inside your PDF.\u003C/strong\u003E Nothing you do changes your PDFs. Save the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file and keep it with the drawings. Sending someone the marked PDF does not send them an editable takeoff: send the project file and the original PDFs, or a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen#keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-hash=\"keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPDFs are matched by contents, not by name.\u003C/strong\u003E A revised sheet saved over the old file name is a new file to TakeoffCraft, with no scale and no marks. Your old marks stay on the old version and keep counting. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen#when-a-pdf-has-changed\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-hash=\"when-a-pdf-has-changed\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnscaled marks are left out, not counted as zero.\u003C/strong\u003E If a sheet has no scale, its runs and areas do not count, and the table says &quot;Incomplete&quot;. In many programs you would just see 0. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale#why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-hash=\"why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThere is no page-size guessing.\u003C/strong\u003E Presets assume the PDF is printed at full size. A half-size or &quot;fit to page&quot; PDF needs \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E. Always use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne item, one main type, more if you ask.\u003C/strong\u003E An item is linear, area or count, and its type locks once it has marks. A condition that gives you both an area and its perimeter is an area item: its perimeter is the \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E variable. A wall with both length and area is a linear item with a height. For a multi-condition item, such as a room with a floor, a base and doors, tick the other kinds under \u003Cstrong\u003EAlso measure\u003C/strong\u003E: each is priced on its own row. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items#one-item-several-kinds-of-marks\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-hash=\"one-item-several-kinds-of-marks\"\u003EOne item, several kinds of marks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFormulas run on totals.\u003C/strong\u003E Assembly formulas use the item&#039;s totals across every sheet, not each run separately. \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E (the number of runs or areas) is there for per-run extras, like one extra stud per wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPerimeter includes cut-outs.\u003C/strong\u003E An area&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E adds the edges of its holes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-areas#read-the-area\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-areas\" data-hash=\"read-the-area\"\u003EMeasure areas with cut-outs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDragging pans.\u003C/strong\u003E A left-button drag on the sheet pans, in every tool except \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E. You never need to switch to a pan tool. The wheel zooms; it does not scroll.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA recovery copy, not a cloud.\u003C/strong\u003E TakeoffCraft keeps a copy of your work in this browser and offers it back after a crash (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen#if-the-tab-or-the-browser-stops\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-hash=\"if-the-tab-or-the-browser-stops\"\u003EIf the tab or the browser stops\u003C/a\u003E), but the takeoff is yours to save: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Cloud saving is not available yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything is in the browser.\u003C/strong\u003E There is nothing to install, and the plans are never uploaded. For a desktop app, TakeoffCraft comes in CadCraft Studio, with the other seven apps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-here-yet\"\u003ENot here yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPer-sheet scale regions are not built yet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E for the full list and what to do instead.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you have done takeoff in PlanSwift or with the measurement tools in Bluebeam Revu, most of TakeoffCraft will feel familiar: a plan set, a scale per sheet, things you measure, and a priced total. The way the pieces fit together is different in places, and those places are below. If you know PlanSwift or On-Screen Takeoff, pick the Takeoff desk menu style in Settings › TakeoffCraft › Menus, keys and mouse: a menu bar with File, Edit, View, Scale, Takeoff, Items, Markup and Export over a row of the takeoff tools, with the scale presets on the sheet bar. See Menus, keys and mouse. Habits mapped You are used to In TakeoffCraft Opening a job or project that stores the plans (PlanSwift) Open the PDFs and the .takeoff.json project side by side. The project stores only the takeoff; the PDFs stay your own files. A markups list with notes, clouds and highlights (Bluebeam) Markup on the sheet bar: notes, clouds and highlights with a status (Open, Answered, Closed), listed under Markups, drawn in the marked PDF. See Notes, clouds and highlights. Measurements saved as markups inside the PDF (Bluebeam) The takeoff lives in the .takeoff.json file. Marked PDF writes a copy with the takeoff drawn in, for sharing; PDF with the takeoff inside writes a copy that carries the takeoff, to reopen in TakeoffCraft. Takeoff items (PlanSwift), custom measurement tools saved in the Tool Chest (Bluebeam) Items under Items: name, type, color, unit, height or depth, waste, rates. Parts and assemblies with formulas (PlanSwift) The item's Assembly: components with a formula using L H A N P. Setting the page scale, or calibrating (both) Preset list or Calibrate in the Scale group, per sheet. Check measures a known dimension. Applying one scale to a page range Not yet: set each sheet. Viewports with different scales on one sheet (Bluebeam) Not yet: one scale per sheet. Polylength and area tools (Bluebeam), linear and area takeoff (PlanSwift) Linear (L) and Area (A). Holding Shift for straight lines Shift snaps to 45° steps, the same idea. Snapping to the drawing's lines On by default: ends, midpoints, intersections and the nearest point on a line, with F3 to turn it off, as in CAD programs. Count tool (both) Count (C). Visual search for symbols (Bluebeam) Auto count (S): box one symbol, review, add. Cut-outs or deductions in areas Cut-out (X) on a selected area, and round cut-outs. Deductions on linear runs Draw the run straight through and press E (Deduct) for the opening. See Measure linear runs. Arc segments in a polyline Arc (R): click a point on the curve, then its end. The Markups list with quantity columns (Bluebeam) The Quantities table, By item or By sheet. Estimating grid with pricing (PlanSwift) The same table: waste, material and labor per unit, extended totals, Job total. Exporting to Excel XLSX with live formulas, or CSV. Escape to stop a tool Esc cancels and returns to Select. Right-click to finish Right-click finishes a run or closes an area here too. There is no right-click menu. Differences that trip people up The takeoff is not inside your PDF. Nothing you do changes your PDFs. Save the .takeoff.json file and keep it with the drawings. Sending someone the marked PDF does not send them an editable takeoff: send the project file and the original PDFs, or a PDF with the takeoff inside. PDFs are matched by contents, not by name. A revised sheet saved over the old file name is a new file to TakeoffCraft, with no scale and no marks. Your old marks stay on the old version and keep counting. See Save and reopen a takeoff. Unscaled marks are left out, not counted as zero. If a sheet has no scale, its runs and areas do not count, and the table says \"Incomplete\". In many programs you would just see 0. See Set the scale. There is no page-size guessing. Presets assume the PDF is printed at full size. A half-size or \"fit to page\" PDF needs Calibrate. Always use Check. One item, one main type, more if you ask. An item is linear, area or count, and its type locks once it has marks. A condition that gives you both an area and its perimeter is an area item: its perimeter is the P variable. A wall with both length and area is a linear item with a height. For a multi-condition item, such as a room with a floor, a base and doors, tick the other kinds under Also measure: each is priced on its own row. See One item, several kinds of marks. Formulas run on totals. Assembly formulas use the item's totals across every sheet, not each run separately. N (the number of runs or areas) is there for per-run extras, like one extra stud per wall. Perimeter includes cut-outs. An area's P adds the edges of its holes. See Measure areas with cut-outs. Dragging pans. A left-button drag on the sheet pans, in every tool except Auto count. You never need to switch to a pan tool. The wheel zooms; it does not scroll. A recovery copy, not a cloud. TakeoffCraft keeps a copy of your work in this browser and offers it back after a crash (If the tab or the browser stops), but the takeoff is yours to save: Ctrl+S. Cloud saving is not available yet. Everything is in the browser. There is nothing to install, and the plans are never uploaded. For a desktop app, TakeoffCraft comes in CadCraft Studio, with the other seven apps. Not here yet Per-sheet scale regions are not built yet. See What is not built yet for the full list and what to do instead.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"settings","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"TakeoffCraft settings","summary":"Show inches to 1/16, 1/8 or 1/4, start new items with a waste percentage, choose where the Auto count slider starts, snap to the drawing's lines or to 45° without holding Shift, set how wide a door Room fill closes, and pick the 3D view's corner indicator.","section":"reference","order":5,"keywords":["settings","preferences","options","precision","fractions","inches","rounding","waste","default waste","auto count","threshold","match","slider","snap","45","angle","shift","view cube","axis icon","corner indicator","3d"],"headings":[{"id":"feet-and-inches","text":"Feet and inches","level":2},{"id":"waste-to-start-with","text":"Waste to start with","level":2},{"id":"auto-count-where-the-match-slider-starts","text":"Auto count: where the match slider starts","level":2},{"id":"snap-to-the-drawing-s-own-lines","text":"Snap to the drawing's own lines","level":2},{"id":"room-fill-how-wide-an-opening-it-closes","text":"Room fill: how wide an opening it closes","level":2},{"id":"snap-to-45-without-holding-shift","text":"Snap to 45° without holding Shift","level":2},{"id":"corner-indicator","text":"Corner indicator","level":2},{"id":"path-tool-the-largest-gap-it-joins","text":"Path tool: the largest gap it joins","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the app bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, panels, toolbar). TakeoffCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset TakeoffCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E puts them back to the start.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"feet-and-inches\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShow lengths to the nearest \u003Cstrong\u003E1/16&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E (the start), \u003Cstrong\u003E1/8&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003E1/4&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E. This changes how lengths read on the sheet, in the panels, in the quantities and in the status bar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the display is rounded. Quantities, costs and the exports are worked out from the exact lengths, and the exports always show 1/16&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"waste-to-start-with\"\u003EWaste to start with\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe waste percentage every new item starts with: 0% (the start), 2%, 3%, 5%, 7.5%, 10%, 15% or 20%. You can still type another waste for each item, in the item editor or straight in the quantities table. Items you already have keep their own waste.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"auto-count-where-the-match-slider-starts\"\u003EAuto count: where the match slider starts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch\u003C/span\u003E slider sits when an \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/auto-count\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/auto-count\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/a\u003E search finishes, from 50% to 95%; 75% is the start. Lower offers more look-alikes for you to reject; higher offers fewer. You can still move the slider during each review.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"snap-to-the-drawing-s-own-lines\"\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s own lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn at the start. While you measure or calibrate, a point near a line of the PDF jumps to its end, its midpoint, a crossing or the nearest point on it. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar turn it on and off too. More: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\"\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s lines\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"room-fill-how-wide-an-opening-it-closes\"\u003ERoom fill: how wide an opening it closes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom fill closes openings up to\u003C/span\u003E sets how wide a door opening or gap the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E tool treats as closed: 3&#039;-0&quot; to 8&#039;-0&quot;, 4&#039;-0&quot; at the start. Wider than your widest door lets a room run into the next one; narrower than a door leaves the room open. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-areas#fill-a-room-with-one-click\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-areas\" data-hash=\"fill-a-room-with-one-click\"\u003EFill a room with one click\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"snap-to-45-without-holding-shift\"\u003ESnap to 45° without holding Shift\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOff at the start. When it is on, runs, outlines, cut-outs and dragged corners go at 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° from the last point without holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to click at any angle. The status bar then reads &quot;Snaps to 45°; Shift frees the angle&quot;. The point you click on an arc&#039;s curve and circles are never snapped; an arc&#039;s end is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-indicator\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\"\u003E3D check view\u003C/a\u003E only: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView cube\u003C/span\u003E (the start: a cube at the top right to click, drag and turn), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis icon\u003C/span\u003E (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom right) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENone\u003C/span\u003E. It changes at once and is kept in this browser. The flat sheet never shows one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"path-tool-the-largest-gap-it-joins\"\u003EPath tool: the largest gap it joins\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPath tool: largest gap to join\u003C/span\u003E is how far apart two line ends can be and still count as one path, in PDF points (0.05 to 60; 1.5 at the start). A drawing&#039;s lines often stop a hair short of each other. Larger joins more; smaller keeps lines apart. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"[\"\u003E[\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E[\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"]\"\u003E]\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E]\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E change it while you pick, and this field follows. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/pick-the-lines\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/pick-the-lines\"\u003EPick the lines\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the app bar or Ctrl+,. The first tab, TakeoffCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, panels, toolbar). TakeoffCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. Reset TakeoffCraft settings puts them back to the start. Feet and inches Show lengths to the nearest 1/16\" (the start), 1/8\" or 1/4\". This changes how lengths read on the sheet, in the panels, in the quantities and in the status bar. Only the display is rounded. Quantities, costs and the exports are worked out from the exact lengths, and the exports always show 1/16\". Waste to start with The waste percentage every new item starts with: 0% (the start), 2%, 3%, 5%, 7.5%, 10%, 15% or 20%. You can still type another waste for each item, in the item editor or straight in the quantities table. Items you already have keep their own waste. Auto count: where the match slider starts Where the Match slider sits when an Auto count search finishes, from 50% to 95%; 75% is the start. Lower offers more look-alikes for you to reject; higher offers fewer. You can still move the slider during each review. Snap to the drawing's own lines On at the start. While you measure or calibrate, a point near a line of the PDF jumps to its end, its midpoint, a crossing or the nearest point on it. F3 and Snap in the status bar turn it on and off too. More: Snap to the drawing's lines. Room fill: how wide an opening it closes Room fill closes openings up to sets how wide a door opening or gap the Room tool treats as closed: 3'-0\" to 8'-0\", 4'-0\" at the start. Wider than your widest door lets a room run into the next one; narrower than a door leaves the room open. See Fill a room with one click. Snap to 45° without holding Shift Off at the start. When it is on, runs, outlines, cut-outs and dragged corners go at 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° from the last point without holding Shift; hold Shift to click at any angle. The status bar then reads \"Snaps to 45°; Shift frees the angle\". The point you click on an arc's curve and circles are never snapped; an arc's end is. Corner indicator For the 3D check view only: View cube (the start: a cube at the top right to click, drag and turn), Axis icon (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom right) or None. It changes at once and is kept in this browser. The flat sheet never shows one. Path tool: the largest gap it joins Path tool: largest gap to join is how far apart two line ends can be and still count as one path, in PDF points (0.05 to 60; 1.5 at the start). A drawing's lines often stop a hair short of each other. Larger joins more; smaller keeps lines apart. [ and ] change it while you pick, and this field follows. See Pick the lines.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"menus-keys-and-mouse","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Menus, keys and mouse","summary":"Pick a menu style that matches the way you already work, give any command your own key, move commands between menus, and set what the wheel and the mouse buttons do.","section":"reference","order":6,"keywords":["menu style","menus","menu bar","ribbon","ribbon tabs","toolbar","keyboard shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","customize","remap","rebind","mouse","wheel","invert","zoom direction","middle button","right button","pan","export","import","switching","familiar","takeoff desk","conditions"],"headings":[{"id":"the-three-styles","text":"The three styles","level":2},{"id":"make-it-your-own","text":"Make it your own","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"take-it-with-you","text":"Take it with you","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft&#039;s commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A \u003Cstrong\u003Emenu style\u003C/strong\u003E only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-three-styles\"\u003EThe three styles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStyle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft&#039;s own toolbar: File, Export, Scale, Takeoff, Edit and View, with the single-letter tool keys. The start.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff desk\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA menu bar (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkup\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E) over one row of the takeoff tools, the way on-screen takeoff programs lay theirs out. Items are called conditions on the row. The same keys, plus \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a new item. The scale presets move to the sheet bar, beside the sheet&#039;s scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff ribbon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERibbon tabs named for the work: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff\u003C/span\u003E (measure, shape, scale, from the drawing, conditions), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkup\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEstimate\u003C/span\u003E (the workbook, marked PDFs, the bid), with a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu and a quick row for open, save, undo and redo. The tools you use all day are large, their neighbours small and stacked. The same keys; the scale presets move to the sheet bar.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three styles keep TakeoffCraft&#039;s keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E select, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E linear, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E area, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E circle, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E room, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E path (pick the PDF&#039;s lines), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cut-out, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E count, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E auto count, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E calibrate, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E check, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E arc mode, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deduct mode, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to fit, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E the 3D check view, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E snap, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sheet bar&#039;s tools (notes, clouds, highlights, Compare, 3D) wait until a PDF is open; picked before that, the status line says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA key that belongs to TakeoffCraft&#039;s own layout but that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (&quot;In My keys, Count is Ctrl+Alt+C&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the arrows always keep their meaning. While you draw a run or an outline, paste or review an auto count, the letters and the numbers of a typed length stay with the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-it-your-own\"\u003EMake it your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the style closest to what you want, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustomize…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys and menus\u003C/span\u003E, find a command (the search box helps). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a key\u003C/span\u003E and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith the menu bar, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere it sits\u003C/span\u003E moves the command to another menu, or to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys only\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMouse\u003C/span\u003E, set the wheel and the buttons that pan the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving a built-in style makes your own copy (&quot;My Takeoff desk&quot;); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms in\u003C/span\u003E (the start) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms out\u003C/span\u003E, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan the sheet with\u003C/strong\u003E: the middle button, the right button, or both. A left drag on empty paper and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with a drag always pan; a right click without dragging always finishes a run or an outline.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag in the 3D views\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEach view&#039;s own\u003C/span\u003E keeps the 3D check view&#039;s buttons (left and middle orbit, right pans). Or set the middle and right buttons to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the button swaps orbit and pan. The left button always orbits and picks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-it-with-you\"\u003ETake it with you\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the style in use as a small \u003Ccode\u003E.ccinput.json\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E adds one, from another computer or from a colleague. An imported file can only point at TakeoffCraft&#039;s own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status line. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this style\u003C/span\u003E removes one of your own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese settings stay in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this style in every app\u003C/span\u003E under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a menu bar or a ribbon), with the changes you made in each app. TakeoffCraft has no style with a command line: when one is chosen for every app, TakeoffCraft keeps its own toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff ribbon\u003C/span\u003E is also the ribbon of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERibbon and project browser\u003C/span\u003E layout (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E): see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/workspace\"\u003EThe TakeoffCraft screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelated: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/settings\"\u003ETakeoffCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other takeoff software\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft's commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A menu style only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in Settings (Ctrl+,) › TakeoffCraft › Menus, keys and mouse; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it. The three styles Style What you get TakeoffCraft TakeoffCraft's own toolbar: File, Export, Scale, Takeoff, Edit and View, with the single-letter tool keys. The start. Takeoff desk A menu bar (File, Edit, View, Scale, Takeoff, Items, Markup, Export) over one row of the takeoff tools, the way on-screen takeoff programs lay theirs out. Items are called conditions on the row. The same keys, plus N for a new item. The scale presets move to the sheet bar, beside the sheet's scale. Takeoff ribbon Ribbon tabs named for the work: Takeoff (measure, shape, scale, from the drawing, conditions), Edit, View, Markup and Estimate (the workbook, marked PDFs, the bid), with a File menu and a quick row for open, save, undo and redo. The tools you use all day are large, their neighbours small and stacked. The same keys; the scale presets move to the sheet bar. All three styles keep TakeoffCraft's keys: V select, L linear, A area, O circle, B room, P path (pick the PDF's lines), X cut-out, C count, S auto count, K calibrate, D check, R arc mode, E deduct mode, F and W to fit, 3 the 3D check view, F3 snap, and Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+A. The sheet bar's tools (notes, clouds, highlights, Compare, 3D) wait until a PDF is open; picked before that, the status line says so. A key that belongs to TakeoffCraft's own layout but that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (\"In My keys, Count is Ctrl+Alt+C\"). Delete, Esc, Enter, Tab, Space and the arrows always keep their meaning. While you draw a run or an outline, paste or review an auto count, the letters and the numbers of a typed length stay with the drawing. Make it your own Pick the style closest to what you want, then click Customize…. On Keys and menus, find a command (the search box helps). Add a key and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off. With the menu bar, Where it sits moves the command to another menu, or to Keys only. On Mouse, set the wheel and the buttons that pan the sheet. Give it a name and click Save. Saving a built-in style makes your own copy (\"My Takeoff desk\"); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save. Mouse Mouse wheel: Scroll up zooms in (the start) or Scroll up zooms out, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move. Pan the sheet with: the middle button, the right button, or both. A left drag on empty paper and Space with a drag always pan; a right click without dragging always finishes a run or an outline. Drag in the 3D views: Each view's own keeps the 3D check view's buttons (left and middle orbit, right pans). Or set the middle and right buttons to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; Shift with the button swaps orbit and pan. The left button always orbits and picks. Take it with you Export saves the style in use as a small .ccinput.json file; Import… adds one, from another computer or from a colleague. An imported file can only point at TakeoffCraft's own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status line. Delete this style removes one of your own. These settings stay in this browser. Use this style in every app under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a menu bar or a ribbon), with the changes you made in each app. TakeoffCraft has no style with a command line: when one is chosen for every app, TakeoffCraft keeps its own toolbar. The Takeoff ribbon is also the ribbon of the Ribbon and project browser layout (Settings › Global › Layout): see The TakeoffCraft screen. Related: Keys and mouse · TakeoffCraft settings · Coming from other takeoff software","lang":"en"},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Keyboard and mouse","summary":"Every key, mouse, trackpad and touch action in TakeoffCraft, grouped, with the platform differences.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keys","shortcuts","hotkeys","keyboard","mouse","trackpad","touch","pan","zoom","wheel","right-click","double-click","space","shift","copy","paste","cut","duplicate","select all"],"headings":[{"id":"tools","text":"Tools","level":2},{"id":"while-drawing","text":"While drawing","level":2},{"id":"selecting-and-editing","text":"Selecting and editing","level":2},{"id":"copy-and-paste","text":"Copy and paste","level":2},{"id":"files","text":"Files","level":2},{"id":"view-and-sheets","text":"View and sheets","level":2},{"id":"3d-view","text":"3D view","level":2},{"id":"auto-count","text":"Auto count","level":2},{"id":"help","text":"Help","level":2},{"id":"dialogs-and-fields","text":"Dialogs and fields","level":2},{"id":"platform-notes","text":"Platform notes","level":2},{"id":"keys-the-browser-keeps","text":"Keys the browser keeps","level":2},{"id":"where-move-lives","text":"Where Move lives","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELetter keys work when the pointer is anywhere on the page and you are not typing in a field. While a dialog is open, its own keys apply. Letters work with or without \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tools\"\u003ETools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E (fill a room with one click)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E on or off (in Linear, Area and Cut-out)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E on or off: the next segments of a run are drawn but not counted\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA digit, \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E while drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E box: type a length or coordinates, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E places the point (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-linear#type-lengths-and-coordinates\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-linear\" data-hash=\"type-lengths-and-coordinates\"\u003EMeasure linear runs\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E (select an area first)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E the scale\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"while-drawing\"\u003EWhile drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace a point (a dot, with Count).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPath: pick a PDF line&#039;s whole connected path, or the outline around a click (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/pick-the-lines\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/pick-the-lines\"\u003EPick the PDF&#039;s lines\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s lines on or off (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\"\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s lines\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E held while clicking\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnap the segment to 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° from the last point. Not for arc points or circles.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish a run, or close an area or cut-out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish a run, or close an area or cut-out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click (without moving)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish a run, or close an area or cut-out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the first point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose an area or cut-out (from the third point).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove the last point (first the pending arc point).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop the pending arc point; then cancel the unfinished mark; then go back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"selecting-and-editing\"\u003ESelecting and editing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a mark (Select tool)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect it and make its item current.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click a mark\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd it to the selection, or take it out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect every mark on the open sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a corner handle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove that point (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E snaps to 45°). Works when one mark is selected.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the selected marks (when you are not drawing).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect none: clear the selection and the check line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-and-paste\"\u003ECopy and paste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the selected marks, with their items.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut: copy the selected marks, then delete them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste: the copy follows the pointer; click where it goes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place: the same spot on the open sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate the selected marks: the copy follows the pointer; click where it goes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut down nothing: cancel a paste or duplicate that is on the pointer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe letters on their own still pick tools (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E); only \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with them copies and pastes. In a text field these keys copy and paste text as usual. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate marks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deletes the selected mark when you are not drawing. If that happens by accident, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese two work even while you are typing in a field.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"view-and-sheets\"\u003EView and sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in and out at the pointer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag (left, middle or right button)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E, a left drag draws the search box instead.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E held + drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan, in any tool.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit page\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit width\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"=\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in at the middle of the view.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom out at the middle of the view.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENext or previous sheet in the sheets list.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECycle full screen modes (Control on every platform, also on a Mac).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen or close this help, a panel at the side.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe 3D check view of the sheet, on or off.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-view\"\u003E3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag, or drag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + drag, or drag with the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom at the pointer. On a trackpad, a two-finger swipe orbits and a pinch zooms.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect the mark under the pointer (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click adds or takes out).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom extents.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBack to the flat sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\"\u003ECheck the takeoff in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"auto-count\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox the symbol to search for (starts the search).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a match box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReject it, or restore it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancel a running search, or discard the review.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"help\"\u003EHelp\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen or close the help panel (also the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button in the app bar). It works in every tool, even while typing in a field.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose the help panel. In its search box, the first Esc clears the search. While the panel is open, an Esc pressed after clicking the sheet closes the panel first; press it again to cancel in TakeoffCraft.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn many Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press fn with F1, or turn on &quot;Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys&quot; in System Settings, under Keyboard.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dialogs-and-fields\"\u003EDialogs and fields\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn a field: apply the value. In the calibrate dialog: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose a dialog (as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E); close the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E form.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove to the next field.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"platform-notes\"\u003EPlatform notes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is the Command key: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Full screen is the exception: Control+Shift+F.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERight-click is a two-finger click on a trackpad, once secondary click is on in System Settings, under Trackpad. TakeoffCraft reads only the secondary button; a Control-click may count as a plain click and add a point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo fingers scrolling on a trackpad zoom the sheet (they do not scroll it). To pan, press and drag, or hold Space and drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPage Up and Page Down are fn with the up and down arrows on keyboards without them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBackspace is the key labeled delete.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is Ctrl: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a laptop touchpad, a two-finger scroll zooms the sheet. To pan, press and drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ELinux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is Ctrl. Some desktops use Ctrl+Alt or Super shortcuts of their own; TakeoffCraft uses none of them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a touchpad, a two-finger scroll zooms the sheet. To pan, press and drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft reads touch as a single pointer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETap places a point, selects a mark or adds a count dot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag with one finger pans the sheet (in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E it draws the search box).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPinch-to-zoom is not supported yet. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit page\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit width\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a keyboard.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo close an area, tap its first point. To finish a run, double-tap where your browser turns a double tap into a double-click, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a keyboard.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThere is no live length readout until you tap, because there is no hover.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA keyboard and mouse or trackpad is the better way to do takeoff today.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EKeys the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBrowsers keep some shortcuts for themselves, and a page cannot use them: closing the tab (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), new tab or window (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), and browser zoom (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command +\"\u003E⌘+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl++\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command −\"\u003E⌘−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). That is why TakeoffCraft zooms the sheet with the wheel and the bare \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keys. If you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command +\"\u003E⌘+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl++\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E by mistake, the whole page grows; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 0\"\u003E⌘0\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+0\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-move-lives\"\u003EWhere Move lives\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no Move command: select a count dot and drag it, or select an area or a line and drag its corner handles.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Letter keys work when the pointer is anywhere on the page and you are not typing in a field. While a dialog is open, its own keys apply. Letters work with or without Shift. Tools Key Tool V Select L Linear A Area O Circle B Room (fill a room with one click) R Arc on or off (in Linear, Area and Cut-out) E Deduct on or off: the next segments of a run are drawn but not counted A digit, @ or # while drawing Open the Type box: type a length or coordinates, Enter places the point (Measure linear runs) X Cut-out (select an area first) C Count S Auto count K Calibrate D Check the scale While drawing Key or action What it does Click Place a point (a dot, with Count). P Path: pick a PDF line's whole connected path, or the outline around a click (Pick the PDF's lines). F3 Snap to the drawing's lines on or off (Snap to the drawing's lines). Shift held while clicking Snap the segment to 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° from the last point. Not for arc points or circles. Double-click Finish a run, or close an area or cut-out. Enter Finish a run, or close an area or cut-out. Right-click (without moving) Finish a run, or close an area or cut-out. Click the first point Close an area or cut-out (from the third point). Backspace or Delete Remove the last point (first the pending arc point). Esc Drop the pending arc point; then cancel the unfinished mark; then go back to Select. Selecting and editing Key or action What it does Click a mark (Select tool) Select it and make its item current. Shift + click a mark Add it to the selection, or take it out. Ctrl+A Select every mark on the open sheet. Drag a corner handle Move that point (Shift snaps to 45°). Works when one mark is selected. Delete or Backspace Delete the selected marks (when you are not drawing). Esc Select none: clear the selection and the check line. Ctrl+Z Undo. Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo. Copy and paste Key What it does Ctrl+C Copy the selected marks, with their items. Ctrl+X Cut: copy the selected marks, then delete them. Ctrl+V Paste: the copy follows the pointer; click where it goes. Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place: the same spot on the open sheet. Ctrl+D Duplicate the selected marks: the copy follows the pointer; click where it goes. Esc Put down nothing: cancel a paste or duplicate that is on the pointer. The letters on their own still pick tools (C is Count, D is Check, X is Cut-out, V is Select, A is Area); only Ctrl with them copies and pastes. In a text field these keys copy and paste text as usual. See Copy, paste and duplicate marks. Backspace deletes the selected mark when you are not drawing. If that happens by accident, Ctrl+Z brings it back. Files Key What it does Ctrl+O Open plans Ctrl+S Save takeoff These two work even while you are typing in a field. View and sheets Key or action What it does Mouse wheel Zoom in and out at the pointer. Drag (left, middle or right button) Pan. In Auto count, a left drag draws the search box instead. Space held + drag Pan, in any tool. F Fit page W Fit width + or = Zoom in at the middle of the view. − Zoom out at the middle of the view. Page Down / Page Up Next or previous sheet in the sheets list. Ctrl+Shift+F Cycle full screen modes (Control on every platform, also on a Mac). F1 Open or close this help, a panel at the side. 3 The 3D check view of the sheet, on or off. 3D view Key or action What it does Drag, or drag with the middle button Orbit. Shift + drag, or drag with the right button Pan. Mouse wheel Zoom at the pointer. On a trackpad, a two-finger swipe orbits and a pinch zooms. Click Select the mark under the pointer (Shift + click adds or takes out). F or W Zoom extents. 3 Back to the flat sheet. See Check the takeoff in 3D. Auto count Key or action What it does Drag a box Box the symbol to search for (starts the search). Click a match box Reject it, or restore it. Esc Cancel a running search, or discard the review. Help Key What it does F1 Open or close the help panel (also the Help button in the app bar). It works in every tool, even while typing in a field. Esc Close the help panel. In its search box, the first Esc clears the search. While the panel is open, an Esc pressed after clicking the sheet closes the panel first; press it again to cancel in TakeoffCraft. On many Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press fn with F1, or turn on \"Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys\" in System Settings, under Keyboard. Dialogs and fields Key What it does Enter In a field: apply the value. In the calibrate dialog: Set the scale. Esc Close a dialog (as Cancel); close the New item form. Tab Move to the next field. Platform notes Ctrl is the Command key: Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O. Full screen is the exception: Control+Shift+F. Right-click is a two-finger click on a trackpad, once secondary click is on in System Settings, under Trackpad. TakeoffCraft reads only the secondary button; a Control-click may count as a plain click and add a point. Two fingers scrolling on a trackpad zoom the sheet (they do not scroll it). To pan, press and drag, or hold Space and drag. Page Up and Page Down are fn with the up and down arrows on keyboards without them. Backspace is the key labeled delete. Ctrl is Ctrl: Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O. On a laptop touchpad, a two-finger scroll zooms the sheet. To pan, press and drag. Ctrl is Ctrl. Some desktops use Ctrl+Alt or Super shortcuts of their own; TakeoffCraft uses none of them. On a touchpad, a two-finger scroll zooms the sheet. To pan, press and drag. TakeoffCraft reads touch as a single pointer: Tap places a point, selects a mark or adds a count dot. Drag with one finger pans the sheet (in Auto count it draws the search box). Pinch-to-zoom is not supported yet. Use Fit page and Fit width, or + and − on a keyboard. To close an area, tap its first point. To finish a run, double-tap where your browser turns a double tap into a double-click, or press Enter on a keyboard. There is no live length readout until you tap, because there is no hover. A keyboard and mouse or trackpad is the better way to do takeoff today. Keys the browser keeps Browsers keep some shortcuts for themselves, and a page cannot use them: closing the tab (Ctrl+W), new tab or window (Ctrl+T, Ctrl+N), and browser zoom (Ctrl++, Ctrl+−). That is why TakeoffCraft zooms the sheet with the wheel and the bare + and − keys. If you press Ctrl++ by mistake, the whole page grows; Ctrl+0 puts it back. See Keys, mice and trackpads. Where Move lives There is no Move command: select a count dot and drag it, or select an area or a line and drag its corner handles.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"entry-formats","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Length, rate and percent entry","summary":"What you can type for distances, heights, money rates and waste percentages, with examples and the messages for input TakeoffCraft cannot read.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["input","format","feet inches","fractions","decimal feet","metric","meters","millimeters","rate","money","dollars","percent","waste","typing"],"headings":[{"id":"lengths","text":"Lengths","level":2},{"id":"arithmetic","text":"Arithmetic","level":3},{"id":"how-lengths-are-shown","text":"How lengths are shown","level":2},{"id":"rates-material-and-labor-per-unit","text":"Rates (material and labor per unit)","level":2},{"id":"waste-percent","text":"Waste percent","level":2},{"id":"names-and-units","text":"Names and units","level":2},{"id":"formulas","text":"Formulas","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"lengths\"\u003ELengths\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUsed by \u003Cstrong\u003EReal distance\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E, and by \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E in the item editor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24 feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24 feet 6½ inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet 6½ inches (a space instead of the dash)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6-1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet 6½ inches (dashes between the parts)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24 feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E30&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E30 inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6½ inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E¾ inch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24 ft 6 in\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24 feet 6 inches (also \u003Ccode\u003Efoot\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Efeet\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Einch\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Einches\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24.5\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24.5 in the default unit (see below)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24.5&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24.5 feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E7.5 m\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E7.5 meters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E750 cm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E750 centimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E7500 mm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E7,500 millimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA plain number is in \u003Cstrong\u003Efeet\u003C/strong\u003E for calibration and for items in LF, SF or SY, and in \u003Cstrong\u003Emeters\u003C/strong\u003E for items in m or m².\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECurly quotes and the prime marks from word processors (′ ″ ’ ”) work like \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. Two apostrophes \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;&#039;\u003C/code\u003E count as \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECommas are ignored: \u003Ccode\u003E1,200 mm\u003C/code\u003E is 1200 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFractions need whole numbers on both sides: \u003Ccode\u003E6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E works, \u003Ccode\u003E6.5 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E does not.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZero and negative distances are refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMessages:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Type a distance, like 24&#039;-0&quot; or 7.5 m.&quot; (the field is empty)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Type a distance greater than zero.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Use feet and inches (24&#039;-6 1/2&quot;), decimal feet, m, cm or mm.&quot; (an unknown unit)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;That distance could not be read. Try 24&#039;-6 1/2&quot; or 24.5.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo clear an item&#039;s height or depth, empty the field.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"arithmetic\"\u003EArithmetic\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery length field, and the type-in box while you draw, also takes a calculation with \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E and brackets: \u003Ccode\u003E4+7/16\u003C/code\u003E (4.4375 in the default unit), \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot; + 3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E(24&#039;-0&quot;)/3\u003C/code\u003E (8 feet), \u003Ccode\u003E2*450mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA dash written into a length is still part of it: \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E is 24 feet 6 inches and \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2\u003C/code\u003E is 6½. Put spaces round a dash to subtract: \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039; - 6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E is 23&#039;-6&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA plain number multiplies or divides a length as a number; added to a length it is in the default unit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eabs\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eround\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eceil\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Efloor\u003C/code\u003E work too. A calculation that cannot be read is refused with where it went wrong.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-lengths-are-shown\"\u003EHow lengths are shown\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELengths show as feet, inches and sixteenths: \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0&#039;-0 3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. Values round to the nearest 1/16 inch for display; the quantities themselves are not rounded. Decimal values show with two decimals and thousands separators, like \u003Ccode\u003E1,234.50 LF\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rates-material-and-labor-per-unit\"\u003ERates (material and labor per unit)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12.5\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E$12.50\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E$12.50\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E$12.50\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1,250\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E$1,250.00\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E0.385\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E$0.385 (up to four decimals; more are rounded)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(empty)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E$0\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMessage: &quot;Type an amount like 12.50.&quot; or &quot;That amount is too large.&quot; Negative rates are not accepted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExtended amounts (material, labor, totals) are worked out to the cent: quantity with waste × rate, rounded half away from zero, exactly like a spreadsheet&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003EROUND(x, 2)\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"waste-percent\"\u003EWaste percent\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E7.5%\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E7.5%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(empty)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUp to two decimals, from 0% to 1000%. Messages: &quot;Type a percentage like 10 or 7.5.&quot; or &quot;Waste above 1000% is not allowed.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"names-and-units\"\u003ENames and units\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EItem and component names: up to 200 characters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA component&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E: any text up to 20 characters, like \u003Ccode\u003EEA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esheets\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECY\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egal\u003C/code\u003E. It is a label only; TakeoffCraft does not convert it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"formulas\"\u003EFormulas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssembly formulas have their own rules: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/formulas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/formulas\"\u003EFormula language\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Lengths Used by Real distance in Calibrate, and by Height and Depth in the item editor. You type It means 24'-0\" 24 feet 24'-6 1/2\" 24 feet 6½ inches 12' 6 1/2\" 12 feet 6½ inches (a space instead of the dash) 12'-6-1/2\" 12 feet 6½ inches (dashes between the parts) 24' 24 feet 30\" 30 inches 6 1/2\" 6½ inches 3/4\" ¾ inch 24 ft 6 in 24 feet 6 inches (also foot, feet, inch, inches) 24.5 24.5 in the default unit (see below) 24.5' 24.5 feet 7.5 m 7.5 meters 750 cm 750 centimeters 7500 mm 7,500 millimeters A plain number is in feet for calibration and for items in LF, SF or SY, and in meters for items in m or m². Curly quotes and the prime marks from word processors (′ ″ ’ ”) work like ' and \". Two apostrophes '' count as \". Commas are ignored: 1,200 mm is 1200 mm. Fractions need whole numbers on both sides: 6 1/2\" works, 6.5 1/2\" does not. Zero and negative distances are refused. Messages: \"Type a distance, like 24'-0\" or 7.5 m.\" (the field is empty) \"Type a distance greater than zero.\" \"Use feet and inches (24'-6 1/2\"), decimal feet, m, cm or mm.\" (an unknown unit) \"That distance could not be read. Try 24'-6 1/2\" or 24.5.\" To clear an item's height or depth, empty the field. Arithmetic Every length field, and the type-in box while you draw, also takes a calculation with +, -, *, / and brackets: 4+7/16 (4.4375 in the default unit), 12'6\" + 3/4\", (24'-0\")/3 (8 feet), 2*450mm. A dash written into a length is still part of it: 24'-6\" is 24 feet 6 inches and 6-1/2 is 6½. Put spaces round a dash to subtract: 24' - 6\" is 23'-6\". A plain number multiplies or divides a length as a number; added to a length it is in the default unit. abs, round, ceil and floor work too. A calculation that cannot be read is refused with where it went wrong. How lengths are shown Lengths show as feet, inches and sixteenths: 12'-6 1/2\", 0'-0 3/4\". Values round to the nearest 1/16 inch for display; the quantities themselves are not rounded. Decimal values show with two decimals and thousands separators, like 1,234.50 LF. Rates (material and labor per unit) You type It means 12.5 $12.50 $12.50 $12.50 1,250 $1,250.00 0.385 $0.385 (up to four decimals; more are rounded) (empty) $0 Message: \"Type an amount like 12.50.\" or \"That amount is too large.\" Negative rates are not accepted. Extended amounts (material, labor, totals) are worked out to the cent: quantity with waste × rate, rounded half away from zero, exactly like a spreadsheet's ROUND(x, 2). Waste percent You type It means 10 10% 7.5% 7.5% (empty) 0% Up to two decimals, from 0% to 1000%. Messages: \"Type a percentage like 10 or 7.5.\" or \"Waste above 1000% is not allowed.\" Names and units Item and component names: up to 200 characters. A component's Unit: any text up to 20 characters, like EA, sheets, CY, gal. It is a label only; TakeoffCraft does not convert it. Formulas Assembly formulas have their own rules: see Formula language.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"scale-presets","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Scale presets","summary":"Every preset in the Preset list - architectural, engineering and metric - and what each assumes about the PDF.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["scale","presets","architectural scale","engineering scale","metric scale","ratio","1/4 inch","1/8 inch","1:100","full size"],"headings":[{"id":"architectural","text":"Architectural","level":2},{"id":"engineering","text":"Engineering","level":2},{"id":"metric","text":"Metric","level":2},{"id":"calibrated-scales","text":"Calibrated scales","level":2},{"id":"unusual-pdfs","text":"Unusual PDFs","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E group has 30 scales in three groups. A preset sets the open sheet&#039;s scale exactly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery preset assumes the PDF page is the drawing&#039;s real paper size: one inch on the PDF page is one inch of paper (PDF pages measure 72 points per inch). If the PDF was resized, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E after setting any scale. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"architectural\"\u003EArchitectural\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPreset\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOne paper inch is\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/16&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E16 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3/32&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10 ft 8 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3/16&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E5 ft 4 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 ft 8 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1 ft 4 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1 1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"engineering\"\u003EEngineering\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPreset\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOne paper inch is\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 20&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E20 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 30&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E30 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 40&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E40 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 50&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E50 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 60&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E60 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 100&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E100 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"metric\"\u003EMetric\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1:1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:10\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:20\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:25\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:50\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:200\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:250\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:500\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:1000\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA metric ratio means one unit on paper is that many units in reality: at \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E, 10 mm on the page is 1 m.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"calibrated-scales\"\u003ECalibrated scales\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA calibrated scale shows as &quot;Calibrated 1&quot; = 4.002&#039; on 24&#039;-6&quot;&quot; in the bar under the sheet: real feet per paper inch, and the distance you typed. When it is within 0.5% of a preset, the bar adds &quot;(matches 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;)&quot; and the sheets list shows the preset&#039;s name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"unusual-pdfs\"\u003EUnusual PDFs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome PDFs declare a larger page unit (a &quot;user unit&quot;); TakeoffCraft takes it into account for presets and for the scale shown. Pages rotated in the PDF are measured the same way as upright ones.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Preset list in the Scale group has 30 scales in three groups. A preset sets the open sheet's scale exactly. Every preset assumes the PDF page is the drawing's real paper size: one inch on the PDF page is one inch of paper (PDF pages measure 72 points per inch). If the PDF was resized, use Calibrate. Use Check after setting any scale. See Set the scale. Architectural Preset One paper inch is 1/16\" = 1'-0\" 16 ft 3/32\" = 1'-0\" 10 ft 8 in 1/8\" = 1'-0\" 8 ft 3/16\" = 1'-0\" 5 ft 4 in 1/4\" = 1'-0\" 4 ft 3/8\" = 1'-0\" 2 ft 8 in 1/2\" = 1'-0\" 2 ft 3/4\" = 1'-0\" 1 ft 4 in 1\" = 1'-0\" 1 ft 1 1/2\" = 1'-0\" 8 in 3\" = 1'-0\" 4 in Engineering Preset One paper inch is 1\" = 10' 10 ft 1\" = 20' 20 ft 1\" = 30' 30 ft 1\" = 40' 40 ft 1\" = 50' 50 ft 1\" = 60' 60 ft 1\" = 100' 100 ft Metric 1:1, 1:2, 1:5, 1:10, 1:20, 1:25, 1:50, 1:100, 1:200, 1:250, 1:500, 1:1000. A metric ratio means one unit on paper is that many units in reality: at 1:100, 10 mm on the page is 1 m. Calibrated scales A calibrated scale shows as \"Calibrated 1\" = 4.002' on 24'-6\"\" in the bar under the sheet: real feet per paper inch, and the distance you typed. When it is within 0.5% of a preset, the bar adds \"(matches 1/4\" = 1'-0\")\" and the sheets list shows the preset's name. Unusual PDFs Some PDFs declare a larger page unit (a \"user unit\"); TakeoffCraft takes it into account for presets and for the scale shown. Pages rotated in the PDF are measured the same way as upright ones.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"formulas","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Formula language","summary":"The rules for assembly quantity formulas - variables, operators, functions, rounding and every error message.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["formula","expression","syntax","functions","ceil","floor","round","min","max","abs","variables","operators","calculation"],"headings":[{"id":"variables","text":"Variables","level":2},{"id":"operators","text":"Operators","level":2},{"id":"functions","text":"Functions","level":2},{"id":"errors","text":"Errors","level":2},{"id":"safe-by-design","text":"Safe by design","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAssembly components get their quantity from a formula. A formula is a small arithmetic expression over the item&#039;s measured values. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E for worked examples.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"variables\"\u003EVariables\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EVariable\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s height\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWall area, L × H\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber of runs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal net area (outlines minus cut-outs)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal perimeter, including cut-out edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s depth or thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber of areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe count\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVariables are in the item&#039;s units: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/units\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/units\"\u003EUnits\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUpper and lower case are the same: \u003Ccode\u003El\u003C/code\u003E is \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly the variables of the item&#039;s own type work. \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E on a linear item gives \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;P&quot; is not a measured value here. Use L, H, A, N.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn empty height or depth is 0.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"operators\"\u003EOperators\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWrite\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E−\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESubtract, or a negative value\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E×\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMultiply\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E÷\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDivide\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E( )\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGroup\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMultiply and divide go before add and subtract: \u003Ccode\u003EL * 2 + 4\u003C/code\u003E is (L × 2) + 4. Use parentheses when in doubt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENumbers can have decimals (\u003Ccode\u003E1.333\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.5\u003C/code\u003E) and exponents (\u003Ccode\u003E1e3\u003C/code\u003E = 1000). Spaces are ignored.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"functions\"\u003EFunctions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFunction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EResult\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExample\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(x)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound up to a whole number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(67.5)\u003C/code\u003E = 68\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efloor(x)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound down to a whole number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efloor(67.5)\u003C/code\u003E = 67\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eround(x)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound to the nearest whole number, halves away from zero\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eround(2.5)\u003C/code\u003E = 3\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eround(x, d)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound to d decimals (0 to 10)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eround(3.14159, 2)\u003C/code\u003E = 3.14\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emin(a, b, …)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe smallest value\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emin(N, 10)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emax(a, b, …)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe largest value\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emax(1, ceil(N / 12))\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eabs(x)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe value without its sign\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eabs(-3)\u003C/code\u003E = 3\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Efloor\u003C/code\u003E ignore tiny computer rounding noise, so a value that should be exactly 1 is never rounded up to 2.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"errors\"\u003EErrors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA formula that cannot be read or worked out shows its error under the field, and the component is left out of the totals (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E header says &quot;Incomplete&quot;). Messages:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a formula, like L or ceil(L / 1.333) + 1.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe formula is empty.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;X&quot; is not a measured value here. Use …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse one of the listed variables.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;X&quot; is not a function here. Use ceil, floor, round, min, max, abs.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse one of those functions.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;#&quot; is not allowed in a formula.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove that character (units, \u003Ccode\u003E%\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E$\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E are not allowed).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA &quot;)&quot; is missing. / There is a &quot;)&quot; without its &quot;(&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBalance the parentheses.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA value is missing before &quot;)&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESomething like \u003Ccode\u003Emax(1, )\u003C/code\u003E: fill in the value.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe formula ends too soon.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESomething like \u003Ccode\u003EL *\u003C/code\u003E: finish it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnexpected &quot;…&quot;. Is an operator missing?\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo values side by side, like \u003Ccode\u003E2L\u003C/code\u003E: write \u003Ccode\u003E2 * L\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eceil() takes 1 value.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive the function the right number of values.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eround(x, digits) needs whole digits from 0 to 10.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a whole number of decimals.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe formula divides by zero.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUsually H is 0: set the item&#039;s height or depth.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe formula does not give a number.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe result is too large to be a number.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat formula is too long (500 characters at most).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShorten it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"safe-by-design\"\u003ESafe by design\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA formula can only do arithmetic with the variables and functions above. It cannot run code or read anything else, so a formula in a project file someone sends you is harmless.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Assembly components get their quantity from a formula. A formula is a small arithmetic expression over the item's measured values. See Assemblies and formulas for worked examples. Variables Item type Variable Meaning Linear L Total length Linear H The item's height Linear A Wall area, L × H Linear N Number of runs Area A Total net area (outlines minus cut-outs) Area P Total perimeter, including cut-out edges Area H The item's depth or thickness Area N Number of areas Count N The count Variables are in the item's units: see Units. Upper and lower case are the same: l is L. Only the variables of the item's own type work. P on a linear item gives \"P\" is not a measured value here. Use L, H, A, N. An empty height or depth is 0. Operators Write Meaning + Add - or − Subtract, or a negative value * or × Multiply / or ÷ Divide ( ) Group Multiply and divide go before add and subtract: L * 2 + 4 is (L × 2) + 4. Use parentheses when in doubt. Numbers can have decimals (1.333, .5) and exponents (1e3 = 1000). Spaces are ignored. Functions Function Result Example ceil(x) Round up to a whole number ceil(67.5) = 68 floor(x) Round down to a whole number floor(67.5) = 67 round(x) Round to the nearest whole number, halves away from zero round(2.5) = 3 round(x, d) Round to d decimals (0 to 10) round(3.14159, 2) = 3.14 min(a, b, …) The smallest value min(N, 10) max(a, b, …) The largest value max(1, ceil(N / 12)) abs(x) The value without its sign abs(-3) = 3 ceil and floor ignore tiny computer rounding noise, so a value that should be exactly 1 is never rounded up to 2. Errors A formula that cannot be read or worked out shows its error under the field, and the component is left out of the totals (the Quantities header says \"Incomplete\"). Messages: Message Fix Type a formula, like L or ceil(L / 1.333) + 1. The formula is empty. \"X\" is not a measured value here. Use … Use one of the listed variables. \"X\" is not a function here. Use ceil, floor, round, min, max, abs. Use one of those functions. \"#\" is not allowed in a formula. Remove that character (units, %, $ and = are not allowed). A \")\" is missing. / There is a \")\" without its \"(\". Balance the parentheses. A value is missing before \")\". Something like max(1, ): fill in the value. The formula ends too soon. Something like L *: finish it. Unexpected \"…\". Is an operator missing? Two values side by side, like 2L: write 2 * L. ceil() takes 1 value. Give the function the right number of values. round(x, digits) needs whole digits from 0 to 10. Use a whole number of decimals. The formula divides by zero. Usually H is 0: set the item's height or depth. The formula does not give a number. The result is too large to be a number. That formula is too long (500 characters at most). Shorten it. Safe by design A formula can only do arithmetic with the variables and functions above. It cannot run code or read anything else, so a formula in a project file someone sends you is harmless.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"units","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Units","summary":"The units an item can report in, the units its formula variables use, and how imperial and metric mix.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["units","lf","sf","sy","sq","square yards","roofing squares","acres","hectares","meters","square meters","ea","each","metric","imperial","conversion","default unit"],"headings":[{"id":"item-units","text":"Item units","level":2},{"id":"units-of-the-formula-variables","text":"Units of the formula variables","level":2},{"id":"where-units-are-fixed","text":"Where units are fixed","level":2},{"id":"scales-and-units-are-separate","text":"Scales and units are separate","level":2},{"id":"precision","text":"Precision","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"item-units\"\u003EItem units\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUnit\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EName\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Elinear feet (default)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare feet (default)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESY\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare yards\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESQ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eroofing squares (100 square feet)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eac\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eacres\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em²\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare meters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eha\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ehectares\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEA\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eeach\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E in the item editor at any time, even after measuring; the quantity is converted at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E list shows the units turned on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E, and always the item&#039;s own unit. A new item starts in the length or area unit starred there, when it is one of the item&#039;s units; otherwise in LF or SF. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/unit-lists\" data-topic=\"basics/unit-lists\"\u003EUnits, typed values and the unit lists\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"units-of-the-formula-variables\"\u003EUnits of the formula variables\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem unit\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EL, P, H\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EA\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare feet (wall area)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare meters (wall area)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESY\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare yards\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESQ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquares\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eac\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eacres\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em²\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare meters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eha\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ehectares\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor SY items, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E is in square yards while \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E stay in feet. A volume from an SY item is \u003Ccode\u003EA * 9 * H / 27\u003C/code\u003E cubic yards; using SF for slabs is simpler.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-units-are-fixed\"\u003EWhere units are fixed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome readouts always use feet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe live readout in the status bar while drawing (feet-inches, SF),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E (feet-inches, LF and SF),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe check result (feet-inches, with decimal feet and meters),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Esegment lengths and perimeters in the spreadsheet&#039;s Marks worksheet (feet-inches).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EItem totals, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table, formulas and the Estimate worksheet use the item&#039;s own unit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scales-and-units-are-separate\"\u003EScales and units are separate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sheet&#039;s scale says how big the drawing is; an item&#039;s unit says how to report it. A metric drawing at \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E can be taken off in LF, and an imperial drawing in m². Calibrating accepts any unit: \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E7.5 m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E7500 mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"precision\"\u003EPrecision\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft measures exactly from the points you click, including true arc lengths and areas, and does not round quantities. Display rounds lengths to 1/16 inch and decimals to two places. Money is rounded only at the extended amounts, to the cent.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Item units Item type Unit Name Linear LF linear feet (default) Linear m meters Area SF square feet (default) Area SY square yards Area SQ roofing squares (100 square feet) Area ac acres Area m² square meters Area ha hectares Count EA each Change it with Unit in the item editor at any time, even after measuring; the quantity is converted at once. The Unit list shows the units turned on in Settings › General › Units, and always the item's own unit. A new item starts in the length or area unit starred there, when it is one of the item's units; otherwise in LF or SF. See Units, typed values and the unit lists. Units of the formula variables Item unit L, P, H A LF feet square feet (wall area) m meters square meters (wall area) SF feet square feet SY feet square yards SQ feet squares ac feet acres m² meters square meters ha meters hectares For SY items, A is in square yards while P and H stay in feet. A volume from an SY item is A * 9 * H / 27 cubic yards; using SF for slabs is simpler. Where units are fixed Some readouts always use feet: the live readout in the status bar while drawing (feet-inches, SF), Selected mark (feet-inches, LF and SF), the check result (feet-inches, with decimal feet and meters), segment lengths and perimeters in the spreadsheet's Marks worksheet (feet-inches). Item totals, the Quantities table, formulas and the Estimate worksheet use the item's own unit. Scales and units are separate A sheet's scale says how big the drawing is; an item's unit says how to report it. A metric drawing at 1:100 can be taken off in LF, and an imperial drawing in m². Calibrating accepts any unit: 24'-6\", 7.5 m, 7500 mm. Precision TakeoffCraft measures exactly from the points you click, including true arc lengths and areas, and does not round quantities. Display rounds lengths to 1/16 inch and decimals to two places. Money is rounded only at the extended amounts, to the cent.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"export-columns","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Export columns","summary":"Every worksheet and column of the XLSX export and the CSV, with the formulas the Estimate worksheet carries.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["export","columns","xlsx","csv","worksheet","estimate sheet","by sheet","marks","scales","formulas","fields"],"headings":[{"id":"estimate-xlsx-worksheet-and-csv","text":"Estimate (XLSX worksheet and CSV)","level":2},{"id":"by-sheet-xlsx-only","text":"By sheet (XLSX only)","level":2},{"id":"marks-xlsx-only","text":"Marks (XLSX only)","level":2},{"id":"by-bid-area-xlsx-only-when-the-takeoff-has-bid-areas","text":"By bid area (XLSX only, when the takeoff has bid areas)","level":2},{"id":"scales-xlsx-only","text":"Scales (XLSX only)","level":2},{"id":"file-names","text":"File names","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"estimate-xlsx-worksheet-and-csv\"\u003EEstimate (XLSX worksheet and CSV)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne row per item, followed by a row per component of its assembly (indented). Then an empty row and the job total.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EContents\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the XLSX\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA Item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem name (bold) or component name (indented)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EB Type\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear, Area, Count or Component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC Unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF, m, SF, SY, m², EA, or the component&#039;s unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ED Quantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasured total, or the formula result\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EE Waste %\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWaste\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPercent (0.10 = 10%)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF Qty with waste\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity × (1 + waste)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula \u003Ccode\u003ED*(1+E)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EG Material per unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial rate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EH Labor per unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabor rate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EI Material\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtended material\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula \u003Ccode\u003EROUND(F*G,2)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJ Labor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtended labor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula \u003Ccode\u003EROUND(F*H,2)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EK Total\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial + labor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula \u003Ccode\u003EI+J\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EL Notes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula used, formula error, marks on sheets with no scale, wall area, perimeter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EJob total row: \u003Ccode\u003ESUM\u003C/code\u003E of columns I, J and K over every row above, and &quot;Incomplete: see the notes above&quot; in Notes when something was left out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA row with a formula error has no quantity or amounts; its note gives the error. The header row is frozen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the CSV, the same columns hold plain values, Waste % is a number of percent (10 for 10%), and money is in dollars with two decimals. Text that a spreadsheet might take for a formula (starting with \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E) gets a leading \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E so it stays text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-sheet-xlsx-only\"\u003EBy sheet (XLSX only)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EContents\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet name (with the PDF name when it is not open)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat item&#039;s quantity on that sheet, in its unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber of marks\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"marks-xlsx-only\"\u003EMarks (XLSX only)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne row per mark.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EContents\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear, Area or Count\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength, net area, or 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF or m; SF, SY or m²; EA\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDetail\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear: the segment lengths, deducted ones marked &quot;(deduct)&quot;, and &quot;deducts (length) of (length) drawn&quot;. Area: gross and cut-out areas when there are cut-outs, and the perimeter. &quot;Sheet has no scale&quot; for unscaled marks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBid area\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly when the takeoff has bid areas: the mark&#039;s area, or &quot;No bid area&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-bid-area-xlsx-only-when-the-takeoff-has-bid-areas\"\u003EBy bid area (XLSX only, when the takeoff has bid areas)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBid area\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe area&#039;s name, or &quot;No bid area&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item, or an indented component under it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s or component&#039;s unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasured in that area (components: the formula on the area&#039;s totals)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQty with waste\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity × (1 + waste)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial, Labor, Total\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtended to the cent, as on Estimate\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENotes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula, errors and unscaled marks, as on Estimate\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach area ends with a &quot;(name) subtotal&quot; row whose costs are \u003Ccode\u003ESUM\u003C/code\u003E formulas over its rows; the last row, \u003Cstrong\u003EAll bid areas\u003C/strong\u003E, adds the subtotals. The \u003Cstrong\u003EMarks\u003C/strong\u003E worksheet then has a seventh column, \u003Cstrong\u003EBid area\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scales-xlsx-only\"\u003EScales (XLSX only)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne row per sheet that has a scale or marks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EContents\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScale\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe preset&#039;s name, or the calibrated scale, or &quot;No scale&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Preset&quot;, or &quot;Calibrated on (the distance you typed)&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeet per PDF unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe exact scale factor TakeoffCraft uses\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"file-names\"\u003EFile names\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(name).xlsx\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E(name).csv\u003C/code\u003E, where the name comes from the first PDF opened in the takeoff.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe CSV is UTF-8 with a byte order mark and Windows line endings, which spreadsheet programs on every platform read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Estimate (XLSX worksheet and CSV) One row per item, followed by a row per component of its assembly (indented). Then an empty row and the job total. Column Contents In the XLSX A Item Item name (bold) or component name (indented) Text B Type Linear, Area, Count or Component Text C Unit LF, m, SF, SY, m², EA, or the component's unit Text D Quantity Measured total, or the formula result Number E Waste % Waste Percent (0.10 = 10%) F Qty with waste Quantity × (1 + waste) Formula D*(1+E) G Material per unit Material rate Number H Labor per unit Labor rate Number I Material Extended material Formula ROUND(F*G,2) J Labor Extended labor Formula ROUND(F*H,2) K Total Material + labor Formula I+J L Notes Formula used, formula error, marks on sheets with no scale, wall area, perimeter Text Job total row: SUM of columns I, J and K over every row above, and \"Incomplete: see the notes above\" in Notes when something was left out. A row with a formula error has no quantity or amounts; its note gives the error. The header row is frozen. In the CSV, the same columns hold plain values, Waste % is a number of percent (10 for 10%), and money is in dollars with two decimals. Text that a spreadsheet might take for a formula (starting with =, +, - or @) gets a leading ' so it stays text. By sheet (XLSX only) Column Contents Sheet Sheet name (with the PDF name when it is not open) Item Item name Quantity That item's quantity on that sheet, in its unit Unit The item's unit Marks Number of marks Marks (XLSX only) One row per mark. Column Contents Sheet Sheet name Item Item name Type Linear, Area or Count Quantity Length, net area, or 1 Unit LF or m; SF, SY or m²; EA Detail Linear: the segment lengths, deducted ones marked \"(deduct)\", and \"deducts (length) of (length) drawn\". Area: gross and cut-out areas when there are cut-outs, and the perimeter. \"Sheet has no scale\" for unscaled marks. Bid area Only when the takeoff has bid areas: the mark's area, or \"No bid area\" By bid area (XLSX only, when the takeoff has bid areas) Column What Bid area The area's name, or \"No bid area\" Item The item, or an indented component under it Unit The item's or component's unit Quantity Measured in that area (components: the formula on the area's totals) Qty with waste Quantity × (1 + waste) Material, Labor, Total Extended to the cent, as on Estimate Notes Formula, errors and unscaled marks, as on Estimate Each area ends with a \"(name) subtotal\" row whose costs are SUM formulas over its rows; the last row, All bid areas, adds the subtotals. The Marks worksheet then has a seventh column, Bid area. Scales (XLSX only) One row per sheet that has a scale or marks. Column Contents Sheet Sheet name Scale The preset's name, or the calibrated scale, or \"No scale\" How \"Preset\", or \"Calibrated on (the distance you typed)\" Feet per PDF unit The exact scale factor TakeoffCraft uses File names (name).xlsx and (name).csv, where the name comes from the first PDF opened in the takeoff. The CSV is UTF-8 with a byte order mark and Windows line endings, which spreadsheet programs on every platform read.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Symptoms, causes and fixes for the messages and surprises you may meet in TakeoffCraft, quoted as the app shows them.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problems","errors","not working","fix","help","refused","wont measure","no scale","missing marks","export failed","offline","touch"],"headings":[{"id":"opening-files","text":"Opening files","level":2},{"id":"measuring","text":"Measuring","level":2},{"id":"scale","text":"Scale","level":2},{"id":"quantities-and-formulas","text":"Quantities and formulas","level":2},{"id":"auto-count","text":"Auto count","level":2},{"id":"saving-and-exporting","text":"Saving and exporting","level":2},{"id":"keys-and-mouse","text":"Keys and mouse","level":2},{"id":"touch-screens","text":"Touch screens","level":2},{"id":"working-offline","text":"Working offline","level":2},{"id":"still-stuck","text":"Still stuck","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft explains most refusals in the status bar at the bottom of the window. Find the message below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-files\"\u003EOpening files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe PDF reader could not load. Reload the page to try again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe app&#039;s PDF reader did not load, usually because you are offline and the app was never saved for offline use.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReconnect and reload. Then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E and wait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(name) is password-protected. Open an unprotected copy.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe PDF needs a password to open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk for an unprotected copy, or remove the password in a PDF program you trust.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(name) could not be read as a PDF (…)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is damaged or not really a PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownload it again, or re-save it from the program that made it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(name) is already open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same PDF (same contents) is open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing to do.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft opens PDFs and .takeoff.json projects. Skipped: …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOther file types were dropped.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConvert drawings to PDF first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing happens when I drop files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe files were dropped outside the browser window, or the browser opened the PDF in a new tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop onto the TakeoffCraft page itself; the &quot;Drop PDFs or a .takeoff.json&quot; frame shows when it will work.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening a big set takes a while\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery PDF is read in full to fingerprint it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWait for the &quot;Opening (name)…&quot; message to clear. Thumbnails fill in as you scroll.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measuring\"\u003EMeasuring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a PDF first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo sheet is open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis sheet has no scale yet, so it can&#039;t be measured. Pick a preset scale or calibrate first (counts work without one).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear, area, circle and cut-out tools need a scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake a linear item first (name it under Items), then click on the sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere is no item of that type.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFill in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E form that opened, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick an item to take off first (Items panel).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe current item was deleted.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick an item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA run needs at least two points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou finished after one click.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick at least two points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn area needs at least three points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EToo few corners.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick at least three corners.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe outline crosses itself, so its area would be wrong. Draw it again without crossing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA figure-eight shape.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw it as two areas, or go round the edge in order.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThose points enclose no area.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAll the points are on one line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick corners that enclose a space.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect an area mark first (Select tool), then choose Cut-out and draw the hole inside it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E needs a selected area.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click the area, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA cut-out has to sit fully inside the area.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe hole crosses or leaves the outline.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the hole inside the area.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut-outs cannot overlap each other.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo holes overlap.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw one hole round both, or keep them apart.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E… The point was put back.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA dragged corner would make the outline cross itself or push a hole outside.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag to a place that keeps the shape valid.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a point on the circle&#039;s edge, away from its center.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe second click was too close to the center.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick further out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClicks pan the sheet instead of adding points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe pointer moved more than a few pixels while the button was down; that counts as a drag.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick without moving, especially on a trackpad.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShift does not snap\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou are placing a point on an arc, or drawing a circle.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe 45° snap applies to straight segments only.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints jump onto a nearby line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s lines is on.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in to click between lines, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn Snap off.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo snap marker appears\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnap is off, the sheet is a scan, or the tool does not snap (Count, Auto count, pasting).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; on a scan there are no lines to snap to.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe length is wrong\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe scale is wrong (often a resized PDF with a preset).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E on a known dimension, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scale\"\u003EScale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick two points further apart.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe calibration points are almost on top of each other.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECalibrate on a longer dimension.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA message in the calibrate dialog, like &quot;That distance could not be read&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe distance is in a form TakeoffCraft cannot read.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/entry-formats\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/entry-formats\"\u003ELength, rate and percent entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis sheet has no scale yet, so there is nothing to check. Calibrate it, or pick a preset.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E on an unscaled sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet a scale first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list is grayed out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo sheet is open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"quantities-and-formulas\"\u003EQuantities and formulas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIncomplete: … (not in the totals)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA formula error, or marks on sheets with no scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/read-quantities#the-incomplete-warning\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/read-quantities\" data-hash=\"the-incomplete-warning\"\u003ERead quantities and the estimate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E+(n) unscaled next to an item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome of its marks are on sheets with no scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive those sheets a scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula error: …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe formula cannot be worked out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/formulas#errors\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/formulas\" data-hash=\"errors\"\u003EFormula language\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis item has marks, so its type cannot change.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe type is locked once there are marks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake a new item of the other type and redraw the marks with it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn item needs a name.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe name field was emptied.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a name.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType an amount like 12.50. / Type a percentage like 10 or 7.5.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA rate or waste could not be read.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/entry-formats\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/entry-formats\"\u003ELength, rate and percent entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA total looks doubled\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item has its own rates and components that carry the same cost.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet the item&#039;s own rates to 0 when its components carry the cost.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis item&#039;s marks are on PDFs that are not open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou clicked an item whose marks are all on PDFs you have not opened.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen those PDFs; their names are listed under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"auto-count\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat box is too small. Draw it around the whole symbol.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe box is tiny.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in and box the whole symbol.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat box is almost empty. Draw it around a symbol.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe box holds only paper.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox the symbol itself.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo matches. Lower the match slider, or box the symbol more tightly.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing scored high enough.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox the symbol tightly, or drag the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch\u003C/span\u003E slider lower.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EToo many matches, including wrong symbols\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESimilar symbols score close to the real one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERaise the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch\u003C/span\u003E slider and click wrong boxes to reject them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMatches missing on other sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThose sheets draw the symbol at another size, or at an angle that is not a 90° step.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESearch those sheets with a box drawn there, or count by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(n) sheets were too large to search at this symbol size.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sheet would be too big at the working size.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox a larger symbol, or count that sheet by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe search failed: …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe background search stopped.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETry again; reload the page if it repeats (save first). Offline, it works only once the app bar shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDragging draws a box instead of panning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat is how \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E works.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag, or drag with the middle button.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving-and-exporting\"\u003ESaving and exporting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing to export yet: make an item and take it off.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere are no items.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake an item first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo marks on the open PDFs yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E has nothing to draw on the open PDFs.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the PDFs that have marks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis PDF is password-protected or encrypted, so a marked copy cannot be written. …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe PDF is encrypted.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E instead, or use an unencrypted copy of the PDF (it then needs its own takeoff, since it is a different file).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe marked PDF could not be written: …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOften: offline, and the app was not saved for offline use, so the PDF writer is missing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGo online and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E again. To avoid it, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E and wait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly one marked PDF downloaded\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser blocked several downloads at once.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAllow multiple downloads for this site, and export again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(file) is not a TakeoffCraft project (it is not valid JSON). / This is not a TakeoffCraft project file.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrong file, or a damaged one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis project was saved by a newer TakeoffCraft. Open it there.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file comes from a newer build.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it in the newer build.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(n) marks were damaged and left out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPart of the file was damaged or edited by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe rest opened. Redraw the missing marks, or open an older copy.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarks missing after reopening\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe PDFs are not open, or one of them changed.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen#when-a-pdf-has-changed\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-hash=\"when-a-pdf-has-changed\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser asks &quot;Leave site?&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere are unsaved changes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStay, and save with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-and-mouse\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESymptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELetter keys do nothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe cursor is in a field (search, item editor, table).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick on the sheet, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E out of the field.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe wheel zooms instead of scrolling\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe wheel always zooms the sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag to pan.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe whole page got bigger or smaller\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBrowser zoom (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command +\"\u003E⌘+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl++\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command −\"\u003E⌘−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 0\"\u003E⌘0\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+0\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Zoom the sheet with the wheel or the bare \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA mark disappeared when I pressed Backspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace deletes the selected mark when you are not drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings it back.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click does not finish on a Mac\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl-click arrives as a normal click.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a two-finger click with secondary click turned on, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"touch-screens\"\u003ETouch screens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETap\u003C/strong\u003E places a point (or selects a mark with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E). One finger dragging pans.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo fingers\u003C/strong\u003E pinch to zoom about the point between them, and pan when they move together. Putting a second finger down cancels what the first one started, so a pinch never leaves a point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFinish\u003C/strong\u003E a run or an outline with a \u003Cstrong\u003Elong press\u003C/strong\u003E (the right click), a \u003Cstrong\u003Edouble tap\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinish\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the bottom of the sheet. The same bar has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E (take the last point back) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E. Areas also close by tapping the first point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFingers get bigger targets: points snap, close and pick from farther away, and corner handles are larger.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETyping lengths needs a keyboard; the on-screen keyboard works when the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E box is focused.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a tablet, a keyboard and a mouse or trackpad make takeoff much easier.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"working-offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome of the app&#039;s files did not download while saving for offline use.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck your connection and choose the button again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrivate windows and some managed computers block offline copies.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a normal window, or another browser.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Saving…&quot; never starts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou are offline; saving needs a connection.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave while online.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe app is old after an update\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offline copy is from an earlier day.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnline, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou want the space back\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offline copy is about 6 MB.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the offline copy\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-stuck\"\u003EStill stuck\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReload the page if the app stops responding. Your takeoff is lost on reload unless you saved it, so save first with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E if you can. For browser questions see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft explains most refusals in the status bar at the bottom of the window. Find the message below. Opening files Message or symptom Cause Fix The PDF reader could not load. Reload the page to try again. The app's PDF reader did not load, usually because you are offline and the app was never saved for offline use. Reconnect and reload. Then choose Save for offline and wait for Offline ready; see Working offline. (name) is password-protected. Open an unprotected copy. The PDF needs a password to open. Ask for an unprotected copy, or remove the password in a PDF program you trust. (name) could not be read as a PDF (…) The file is damaged or not really a PDF. Download it again, or re-save it from the program that made it. (name) is already open. The same PDF (same contents) is open. Nothing to do. TakeoffCraft opens PDFs and .takeoff.json projects. Skipped: … Other file types were dropped. Convert drawings to PDF first. Nothing happens when I drop files The files were dropped outside the browser window, or the browser opened the PDF in a new tab. Drop onto the TakeoffCraft page itself; the \"Drop PDFs or a .takeoff.json\" frame shows when it will work. Opening a big set takes a while Every PDF is read in full to fingerprint it. Wait for the \"Opening (name)…\" message to clear. Thumbnails fill in as you scroll. Measuring Message or symptom Cause Fix Open a PDF first. No sheet is open. Open a PDF. This sheet has no scale yet, so it can't be measured. Pick a preset scale or calibrate first (counts work without one). Linear, area, circle and cut-out tools need a scale. Set the scale. Make a linear item first (name it under Items), then click on the sheet. There is no item of that type. Fill in the New item form that opened, and choose Add. Pick an item to take off first (Items panel). The current item was deleted. Click an item under Items. A run needs at least two points. You finished after one click. Click at least two points. An area needs at least three points. Too few corners. Click at least three corners. The outline crosses itself, so its area would be wrong. Draw it again without crossing. A figure-eight shape. Draw it as two areas, or go round the edge in order. Those points enclose no area. All the points are on one line. Click corners that enclose a space. Select an area mark first (Select tool), then choose Cut-out and draw the hole inside it. Cut-out needs a selected area. Press V, click the area, then X. A cut-out has to sit fully inside the area. The hole crosses or leaves the outline. Draw the hole inside the area. Cut-outs cannot overlap each other. Two holes overlap. Draw one hole round both, or keep them apart. … The point was put back. A dragged corner would make the outline cross itself or push a hole outside. Drag to a place that keeps the shape valid. Click a point on the circle's edge, away from its center. The second click was too close to the center. Click further out. Clicks pan the sheet instead of adding points The pointer moved more than a few pixels while the button was down; that counts as a drag. Click without moving, especially on a trackpad. Shift does not snap You are placing a point on an arc, or drawing a circle. The 45° snap applies to straight segments only. Points jump onto a nearby line Snap to the drawing's lines is on. Zoom in to click between lines, or press F3 to turn Snap off. No snap marker appears Snap is off, the sheet is a scan, or the tool does not snap (Count, Auto count, pasting). Press F3; on a scan there are no lines to snap to. The length is wrong The scale is wrong (often a resized PDF with a preset). Use Check on a known dimension, then Calibrate. Scale Message or symptom Cause Fix Pick two points further apart. The calibration points are almost on top of each other. Calibrate on a longer dimension. A message in the calibrate dialog, like \"That distance could not be read\" The distance is in a form TakeoffCraft cannot read. See Length, rate and percent entry. This sheet has no scale yet, so there is nothing to check. Calibrate it, or pick a preset. Check on an unscaled sheet. Set a scale first. The Preset list is grayed out No sheet is open. Open a PDF. Quantities and formulas Message or symptom Cause Fix Incomplete: … (not in the totals) A formula error, or marks on sheets with no scale. See Read quantities and the estimate. +(n) unscaled next to an item Some of its marks are on sheets with no scale. Give those sheets a scale. Formula error: … The formula cannot be worked out. See Formula language. This item has marks, so its type cannot change. The type is locked once there are marks. Make a new item of the other type and redraw the marks with it. An item needs a name. The name field was emptied. Type a name. Type an amount like 12.50. / Type a percentage like 10 or 7.5. A rate or waste could not be read. See Length, rate and percent entry. A total looks doubled The item has its own rates and components that carry the same cost. Set the item's own rates to 0 when its components carry the cost. This item's marks are on PDFs that are not open. You clicked an item whose marks are all on PDFs you have not opened. Open those PDFs; their names are listed under Sheets. Auto count Message or symptom Cause Fix That box is too small. Draw it around the whole symbol. The box is tiny. Zoom in and box the whole symbol. That box is almost empty. Draw it around a symbol. The box holds only paper. Box the symbol itself. No matches. Lower the match slider, or box the symbol more tightly. Nothing scored high enough. Box the symbol tightly, or drag the Match slider lower. Too many matches, including wrong symbols Similar symbols score close to the real one. Raise the Match slider and click wrong boxes to reject them. Matches missing on other sheets Those sheets draw the symbol at another size, or at an angle that is not a 90° step. Search those sheets with a box drawn there, or count by hand. (n) sheets were too large to search at this symbol size. The sheet would be too big at the working size. Box a larger symbol, or count that sheet by hand. The search failed: … The background search stopped. Try again; reload the page if it repeats (save first). Offline, it works only once the app bar shows Offline ready. Dragging draws a box instead of panning That is how Auto count works. Hold Space and drag, or drag with the middle button. Saving and exporting Message or symptom Cause Fix Nothing to export yet: make an item and take it off. There are no items. Make an item first. No marks on the open PDFs yet. Marked PDF has nothing to draw on the open PDFs. Open the PDFs that have marks. This PDF is password-protected or encrypted, so a marked copy cannot be written. … The PDF is encrypted. Export XLSX instead, or use an unencrypted copy of the PDF (it then needs its own takeoff, since it is a different file). The marked PDF could not be written: … Often: offline, and the app was not saved for offline use, so the PDF writer is missing. Go online and choose Marked PDF again. To avoid it, choose Save for offline and wait for Offline ready. Only one marked PDF downloaded The browser blocked several downloads at once. Allow multiple downloads for this site, and export again. (file) is not a TakeoffCraft project (it is not valid JSON). / This is not a TakeoffCraft project file. Wrong file, or a damaged one. Pick the .takeoff.json file. This project was saved by a newer TakeoffCraft. Open it there. The file comes from a newer build. Open it in the newer build. (n) marks were damaged and left out. Part of the file was damaged or edited by hand. The rest opened. Redraw the missing marks, or open an older copy. Marks missing after reopening The PDFs are not open, or one of them changed. See Save and reopen a takeoff. The browser asks \"Leave site?\" There are unsaved changes. Stay, and save with Ctrl+S. Keys and mouse Symptom Cause Fix Letter keys do nothing The cursor is in a field (search, item editor, table). Click on the sheet, or press Tab out of the field. The wheel zooms instead of scrolling The wheel always zooms the sheet. Drag to pan. The whole page got bigger or smaller Browser zoom (Ctrl++ or Ctrl+−). Press Ctrl+0. Zoom the sheet with the wheel or the bare + and −. A mark disappeared when I pressed Backspace Backspace deletes the selected mark when you are not drawing. Ctrl+Z brings it back. Right-click does not finish on a Mac Control-click arrives as a normal click. Use a two-finger click with secondary click turned on, or press Enter. Touch screens On a touch screen: Tap places a point (or selects a mark with Select). One finger dragging pans. Two fingers pinch to zoom about the point between them, and pan when they move together. Putting a second finger down cancels what the first one started, so a pinch never leaves a point. Finish a run or an outline with a long press (the right click), a double tap, or Finish in the bar at the bottom of the sheet. The same bar has Back (take the last point back) and Cancel. Areas also close by tapping the first point. Fingers get bigger targets: points snap, close and pick from farther away, and corner handles are larger. Typing lengths needs a keyboard; the on-screen keyboard works when the Type box is focused. On a tablet, a keyboard and a mouse or trackpad make takeoff much easier. Working offline Button or symptom Cause Fix Could not save Some of the app's files did not download while saving for offline use. Check your connection and choose the button again. Offline not available Private windows and some managed computers block offline copies. Use a normal window, or another browser. \"Saving…\" never starts You are offline; saving needs a connection. Save while online. The app is old after an update The offline copy is from an earlier day. Online, choose Offline ready, then Save again (get the newest version). You want the space back The offline copy is about 6 MB. Choose Offline ready, then Remove the offline copy. See Working offline. Still stuck Reload the page if the app stops responding. Your takeoff is lost on reload unless you saved it, so save first with Ctrl+S if you can. For browser questions see Browsers and computers.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"What is not built yet","summary":"Takeoff features this build does not have, and what to do instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["limits","missing","not yet","roadmap","planned","workaround","per run","cloud","desktop"],"headings":[{"id":"measuring","text":"Measuring","level":2},{"id":"items-and-pricing","text":"Items and pricing","level":2},{"id":"drawings-dwg-and-dxf","text":"Drawings (DWG and DXF)","level":2},{"id":"auto-count","text":"Auto count","level":2},{"id":"files-and-sharing","text":"Files and sharing","level":2},{"id":"screen-and-input","text":"Screen and input","level":2},{"id":"3d-view","text":"3D view","level":2},{"id":"bidding","text":"Bidding","level":2},{"id":"panels","text":"Panels","level":2},{"id":"spreadsheets","text":"Spreadsheets","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft is in development. This list is what the current build does not do, and how to get the job done anyway.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measuring\"\u003EMeasuring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInserting a point in the middle of a run or outline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the mark and draw it again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox (window) selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click each mark, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for every mark on the sheet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate marks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERotating, mirroring or rescaling a pasted copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste on a sheet at the same scale and in the same orientation; draw the rest by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReading a scale from a scanned sheet, or from a graphic scale bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick the preset, or calibrate on the bar&#039;s length.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMore than one scale per sheet (detail areas, enlarged plans)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasure the part at the sheet&#039;s scale, and take off the detail on its own enlarged sheet if the set has one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"items-and-pricing\"\u003EItems and pricing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly formulas that use an item&#039;s other kinds of marks (the doors of a room item in its floor&#039;s formulas)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormulas use the main type&#039;s totals; price the other kinds on their own rows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly formulas worked out run by run\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormulas use the item&#039;s totals; use \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E for per-run extras.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormulas that use another item&#039;s quantities\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERepeat the measurement in that item, or work it out in the spreadsheet export.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECrews on assembly components, or a working day other than 8 hours\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive the crew to the item; read crew-days for another day length as crew-hours ÷ your day.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarkups other than notes, clouds and highlights (arrows, dimensions, stamps), or replies on a note\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrite it in the note&#039;s text; change its status as it is settled.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA price library shared by a team, or kept with your account\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe library is kept in this browser: export it as a CSV and import it on the other computer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrade packs reviewed by working estimators\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery pack is a starter: check its assumptions against your job and your products before you price with it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/trade-packs\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/trade-packs\"\u003ETrade packs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrices in trade packs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPacks bring assemblies only: set your own rates, or import your price list as a CSV.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERenaming the takeoff\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe name comes from the first PDF; rename the downloaded files.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENaming sheets by hand\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheets are named from the PDF&#039;s page labels; search by file name and page.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings-dwg-and-dxf\"\u003EDrawings (DWG and DXF)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot style tables, pen assignments and CTB files on a drawing&#039;s sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA drawing is drawn black, or in its colors darkened for paper; line weights come from the drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening a binary DXF, or a DWG older than AutoCAD 2000\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave it as an ASCII DXF or a newer DWG.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinding a linked drawing (xref) by the folder it sits in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the linked drawing&#039;s file too, with the host: it is found by file name among the open files.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasuring 3D polylines, meshes, solids and regions\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount them by layer and type, or measure the plan view with the Linear and Area tools.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText in a marked PDF from a drawing in scripts other than Latin\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECharacters Helvetica has no glyph for print as &quot;?&quot; in the marked PDF; the drawing on screen is unaffected.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA hand-over of a drawing to another app from the sheet bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the same file in that app.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA drawing that carries the takeoff inside it, as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/span\u003E does for PDFs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E: the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E reopens on the same drawing, found by its contents.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing or counting PDF underlays, images and OLE objects placed in a drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImages show as a frame only. Open the underlay PDF itself as a sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"auto-count\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinding symbols on a sheet with no scale drawn at another size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive the sheet its scale, or search it with a box drawn there.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinding mirrored symbols\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount those by hand, or search again with a box around a mirrored one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-and-sharing\"\u003EFiles and sharing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne PDF that both shows the marks and carries the takeoff\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E for people to see the marks; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/span\u003E to reopen in TakeoffCraft.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA CSV of quantities by sheet or of the marks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E: its By sheet and Marks worksheets have them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available yet. Save project files to your computer and back them up.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA separate TakeoffCraft desktop app\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom 0.5 the desktop app is CadCraft Studio, with all eight apps in one install. TakeoffCraft also runs in the browser.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"screen-and-input\"\u003EScreen and input\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA right-click (or long-press) menu\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse the toolbar, the panels and the keys. A long press finishes a run or an outline.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERotating the view\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPages rotated in the PDF already show upright.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-view\"\u003E3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing or editing marks in 3D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGo back to the sheet (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) to draw; select and delete work in 3D.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELining up stacked floors plotted at different places on their pages\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStack floors plotted alike; check the others one sheet at a time.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount posts sized like the counted object\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery post on a sheet is the same size; it marks where a count is, not what it is.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bidding\"\u003EBidding\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAddenda to a package that was already sent, award letters, a scope sheet each bidder signs off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESend a new package, and write the award yourself. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\"\u003ESend a package for bids\u003C/a\u003E covers what is built.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESending bid invitations from any account: while it is tested, mail to outside bidders is switched off except for accounts the CadCraft team has enabled\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownload the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScope CSV\u003C/span\u003E, email it yourself and type the bids in.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESending bid packages from a Studio window\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESend from TakeoffCraft in the browser.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERepricing an alternate&#039;s bid area with a chosen bid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlternates and bid-area subtotals use your own prices; adjust the alternate by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAutomatic reminders before a subcontractor&#039;s insurance or license date runs out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe date is flagged on the Subcontractors page once it is past or within 30 days; check it when you invite.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"panels\"\u003EPanels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoving the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panels move. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EArrange the panels\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"spreadsheets\"\u003ESpreadsheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe XLSX workbook has been checked in LibreOffice Calc, which recalculated every total to the same cent as TakeoffCraft. It has not been opened in Excel yet. If a spreadsheet program shows something unexpected, the CSV export holds the same estimate as plain values.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft is in development. This list is what the current build does not do, and how to get the job done anyway. Measuring Not built yet Instead, today Inserting a point in the middle of a run or outline Delete the mark and draw it again. Box (window) selection Shift + click each mark, or Ctrl+A for every mark on the sheet. See Copy, paste and duplicate marks. Rotating, mirroring or rescaling a pasted copy Paste on a sheet at the same scale and in the same orientation; draw the rest by hand. Reading a scale from a scanned sheet, or from a graphic scale bar Pick the preset, or calibrate on the bar's length. More than one scale per sheet (detail areas, enlarged plans) Measure the part at the sheet's scale, and take off the detail on its own enlarged sheet if the set has one. Items and pricing Not built yet Instead, today Assembly formulas that use an item's other kinds of marks (the doors of a room item in its floor's formulas) Formulas use the main type's totals; price the other kinds on their own rows. Assembly formulas worked out run by run Formulas use the item's totals; use N for per-run extras. Formulas that use another item's quantities Repeat the measurement in that item, or work it out in the spreadsheet export. Crews on assembly components, or a working day other than 8 hours Give the crew to the item; read crew-days for another day length as crew-hours ÷ your day. Markups other than notes, clouds and highlights (arrows, dimensions, stamps), or replies on a note Write it in the note's text; change its status as it is settled. A price library shared by a team, or kept with your account The library is kept in this browser: export it as a CSV and import it on the other computer. Trade packs reviewed by working estimators Every pack is a starter: check its assumptions against your job and your products before you price with it. See Trade packs. Prices in trade packs Packs bring assemblies only: set your own rates, or import your price list as a CSV. Renaming the takeoff The name comes from the first PDF; rename the downloaded files. Naming sheets by hand Sheets are named from the PDF's page labels; search by file name and page. Drawings (DWG and DXF) Not built yet Instead, today Plot style tables, pen assignments and CTB files on a drawing's sheets A drawing is drawn black, or in its colors darkened for paper; line weights come from the drawing. Opening a binary DXF, or a DWG older than AutoCAD 2000 Save it as an ASCII DXF or a newer DWG. Finding a linked drawing (xref) by the folder it sits in Open the linked drawing's file too, with the host: it is found by file name among the open files. Measuring 3D polylines, meshes, solids and regions Count them by layer and type, or measure the plan view with the Linear and Area tools. Text in a marked PDF from a drawing in scripts other than Latin Characters Helvetica has no glyph for print as \"?\" in the marked PDF; the drawing on screen is unaffected. A hand-over of a drawing to another app from the sheet bar Open the same file in that app. A drawing that carries the takeoff inside it, as PDF with the takeoff inside does for PDFs Save takeoff: the .takeoff.json reopens on the same drawing, found by its contents. Drawing or counting PDF underlays, images and OLE objects placed in a drawing Images show as a frame only. Open the underlay PDF itself as a sheet. Auto count Not built yet Instead, today Finding symbols on a sheet with no scale drawn at another size Give the sheet its scale, or search it with a box drawn there. Finding mirrored symbols Count those by hand, or search again with a box around a mirrored one. Files and sharing Not built yet Instead, today One PDF that both shows the marks and carries the takeoff Marked PDF for people to see the marks; PDF with the takeoff inside to reopen in TakeoffCraft. A CSV of quantities by sheet or of the marks Use XLSX: its By sheet and Marks worksheets have them. Cloud saving Not available yet. Save project files to your computer and back them up. A separate TakeoffCraft desktop app From 0.5 the desktop app is CadCraft Studio, with all eight apps in one install. TakeoffCraft also runs in the browser. Screen and input Not built yet Instead, today A right-click (or long-press) menu Use the toolbar, the panels and the keys. A long press finishes a run or an outline. Rotating the view Pages rotated in the PDF already show upright. 3D view Not built yet Instead, today Drawing or editing marks in 3D Go back to the sheet (3) to draw; select and delete work in 3D. Lining up stacked floors plotted at different places on their pages Stack floors plotted alike; check the others one sheet at a time. Count posts sized like the counted object Every post on a sheet is the same size; it marks where a count is, not what it is. Bidding Not built yet Instead, today Addenda to a package that was already sent, award letters, a scope sheet each bidder signs off Send a new package, and write the award yourself. Send a package for bids covers what is built. Sending bid invitations from any account: while it is tested, mail to outside bidders is switched off except for accounts the CadCraft team has enabled Download the Scope CSV, email it yourself and type the bids in. Sending bid packages from a Studio window Send from TakeoffCraft in the browser. Repricing an alternate's bid area with a chosen bid Alternates and bid-area subtotals use your own prices; adjust the alternate by hand. Automatic reminders before a subcontractor's insurance or license date runs out The date is flagged on the Subcontractors page once it is past or within 30 days; check it when you invite. Panels Not built yet Instead, today Moving the Quantities table Only the Sheets and Items panels move. See Arrange the panels. Spreadsheets The XLSX workbook has been checked in LibreOffice Calc, which recalculated every total to the same cent as TakeoffCraft. It has not been opened in Excel yet. If a spreadsheet program shows something unexpected, the CSV export holds the same estimate as plain values.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"Como usar esta ajuda","summary":"Abra a ajuda, pesquise nela e veja os passos para o seu computador.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["ajuda","f1","pesquisa","tutoriais","manual"],"headings":[{"id":"abrir-a-ajuda","text":"Abrir a ajuda","level":2},{"id":"encontrar-um-t-pico","text":"Encontrar um tópico","level":2},{"id":"passos-para-o-seu-computador","text":"Passos para o seu computador","level":2},{"id":"a-mesma-ajuda-no-site","text":"A mesma ajuda no site","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETodo app traz sua própria ajuda. Ela faz parte do app, então abre sem conexão e sempre descreve a versão que você está executando.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"abrir-a-ajuda\"\u003EAbrir a ajuda\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ou escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAjuda\u003C/span\u003E na barra no alto do app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ou escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFechar\u003C/span\u003E para guardá-la. Seu desenho continua como estava.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENa maioria dos teclados de Mac, a fileira de cima controla brilho e som. Pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E junto com a tecla \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (ou a tecla do globo), ou use o botão \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAjuda\u003C/span\u003E. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003ETeclas em um Mac\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"encontrar-um-t-pico\"\u003EEncontrar um tópico\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDigite na caixa de pesquisa no alto da ajuda. Os resultados se atualizam conforme você digita e incluem nomes de comandos de outros programas, então digitar o que você digitaria em outro lugar geralmente leva à página certa.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOs tópicos são agrupados do mesmo jeito em todos os apps: primeiros passos, passo a passo, vindo de outro software, referência e solução de problemas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOs links dentro de um tópico abrem o próximo tópico no mesmo painel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVoltar\u003C/span\u003E leva de volta ao ponto onde você estava.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"passos-para-o-seu-computador\"\u003EPassos para o seu computador\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlguns passos diferem entre Mac, Windows e Linux, entre navegadores, e entre estar online e offline. A ajuda descobre qual se aplica a você e mostra esses passos. A linha no alto de um tópico diz o que ela escolheu, por exemplo &quot;Mostrando os passos para Mac, Chrome ou Edge, online&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlterar\u003C/span\u003E nessa linha para selecionar outro computador ou navegador, por exemplo para ajudar um colega.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMostrar tudo\u003C/span\u003E para ver todas as versões de todos os passos, cada uma identificada.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOs atalhos de teclado são escritos como o seu teclado os identifica: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E aparece como ⌘S em um Mac e Ctrl+S no Windows e no Linux.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-mesma-ajuda-no-site\"\u003EA mesma ajuda no site\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO site mostra os mesmos tópicos em getcadcraft.com/tutorials, com os passos de cada plataforma. No app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir no site\u003C/span\u003E no fim de um tópico abre essa página quando você está online.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Todo app traz sua própria ajuda. Ela faz parte do app, então abre sem conexão e sempre descreve a versão que você está executando. Abrir a ajuda Pressione F1 ou escolha Ajuda na barra no alto do app. Pressione Esc ou escolha Fechar para guardá-la. Seu desenho continua como estava. Na maioria dos teclados de Mac, a fileira de cima controla brilho e som. Pressione F1 junto com a tecla fn (ou a tecla do globo), ou use o botão Ajuda. Veja Teclas em um Mac. Encontrar um tópico Digite na caixa de pesquisa no alto da ajuda. Os resultados se atualizam conforme você digita e incluem nomes de comandos de outros programas, então digitar o que você digitaria em outro lugar geralmente leva à página certa. Os tópicos são agrupados do mesmo jeito em todos os apps: primeiros passos, passo a passo, vindo de outro software, referência e solução de problemas. Os links dentro de um tópico abrem o próximo tópico no mesmo painel. Voltar leva de volta ao ponto onde você estava. Passos para o seu computador Alguns passos diferem entre Mac, Windows e Linux, entre navegadores, e entre estar online e offline. A ajuda descobre qual se aplica a você e mostra esses passos. A linha no alto de um tópico diz o que ela escolheu, por exemplo \"Mostrando os passos para Mac, Chrome ou Edge, online\". Escolha Alterar nessa linha para selecionar outro computador ou navegador, por exemplo para ajudar um colega. Escolha Mostrar tudo para ver todas as versões de todos os passos, cada uma identificada. Os atalhos de teclado são escritos como o seu teclado os identifica: Ctrl+S aparece como ⌘S em um Mac e Ctrl+S no Windows e no Linux. A mesma ajuda no site O site mostra os mesmos tópicos em getcadcraft.com/tutorials, com os passos de cada plataforma. No app, Abrir no site no fim de um tópico abre essa página quando você está online.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"Navegadores e computadores","summary":"Em quais navegadores os apps funcionam, o que muda entre eles e o que seu computador precisa ter.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","navegador","mac","windows","linux","ipad","tablet","webgl","requisitos","memória"],"headings":[{"id":"qual-navegador","text":"Qual navegador","level":2},{"id":"o-que-muda-entre-navegadores","text":"O que muda entre navegadores","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-e-linux","text":"Mac, Windows e Linux","level":2},{"id":"tablets-e-telas-sens-veis-ao-toque","text":"Tablets e telas sensíveis ao toque","level":2},{"id":"o-navegador-e-o-sistema-mais-antigos","text":"O navegador e o sistema mais antigos","level":2},{"id":"o-que-seu-computador-precisa-ter","text":"O que seu computador precisa ter","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOs apps funcionam dentro de um navegador. Eles fazem o trabalho no seu próprio computador: seus arquivos são abertos ali e nunca enviados para nós (veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EPara onde vão seus arquivos\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"qual-navegador\"\u003EQual navegador\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse uma versão atualizada de um destes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENavegador\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMecanismo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EObservações\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EO mais completo: seleção de pastas, salvamento para uso offline, tratamento de arquivos.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFunciona. Abrir uma pasta inteira usa um seletor mais simples. O salvamento offline pode não funcionar em janelas privadas.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFunciona no Mac e no iPad. Abrir uma pasta inteira usa um seletor mais simples. O Safari pode apagar uma cópia offline que você não usa há algumas semanas.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOs três mecanismos servem para o trabalho do dia a dia. Quando um app faz algo de forma diferente em um deles, a ajuda mostra os passos para o seu navegador.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-muda-entre-navegadores\"\u003EO que muda entre navegadores\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAbrir uma pasta.\u003C/strong\u003E Os navegadores Chromium mostram um seletor de pastas de verdade e podem ler a pasta diretamente. O Firefox e o Safari pedem que você escolha uma pasta e depois leem todos os arquivos dela, o que demora mais para pastas de projeto grandes; arrastar a pasta para o app funciona nos três.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESalvar.\u003C/strong\u003E Os apps salvam baixando um arquivo. O seu navegador decide para onde vão os downloads e se pergunta antes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo Chrome ou no Edge, ative &quot;Perguntar onde salvar cada arquivo antes de fazer download&quot; (em Configurações, em Downloads) para escolher uma pasta toda vez.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo Firefox, abra Configurações, depois Geral, depois Downloads, e escolha &quot;Sempre perguntar onde salvar os arquivos&quot; para escolher uma pasta toda vez.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo Safari, abra Ajustes, depois Geral, e defina &quot;Local de download de arquivos&quot; como &quot;Perguntar para cada download&quot; para escolher uma pasta toda vez.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-e-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows e Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOs apps são iguais em todos os computadores. O que muda é o teclado: os atalhos são escritos automaticamente para o seu computador. Veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003ETeclas, mouses e trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tablets-e-telas-sens-veis-ao-toque\"\u003ETablets e telas sensíveis ao toque\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlguns apps aceitam toque (um dedo desenha ou seleciona, dois dedos fazem pinça para zoom e pan). A ajuda de cada app diz se aceita. No iPad, use o Safari ou o Chrome. Um teclado ajuda nos apps que usam comandos digitados.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-navegador-e-o-sistema-mais-antigos\"\u003EO navegador e o sistema mais antigos\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELemos estes dados do código dos apps 0.7, não de um palpite: cada arquivo foi analisado e cada recurso da linguagem que ele usa foi consultado nos dados de compatibilidade publicados dos navegadores. Abaixo destas versões os apps nem conseguem iniciar, porque o navegador não entende o código.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EVersão mais antiga que consegue iniciar os apps\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium 94\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E93\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari (Mac, iPad, iPhone)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E16.4 (macOS 13.3, iPadOS e iOS 16.4)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO CadCraft Studio traz seu próprio mecanismo web no Windows, então precisa do Windows 10 ou 11; no Mac ele usa o do sistema, então precisa do macOS 13.3 ou posterior. Configurações › Atualizações no Studio mostra em que sua cópia roda.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEsta é a versão mais antiga que \u003Cem\u003Einicia\u003C/em\u003E, lida do código. Não é uma lista de navegadores antigos em que executamos os apps, então um navegador mais antigo que inicie ainda pode não ter algum recurso.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-seu-computador-precisa-ter\"\u003EO que seu computador precisa ter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEstes são mínimos planejados para os apps, não requisitos medidos:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB de memória (16 GB são recomendados para modelos federados grandes).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUm chip gráfico com WebGL 2 para os apps 3D. Se uma vista 3D continua em branco, verifique se a aceleração de hardware está ativada nas configurações do navegador.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUma tela com pelo menos 1280 pixels de largura para as barras de ferramentas completas; telas menores as quebram em linhas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EArquivos muito grandes (conjuntos de plantas grandes, modelos grandes) usam muita memória em uma guia do navegador. Feche outras guias pesadas se um app ficar lento.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Os apps funcionam dentro de um navegador. Eles fazem o trabalho no seu próprio computador: seus arquivos são abertos ali e nunca enviados para nós (veja Para onde vão seus arquivos). Qual navegador Use uma versão atualizada de um destes: Navegador Mecanismo Observações Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium O mais completo: seleção de pastas, salvamento para uso offline, tratamento de arquivos. Firefox Gecko Funciona. Abrir uma pasta inteira usa um seletor mais simples. O salvamento offline pode não funcionar em janelas privadas. Safari WebKit Funciona no Mac e no iPad. Abrir uma pasta inteira usa um seletor mais simples. O Safari pode apagar uma cópia offline que você não usa há algumas semanas. Os três mecanismos servem para o trabalho do dia a dia. Quando um app faz algo de forma diferente em um deles, a ajuda mostra os passos para o seu navegador. O que muda entre navegadores Abrir uma pasta. Os navegadores Chromium mostram um seletor de pastas de verdade e podem ler a pasta diretamente. O Firefox e o Safari pedem que você escolha uma pasta e depois leem todos os arquivos dela, o que demora mais para pastas de projeto grandes; arrastar a pasta para o app funciona nos três. Salvar. Os apps salvam baixando um arquivo. O seu navegador decide para onde vão os downloads e se pergunta antes. No Chrome ou no Edge, ative \"Perguntar onde salvar cada arquivo antes de fazer download\" (em Configurações, em Downloads) para escolher uma pasta toda vez. No Firefox, abra Configurações, depois Geral, depois Downloads, e escolha \"Sempre perguntar onde salvar os arquivos\" para escolher uma pasta toda vez. No Safari, abra Ajustes, depois Geral, e defina \"Local de download de arquivos\" como \"Perguntar para cada download\" para escolher uma pasta toda vez. Mac, Windows e Linux Os apps são iguais em todos os computadores. O que muda é o teclado: os atalhos são escritos automaticamente para o seu computador. Veja Teclas, mouses e trackpads. Tablets e telas sensíveis ao toque Alguns apps aceitam toque (um dedo desenha ou seleciona, dois dedos fazem pinça para zoom e pan). A ajuda de cada app diz se aceita. No iPad, use o Safari ou o Chrome. Um teclado ajuda nos apps que usam comandos digitados. O navegador e o sistema mais antigos Lemos estes dados do código dos apps 0.7, não de um palpite: cada arquivo foi analisado e cada recurso da linguagem que ele usa foi consultado nos dados de compatibilidade publicados dos navegadores. Abaixo destas versões os apps nem conseguem iniciar, porque o navegador não entende o código. Versão mais antiga que consegue iniciar os apps Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium 94 Firefox 93 Safari (Mac, iPad, iPhone) 16.4 (macOS 13.3, iPadOS e iOS 16.4) O CadCraft Studio traz seu próprio mecanismo web no Windows, então precisa do Windows 10 ou 11; no Mac ele usa o do sistema, então precisa do macOS 13.3 ou posterior. Configurações › Atualizações no Studio mostra em que sua cópia roda. Esta é a versão mais antiga que inicia, lida do código. Não é uma lista de navegadores antigos em que executamos os apps, então um navegador mais antigo que inicie ainda pode não ter algum recurso. O que seu computador precisa ter Estes são mínimos planejados para os apps, não requisitos medidos: 8 GB de memória (16 GB são recomendados para modelos federados grandes). Um chip gráfico com WebGL 2 para os apps 3D. Se uma vista 3D continua em branco, verifique se a aceleração de hardware está ativada nas configurações do navegador. Uma tela com pelo menos 1280 pixels de largura para as barras de ferramentas completas; telas menores as quebram em linhas. Arquivos muito grandes (conjuntos de plantas grandes, modelos grandes) usam muita memória em uma guia do navegador. Feche outras guias pesadas se um app ficar lento.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"Teclas, mouses e trackpads","summary":"Como os atalhos são escritos, teclas de função em um Mac, clique com o botão direito e pan sem mouse.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["teclado","atalho","ctrl","command","cmd","option","fn","teclas de função","trackpad","clique direito","botão do meio","magic mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"como-os-atalhos-s-o-escritos","text":"Como os atalhos são escritos","level":2},{"id":"teclas-de-fun-o-em-um-mac","text":"Teclas de função em um Mac","level":2},{"id":"atalhos-que-o-navegador-mant-m","text":"Atalhos que o navegador mantém","level":2},{"id":"bot-es-do-mouse-em-um-trackpad","text":"Botões do mouse em um trackpad","level":2},{"id":"digitar-em-uma-caixa-de-texto","text":"Digitar em uma caixa de texto","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"como-os-atalhos-s-o-escritos\"\u003EComo os atalhos são escritos\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA ajuda escreve cada atalho como o seu teclado o identifica.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEscrito\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEm um Mac\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENo Windows e no Linux\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECommand (⌘) e S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl e S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) e F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift e F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOption (⌥) e ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt e ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea tecla Delete\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn e Delete (apagar para a frente)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUm sinal de mais significa manter as teclas pressionadas juntas. &quot;Depois&quot; significa pressionar uma após a outra.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA maioria dos atalhos dos apps aceita Command ou Control em um Mac, então \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E funciona dos dois jeitos. Alguns, como a tela cheia (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), usam a tecla Control em todos os computadores; a ajuda avisa onde isso se aplica.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"teclas-de-fun-o-em-um-mac\"\u003ETeclas de função em um Mac\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENa maioria dos teclados de Mac, a fileira de cima controla brilho, volume e mídia. Para enviar F1 a F12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Emantenha pressionada a tecla \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (ou a tecla do globo) enquanto pressiona a tecla, ou\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eabra \u003Cstrong\u003EAjustes do Sistema\u003C/strong\u003E, depois \u003Cstrong\u003ETeclado\u003C/strong\u003E, depois \u003Cstrong\u003EAtalhos de Teclado\u003C/strong\u003E, depois \u003Cstrong\u003ETeclas de Função\u003C/strong\u003E, e ative &quot;Usar as teclas F1, F2 etc. como teclas de função padrão&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlgumas teclas F também são reservadas pelo próprio macOS (por exemplo, Mission Control). Se uma tecla não faz nada no app, tente com fn ou use o botão da barra de ferramentas.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"atalhos-que-o-navegador-mant-m\"\u003EAtalhos que o navegador mantém\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUma página da web não consegue tomar alguns atalhos do navegador, aconteça o que acontecer. Em todos os apps:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sempre abrem ou fecham janelas e guias do navegador. Os apps usam botões para Novo e Fechar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E em um Mac e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E no Windows encerram o navegador. Se houver trabalho não salvo, o navegador pergunta antes (&quot;Sair do site?&quot;); escolha ficar e depois salve.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA tela cheia do próprio navegador (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F, ou o botão verde da janela\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) continua funcionando. Os apps também têm seus próprios modos de tela em \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, que podem ocultar também as barras do app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bot-es-do-mouse-em-um-trackpad\"\u003EBotões do mouse em um trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOs apps usam o botão direito e o botão do meio. Sem um mouse de três botões:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClique com o botão direito:\u003C/strong\u003E clique com dois dedos. Ative &quot;Clique secundário&quot; em \u003Cstrong\u003EAjustes do Sistema\u003C/strong\u003E, depois \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, se o clique com dois dedos não fizer nada. Manter \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pressionada ao clicar abre o menu do navegador, mas dentro de um desenho alguns apps o leem como um clique comum (com Control pressionada), então prefira o clique com dois dedos.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArrastar com o botão do meio (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E cada app oferece outra forma de fazer pan, como manter \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pressionada enquanto arrasta ou arrastar com o botão direito. A página de teclado do app diz qual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom com a roda:\u003C/strong\u003E a rolagem com dois dedos no trackpad dá zoom na maioria dos apps. O gesto de pinça também dá zoom onde o app o aceita.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClique com o botão direito:\u003C/strong\u003E clique com dois dedos ou clique no canto inferior direito do touchpad. Isso depende das configurações do seu touchpad.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArrastar com o botão do meio (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E cada app oferece outra forma de fazer pan, como manter \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pressionada enquanto arrasta ou arrastar com o botão direito. A página de teclado do app diz qual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom com a roda:\u003C/strong\u003E a rolagem com dois dedos no touchpad dá zoom na maioria dos apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"digitar-em-uma-caixa-de-texto\"\u003EDigitar em uma caixa de texto\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEnquanto você digita em uma caixa (um nome, uma nota, uma pesquisa), os atalhos de uma letra do app ficam desligados, então digitar &quot;L&quot; digita um L. Pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ou clique no desenho para devolver as teclas ao app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Como os atalhos são escritos A ajuda escreve cada atalho como o seu teclado o identifica. Escrito Em um Mac No Windows e no Linux Ctrl+S Command (⌘) e S Ctrl e S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) e F Ctrl, Shift e F Alt+← Option (⌥) e ← Alt e ← Backspace a tecla Delete Backspace Delete fn e Delete (apagar para a frente) Delete Enter Return Enter Um sinal de mais significa manter as teclas pressionadas juntas. \"Depois\" significa pressionar uma após a outra. A maioria dos atalhos dos apps aceita Command ou Control em um Mac, então Ctrl+Z funciona dos dois jeitos. Alguns, como a tela cheia (Ctrl+Shift+F), usam a tecla Control em todos os computadores; a ajuda avisa onde isso se aplica. Teclas de função em um Mac Na maioria dos teclados de Mac, a fileira de cima controla brilho, volume e mídia. Para enviar F1 a F12: mantenha pressionada a tecla fn (ou a tecla do globo) enquanto pressiona a tecla, ou abra Ajustes do Sistema, depois Teclado, depois Atalhos de Teclado, depois Teclas de Função, e ative \"Usar as teclas F1, F2 etc. como teclas de função padrão\". Algumas teclas F também são reservadas pelo próprio macOS (por exemplo, Mission Control). Se uma tecla não faz nada no app, tente com fn ou use o botão da barra de ferramentas. Atalhos que o navegador mantém Uma página da web não consegue tomar alguns atalhos do navegador, aconteça o que acontecer. Em todos os apps: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T e Ctrl+W sempre abrem ou fecham janelas e guias do navegador. Os apps usam botões para Novo e Fechar. Ctrl+Q em um Mac e Alt+F4 no Windows encerram o navegador. Se houver trabalho não salvo, o navegador pergunta antes (\"Sair do site?\"); escolha ficar e depois salve. A tela cheia do próprio navegador (Control-Command-F, ou o botão verde da janela / F11) continua funcionando. Os apps também têm seus próprios modos de tela em Ctrl+Shift+F, que podem ocultar também as barras do app. Botões do mouse em um trackpad Os apps usam o botão direito e o botão do meio. Sem um mouse de três botões: Clique com o botão direito: clique com dois dedos. Ative \"Clique secundário\" em Ajustes do Sistema, depois Trackpad, se o clique com dois dedos não fizer nada. Manter Ctrl pressionada ao clicar abre o menu do navegador, mas dentro de um desenho alguns apps o leem como um clique comum (com Control pressionada), então prefira o clique com dois dedos. Arrastar com o botão do meio (pan): cada app oferece outra forma de fazer pan, como manter Space pressionada enquanto arrasta ou arrastar com o botão direito. A página de teclado do app diz qual. Zoom com a roda: a rolagem com dois dedos no trackpad dá zoom na maioria dos apps. O gesto de pinça também dá zoom onde o app o aceita. Clique com o botão direito: clique com dois dedos ou clique no canto inferior direito do touchpad. Isso depende das configurações do seu touchpad. Arrastar com o botão do meio (pan): cada app oferece outra forma de fazer pan, como manter Space pressionada enquanto arrasta ou arrastar com o botão direito. A página de teclado do app diz qual. Zoom com a roda: a rolagem com dois dedos no touchpad dá zoom na maioria dos apps. Digitar em uma caixa de texto Enquanto você digita em uma caixa (um nome, uma nota, uma pesquisa), os atalhos de uma letra do app ficam desligados, então digitar \"L\" digita um L. Pressione Esc ou clique no desenho para devolver as teclas ao app.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"Localizar uma ferramenta ou configuração","summary":"Pressione Ctrl+K e digite algumas letras para executar qualquer ferramenta, alterar uma configuração ou abrir um tópico da ajuda.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["localizar","pesquisar","busca","paleta de comandos","abertura rápida","executar comando","ctrl k","cmd k","ferramenta","configuração","atalho"],"headings":[{"id":"localizar-e-executar","text":"Localizar e executar","level":2},{"id":"o-que-saber","text":"O que saber","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETodo app tem uma caixa de pesquisa para suas ferramentas, suas configurações e esta ajuda. Você não precisa saber onde uma ferramenta fica na barra de ferramentas.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"localizar-e-executar\"\u003ELocalizar e executar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ou escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELocalizar\u003C/span\u003E na barra no alto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDigite algumas letras do que procura: &quot;concordância&quot;, &quot;texto do painel&quot;, &quot;unidades&quot;. Cada palavra digitada precisa corresponder, então acrescente uma segunda palavra para reduzir a lista.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E para executar a primeira correspondência, ou mova-se antes com as teclas de seta. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fecha a caixa.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUma ferramenta é executada como se você tivesse clicado nela na barra de ferramentas. Uma configuração abre \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E nessa configuração. Um tópico da ajuda abre aqui, no painel de ajuda.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-saber\"\u003EO que saber\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EO que você usou por último vem primeiro na próxima vez, então uma ferramenta de uso frequente fica a uma tecla de distância.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFerramentas que não podem ser executadas agora, por exemplo antes de abrir um arquivo, aparecem na lista, mas esmaecidas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAs ferramentas dos complementos também estão na lista, depois que o complemento é ativado.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Todo app tem uma caixa de pesquisa para suas ferramentas, suas configurações e esta ajuda. Você não precisa saber onde uma ferramenta fica na barra de ferramentas. Localizar e executar Pressione Ctrl+K ou escolha Localizar na barra no alto. Digite algumas letras do que procura: \"concordância\", \"texto do painel\", \"unidades\". Cada palavra digitada precisa corresponder, então acrescente uma segunda palavra para reduzir a lista. Pressione Enter para executar a primeira correspondência, ou mova-se antes com as teclas de seta. Esc fecha a caixa. Uma ferramenta é executada como se você tivesse clicado nela na barra de ferramentas. Uma configuração abre Configurações nessa configuração. Um tópico da ajuda abre aqui, no painel de ajuda. O que saber O que você usou por último vem primeiro na próxima vez, então uma ferramenta de uso frequente fica a uma tecla de distância. Ferramentas que não podem ser executadas agora, por exemplo antes de abrir um arquivo, aparecem na lista, mas esmaecidas. As ferramentas dos complementos também estão na lista, depois que o complemento é ativado.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"Organizar os painéis e usar um segundo monitor","summary":"Acople os painéis laterais à esquerda, à direita ou embaixo, empilhe-os, agrupe-os em guias, fixe ou oculte-os, faça-os flutuar sobre o desenho, deixe o layout mais denso, escolha quantas linhas a barra de ferramentas usa ou mova-os para uma janela própria em outra tela.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["painéis","acoplar","flutuar","mover painel","arrastar","layout","guias","grupo de guias","empilhar","fixar","ocultação automática","ocultar painel","fechar painel","compacta","densidade do layout","denso","linhas da barra de ferramentas","quebrar","1 linha","2 linhas","linhas da barra","segundo monitor","duas telas","monitor duplo","vários monitores","janela","pop-up","redefinir layout"],"headings":[{"id":"mover-um-painel-na-janela","text":"Mover um painel na janela","level":2},{"id":"agrupar-pain-is-em-guias","text":"Agrupar painéis em guias","level":2},{"id":"fixar-ocultar-e-trazer-de-volta","text":"Fixar, ocultar e trazer de volta","level":2},{"id":"deixar-o-layout-mais-denso","text":"Deixar o layout mais denso","level":2},{"id":"escolher-quantas-linhas-a-barra-de-ferramentas-usa","text":"Escolher quantas linhas a barra de ferramentas usa","level":2},{"id":"usar-o-teclado","text":"Usar o teclado","level":2},{"id":"colocar-os-pain-is-em-um-segundo-monitor","text":"Colocar os painéis em um segundo monitor","level":2},{"id":"mais-de-uma-janela-de-pain-is","text":"Mais de uma janela de painéis","level":2},{"id":"mover-uma-caixa-de-di-logo","text":"Mover uma caixa de diálogo","level":2},{"id":"recolocar-os-pain-is","text":"Recolocar os painéis","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOs painéis ao lado do desenho podem ser movidos. Coloque-os onde forem mais úteis para você, ou dê a eles uma janela própria em um segundo monitor, para que o desenho ocupe todo o primeiro.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECada painel tem uma barra de título: uma barra fina com o nome do painel, um menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E, um botão de fixar e um botão de fechar no topo do painel. Versões anteriores à 0.7 têm apenas o nome e o menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mover-um-painel-na-janela\"\u003EMover um painel na janela\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArraste o painel pelo nome, no topo.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEnquanto arrasta, áreas com contorno aparecem à esquerda, à direita e embaixo do desenho. Solte o painel sobre uma delas para acoplá-lo ali. A área sobre a qual você está escurece.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESolte-o em qualquer outro ponto sobre o desenho para fazê-lo flutuar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPainéis podem dividir um mesmo lado: eles são empilhados, e cada linha entre dois deles pode ser arrastada para dividir o espaço (com a linha em foco, as teclas de seta a movem, e clicar duas vezes nela divide o espaço igualmente). Arraste a borda interna de um lado acoplado para deixá-lo mais largo ou mais estreito; clique duas vezes na borda para restaurá-la.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUm painel flutuante fica sobre o desenho. Mova-o pelo nome e redimensione-o arrastando seu canto inferior direito (ou use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E até o canto e depois as teclas de seta). Clique duas vezes no nome dele para acoplá-lo de novo onde estava.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EArraste com um dedo pelo nome do painel. Arraste o canto inferior direito de um painel flutuante com um dedo para redimensioná-lo, e as bordas entre painéis acoplados do mesmo jeito. Para flutuar ou acoplar sem arrastar, use o menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"agrupar-pain-is-em-guias\"\u003EAgrupar painéis em guias\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDois painéis podem dividir uma mesma moldura e mostrar um por vez. Arraste um painel pelo nome até a barra de título de outro painel (a barra fica com contorno enquanto você está sobre ela) e solte: a moldura passa a ter uma guia para cada painel, na barra de título. Clique em uma guia para mostrar aquele painel. Com uma guia em foco, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E trocam de guia, e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E vão para a primeira e para a última.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPara tirar um painel do grupo, escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESair do grupo de guias\u003C/span\u003E no menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E dele, ou arraste-o para outro lugar. Escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAgrupar com\u003C/span\u003E e o nome de um painel para agrupá-los sem arrastar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fixar-ocultar-e-trazer-de-volta\"\u003EFixar, ocultar e trazer de volta\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EO botão de fixar na barra de título de um painel o torna de \u003Cstrong\u003Eocultação automática\u003C/strong\u003E: o painel sai do lado em que estava acoplado e vira uma pequena guia nessa borda. Aponte para a guia, foque-a ou clique nela, e o painel desliza sobre o desenho; afaste-se (ou pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) e ele volta a se recolher. Clique de novo no botão de fixar, ou escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDesafixar\u003C/span\u003E, para acoplá-lo definitivamente.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EO botão de fechar, ou \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOcultar este painel\u003C/span\u003E no menu, oculta o painel e fecha o espaço do lado acoplado. Uma guia tracejada na borda o traz de volta: clique nela, ou foque-a e pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"deixar-o-layout-mais-denso\"\u003EDeixar o layout mais denso\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDensidade do layout\u003C/span\u003E tem duas opções. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfortável\u003C/span\u003E é o padrão. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompacta\u003C/span\u003E reduz a altura das linhas, o espaçamento interno dos painéis, os botões da barra de ferramentas e o texto dos painéis desse aplicativo, para caber mais na tela. A escolha é mantida em cada aplicativo. Uma tela de toque mantém os botões grandes nos dois casos, e um celular mantém o próprio layout. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedefinir layout\u003C/span\u003E não altera a densidade que você escolheu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"escolher-quantas-linhas-a-barra-de-ferramentas-usa\"\u003EEscolher quantas linhas a barra de ferramentas usa\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinhas da barra de ferramentas\u003C/span\u003E tem três opções. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuebrar\u003C/span\u003E é o padrão: todos os grupos de ferramentas aparecem, em quantas linhas forem necessárias. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E2 linhas\u003C/span\u003E mantém a barra de ferramentas em duas linhas e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E1 linha\u003C/span\u003E em uma, para que o desenho ganhe altura; os grupos que não cabem rolam para o lado dentro da barra (use a roda do mouse, um deslize com dois dedos ou \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E até uma ferramenta). Uma barra que já cabe em duas linhas continua como está com \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E2 linhas\u003C/span\u003E, porque uma grade só a deixaria mais alta. A escolha é mantida em cada aplicativo. Uma tela de toque e um celular mantêm o próprio layout. O CadCraft tem uma configuração própria para a barra de ferramentas, em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"usar-o-teclado\"\u003EUsar o teclado\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO botão \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E de cada painel abre um menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAcoplar à esquerda\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAcoplar à direita\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAcoplar embaixo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlutuar sobre o desenho\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFixar na borda\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOcultar este painel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAgrupar com\u003C/span\u003E outro painel, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover para uma nova janela\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecolocar\u003C/span\u003E. Use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E até o botão, pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, mova com as teclas de seta e pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E de novo. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fecha o menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"colocar-os-pain-is-em-um-segundo-monitor\"\u003EColocar os painéis em um segundo monitor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E em um painel e depois \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover todos os painéis para uma nova janela\u003C/span\u003E. Você também pode escolher \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover painéis para uma nova janela\u003C/span\u003E em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUma pequena janela abre com os painéis dentro. Arraste-a para o outro monitor e deixe-a do tamanho que quiser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EContinue trabalhando: os painéis continuam atuando sobre o desenho como antes, e o desenho ocupa a janela principal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOs atalhos continuam funcionando enquanto a janela de painéis está em primeiro plano: as teclas pressionadas ali, exceto ao digitar em uma caixa, vão para o desenho. Perguntas e caixas de diálogo que o aplicativo faz continuam abrindo na janela principal, então procure por elas lá quando uma aparecer. O tema e o tamanho do texto dos painéis seguem a janela principal.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO Chrome e o Edge podem posicionar a janela na outra tela para você. Com uma segunda tela conectada, escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColocar na minha outra tela\u003C/span\u003E na janela de painéis (ou \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColocar os painéis na minha outra tela\u003C/span\u003E no menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E de um painel). O navegador pergunta uma vez se este site pode ver suas telas; depois que você permitir, a janela de painéis abre nessa tela sempre.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EArraste a janela de painéis para o outro monitor por conta própria. Na próxima vez, ela abre no mesmo lugar e com o mesmo tamanho.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mais-de-uma-janela-de-pain-is\"\u003EMais de uma janela de painéis\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECada painel pode ter uma janela própria: até quatro janelas de painéis, por exemplo uma por monitor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E de um painel, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover para uma nova janela\u003C/span\u003E abre outra janela para esse painel, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover para a janela de painéis 2\u003C/span\u003E (e assim por diante) o move para uma que já esteja aberta. A segunda janela se chama &quot;painéis 2&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArraste um painel pelo nome de uma janela para outra: solte sobre a janela principal para acoplá-lo ali (as áreas com contorno aparecem enquanto você está sobre elas) ou para fazê-lo flutuar, e sobre outra janela de painéis para movê-lo para dentro dela. Solte em qualquer outro lugar e ele continua onde estava.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUma janela de painéis sem painéis restantes se fecha sozinha.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDepois de recarregar, o aplicativo oferece de novo suas janelas de painéis. Os navegadores abrem uma janela por clique, então escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir a próxima\u003C/span\u003E uma vez para cada janela.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mover-uma-caixa-de-di-logo\"\u003EMover uma caixa de diálogo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EToda caixa de diálogo pode ser afastada do desenho: pressione e arraste sua barra de título. A caixa lembra onde você a deixou, cada uma por conta própria, e volta a esse lugar na próxima vez que abrir. Clique duas vezes na barra de título para centralizá-la de novo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPelo teclado, pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+uma tecla de seta, com o foco dentro da caixa, para movê-la 16 pixels por vez; adicione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E para mover 64. Uma caixa que fique fora da tela (depois de uma janela ou tela menor) é trazida de volta.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEm um celular, as caixas de diálogo continuam ocupando a largura toda e não são movidas.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo CadCraft Studio, a caixa \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlotar para PDF\u003C/span\u003E do CadCraft, que você pode deixar aberta enquanto trabalha, tem um botão na barra de título, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir em uma janela própria\u003C/span\u003E. A caixa passa para uma janela própria, que você pode colocar em outro monitor. Fechar a caixa fecha a janela, e fechar a janela fecha a caixa.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"recolocar-os-pain-is\"\u003ERecolocar os painéis\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecolocar os painéis\u003C/span\u003E na janela de painéis, ou simplesmente feche-a. Os painéis voltam para onde estavam.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFechar ou recarregar a janela principal também fecha a janela de painéis. Quando o aplicativo abrir de novo, ele informa que seus painéis estavam em uma janela própria; escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir\u003C/span\u003E para trazer a janela de volta, ou \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EManter aqui\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedefinir layout\u003C/span\u003E devolve cada painel ao lugar onde começou.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESe nada abrir ao mover painéis para uma nova janela, o navegador bloqueou o pop-up. O aplicativo informa isso e mantém os painéis onde estavam. Permita pop-ups para getcadcraft.com e tente de novo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO layout é mantido para cada aplicativo neste navegador, junto com suas outras configurações.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Os painéis ao lado do desenho podem ser movidos. Coloque-os onde forem mais úteis para você, ou dê a eles uma janela própria em um segundo monitor, para que o desenho ocupe todo o primeiro. Cada painel tem uma barra de título: uma barra fina com o nome do painel, um menu ⋯, um botão de fixar e um botão de fechar no topo do painel. Versões anteriores à 0.7 têm apenas o nome e o menu ⋯. Mover um painel na janela Arraste o painel pelo nome, no topo. Enquanto arrasta, áreas com contorno aparecem à esquerda, à direita e embaixo do desenho. Solte o painel sobre uma delas para acoplá-lo ali. A área sobre a qual você está escurece. Solte-o em qualquer outro ponto sobre o desenho para fazê-lo flutuar. Painéis podem dividir um mesmo lado: eles são empilhados, e cada linha entre dois deles pode ser arrastada para dividir o espaço (com a linha em foco, as teclas de seta a movem, e clicar duas vezes nela divide o espaço igualmente). Arraste a borda interna de um lado acoplado para deixá-lo mais largo ou mais estreito; clique duas vezes na borda para restaurá-la. Um painel flutuante fica sobre o desenho. Mova-o pelo nome e redimensione-o arrastando seu canto inferior direito (ou use Tab até o canto e depois as teclas de seta). Clique duas vezes no nome dele para acoplá-lo de novo onde estava. Arraste com um dedo pelo nome do painel. Arraste o canto inferior direito de um painel flutuante com um dedo para redimensioná-lo, e as bordas entre painéis acoplados do mesmo jeito. Para flutuar ou acoplar sem arrastar, use o menu ⋯. Agrupar painéis em guias Dois painéis podem dividir uma mesma moldura e mostrar um por vez. Arraste um painel pelo nome até a barra de título de outro painel (a barra fica com contorno enquanto você está sobre ela) e solte: a moldura passa a ter uma guia para cada painel, na barra de título. Clique em uma guia para mostrar aquele painel. Com uma guia em foco, ← e → trocam de guia, e Home e End vão para a primeira e para a última. Para tirar um painel do grupo, escolha Sair do grupo de guias no menu ⋯ dele, ou arraste-o para outro lugar. Escolha Agrupar com e o nome de um painel para agrupá-los sem arrastar. Fixar, ocultar e trazer de volta O botão de fixar na barra de título de um painel o torna de ocultação automática: o painel sai do lado em que estava acoplado e vira uma pequena guia nessa borda. Aponte para a guia, foque-a ou clique nela, e o painel desliza sobre o desenho; afaste-se (ou pressione Esc) e ele volta a se recolher. Clique de novo no botão de fixar, ou escolha Desafixar, para acoplá-lo definitivamente. O botão de fechar, ou Ocultar este painel no menu, oculta o painel e fecha o espaço do lado acoplado. Uma guia tracejada na borda o traz de volta: clique nela, ou foque-a e pressione Enter. Deixar o layout mais denso Configurações › Geral › Densidade do layout tem duas opções. Confortável é o padrão. Compacta reduz a altura das linhas, o espaçamento interno dos painéis, os botões da barra de ferramentas e o texto dos painéis desse aplicativo, para caber mais na tela. A escolha é mantida em cada aplicativo. Uma tela de toque mantém os botões grandes nos dois casos, e um celular mantém o próprio layout. Redefinir layout não altera a densidade que você escolheu. Escolher quantas linhas a barra de ferramentas usa Configurações › Geral › Linhas da barra de ferramentas tem três opções. Quebrar é o padrão: todos os grupos de ferramentas aparecem, em quantas linhas forem necessárias. 2 linhas mantém a barra de ferramentas em duas linhas e 1 linha em uma, para que o desenho ganhe altura; os grupos que não cabem rolam para o lado dentro da barra (use a roda do mouse, um deslize com dois dedos ou Tab até uma ferramenta). Uma barra que já cabe em duas linhas continua como está com 2 linhas, porque uma grade só a deixaria mais alta. A escolha é mantida em cada aplicativo. Uma tela de toque e um celular mantêm o próprio layout. O CadCraft tem uma configuração própria para a barra de ferramentas, em Configurações › CadCraft. Usar o teclado O botão ⋯ de cada painel abre um menu: Acoplar à esquerda, Acoplar à direita, Acoplar embaixo, Flutuar sobre o desenho, Fixar na borda, Ocultar este painel, Agrupar com outro painel, Mover para uma nova janela e Recolocar. Use Tab até o botão, pressione Enter, mova com as teclas de seta e pressione Enter de novo. Esc fecha o menu. Colocar os painéis em um segundo monitor Escolha ⋯ em um painel e depois Mover todos os painéis para uma nova janela. Você também pode escolher Mover painéis para uma nova janela em Configurações. Uma pequena janela abre com os painéis dentro. Arraste-a para o outro monitor e deixe-a do tamanho que quiser. Continue trabalhando: os painéis continuam atuando sobre o desenho como antes, e o desenho ocupa a janela principal. Os atalhos continuam funcionando enquanto a janela de painéis está em primeiro plano: as teclas pressionadas ali, exceto ao digitar em uma caixa, vão para o desenho. Perguntas e caixas de diálogo que o aplicativo faz continuam abrindo na janela principal, então procure por elas lá quando uma aparecer. O tema e o tamanho do texto dos painéis seguem a janela principal. O Chrome e o Edge podem posicionar a janela na outra tela para você. Com uma segunda tela conectada, escolha Colocar na minha outra tela na janela de painéis (ou Colocar os painéis na minha outra tela no menu ⋯ de um painel). O navegador pergunta uma vez se este site pode ver suas telas; depois que você permitir, a janela de painéis abre nessa tela sempre. Arraste a janela de painéis para o outro monitor por conta própria. Na próxima vez, ela abre no mesmo lugar e com o mesmo tamanho. Mais de uma janela de painéis Cada painel pode ter uma janela própria: até quatro janelas de painéis, por exemplo uma por monitor. No menu ⋯ de um painel, Mover para uma nova janela abre outra janela para esse painel, e Mover para a janela de painéis 2 (e assim por diante) o move para uma que já esteja aberta. A segunda janela se chama \"painéis 2\". Arraste um painel pelo nome de uma janela para outra: solte sobre a janela principal para acoplá-lo ali (as áreas com contorno aparecem enquanto você está sobre elas) ou para fazê-lo flutuar, e sobre outra janela de painéis para movê-lo para dentro dela. Solte em qualquer outro lugar e ele continua onde estava. Uma janela de painéis sem painéis restantes se fecha sozinha. Depois de recarregar, o aplicativo oferece de novo suas janelas de painéis. Os navegadores abrem uma janela por clique, então escolha Abrir a próxima uma vez para cada janela. Mover uma caixa de diálogo Toda caixa de diálogo pode ser afastada do desenho: pressione e arraste sua barra de título. A caixa lembra onde você a deixou, cada uma por conta própria, e volta a esse lugar na próxima vez que abrir. Clique duas vezes na barra de título para centralizá-la de novo. Pelo teclado, pressione Alt+Shift+uma tecla de seta, com o foco dentro da caixa, para movê-la 16 pixels por vez; adicione Ctrl para mover 64. Uma caixa que fique fora da tela (depois de uma janela ou tela menor) é trazida de volta. Em um celular, as caixas de diálogo continuam ocupando a largura toda e não são movidas. No CadCraft Studio, a caixa Plotar para PDF do CadCraft, que você pode deixar aberta enquanto trabalha, tem um botão na barra de título, Abrir em uma janela própria. A caixa passa para uma janela própria, que você pode colocar em outro monitor. Fechar a caixa fecha a janela, e fechar a janela fecha a caixa. Recolocar os painéis Escolha Recolocar os painéis na janela de painéis, ou simplesmente feche-a. Os painéis voltam para onde estavam. Fechar ou recarregar a janela principal também fecha a janela de painéis. Quando o aplicativo abrir de novo, ele informa que seus painéis estavam em uma janela própria; escolha Abrir para trazer a janela de volta, ou Manter aqui. Configurações › Geral › Redefinir layout devolve cada painel ao lugar onde começou. Se nada abrir ao mover painéis para uma nova janela, o navegador bloqueou o pop-up. O aplicativo informa isso e mantém os painéis onde estavam. Permita pop-ups para getcadcraft.com e tente de novo. O layout é mantido para cada aplicativo neste navegador, junto com suas outras configurações.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"layout-ribbon","scope":"basics","title":"Trabalhar no layout de faixa de opções e navegador do projeto","summary":"Mude o app para uma faixa de opções de guias sobre o desenho, com Propriedades e um navegador do projeto à esquerda e uma guia para cada vista aberta, e volte ao layout clássico quando quiser.","section":"start","order":36,"keywords":["layout","área de trabalho","faixa de opções","guias da faixa","navegador do projeto","navegador","paletas","paleta de propriedades","guias de vista","barra de status","acesso rápido","recolher faixa","minimizar faixa","janela da faixa","layout clássico","mudar layout","alternar layout","acoplado"],"headings":[{"id":"escolher-o-layout","text":"Escolher o layout","level":2},{"id":"o-que-fica-onde","text":"O que fica onde","level":2},{"id":"usar-a-faixa-de-op-es-pelo-teclado","text":"Usar a faixa de opções pelo teclado","level":2},{"id":"recolher-a-faixa-ou-dar-a-ela-uma-janela-pr-pria","text":"Recolher a faixa ou dar a ela uma janela própria","level":2},{"id":"seus-pain-is-continuam-seus","text":"Seus painéis continuam seus","level":2},{"id":"voltar-ao-layout-cl-ssico","text":"Voltar ao layout clássico","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFaixa de opções e navegador do projeto\u003C/strong\u003E é um dos três layouts de área de trabalho do app. Uma faixa de opções de guias corre pelo alto, as Propriedades e um navegador do projeto dividem o painel da esquerda, cada vista que você abre ganha uma guia sobre o desenho e uma barra de status fica embaixo. As ferramentas são as do próprio app: tudo o que você faz no layout clássico funciona aqui também, e seu desenho, seu estilo de menu e suas teclas continuam como estão.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"escolher-o-layout\"\u003EEscolher o layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAbra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E (a engrenagem, ou \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha a guia \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E e vá a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÁrea de trabalho\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFaixa de opções e navegador do projeto\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO layout muda na hora. A escolha vale para todos os apps neste navegador: cada app passa a abrir nele, e ele já está no lugar antes de a página ser desenhada, então você nunca vê o layout clássico primeiro.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-fica-onde\"\u003EO que fica onde\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra de título:\u003C/strong\u003E o nome do app, botões de acesso rápido (abrir, salvar, desfazer, refazer), o nome do arquivo aberto, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EO que cada ferramenta faz aqui\u003C/span\u003E (uma lista de todas as ferramentas com uma caixa de pesquisa, dizendo quais estão prontas agora e por que as outras não estão), tela cheia, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAjuda\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E. Neste layout, a barra de título toma o lugar da barra do app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFaixa de opções:\u003C/strong\u003E as ferramentas do app em guias, cada guia em grupos. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArquivo\u003C/span\u003E à esquerda abre, salva e o resto. Alguns apps acrescentam uma guia própria enquanto você trabalha em um modo ou área de trabalho específico; ela fica acesa enquanto vale e some depois.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra de opções:\u003C/strong\u003E sob a faixa, o que a ferramenta em execução precisa de você, ou o que está selecionado.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPainel da esquerda:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPropriedades\u003C/span\u003E em cima e, embaixo, as paletas (o navegador do projeto e o conteúdo próprio do painel) como guias. Arraste a linha entre elas para dividir o espaço.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGuias de vista:\u003C/strong\u003E uma guia por vista aberta (uma folha, uma planta, uma cena…). Feche uma com seu botão de fechar; o arquivo continua aberto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra de vista e barra de status:\u003C/strong\u003E sob o desenho, os controles da própria vista (escala, zoom); embaixo, as mensagens do app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUma ferramenta que não pode ser executada agora fica esmaecida, e sua dica explica por quê (por exemplo &quot;Abra um arquivo primeiro&quot;, ou que o app ainda não tem essa ferramenta). Clique nela, ou pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sobre ela, para ler o motivo em uma caixa.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"usar-a-faixa-de-op-es-pelo-teclado\"\u003EUsar a faixa de opções pelo teclado\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECom uma guia da faixa em foco, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E movem entre as guias e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E vão à primeira e à última. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E encerra primeiro a ferramenta em execução, como no layout clássico, e todos os atalhos e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (modos de tela) funcionam como antes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"recolher-a-faixa-ou-dar-a-ela-uma-janela-pr-pria\"\u003ERecolher a faixa ou dar a ela uma janela própria\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClique duas vezes em uma guia da faixa para recolher a faixa até suas guias; um clique em uma guia mostra então suas ferramentas até você seguir adiante. Clique duas vezes de novo para mantê-la aberta. O botão \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover a faixa de opções\u003C/span\u003E na ponta direita da faixa também tem \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecolher a faixa de opções\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EManter a faixa de opções aberta\u003C/span\u003E. Cada app lembra a escolha.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover a faixa de opções\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover para uma nova janela\u003C/span\u003E coloca a faixa em uma janela própria, por exemplo em um segundo monitor. As teclas pressionadas ali chegam ao app. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecolocar a faixa de opções\u003C/span\u003E, ou fechar essa janela, a traz de volta.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAs configurações da barra de ferramentas valem para a faixa: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBarra de ferramentas\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÍcones e rótulos\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESomente ícones\u003C/span\u003E (a faixa então mostra duas fileiras de ícones pequenos por grupo) ou \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESomente rótulos\u003C/span\u003E, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMostrar nomes dos grupos (Arquivo, Desenhar, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"seus-pain-is-continuam-seus\"\u003ESeus painéis continuam seus\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOs painéis da esquerda e da direita mantêm tudo o que está descrito em \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EOrganizar os painéis\u003C/a\u003E: acoplar, flutuar, fixar, ocultar, guias, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover para uma nova janela\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedefinir layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"voltar-ao-layout-cl-ssico\"\u003EVoltar ao layout clássico\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAbra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÁrea de trabalho\u003C/span\u003E e escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft clássico\u003C/span\u003E. Cada barra de ferramentas, guia e painel volta ao lugar onde estava.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEm um celular, ou em uma janela de 760 pixels de largura ou menos, o app usa seu layout de celular, qualquer que seja a sua escolha. Uma janela mais larga traz de volta o seu layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPara experimentar outro arranjo, veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Eo layout de caixa de ferramentas e caixa de informações\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Faixa de opções e navegador do projeto é um dos três layouts de área de trabalho do app. Uma faixa de opções de guias corre pelo alto, as Propriedades e um navegador do projeto dividem o painel da esquerda, cada vista que você abre ganha uma guia sobre o desenho e uma barra de status fica embaixo. As ferramentas são as do próprio app: tudo o que você faz no layout clássico funciona aqui também, e seu desenho, seu estilo de menu e suas teclas continuam como estão. Escolher o layout Abra Configurações (a engrenagem, ou Ctrl+,). Escolha a guia Global e vá a Layout › Área de trabalho. Escolha Faixa de opções e navegador do projeto. O layout muda na hora. A escolha vale para todos os apps neste navegador: cada app passa a abrir nele, e ele já está no lugar antes de a página ser desenhada, então você nunca vê o layout clássico primeiro. O que fica onde Barra de título: o nome do app, botões de acesso rápido (abrir, salvar, desfazer, refazer), o nome do arquivo aberto, O que cada ferramenta faz aqui (uma lista de todas as ferramentas com uma caixa de pesquisa, dizendo quais estão prontas agora e por que as outras não estão), tela cheia, Ajuda e Configurações. Neste layout, a barra de título toma o lugar da barra do app. Faixa de opções: as ferramentas do app em guias, cada guia em grupos. Arquivo à esquerda abre, salva e o resto. Alguns apps acrescentam uma guia própria enquanto você trabalha em um modo ou área de trabalho específico; ela fica acesa enquanto vale e some depois. Barra de opções: sob a faixa, o que a ferramenta em execução precisa de você, ou o que está selecionado. Painel da esquerda: Propriedades em cima e, embaixo, as paletas (o navegador do projeto e o conteúdo próprio do painel) como guias. Arraste a linha entre elas para dividir o espaço. Guias de vista: uma guia por vista aberta (uma folha, uma planta, uma cena…). Feche uma com seu botão de fechar; o arquivo continua aberto. Barra de vista e barra de status: sob o desenho, os controles da própria vista (escala, zoom); embaixo, as mensagens do app. Uma ferramenta que não pode ser executada agora fica esmaecida, e sua dica explica por quê (por exemplo \"Abra um arquivo primeiro\", ou que o app ainda não tem essa ferramenta). Clique nela, ou pressione Enter sobre ela, para ler o motivo em uma caixa. Usar a faixa de opções pelo teclado Com uma guia da faixa em foco, ← e → movem entre as guias e Home e End vão à primeira e à última. Esc encerra primeiro a ferramenta em execução, como no layout clássico, e todos os atalhos e Ctrl+Shift+F (modos de tela) funcionam como antes. Recolher a faixa ou dar a ela uma janela própria Clique duas vezes em uma guia da faixa para recolher a faixa até suas guias; um clique em uma guia mostra então suas ferramentas até você seguir adiante. Clique duas vezes de novo para mantê-la aberta. O botão Mover a faixa de opções na ponta direita da faixa também tem Recolher a faixa de opções e Manter a faixa de opções aberta. Cada app lembra a escolha. Mover a faixa de opções › Mover para uma nova janela coloca a faixa em uma janela própria, por exemplo em um segundo monitor. As teclas pressionadas ali chegam ao app. Recolocar a faixa de opções, ou fechar essa janela, a traz de volta. As configurações da barra de ferramentas valem para a faixa: Configurações › Global › Barra de ferramentas › Ícones e rótulos, Somente ícones (a faixa então mostra duas fileiras de ícones pequenos por grupo) ou Somente rótulos, e Mostrar nomes dos grupos (Arquivo, Desenhar, …). Seus painéis continuam seus Os painéis da esquerda e da direita mantêm tudo o que está descrito em Organizar os painéis: acoplar, flutuar, fixar, ocultar, guias, Mover para uma nova janela e Redefinir layout. Voltar ao layout clássico Abra Configurações › Global › Layout › Área de trabalho e escolha CadCraft clássico. Cada barra de ferramentas, guia e painel volta ao lugar onde estava. Em um celular, ou em uma janela de 760 pixels de largura ou menos, o app usa seu layout de celular, qualquer que seja a sua escolha. Uma janela mais larga traz de volta o seu layout. Para experimentar outro arranjo, veja o layout de caixa de ferramentas e caixa de informações.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"layout-toolbox","scope":"basics","title":"Trabalhar no layout de caixa de ferramentas e caixa de informações","summary":"Mude o app para uma coluna de ferramentas à esquerda com espaços para as suas, uma caixa de informações no alto que acompanha a ferramenta em execução ou o que você escolheu, um navegador à direita, uma guia para cada vista, opções rápidas e uma paleta de edição, e volte ao layout clássico quando quiser.","section":"start","order":37,"keywords":["layout","área de trabalho","caixa de ferramentas","coluna de ferramentas","caixa de informações","barra de informações","navegador","mapa do projeto","barra rápida","opções rápidas","paleta de edição","paleta de acesso","minhas ferramentas","ferramentas favoritas","guias de vista","janela da caixa de ferramentas","layout clássico","mudar layout","alternar layout"],"headings":[{"id":"escolher-o-layout","text":"Escolher o layout","level":2},{"id":"o-que-fica-onde","text":"O que fica onde","level":2},{"id":"dar-caixa-de-ferramentas-uma-janela-pr-pria","text":"Dar à caixa de ferramentas uma janela própria","level":2},{"id":"seus-pain-is-continuam-seus","text":"Seus painéis continuam seus","level":2},{"id":"voltar-ao-layout-cl-ssico","text":"Voltar ao layout clássico","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECaixa de ferramentas e caixa de informações\u003C/strong\u003E é um dos três layouts de área de trabalho do app. As ferramentas de desenho ficam em uma coluna à esquerda, uma caixa de informações no alto mostra o que a ferramenta em execução precisa ou o que você selecionou, cada vista tem uma guia e uma barra rápida sob o desenho reúne algumas opções do dia a dia. As ferramentas são as do próprio app: tudo o que você faz no layout clássico funciona aqui também, e seu desenho, seu estilo de menu e suas teclas continuam como estão.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"escolher-o-layout\"\u003EEscolher o layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAbra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E (a engrenagem, ou \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha a guia \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E e vá a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÁrea de trabalho\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECaixa de ferramentas e caixa de informações\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO layout muda na hora. A escolha vale para todos os apps neste navegador: cada app passa a abrir nele, e ele já está no lugar antes de a página ser desenhada, então você nunca vê o layout clássico primeiro.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-fica-onde\"\u003EO que fica onde\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra de ferramentas:\u003C/strong\u003E a barra de ferramentas do próprio app continua no alto da área de desenho, com seus menus.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECaixa de informações:\u003C/strong\u003E sob a barra de ferramentas. Enquanto uma ferramenta é executada, mostra a ferramenta e o que ela pede. Quando você escolhe algo, mostra suas principais configurações (para uma parede: tipo, espessura, comprimento, linha de localização e altura), e você pode alterá-las ali mesmo: uma alteração ali é igual a uma em Propriedades, e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E a desfaz. Sem nada escolhido, diz &quot;Nada selecionado&quot;. Cada app acrescenta aqui suas próprias caixas, como a camada ou o nível atual, ou o documento aberto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECaixa de ferramentas:\u003C/strong\u003E a coluna à esquerda, com as ferramentas do app em grupos. Clique no nome de um grupo para recolhê-lo ou abri-lo de novo. A ferramenta em execução fica acesa.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMinhas ferramentas:\u003C/strong\u003E o primeiro grupo da caixa de ferramentas, oito espaços vazios para as ferramentas que você mais usa. Clique em um espaço vazio para escolher qualquer ferramenta ou comando do app; ou clique com o botão direito em uma ferramenta da caixa e escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdicionar a Minhas ferramentas\u003C/span\u003E; ou arraste uma ferramenta para um espaço. Clique com o botão direito em um espaço para alterá-lo ou esvaziá-lo. Seus espaços são guardados por app neste navegador.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENavegador:\u003C/strong\u003E o painel da direita. O navegador do projeto do app fica em cima (plantas, cortes, elevações, vistas 3D, tabelas, folhas, conforme o app tem): clique em um para abri-lo. Embaixo, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPropriedades\u003C/span\u003E e uma guia com os demais conteúdos do painel da direita.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGuias de vista:\u003C/strong\u003E uma guia para cada vista do arquivo aberto (uma folha, uma planta, uma cena…), sobre o desenho.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra rápida:\u003C/strong\u003E sob o desenho, as configurações de vista e as opções do dia a dia que o app tem, como a escala, o nível de detalhe, o alcance da vista, ajustar, planta, dividir ou 3D, snaps e unidades.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPaleta de edição:\u003C/strong\u003E clique em algo no desenho e uma pequena paleta aparece junto ao ponteiro com os comandos de edição do app para esse item, como mover, copiar, girar, espelhar, duplicar ou excluir, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPropriedades\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, a roda do mouse, outro clique ou seu botão de fechar a guardam.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUma ferramenta que não pode ser executada agora fica esmaecida, e sua dica explica por quê. Clique nela, ou pressione \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sobre ela, para ler o motivo em uma caixa.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E encerra primeiro a ferramenta em execução, como no layout clássico, e todos os atalhos e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (modos de tela) funcionam como antes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dar-caixa-de-ferramentas-uma-janela-pr-pria\"\u003EDar à caixa de ferramentas uma janela própria\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO botão \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover a caixa de ferramentas\u003C/span\u003E no alto da caixa de ferramentas tem \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover para uma nova janela\u003C/span\u003E: a caixa de ferramentas abre em uma janela estreita própria que você pode colocar ao lado do app ou em um segundo monitor. As teclas pressionadas ali chegam ao app. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecolocar a caixa de ferramentas\u003C/span\u003E, ou fechar essa janela, a traz de volta.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAs configurações da barra de ferramentas valem também para a caixa de ferramentas: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBarra de ferramentas\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÍcones e rótulos\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESomente ícones\u003C/span\u003E ou \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESomente rótulos\u003C/span\u003E, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMostrar nomes dos grupos (Arquivo, Desenhar, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"seus-pain-is-continuam-seus\"\u003ESeus painéis continuam seus\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOs painéis da esquerda e da direita mantêm tudo o que está descrito em \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EOrganizar os painéis\u003C/a\u003E: acoplar, flutuar, fixar, ocultar, guias, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover para uma nova janela\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedefinir layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"voltar-ao-layout-cl-ssico\"\u003EVoltar ao layout clássico\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAbra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÁrea de trabalho\u003C/span\u003E e escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft clássico\u003C/span\u003E. Cada barra de ferramentas, guia e painel volta ao lugar onde estava.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEm um celular, ou em uma janela de 760 pixels de largura ou menos, o app usa seu layout de celular, qualquer que seja a sua escolha. Uma janela mais larga traz de volta o seu layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPara experimentar outro arranjo, veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Eo layout de faixa de opções e navegador do projeto\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Caixa de ferramentas e caixa de informações é um dos três layouts de área de trabalho do app. As ferramentas de desenho ficam em uma coluna à esquerda, uma caixa de informações no alto mostra o que a ferramenta em execução precisa ou o que você selecionou, cada vista tem uma guia e uma barra rápida sob o desenho reúne algumas opções do dia a dia. As ferramentas são as do próprio app: tudo o que você faz no layout clássico funciona aqui também, e seu desenho, seu estilo de menu e suas teclas continuam como estão. Escolher o layout Abra Configurações (a engrenagem, ou Ctrl+,). Escolha a guia Global e vá a Layout › Área de trabalho. Escolha Caixa de ferramentas e caixa de informações. O layout muda na hora. A escolha vale para todos os apps neste navegador: cada app passa a abrir nele, e ele já está no lugar antes de a página ser desenhada, então você nunca vê o layout clássico primeiro. O que fica onde Barra de ferramentas: a barra de ferramentas do próprio app continua no alto da área de desenho, com seus menus. Caixa de informações: sob a barra de ferramentas. Enquanto uma ferramenta é executada, mostra a ferramenta e o que ela pede. Quando você escolhe algo, mostra suas principais configurações (para uma parede: tipo, espessura, comprimento, linha de localização e altura), e você pode alterá-las ali mesmo: uma alteração ali é igual a uma em Propriedades, e Ctrl+Z a desfaz. Sem nada escolhido, diz \"Nada selecionado\". Cada app acrescenta aqui suas próprias caixas, como a camada ou o nível atual, ou o documento aberto. Caixa de ferramentas: a coluna à esquerda, com as ferramentas do app em grupos. Clique no nome de um grupo para recolhê-lo ou abri-lo de novo. A ferramenta em execução fica acesa. Minhas ferramentas: o primeiro grupo da caixa de ferramentas, oito espaços vazios para as ferramentas que você mais usa. Clique em um espaço vazio para escolher qualquer ferramenta ou comando do app; ou clique com o botão direito em uma ferramenta da caixa e escolha Adicionar a Minhas ferramentas; ou arraste uma ferramenta para um espaço. Clique com o botão direito em um espaço para alterá-lo ou esvaziá-lo. Seus espaços são guardados por app neste navegador. Navegador: o painel da direita. O navegador do projeto do app fica em cima (plantas, cortes, elevações, vistas 3D, tabelas, folhas, conforme o app tem): clique em um para abri-lo. Embaixo, Propriedades e uma guia com os demais conteúdos do painel da direita. Guias de vista: uma guia para cada vista do arquivo aberto (uma folha, uma planta, uma cena…), sobre o desenho. Barra rápida: sob o desenho, as configurações de vista e as opções do dia a dia que o app tem, como a escala, o nível de detalhe, o alcance da vista, ajustar, planta, dividir ou 3D, snaps e unidades. Paleta de edição: clique em algo no desenho e uma pequena paleta aparece junto ao ponteiro com os comandos de edição do app para esse item, como mover, copiar, girar, espelhar, duplicar ou excluir, e Propriedades. Esc, a roda do mouse, outro clique ou seu botão de fechar a guardam. Uma ferramenta que não pode ser executada agora fica esmaecida, e sua dica explica por quê. Clique nela, ou pressione Enter sobre ela, para ler o motivo em uma caixa. Esc encerra primeiro a ferramenta em execução, como no layout clássico, e todos os atalhos e Ctrl+Shift+F (modos de tela) funcionam como antes. Dar à caixa de ferramentas uma janela própria O botão Mover a caixa de ferramentas no alto da caixa de ferramentas tem Mover para uma nova janela: a caixa de ferramentas abre em uma janela estreita própria que você pode colocar ao lado do app ou em um segundo monitor. As teclas pressionadas ali chegam ao app. Recolocar a caixa de ferramentas, ou fechar essa janela, a traz de volta. As configurações da barra de ferramentas valem também para a caixa de ferramentas: Configurações › Global › Barra de ferramentas › Ícones e rótulos, Somente ícones ou Somente rótulos, e Mostrar nomes dos grupos (Arquivo, Desenhar, …). Seus painéis continuam seus Os painéis da esquerda e da direita mantêm tudo o que está descrito em Organizar os painéis: acoplar, flutuar, fixar, ocultar, guias, Mover para uma nova janela e Redefinir layout. Voltar ao layout clássico Abra Configurações › Global › Layout › Área de trabalho e escolha CadCraft clássico. Cada barra de ferramentas, guia e painel volta ao lugar onde estava. Em um celular, ou em uma janela de 760 pixels de largura ou menos, o app usa seu layout de celular, qualquer que seja a sua escolha. Uma janela mais larga traz de volta o seu layout. Para experimentar outro arranjo, veja o layout de faixa de opções e navegador do projeto.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"layout-own","scope":"basics","title":"Organize seu próprio layout e salve suas configurações","summary":"Escolha qual painel mostra o navegador do projeto, as Propriedades ou o conteúdo próprio do painel, onde fica a caixa de ferramentas e o que as barras contêm, salve como seu layout e guarde configurações completas com um nome para alternar entre elas.","section":"start","order":39,"keywords":["layout","área de trabalho","organizar","personalizar","meu layout","layout próprio","painéis","editores","navegador do projeto","propriedades","caixa de ferramentas","barra rápida","caixa de informações","ambiente de trabalho","configuração","perfil","salvar configuração","alternar configuração","exportar configuração","importar configuração","ccenv"],"headings":[{"id":"organize-seu-pr-prio-layout","text":"Organize seu próprio layout","level":2},{"id":"salve-sua-configura-o-como-ambiente-de-trabalho","text":"Salve sua configuração como ambiente de trabalho","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAlém dos três layouts embutidos, você pode organizar um layout só seu e salvar toda a sua configuração com um nome para voltar a ela em um só passo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"organize-seu-pr-prio-layout\"\u003EOrganize seu próprio layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAbra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfigurações\u003C/span\u003E (a engrenagem, ou \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E, depois \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrganizar seu próprio layout…\u003C/span\u003E. A caixa de diálogo parte do layout que você usa agora.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDê um nome a ele e escolha:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESobre o desenho:\u003C/strong\u003E a barra de ferramentas do app, uma faixa de opções ou nenhuma; uma barra de título; uma caixa de informações e quais controles ela contém.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAo lado do desenho:\u003C/strong\u003E a caixa de ferramentas à esquerda, à direita ou nenhuma, com ou sem \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMinhas ferramentas\u003C/span\u003E; qual painel contém as paletas, qual fica no alto (o navegador do projeto ou as Propriedades, por exemplo) e quais são guias embaixo dele.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA área de desenho:\u003C/strong\u003E guias de vista, uma barra rápida e uma barra de vista com os controles que você marcar, a paleta de edição, uma barra de status.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAparência:\u003C/strong\u003E tamanho, contornos dos botões e estilo das guias.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUsar este layout\u003C/span\u003E. O app passa a ele na hora, e todo app que tem layouts também o usa.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESó é oferecido o que este app tem. Seu layout aparece em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDe complementos e arquivos\u003C/span\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExportar este layout\u003C/span\u003E o salva como um arquivo que você pode dar a um colega, e o botão de remover o tira da lista. Os painéis que ele preenche continuam podendo ser acoplados, flutuar e ir para janelas próprias, como em \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EOrganizar os painéis\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"salve-sua-configura-o-como-ambiente-de-trabalho\"\u003ESalve sua configuração como ambiente de trabalho\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUm ambiente de trabalho é toda a sua configuração para todos os apps neste navegador: o layout, o estilo de menu e suas teclas, o estilo da barra de ferramentas, os painéis e seus tamanhos, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMinhas ferramentas\u003C/span\u003E. O tema e o idioma não fazem parte dele.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESalvar:\u003C/strong\u003E digite um nome em \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAmbientes de trabalho\u003C/span\u003E e escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar minha configuração\u003C/span\u003E. Salvar com um nome que já existe substitui essa configuração.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlternar:\u003C/strong\u003E escolha uma configuração na lista e escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlternar para ela\u003C/span\u003E. O app recarrega nessa configuração; se houver trabalho não salvo, o app pergunta antes, como em qualquer recarga.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECompartilhar:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExportar\u003C/span\u003E salva a configuração escolhida como um arquivo \u003Ccode\u003E.ccenv.json\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImportar uma configuração…\u003C/span\u003E adiciona uma a partir de um arquivo. Um arquivo só define essas escolhas, nada mais.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemover:\u003C/strong\u003E escolha uma configuração e escolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemover\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVocê pode manter até doze configurações.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPara experimentar antes os arranjos embutidos, veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Eo layout de caixa de ferramentas e caixa de informações\u003C/a\u003E e \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Eo layout de faixa de opções e navegador do projeto\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Além dos três layouts embutidos, você pode organizar um layout só seu e salvar toda a sua configuração com um nome para voltar a ela em um só passo. Organize seu próprio layout Abra Configurações (a engrenagem, ou Ctrl+,), escolha Global, depois Layout. Escolha Organizar seu próprio layout…. A caixa de diálogo parte do layout que você usa agora. Dê um nome a ele e escolha: Sobre o desenho: a barra de ferramentas do app, uma faixa de opções ou nenhuma; uma barra de título; uma caixa de informações e quais controles ela contém. Ao lado do desenho: a caixa de ferramentas à esquerda, à direita ou nenhuma, com ou sem Minhas ferramentas; qual painel contém as paletas, qual fica no alto (o navegador do projeto ou as Propriedades, por exemplo) e quais são guias embaixo dele. A área de desenho: guias de vista, uma barra rápida e uma barra de vista com os controles que você marcar, a paleta de edição, uma barra de status. Aparência: tamanho, contornos dos botões e estilo das guias. Escolha Usar este layout. O app passa a ele na hora, e todo app que tem layouts também o usa. Só é oferecido o que este app tem. Seu layout aparece em De complementos e arquivos; Exportar este layout o salva como um arquivo que você pode dar a um colega, e o botão de remover o tira da lista. Os painéis que ele preenche continuam podendo ser acoplados, flutuar e ir para janelas próprias, como em Organizar os painéis. Salve sua configuração como ambiente de trabalho Um ambiente de trabalho é toda a sua configuração para todos os apps neste navegador: o layout, o estilo de menu e suas teclas, o estilo da barra de ferramentas, os painéis e seus tamanhos, e Minhas ferramentas. O tema e o idioma não fazem parte dele. Salvar: digite um nome em Ambientes de trabalho e escolha Salvar minha configuração. Salvar com um nome que já existe substitui essa configuração. Alternar: escolha uma configuração na lista e escolha Alternar para ela. O app recarrega nessa configuração; se houver trabalho não salvo, o app pergunta antes, como em qualquer recarga. Compartilhar: Exportar salva a configuração escolhida como um arquivo .ccenv.json; Importar uma configuração… adiciona uma a partir de um arquivo. Um arquivo só define essas escolhas, nada mais. Remover: escolha uma configuração e escolha Remover. Você pode manter até doze configurações. Para experimentar antes os arranjos embutidos, veja o layout de caixa de ferramentas e caixa de informações e o layout de faixa de opções e navegador do projeto.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"addons-basics","scope":"basics","title":"Add-ons and your account list","summary":"How add-ons work in every app and edition. Add one once in the Library and it follows your account, loads the first time an app needs it, and updates inside its major version. Sign in on a computer, pin a version, or keep working offline.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["add-ons","library","add","remove","update","pin","lab channel","your add-ons","sign in","offline","this file uses","engine","needs","orphan","team add-ons"],"headings":[{"id":"what-an-add-on-is","text":"What an add-on is","level":2},{"id":"your-list","text":"Your list","level":2},{"id":"needs","text":"Needs","level":2},{"id":"files-that-use-add-ons","text":"Files that use add-ons","level":2},{"id":"offline","text":"Offline","level":2},{"id":"ipad-and-iphone","text":"iPad and iPhone","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-add-on-is\"\u003EWhat an add-on is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn add-on adds features (a new tool or panel) or data (regulation packs, materials, trade packs) to an app. You add it once in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E and it is on your account list, so it follows you to every edition: the browser, Studio, the portable build and the apps on a tablet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-list\"\u003EYour list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour add-ons\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESign in\u003C/strong\u003E connects this app to your account. Signed out, the app keeps a list for this device only, and offers to merge it into your account when you sign in. In Studio the sign-in shows a short code to approve in your browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E takes an add-on off the list. It is refused while another add-on you keep needs it, and the screen names that add-on. When you remove something, anything that was brought in only for it is taken off too, and the screen says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/strong\u003E lists every change together. Updates inside the same major version arrive by themselves. A new major version, a changed permission, a pinned version or a lab version always asks you first, and an update is all or nothing: if one file fails its check, nothing changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep a version\u003C/strong\u003E pins it. \u003Cstrong\u003EFollow newest\u003C/strong\u003E unpins it. \u003Cstrong\u003ETry lab versions\u003C/strong\u003E moves one add-on to the lab channel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERoll back\u003C/strong\u003E returns to the version you had.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"needs\"\u003ENeeds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn add-on can need other add-ons or a shared engine. Add brings them along and tells you what it also adds, how big it is and which permissions it asks for. Engines are shared: one copy serves everything that needs it, and a copy nothing needs is freed after 30 days.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-that-use-add-ons\"\u003EFiles that use add-ons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing remembers the add-ons whose data it holds. Open it on a computer that does not have them and the app says &quot;This file uses ...&quot; and offers to add them. Until you do, the data stays in the file untouched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offline\"\u003EOffline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload everything for offline\u003C/span\u003E fetches every add-on on your list. Once fetched, Studio and the portable build work with no connection. Each download is checked against its checksum and its signature before it is used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ipad-and-iphone\"\u003EiPad and iPhone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd-ons that are only data download as usual. Code add-ons made by CadCraft are already inside the app and are switched on from your list. Code add-ons from outside makers are not offered on the iPad and iPhone apps; the browser app (Add to Home Screen) has them.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What an add-on is An add-on adds features (a new tool or panel) or data (regulation packs, materials, trade packs) to an app. You add it once in the Library and it is on your account list, so it follows you to every edition: the browser, Studio, the portable build and the apps on a tablet. Your list Open Settings › Add-ons › Your add-ons. Sign in connects this app to your account. Signed out, the app keeps a list for this device only, and offers to merge it into your account when you sign in. In Studio the sign-in shows a short code to approve in your browser. Remove takes an add-on off the list. It is refused while another add-on you keep needs it, and the screen names that add-on. When you remove something, anything that was brought in only for it is taken off too, and the screen says which. Check for updates lists every change together. Updates inside the same major version arrive by themselves. A new major version, a changed permission, a pinned version or a lab version always asks you first, and an update is all or nothing: if one file fails its check, nothing changes. Keep a version pins it. Follow newest unpins it. Try lab versions moves one add-on to the lab channel. Roll back returns to the version you had. Needs An add-on can need other add-ons or a shared engine. Add brings them along and tells you what it also adds, how big it is and which permissions it asks for. Engines are shared: one copy serves everything that needs it, and a copy nothing needs is freed after 30 days. Files that use add-ons A drawing remembers the add-ons whose data it holds. Open it on a computer that does not have them and the app says \"This file uses ...\" and offers to add them. Until you do, the data stays in the file untouched. Offline Download everything for offline fetches every add-on on your list. Once fetched, Studio and the portable build work with no connection. Each download is checked against its checksum and its signature before it is used. iPad and iPhone Add-ons that are only data download as usual. Code add-ons made by CadCraft are already inside the app and are switched on from your list. Code add-ons from outside makers are not offered on the iPad and iPhone apps; the browser app (Add to Home Screen) has them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"Trabalhar offline","summary":"Salve um app para uso offline, o que funciona sem conexão e o que precisa de uma.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["offline","sem internet","modo avião","visita à obra","cache","service worker","instalar","baixar","conexão"],"headings":[{"id":"salvar-um-app-para-uso-offline","text":"Salvar um app para uso offline","level":2},{"id":"o-que-funciona-offline","text":"O que funciona offline","level":2},{"id":"o-que-precisa-de-conex-o","text":"O que precisa de conexão","level":2},{"id":"o-que-saber","text":"O que saber","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOs apps fazem o trabalho no seu computador, então a maior parte funciona sem conexão. O navegador só precisa ter os arquivos do app salvos antes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"salvar-um-app-para-uso-offline\"\u003ESalvar um app para uso offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAbra o app enquanto estiver online.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEscolha \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E na barra no alto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAguarde &quot;Pronto para uso offline&quot;. Os arquivos do app, seus leitores de arquivo e esta ajuda agora estão salvos no seu navegador. Apps que leem arquivos DWG ou PDF salvam também seus leitores, então na primeira vez isso pode levar um minuto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFaça isso uma vez por app, em cada navegador que você usa. Para voltar ao app depois sem conexão, abra o mesmo endereço usado antes ou um favorito dele.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVocê está offline agora. Se este app foi salvo para uso offline, tudo abaixo funciona. Se não foi, você ainda pode usar o que já foi carregado nesta guia, mas não a recarregue até estar online de novo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-funciona-offline\"\u003EO que funciona offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAbrir arquivos do seu computador, desenhar, editar, medir e verificar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESalvar e exportar: todo salvamento é um download para o seu computador.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEsta ajuda, inclusive a pesquisa.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-precisa-de-conex-o\"\u003EO que precisa de conexão\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAbrir o app pela primeira vez e salvá-lo para uso offline.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EObter uma versão mais nova do app. Quando você está online, o app pega a versão mais recente na próxima vez que abre, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalvar para uso offline\u003C/span\u003E atualiza a cópia salva.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks para o site, como \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbrir no site\u003C/span\u003E e links para outras páginas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EO salvamento na nuvem e a sincronização entre computadores, que ainda não estão disponíveis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-saber\"\u003EO que saber\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUma cópia salva pertence a um navegador em um computador. Limpar os dados de site do navegador a remove.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EJanelas privadas de alguns navegadores e alguns computadores de trabalho com restrições não permitem o salvamento offline. O botão avisa quando não consegue salvar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO Safari remove os dados salvos de sites que você não visita há várias semanas. Abra o app de vez em quando, online, ou salve-o de novo antes de uma viagem.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EO CadCraft Studio, o app desktop com os oito apps em uma só instalação, está em getcadcraft.com/download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Os apps fazem o trabalho no seu computador, então a maior parte funciona sem conexão. O navegador só precisa ter os arquivos do app salvos antes. Salvar um app para uso offline Abra o app enquanto estiver online. Escolha Salvar para uso offline na barra no alto. Aguarde \"Pronto para uso offline\". Os arquivos do app, seus leitores de arquivo e esta ajuda agora estão salvos no seu navegador. Apps que leem arquivos DWG ou PDF salvam também seus leitores, então na primeira vez isso pode levar um minuto. Faça isso uma vez por app, em cada navegador que você usa. Para voltar ao app depois sem conexão, abra o mesmo endereço usado antes ou um favorito dele. Você está offline agora. Se este app foi salvo para uso offline, tudo abaixo funciona. Se não foi, você ainda pode usar o que já foi carregado nesta guia, mas não a recarregue até estar online de novo. O que funciona offline Abrir arquivos do seu computador, desenhar, editar, medir e verificar. Salvar e exportar: todo salvamento é um download para o seu computador. Esta ajuda, inclusive a pesquisa. O que precisa de conexão Abrir o app pela primeira vez e salvá-lo para uso offline. Obter uma versão mais nova do app. Quando você está online, o app pega a versão mais recente na próxima vez que abre, e Salvar para uso offline atualiza a cópia salva. Links para o site, como Abrir no site e links para outras páginas. O salvamento na nuvem e a sincronização entre computadores, que ainda não estão disponíveis. O que saber Uma cópia salva pertence a um navegador em um computador. Limpar os dados de site do navegador a remove. Janelas privadas de alguns navegadores e alguns computadores de trabalho com restrições não permitem o salvamento offline. O botão avisa quando não consegue salvar. O Safari remove os dados salvos de sites que você não visita há várias semanas. Abra o app de vez em quando, online, ou salve-o de novo antes de uma viagem. O CadCraft Studio, o app desktop com os oito apps em uma só instalação, está em getcadcraft.com/download.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"Para onde vão seus arquivos","summary":"Os arquivos ficam no seu computador, como funciona o salvamento e o que o navegador memoriza.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["privacidade","upload","nuvem","salvar","baixar","armazenamento","local","segurança","confidencial"],"headings":[{"id":"seus-arquivos-ficam-no-seu-computador","text":"Seus arquivos ficam no seu computador","level":2},{"id":"como-funciona-o-salvamento","text":"Como funciona o salvamento","level":2},{"id":"o-que-o-navegador-memoriza","text":"O que o navegador memoriza","level":2},{"id":"salvamento-na-nuvem","text":"Salvamento na nuvem","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"seus-arquivos-ficam-no-seu-computador\"\u003ESeus arquivos ficam no seu computador\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAo abrir um desenho, modelo ou PDF, o app o lê dentro do seu navegador. \u003Cstrong\u003EO arquivo não é enviado.\u003C/strong\u003E Nós nunca o vemos, e tudo funciona do mesmo jeito sem conexão.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"como-funciona-o-salvamento\"\u003EComo funciona o salvamento\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUma página do navegador não pode sobrescrever arquivos do seu computador sozinha, então os apps salvam \u003Cstrong\u003Ebaixando\u003C/strong\u003E um novo arquivo:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EO download vai para onde o navegador coloca os downloads, normalmente a pasta Downloads, a menos que você tenha configurado o navegador para perguntar a cada vez (veja \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003ENavegadores e computadores\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApps que alteram um arquivo salvam uma cópia com um novo nome, como &quot;(editado)&quot; ou &quot;(com marcações)&quot;, para que o original fique intacto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESe você fechar a guia com trabalho não salvo, o navegador pergunta antes (&quot;Sair do site?&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"o-que-o-navegador-memoriza\"\u003EO que o navegador memoriza\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlguns apps memorizam pequenas configurações no navegador, como o nome do autor ou os estilos das ferramentas. Elas ficam nesse navegador, nesse computador, e limpar os dados do site as remove. A ajuda de cada app diz o que ele guarda.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"salvamento-na-nuvem\"\u003ESalvamento na nuvem\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EO salvamento na nuvem e a sincronização entre computadores fazem parte da licença comercial. Ainda não estão disponíveis. Até lá, mantenha seus arquivos onde guarda seus outros arquivos de projeto e faça backup da forma habitual.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Seus arquivos ficam no seu computador Ao abrir um desenho, modelo ou PDF, o app o lê dentro do seu navegador. O arquivo não é enviado. Nós nunca o vemos, e tudo funciona do mesmo jeito sem conexão. Como funciona o salvamento Uma página do navegador não pode sobrescrever arquivos do seu computador sozinha, então os apps salvam baixando um novo arquivo: O download vai para onde o navegador coloca os downloads, normalmente a pasta Downloads, a menos que você tenha configurado o navegador para perguntar a cada vez (veja Navegadores e computadores). Apps que alteram um arquivo salvam uma cópia com um novo nome, como \"(editado)\" ou \"(com marcações)\", para que o original fique intacto. Se você fechar a guia com trabalho não salvo, o navegador pergunta antes (\"Sair do site?\"). O que o navegador memoriza Alguns apps memorizam pequenas configurações no navegador, como o nome do autor ou os estilos das ferramentas. Elas ficam nesse navegador, nesse computador, e limpar os dados do site as remove. A ajuda de cada app diz o que ele guarda. Salvamento na nuvem O salvamento na nuvem e a sincronização entre computadores fazem parte da licença comercial. Ainda não estão disponíveis. Até lá, mantenha seus arquivos onde guarda seus outros arquivos de projeto e faça backup da forma habitual.","lang":"pt-BR"},{"slug":"connect-ai-windows","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on Windows","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":63,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","windows"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-macos","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on a Mac","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":64,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","macos"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codex/config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codex/config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-linux","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on Linux","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":65,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","linux"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codex/config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codex/config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-mobile","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on a phone or tablet","summary":"How your own AI app reaches the CadCraft apps from a phone or tablet: through the hosted connector, with CadCraft Studio open on a computer. Includes the one-line test.","section":"start","order":66,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","android","ipad","phone","tablet","hosted connector","test"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how","text":"How","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"what-it-can-and-cannot-do","text":"What it can and cannot do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn AI app on a phone or tablet cannot read a settings file on your computer, so it does not use the paste cards. It connects to a \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead, and CadCraft Studio must be open on a computer you own to answer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how\"\u003EHow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the computer, open \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EHosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio shows the address to add and asks you to sign in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn your AI app, add a connector (a &quot;remote&quot; or &quot;custom&quot; connector) with that address and sign in when asked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep Studio open. When it is closed the AI app says CadCraft is not reachable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-can-and-cannot-do\"\u003EWhat it can and cannot do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe hosted connector carries only the tool calls and their answers for the apps and permissions you allowed. It never holds your files, and the answers are marked as data from your files, not as instructions. You can disconnect it in Studio at any time.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn An AI app on a phone or tablet cannot read a settings file on your computer, so it does not use the paste cards. It connects to a hosted connector instead, and CadCraft Studio must be open on a computer you own to answer. How On the computer, open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI and choose Hosted connector. Studio shows the address to add and asks you to sign in. In your AI app, add a connector (a \"remote\" or \"custom\" connector) with that address and sign in when asked. Keep Studio open. When it is closed the AI app says CadCraft is not reachable. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. What it can and cannot do The hosted connector carries only the tool calls and their answers for the apps and permissions you allowed. It never holds your files, and the answers are marked as data from your files, not as instructions. You can disconnect it in Studio at any time.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"local-models","scope":"basics","title":"Use an AI that runs on your own computer","summary":"Work with a local AI model and no network: through an AI app that can use tools, .","section":"start","order":66,"keywords":["ai","assistant","local","offline","ollama","lm studio","llama.cpp","jan","air-gapped","private","model","ask box"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how","text":"How","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to use an AI model that runs on your own computer, so nothing you ask leaves it. This suits firms that cannot send drawings to an outside service. The apps contain no AI; the model asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how\"\u003EHow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an AI app on this computer that can use tools. LM Studio, Jan and llama.cpp&#039;s server can each use tools through the Model Context Protocol (&quot;MCP&quot;). Add the single \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E entry shown in \u003Cstrong\u003EConnect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E, open CadCraft Studio, then ask your question in that app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EProgram\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECan use tools through MCP\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELM Studio\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ellama.cpp (llama-server)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, start it with \u003Ccode\u003E--jinja\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, turn on tool calling for the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOllama\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot on its own: use it as the model, with Jan or an editor in front\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis list is taken from each program&#039;s own documentation. It has not yet been tried with every program on every system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERaise the model&#039;s context length before you start; the default in some programs is small and tool descriptions will not fit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESmall models call tools less reliably than large hosted ones. If the answer says what it would do but nothing changes, try a larger model or a model marked as able to use tools.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything else stays the same: the permission levels, \u003Cstrong\u003ESuggest\u003C/strong\u003E mode, the folders you allowed and the activity list. A model on this computer sends nothing out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to use an AI model that runs on your own computer, so nothing you ask leaves it. This suits firms that cannot send drawings to an outside service. The apps contain no AI; the model asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. How Use an AI app on this computer that can use tools. LM Studio, Jan and llama.cpp's server can each use tools through the Model Context Protocol (\"MCP\"). Add the single cadcraft entry shown in Connect your AI, open CadCraft Studio, then ask your question in that app. Program Can use tools through MCP LM Studio Yes llama.cpp (llama-server) Yes, start it with --jinja Jan Yes, turn on tool calling for the model Ollama Not on its own: use it as the model, with Jan or an editor in front This list is taken from each program's own documentation. It has not yet been tried with every program on every system. Good to know Raise the model's context length before you start; the default in some programs is small and tool descriptions will not fit. Small models call tools less reliably than large hosted ones. If the answer says what it would do but nothing changes, try a larger model or a model marked as able to use tools. Everything else stays the same: the permission levels, Suggest mode, the folders you allowed and the activity list. A model on this computer sends nothing out.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"engines","scope":"basics","title":"Run an engine on a model","summary":"The Engines panel runs the shared calculation engines (schematics, pipe and power networks, motion, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems, PLC) on a model you paste.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["engines","schematic","network","power","load flow","robot","kinematics","queue","simulation","schedule","critical path","requirements","risk","fault tree","modbus","circuit","spice","board","pcb","modelica","plc"],"headings":[{"id":"run-one","text":"Run one","level":2},{"id":"controls-on-a-model-or-on-the-3d-scene","text":"Controls on a model, or on the 3D scene","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESome calculations are the same in every app, so they live in one panel instead of eight. Open \u003Cstrong\u003EEngines\u003C/strong\u003E from the button in the bar at the top, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and type &quot;engines&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-one\"\u003ERun one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose an engine from the list: schematics, pipe networks and power systems, motion and robots, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems or controls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a worked example, or replace the text with your own model. Most engines read JSON; circuits, systems and controls read their own text (a circuit deck, Modelica, structured text).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The results come back as tables, with the engine&#039;s own problems above them and the limits of its method below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset\u003C/span\u003E puts the example text back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"controls-on-a-model-or-on-the-3d-scene\"\u003EControls on a model, or on the 3D scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe controls engine also takes a JSON input that ties a structured-text program to something it can sense and command. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe program bound to a flow model\u003C/span\u003E is the queues engine&#039;s model. In ModelCraft, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe program bound to the 3D scene\u003C/span\u003E ties it to the placements (groups and components) of the open model: a sensor reads \u003Ccode\u003EName.x\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.y\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.z\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.rotZ\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EName.hidden\u003C/code\u003E, a command writes one of them, and the program decides. Position sensors are whole numbers, so give each a \u003Ccode\u003Escale\u003C/code\u003E (1000 turns metres into millimetres); a command is on or off, and \u003Ccode\u003Evalues\u003C/code\u003E says what off and on mean (\u003Ccode\u003E[0, 2]\u003C/code\u003E puts the placement at 0 or 2 m). A run changes nothing in the model: the results table shows where each placement would end up. Add \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;keep&quot;: true\u003C/code\u003E to write the final state into the model as one undo step. A placement that is not in the model is reported by name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything runs in this page. Nothing is uploaded, and nothing is saved in your file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProblems the engine finds also appear in the Problems panel, under their own source.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe examples are small on purpose, with answers you can check by hand. They are a way to see what an engine does, not a design.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe results are calculation aids. A person who is responsible for the design checks them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Some calculations are the same in every app, so they live in one panel instead of eight. Open Engines from the button in the bar at the top, or press Ctrl+K and type \"engines\". Run one Choose an engine from the list: schematics, pipe networks and power systems, motion and robots, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems or controls. Choose a worked example, or replace the text with your own model. Most engines read JSON; circuits, systems and controls read their own text (a circuit deck, Modelica, structured text). Press Run. The results come back as tables, with the engine's own problems above them and the limits of its method below. Reset puts the example text back. Controls on a model, or on the 3D scene The controls engine also takes a JSON input that ties a structured-text program to something it can sense and command. The program bound to a flow model is the queues engine's model. In ModelCraft, The program bound to the 3D scene ties it to the placements (groups and components) of the open model: a sensor reads Name.x, Name.y, Name.z, Name.rotZ or Name.hidden, a command writes one of them, and the program decides. Position sensors are whole numbers, so give each a scale (1000 turns metres into millimetres); a command is on or off, and values says what off and on mean ([0, 2] puts the placement at 0 or 2 m). A run changes nothing in the model: the results table shows where each placement would end up. Add \"keep\": true to write the final state into the model as one undo step. A placement that is not in the model is reported by name. Things to know Everything runs in this page. Nothing is uploaded, and nothing is saved in your file. Problems the engine finds also appear in the Problems panel, under their own source. The examples are small on purpose, with answers you can check by hand. They are a way to see what an engine does, not a design. The results are calculation aids. A person who is responsible for the design checks them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"assistants-recipes","scope":"basics","title":"Recipes for AI assistants","summary":"Worked examples for using your own AI assistant with the CadCraft apps: what to ask, the tools it calls, and the permissions each needs. Every recipe here is run by our tests with the same calls.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["ai","assistant","mcp","recipes","examples","prompts","automation","batch","convert","plot","check","takeoff","cut","fill","parameters","script"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"recipes","text":"Recipes","level":2},{"id":"tidy-a-drawing","text":"Tidy a drawing","level":3},{"id":"plot-a-set-of-sheets","text":"Plot a set of sheets","level":3},{"id":"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant","text":"Convert drawings for a consultant","level":3},{"id":"check-drawings-before-sending-them","text":"Check drawings before sending them","level":3},{"id":"ask-a-building-model-questions","text":"Ask a building model questions","level":3},{"id":"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft","text":"Draw a room in BimCraft","level":3},{"id":"total-a-takeoff","text":"Total a takeoff","level":3},{"id":"site-numbers","text":"Site numbers","level":3},{"id":"try-a-part-variant","text":"Try a part variant","level":3},{"id":"fill-in-a-title-block","text":"Fill in a title block","level":3},{"id":"your-files-and-your-assistant","text":"Your files and your assistant","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReady-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps&#039; tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are two ways in. In \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). \u003Cstrong\u003EWithout a window\u003C/strong\u003E, the CadCraft file server for assistants (\u003Ccode\u003Enpx @getcadcraft/mcp --root &lt;folder&gt;\u003C/code\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"recipes\"\u003ERecipes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"tidy-a-drawing\"\u003ETidy a drawing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_open {path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg&quot;}            → d1\ndrawing_describe {doc: &quot;d1&quot;}                                    layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses\ncommand_run {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, input: &quot;PURGE&quot;}                         one undo step: &quot;Assistant: PURGE&quot;\ndrawing_save {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plot-a-set-of-sheets\"\u003EPlot a set of sheets\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_open {path: …}  ·  drawing_describe {doc}                the sheets of each drawing\ndrawing_plot {doc, layout: &quot;&lt;sheet&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Plots/&lt;drawing&gt; &lt;sheet&gt;.pdf&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant\"\u003EConvert drawings for a consultant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_convert {path: &quot;Kitchen/&lt;file&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Out/&lt;file&gt;.dwg&quot;, version: &quot;2013&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"check-drawings-before-sending-them\"\u003ECheck drawings before sending them\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [&quot;0&quot;]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. Keep your office&#039;s rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json\u003C/code\u003E (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ask-a-building-model-questions\"\u003EAsk a building model questions\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eifc_open {path: &quot;house.ifc&quot;}                                    → d1\nifc_query {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, class: &quot;IfcDoor&quot;}                         each door with its level\nifc_properties {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, id: &lt;door&gt;}                          Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. In Studio, BimCraft&#039;s own tools answer the same about the model you have open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft\"\u003EDraw a room in BimCraft\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ewalls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true}     one undo step\nopening_add {wall: &quot;&lt;the first wall&#039;s GlobalId&gt;&quot;, kind: &quot;door&quot;}                    one undo step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_new\u003C/code\u003E starts a building first and \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_save\u003C/code\u003E writes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"total-a-takeoff\"\u003ETotal a takeoff\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_open {path: &quot;job.takeoff.json&quot;}  ·  takeoff_estimate {doc}  ·  takeoff_export {doc, out: &quot;job.xlsx&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"site-numbers\"\u003ESite numbers\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How much cut and fill does the pad need?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esite_open {path: &quot;site.civilcraft.json&quot;}  ·  surface_volume {doc}       cut, fill and net, per pad\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. With a level instead of a design: \u003Ccode\u003Esurface_volume {doc, datum: 100}\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"try-a-part-variant\"\u003ETry a part variant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epart_open {path: &quot;screw.partcraft.json&quot;}  ·  params_list {doc}\nparam_set {doc, name: &quot;l&quot;, value: 40}                           one undo step, the part rebuilt\nmass_properties {doc}                                            volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit; Save (\u003Ccode\u003Epart_save\u003C/code\u003E) to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fill-in-a-title-block\"\u003EFill in a title block\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eblock_insert {doc, block: &quot;TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD&quot;, at: [0, 0], attributes: {&quot;SHEET-NO&quot;: &quot;A-101&quot;, &quot;SHEET-TITLE&quot;: &quot;Kitchen plan&quot;}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-and-your-assistant\"\u003EYour files and your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps never send your files anywhere. \u003Cstrong\u003EBut what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant:\u003C/strong\u003E a cloud assistant&#039;s maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job&#039;s folders out of the ones you allow.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn Ready-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps' tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working. There are two ways in. In CadCraft Studio, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). Without a window, the CadCraft file server for assistants (npx @getcadcraft/mcp --root \u003Cfolder\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the cadcraft command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways. Start read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with. Recipes Tidy a drawing Ask: \"List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.\" The assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE: drawing_open {path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg\"} → d1 drawing_describe {doc: \"d1\"} layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses command_run {doc: \"d1\", input: \"PURGE\"} one undo step: \"Assistant: PURGE\" drawing_save {doc: \"d1\", path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg\"} Needs Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay. Plot a set of sheets Ask: \"Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_open {path: …} · drawing_describe {doc} the sheets of each drawing drawing_plot {doc, layout: \"\u003Csheet\u003E\", out: \"Plots/\u003Cdrawing\u003E \u003Csheet\u003E.pdf\"} Needs Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: cadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all. Convert drawings for a consultant Ask: \"Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_convert {path: \"Kitchen/\u003Cfile\u003E\", out: \"Out/\u003Cfile\u003E.dwg\", version: \"2013\"} Needs Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: cadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013. Check drawings before sending them Ask: \"Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.\" drawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [\"0\"]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}} Needs Read. Keep your office's rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: cadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script). Ask a building model questions Ask: \"How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?\" ifc_open {path: \"house.ifc\"} → d1 ifc_query {doc: \"d1\", class: \"IfcDoor\"} each door with its level ifc_properties {doc: \"d1\", id: \u003Cdoor\u003E} Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal Needs Read. In Studio, BimCraft's own tools answer the same about the model you have open. Draw a room in BimCraft Ask: \"Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.\" walls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true} one undo step opening_add {wall: \"\u003Cthe first wall's GlobalId\u003E\", kind: \"door\"} one undo step Needs Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, ifc_new starts a building first and ifc_save writes it. Total a takeoff Ask: \"Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.\" takeoff_open {path: \"job.takeoff.json\"} · takeoff_estimate {doc} · takeoff_export {doc, out: \"job.xlsx\"} Needs Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so. Site numbers Ask: \"How much cut and fill does the pad need?\" site_open {path: \"site.civilcraft.json\"} · surface_volume {doc} cut, fill and net, per pad Needs Read. With a level instead of a design: surface_volume {doc, datum: 100}. Try a part variant Ask: \"Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.\" part_open {path: \"screw.partcraft.json\"} · params_list {doc} param_set {doc, name: \"l\", value: 40} one undo step, the part rebuilt mass_properties {doc} volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia Needs Read and Edit; Save (part_save) to keep it. Fill in a title block Ask: \"Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.\" block_insert {doc, block: \"TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD\", at: [0, 0], attributes: {\"SHEET-NO\": \"A-101\", \"SHEET-TITLE\": \"Kitchen plan\"}} Needs Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults. Your files and your assistant The apps never send your files anywhere. But what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant: a cloud assistant's maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job's folders out of the ones you allow.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"community-testing","scope":"basics","title":"Community testing","summary":"How to try a test on the Community testing page and send your result.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["community","testing","test","results","report","feedback","files","passed","failing"],"headings":[{"id":"send-a-result","text":"Send a result","level":2},{"id":"sending-files","text":"Sending files","level":2},{"id":"your-privacy","text":"Your privacy","level":2},{"id":"what-counts-as-known-not-to-work","text":"What counts as \"known not to work\"","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESome checks need a real computer, a real file or a real device. The Community testing page lists them so anyone can help.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-result\"\u003ESend a result\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003ECommunity testing\u003C/strong\u003E page on the website and choose a test.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUse the public build named in \u003Cstrong\u003EBefore you start\u003C/strong\u003E, never a lab build.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFollow the \u003Cstrong\u003ESteps\u003C/strong\u003E and compare with the \u003Cstrong\u003EExpected result\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in \u003Cstrong\u003ESend a result\u003C/strong\u003E: passed, still failing, partly works, or could not run it. Say which system you used and what happened.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou do not need an account to send a result. A signed-in account is needed only to send files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sending-files\"\u003ESending files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a test asks for files, drag them into the framed area (or click it, or press Enter or Space on it to choose them). You can send several at once; each shows its name and size and a Remove button. The form says which file types and sizes we take. We never take programs, scripts or web pages.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-privacy\"\u003EYour privacy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA result sent while you are signed in carries your account so we can write back. It is never shown on the page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYour first name is shown next to a result only if you tick \u003Cstrong\u003EShow my first name\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENotes are shown to others only if you tick \u003Cstrong\u003EAnyone may read my notes\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFiles are seen only by the CadCraft team, kept privately and deleted automatically after the number of days the form states. Remove anything private from a file first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-counts-as-known-not-to-work\"\u003EWhat counts as &quot;known not to work&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach test lists what is not built yet. Please do not report those as bugs.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Some checks need a real computer, a real file or a real device. The Community testing page lists them so anyone can help. Send a result Open the Community testing page on the website and choose a test. Use the public build named in Before you start, never a lab build. Follow the Steps and compare with the Expected result. Fill in Send a result: passed, still failing, partly works, or could not run it. Say which system you used and what happened. You do not need an account to send a result. A signed-in account is needed only to send files. Sending files When a test asks for files, drag them into the framed area (or click it, or press Enter or Space on it to choose them). You can send several at once; each shows its name and size and a Remove button. The form says which file types and sizes we take. We never take programs, scripts or web pages. Your privacy A result sent while you are signed in carries your account so we can write back. It is never shown on the page. Your first name is shown next to a result only if you tick Show my first name. Notes are shown to others only if you tick Anyone may read my notes. Files are seen only by the CadCraft team, kept privately and deleted automatically after the number of days the form states. Remove anything private from a file first. What counts as \"known not to work\" Each test lists what is not built yet. Please do not report those as bugs.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-together-live","scope":"basics","title":"Work together live","summary":"Share the file open in an app so teammates edit it with you on your own network with a short code, and join someone else's session. Invite links over the internet are not open yet.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["live","studio live","work together","collaborate","share live","join","code","qr","invite link","link","host","approve","guest","network","relay","license","many people","limit","allow anyway","slow","recent edits","edit log","who changed","history"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"share-the-file-you-have-open","text":"Share the file you have open","level":2},{"id":"let-people-in","text":"Let people in","level":2},{"id":"when-many-people-join","text":"When many people join","level":2},{"id":"codes-on-your-own-network","text":"Codes on your own network","level":2},{"id":"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet","text":"Invite links over the internet: not open yet","level":2},{"id":"join-someone-else-s-session","text":"Join someone else's session","level":2},{"id":"who-changed-what-recent-edits","text":"Who changed what: Recent edits","level":2},{"id":"assistants-in-a-session","text":"Assistants in a session","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app&#039;s own page about live sessions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-the-file-you-have-open\"\u003EShare the file you have open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E in the app&#039;s bar, type the name others should see, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShare live\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStudio shows a code like \u003Ccode\u003EK7-Q4M2X\u003C/code\u003E with a picture to scan and the computer&#039;s address. Give the code to the people you want to invite.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-people-in\"\u003ELet people in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E lets them change the file with you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E turns them away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E shows everyone. Change a person&#039;s role there, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can&#039;t come back without a new invitation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWhen many people join\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA session&#039;s usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo allow more before anyone knocks, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow more people…\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to 20\u003C/span\u003E puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"codes-on-your-own-network\"\u003ECodes on your own network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA code works for 10 minutes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew code\u003C/span\u003E makes another; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop inviting\u003C/span\u003E closes it. People already in stay.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne-time code\u003C/span\u003E lets one person in per code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESessions on your own network are free, for everyone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet\"\u003EInvite links over the internet: not open yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInvite links aren&#039;t open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E part of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen invite links open, this is how they will work:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvite by link\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy the link and send it like a meeting link.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe link&#039;s secret part (after \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can&#039;t read, and you still approve each person. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew link\u003C/span\u003E makes the old one stop working at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop the link\u003C/span\u003E ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaking an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-someone-else-s-session\"\u003EJoin someone else&#039;s session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft Studio&#039;s home screen, find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin a live session\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host&#039;s link here too.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your name and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host&#039;s file once the host lets you in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout Studio, scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E, then under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin with the drawing open here\u003C/span\u003E type the code or the address and code, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin here\u003C/span\u003E. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host&#039;s drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can&#039;t find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"who-changed-what-recent-edits\"\u003EWho changed what: Recent edits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Live panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003ERecent edits\u003C/strong\u003E: who made each change and what it was (the step&#039;s name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow more\u003C/span\u003E lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly what is selected\u003C/span\u003E keeps just the edits that touched it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list is made in your own window from the session&#039;s edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants-in-a-session\"\u003EAssistants in a session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf someone turned on assistants in their app&#039;s settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host&#039;s own assistant joins at once; anyone else&#039;s waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host chooses \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd the session\u003C/span\u003E, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave\u003C/span\u003E does the same for you alone.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app's own page about live sessions. Share the file you have open Open the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer. Choose Live in the app's bar, type the name others should see, then choose Share live. Studio shows a code like K7-Q4M2X with a picture to scan and the computer's address. Give the code to the people you want to invite. Your file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves. Let people in Nobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet: Edit lets them change the file with you. View only lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back. Deny turns them away. The list under In the session shows everyone. Change a person's role there, or Remove them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can't come back without a new invitation. When many people join A session's usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you. When someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose: Allow anyway lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your Edit, View only or Deny. Keep it at 20 turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small. To allow more before anyone knocks, choose Allow more people… under In the session. Back to 20 puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again. If things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps. Codes on your own network A code works for 10 minutes. New code makes another; Stop inviting closes it. People already in stay. One-time code lets one person in per code. After 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you. Sessions on your own network are free, for everyone. Invite links over the internet: not open yet Invite links aren't open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the Over the internet part of the Live panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link. When invite links open, this is how they will work: Under Over the internet, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then Invite by link. Copy the link and send it like a meeting link. The link's secret part (after #) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can't read, and you still approve each person. New link makes the old one stop working at once; Stop the link ends it. Making an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license. Join someone else's session In CadCraft Studio's home screen, find Join a live session. Type the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host's link here too.) Type your name and choose Join. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host's file once the host lets you in. Without Studio, scan the picture under the host's code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser. In CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose Live, then under Join with the drawing open here type the code or the address and code, and choose Join here. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host's drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is. Guest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can't find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet. Who changed what: Recent edits The Live panel lists Recent edits: who made each change and what it was (the step's name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. Show more lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and Only what is selected keeps just the edits that touched it. The list is made in your own window from the session's edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so. Assistants in a session If someone turned on assistants in their app's settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host's own assistant joins at once; anyone else's waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: Allow or Deny. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves. When the session ends The host chooses End the session, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. Leave does the same for you alone.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"pen-and-touch","scope":"basics","title":"Draw with a pen on a tablet","summary":"What the app does when you draw with a pen on a touch screen: it ignores your palm, can leave the drawing to the pen while your fingers move the view, and lets the pen's buttons and a two-finger tap undo.","section":"tasks","order":94,"keywords":["pen","stylus","tablet","touch","palm","palm rejection","finger","fingers","two fingers","tap","undo","redo","side button","barrel button","eraser","pressure","hover","phone"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time","text":"A pen is noticed the first time","level":2},{"id":"your-palm-is-ignored","text":"Your palm is ignored","level":2},{"id":"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view","text":"Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view","level":2},{"id":"undo-with-a-tap","text":"Undo with a tap","level":2},{"id":"the-pen-s-buttons","text":"The pen's buttons","level":2},{"id":"these-are-the-device-s-settings","text":"These are the device's settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time\"\u003EA pen is noticed the first time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-palm-is-ignored\"\u003EYour palm is ignored\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIgnore my palm while I use a pen\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view\"\u003ELet the pen draw and your fingers move the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw and select\u003C/span\u003E (the start): a finger does what the pen does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly move the view\u003C/span\u003E: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-with-a-tap\"\u003EUndo with a tap\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETap with two fingers to undo, three to redo\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-pen-s-buttons\"\u003EThe pen&#039;s buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen side button\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does (a right click)\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen eraser end\u003C/span\u003E (on pens that have one): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"these-are-the-device-s-settings\"\u003EThese are the device&#039;s settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E and resetting an app&#039;s settings leave them as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPen pressure and tilt don&#039;t change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen&#039;s pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in Settings › General › Pen and touch, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen. A pen is noticed the first time Nothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual. Your palm is ignored With Ignore my palm while I use a pen on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw. If a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work. Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view Under Fingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here: Draw and select (the start): a finger does what the pen does. Only move the view: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual. The toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way. Undo with a tap With Tap with two fingers to undo, three to redo on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap. The pen's buttons Pen side button: What the app does (a right click) at the start, or Undo or Redo. Pen eraser end (on pens that have one): What the app does at the start, or Undo or Redo. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches. These are the device's settings The pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. Reset the layout and resetting an app's settings leave them as they are. Not built yet Pen pressure and tilt don't change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen's pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"input-devices","scope":"basics","title":"Use a trackpad, 3D mouse or controller","summary":"Set what a trackpad, your mouse's side buttons, a pen, finger taps, a 3D mouse, a game controller or a MIDI controller does, for every app or for one app.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["trackpad","touchpad","two fingers","pinch","scroll","mouse","side button","back button","forward button","tilt wheel","pen","stylus","eraser","tap","3D mouse","space mouse","6 axis","game controller","gamepad","joystick","MIDI","knob","fader","pad","input device","customize","assign"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"every-app-or-only-this-app","text":"Every app, or only this app","level":2},{"id":"trackpad","text":"Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons","text":"Mouse buttons","level":2},{"id":"pen-and-finger-taps","text":"Pen and finger taps","level":2},{"id":"3d-mouse","text":"3D mouse","level":2},{"id":"game-controller","text":"Game controller","level":2},{"id":"midi-controller","text":"MIDI controller","level":2},{"id":"share-your-settings","text":"Share your settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to choose what each of your input devices does in the app. The settings are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInput devices\u003C/span\u003E, one card per device. Until you change something, every device works as it did before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"every-app-or-only-this-app\"\u003EEvery app, or only this app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the top, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThese settings are for\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery app\u003C/span\u003E (the start): what you set applies in every app that has no settings of its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly\u003C/span\u003E this app: the app takes a copy of the shared settings and keeps its own from then on. Change them here without touching the other apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery app\u003C/span\u003E again to drop this app&#039;s own settings and go back to the shared ones. The settings are kept on this device, in every app window and in the desktop app&#039;s apps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trackpad\"\u003ETrackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-finger scroll\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan the view\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit (3D views)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPinch\u003C/span\u003E: zoom, as the app does, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse the direction\u003C/span\u003E apply when you have chosen pan, zoom or orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe app tells a trackpad from a mouse wheel by the way it scrolls, so a mouse wheel keeps doing what it does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons\"\u003EMouse buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack side button\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForward side button\u003C/span\u003E: run any command, such as Undo and Redo, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit everything in view\u003C/span\u003E. A side button set to a command no longer takes the browser back a page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWheel tilted sideways\u003C/span\u003E: what the app does, pan the view, or nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe wheel&#039;s zoom direction and which buttons pan or orbit are in the app&#039;s own tab, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pen-and-finger-taps\"\u003EPen and finger taps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide button\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser end\u003C/span\u003E: what the app does, or any command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-finger tap\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThree-finger tap\u003C/span\u003E (they start as Undo and Redo): any command, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPalm rejection and what your fingers do once you use a pen stay under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-mouse\"\u003E3D mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect a 3D mouse\u003C/span\u003E and pick it in the browser&#039;s list; the browser remembers it for this site. Then push, lift, tilt and twist the cap to move the view under the pointer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach movement (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush left and right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush forward and back\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELift and press\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilt forward and back\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilt left and right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwist\u003C/span\u003E) can pan, zoom, orbit or do nothing, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse\u003C/span\u003E turns it round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDead zone\u003C/span\u003E (how far the cap moves before anything happens) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly the strongest movement at a time\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach button runs a command or fits the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA 3D mouse needs Chrome or Edge in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"game-controller\"\u003EGame controller\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlug in a controller and press any button so the browser shows it to the app. The sticks pan, orbit or zoom; the triggers zoom in and out; every button can run a command. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDead zone\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse up and down on the sticks\u003C/span\u003E are there too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"midi-controller\"\u003EMIDI controller\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect MIDI\u003C/span\u003E. Then, for each control: pick what it should do under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a control for\u003C/span\u003E (a knob or fader pans, zooms or orbits; a pad or key runs a command), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearn it\u003C/span\u003E, and turn the knob or press the pad. Each learned control is listed, with a button to remove it. MIDI needs Chrome or Edge in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-your-settings\"\u003EShare your settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the settings shown as a file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E reads one back (settings this version doesn&#039;t know are left out, and you&#039;re told how many). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the defaults\u003C/span\u003E undoes every change for the settings shown.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA wheel dial (such as the ones on Windows) and the desktop app&#039;s own drivers for 3D mice are not supported yet. A 3D mouse, controller or knob moves the view only in apps that have handed their views over; their buttons work everywhere.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to choose what each of your input devices does in the app. The settings are in Settings › Global › Input devices, one card per device. Until you change something, every device works as it did before. Every app, or only this app At the top, These settings are for: Every app (the start): what you set applies in every app that has no settings of its own. Only this app: the app takes a copy of the shared settings and keeps its own from then on. Change them here without touching the other apps. Choose Every app again to drop this app's own settings and go back to the shared ones. The settings are kept on this device, in every app window and in the desktop app's apps. Trackpad Two-finger scroll: What the app does, Pan the view, Zoom or Orbit (3D views). Pinch: zoom, as the app does, or Nothing. Speed and Reverse the direction apply when you have chosen pan, zoom or orbit. The app tells a trackpad from a mouse wheel by the way it scrolls, so a mouse wheel keeps doing what it does. Mouse buttons Back side button and Forward side button: run any command, such as Undo and Redo, or Fit everything in view. A side button set to a command no longer takes the browser back a page. Wheel tilted sideways: what the app does, pan the view, or nothing. The wheel's zoom direction and which buttons pan or orbit are in the app's own tab, under Menus, keys and mouse. Pen and finger taps Side button and Eraser end: what the app does, or any command. Two-finger tap and Three-finger tap (they start as Undo and Redo): any command, or Nothing. Palm rejection and what your fingers do once you use a pen stay under Pen and touch. 3D mouse Choose Connect a 3D mouse and pick it in the browser's list; the browser remembers it for this site. Then push, lift, tilt and twist the cap to move the view under the pointer: Each movement (Push left and right, Push forward and back, Lift and press, Tilt forward and back, Tilt left and right, Twist) can pan, zoom, orbit or do nothing, and Reverse turns it round. Speed, Dead zone (how far the cap moves before anything happens) and Only the strongest movement at a time. Each button runs a command or fits the view. A 3D mouse needs Chrome or Edge in the browser. Game controller Plug in a controller and press any button so the browser shows it to the app. The sticks pan, orbit or zoom; the triggers zoom in and out; every button can run a command. Speed, Dead zone and Reverse up and down on the sticks are there too. MIDI controller Choose Connect MIDI. Then, for each control: pick what it should do under Add a control for (a knob or fader pans, zooms or orbits; a pad or key runs a command), choose Learn it, and turn the knob or press the pad. Each learned control is listed, with a button to remove it. MIDI needs Chrome or Edge in the browser. Share your settings Export saves the settings shown as a file; Import reads one back (settings this version doesn't know are left out, and you're told how many). Back to the defaults undoes every change for the settings shown. Not built yet A wheel dial (such as the ones on Windows) and the desktop app's own drivers for 3D mice are not supported yet. A 3D mouse, controller or knob moves the view only in apps that have handed their views over; their buttons work everywhere.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"full-screen-crosshair","scope":"basics","title":"Full-screen crosshair","summary":"Two thin lines through the pointer, edge to edge across the drawing or view, to check by eye that things line up. One switch turns it on in every app.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["full screen crosshair","full-screen cursor","crosshair","cursor","cursorsize","cursorsize 100","edge to edge","line up","alignment","align","guide lines","cross hairs"],"headings":[{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does","text":"What it does","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe full-screen crosshair draws two thin lines through the pointer: one across the whole width of the drawing or view under it, one down its whole height. Move the pointer to an edge or a corner and you can see at once whether other things sit on the same line. It is the same as AutoCAD&#039;s \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E 100.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), go to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E and tick \u003Cstrong\u003ECrosshair from edge to edge in every app\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EFull-screen crosshair\u003C/strong\u003E. It is one switch for all eight apps on this computer: turn it on in one app and the others have it too, also in tabs that are already open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe lines show while the pointer is over a drawing or a view, also when no tool is running.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EToolbars, cards and hints that sit over the view stay on top of the lines; a dialog never gets them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a finger nothing changes: the lines follow a mouse, a trackpad or a pen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft the crosshair is drawn by the drawing itself: it turns with the coordinate system (UCS), and with the view split it stays in the pane you are working in. CadCraft also has it as a cursor size of its own: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003ECursor\u003C/strong\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EFull screen\u003C/strong\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E and 100.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The full-screen crosshair draws two thin lines through the pointer: one across the whole width of the drawing or view under it, one down its whole height. Move the pointer to an edge or a corner and you can see at once whether other things sit on the same line. It is the same as AutoCAD's CURSORSIZE 100. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,), go to General and tick Crosshair from edge to edge in every app under Full-screen crosshair. It is one switch for all eight apps on this computer: turn it on in one app and the others have it too, also in tabs that are already open. What it does The lines show while the pointer is over a drawing or a view, also when no tool is running. Toolbars, cards and hints that sit over the view stay on top of the lines; a dialog never gets them. With a finger nothing changes: the lines follow a mouse, a trackpad or a pen. In CadCraft the crosshair is drawn by the drawing itself: it turns with the coordinate system (UCS), and with the view split it stays in the pane you are working in. CadCraft also has it as a cursor size of its own: Settings › CadCraft › Cursor › Full screen, or type CURSORSIZE and 100.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"unit-lists","scope":"basics","title":"Units, typed values and the unit lists","summary":"Type a length in any unit with its mark, choose which units the unit lists show, and star the unit new work starts in. A file always keeps its own unit.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["units","unit list","metric","imperial","feet","inches","feet and inches","meters","millimeters","US survey foot","survey feet","international foot","yards","miles","nautical mile","chain","rod","link","furlong","fathom","point","pica","acre","hectare","square","roofing square","acre-foot","board foot","gallon","gon","grad","radian","degrees minutes seconds","slope","percent","H:V","rise run","default unit","star","INSUNITS"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"type-a-length-in-any-unit","text":"Type a length in any unit","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-units-the-lists-show","text":"Choose the units the lists show","level":2},{"id":"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used","text":"Where the lists and the star are used","level":2},{"id":"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit","text":"A file keeps its own unit","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-a-length-in-any-unit\"\u003EType a length in any unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit&#039;s mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ft 6 in\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet and inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E450 mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3.2 m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.5 km\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2 dm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E400 nm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emetric lengths (\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is the nanometer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2.5 mi\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E250 mil\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyards, miles, thousandths of an inch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 US mi\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe US survey foot and mile\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2 rd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E100 li\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ch\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 fur\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erods, links, chains, furlongs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1 nmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1 NM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6 ftm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enautical miles, fathoms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12 pt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 pc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epoints and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type \u003Ccode\u003Enmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ENM\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ENm\u003C/code\u003E is refused, because it could be either.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eft\u003C/code\u003E is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type \u003Ccode\u003EUS ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eusft\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Esurvey ft\u003C/code\u003E. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: \u003Ccode\u003E2*3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot; + 3 ch\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so \u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 and \u003Ccode\u003E10 ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 project feet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-units-the-lists-show\"\u003EChoose the units the lists show\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear on the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E, and find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E. There is one list for each kind: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVolume\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file&#039;s unit, or the one for your region).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the unit lists\u003C/span\u003E puts the starting lists back, with no stars.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used\"\u003EWhere the lists and the star are used\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EApp\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat follows the switches\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat the star sets\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s units list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModelCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list beside the value box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit of a new model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list on the plan toolbar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring and position units in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, and the unit in the calibrate dialog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring unit, until MarkupCraft&#039;s own settings have been saved\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit lists of an item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength and area: the unit of a new item\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe angle and slope lists are saved, but no app&#039;s box follows them yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit\"\u003EA file keeps its own unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give. Type a length in any unit In a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit's mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in. Type Means 12'6\", 12' 6 1/2\", 12'-6\", 3 ft 6 in feet and inches 450 mm, 3.2 m, 1.5 km, 2 dm, 400 nm metric lengths (nm is the nanometer) 3 yd, 2.5 mi, 250 mil yards, miles, thousandths of an inch 10 US ft, 1 US mi the US survey foot and mile 2 rd, 100 li, 3 ch, 1 fur rods, links, chains, furlongs 1 nmi or 1 NM, 6 ftm nautical miles, fathoms 12 pt, 3 pc points and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch) nm is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type nmi or NM. Nm is refused, because it could be either. ft is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type US ft, usft or survey ft. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones. Sums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: 2*3 yd, 12'6\" + 3 ch. In CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch. In CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so 10 US ft is 10 and 10 ft is 10 project feet. Choose the units the lists show Open Settings (the gear on the app bar, or Ctrl+,), choose General, and find Units. There is one list for each kind: Length, Area, Volume, Angle and Slope. Each unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps. Click the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file's unit, or the one for your region). Reset the unit lists puts the starting lists back, with no stars. The choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them. Where the lists and the star are used App What follows the switches What the star sets CadCraft The units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry CivilCraft The project's units list Length: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in ModelCraft The units list beside the value box The unit of a new model BimCraft The units list on the plan toolbar The display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept) MarkupCraft The measuring and position units in Settings, and the unit in the calibrate dialog The measuring unit, until MarkupCraft's own settings have been saved TakeoffCraft The unit lists of an item Length and area: the unit of a new item The angle and slope lists are saved, but no app's box follows them yet. A file keeps its own unit The settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in Settings.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"lost-toolbar","scope":"basics","title":"Where did my toolbar go?","summary":"The bars, menus or panels went missing. Here is what hides them on purpose, the keys that bring them back, and one button that puts the whole layout back.","section":"trouble","order":5,"keywords":["toolbar gone","toolbar missing","lost toolbar","lost menu","menu disappeared","ribbon disappeared","bars hidden","show bars","full screen","panels gone","reset layout","layout","ribbon","collapsed","locked toolbar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout","text":"The quickest fix: reset the layout","level":2},{"id":"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen","text":"The bars are hidden (full screen)","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-looks-different","text":"The toolbar looks different","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-s-own-menu","text":"The toolbar's own menu","level":2},{"id":"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small","text":"Everything is too big or too small","level":2},{"id":"the-panels-are-gone","text":"The panels are gone","level":2},{"id":"on-a-phone","text":"On a phone","level":2},{"id":"still-missing","text":"Still missing?","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ENothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout\"\u003EThe quickest fix: reset the layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear button in the app bar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app&#039;s own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E in Studio&#039;s own window also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. Each app&#039;s windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen\"\u003EThe bars are hidden (full screen)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe screen-mode button in the app bar, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the bars are hidden, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave this mode, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScreen\u003C/span\u003E: the app does what is chosen there when it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab, or tap the top edge of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-looks-different\"\u003EThe toolbar looks different\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools in another order, or tools you can&#039;t find:\u003C/strong\u003E someone arranged the toolbar. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOriginal order\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly icons, or only words:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar buttons\u003C/span\u003E in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools won&#039;t move:\u003C/strong\u003E the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E, or clear \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA ribbon that shows only its tab names:\u003C/strong\u003E it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMenus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar:\u003C/strong\u003E a menu style is on. Pick the app&#039;s own style under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › the app&#039;s tab › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E. Every command is in every style; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E finds any of them by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-s-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone arranging\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, when it looks right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: a locked toolbar can&#039;t be arranged or reset by accident.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: tools back in their first order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon menu style: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollapse the ribbon\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin the ribbon open\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a keyboard, the menu key (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift F10\"\u003E⇧ F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens it for the tool that has the focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanel text size\u003C/span\u003E beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio&#039;s own window has one in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-panels-are-gone\"\u003EThe panels are gone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for a second window called after the app with &quot;panels&quot;, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanels\u003C/span\u003E, which brings every panel home.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-phone\"\u003EOn a phone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-missing\"\u003EStill missing?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind any tool by name with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app&#039;s name and what you were doing.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Nothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back. The quickest fix: reset the layout Open Settings with the gear button in the app bar, or press Ctrl+,. Choose the General tab. Under Layout, choose Reset the layout. The toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app's own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under Menus, keys and mouse). In CadCraft Studio, Settings in Studio's own window also has Reset the layout in every app. Each app's windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout. The bars are hidden (full screen) The screen-mode button in the app bar, and Ctrl+Shift+F, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back. While the bars are hidden, a small Show bars tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press Ctrl+Shift+F to leave this mode, or Esc to leave full screen. If the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at Settings › General › Screen: the app does what is chosen there when it opens. Tap the Show bars tab, or tap the top edge of the screen. The toolbar looks different Tools in another order, or tools you can't find: someone arranged the toolbar. Choose Original order under Settings › General › Toolbar. Only icons, or only words: Toolbar buttons in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name. Tools won't move: the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose Unlock the toolbar, or clear Lock the toolbar under Settings › General › Toolbar. A ribbon that shows only its tab names: it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open. Menus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar: a menu style is on. Pick the app's own style under Settings › the app's tab › Menus, keys and mouse. Every command is in every style; Ctrl+K finds any of them by name. The toolbar's own menu Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu: Arrange the toolbar: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose Done arranging in the same menu, or press Esc, when it looks right. Lock the toolbar / Unlock the toolbar: a locked toolbar can't be arranged or reset by accident. Reset the toolbar: tools back in their first order. With a ribbon menu style: Collapse the ribbon or Pin the ribbon open. On a keyboard, the menu key (or Shift+F10) opens it for the tool that has the focus. Everything is too big or too small Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. Panel text size beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio's own window has one in its Settings too. The panels are gone Panels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window). Look for a second window called after the app with \"panels\", and choose Put the panels back in it. Or choose Reset layout under Settings › General › Panels, which brings every panel home. On a phone On a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The Tools button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and Move puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen. Still missing? Find any tool by name with Ctrl+K, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app's name and what you were doing.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does.","lang":"en"}],"locale":"pt-BR","chain":["pt-BR","pt","en"]};
